{
  "raw": {
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49039": {
      "title": "Category Manager – Hard Facilities Management  - Estates",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Commercial and Procurement; London; £43,981-£54,019​; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00357 About us: UCL is London’s leading multidisciplinary university, with more than 18,000 staff and over 51,000 students from more than 150 countries. Alongside our global reputation for teaching and research, UCL has a significant and diverse commercial landscape, spending over £900 million each year with third-party suppliers. Our Commercial & Procurement function plays an important role in ensuring this spend delivers value for UCL. We are focused on effective sourcing, strong supplier management and better demand management, while supporting UCL’s Sustainable Procurement Strategy and creating the financial capacity to deliver our ambitious 2034 strategic plan. About the role: We are looking for a Category Manager – Facilities Management to join our Commercial & Procurement Services team within Estates. Reporting to the Head of Category – Facility Operations, you will manage procurement and commercial activity across the facilities categories including procurement of furniture, cleaning and logistics services. Working closely with stakeholders to understand their requirements and deliver effective commercial solutions. You will develop category plans, manage tenders and contract negotiations, support supplier and contract management, and monitor supplier performance. A key part of the role will be identifying opportunities to deliver efficiencies, cost savings and value for money. You will develop negotiation strategies, manage commercial arrangements and support the transition of new suppliers, while providing advice on contractual, commercial and compliance matters. You will also contribute to continuous improvement, innovation and UCL’s wider procurement and sustainability objectives. A full job description is attached to this advert. About you: You will be a commercially minded procurement professional with the ability to build effective relationships and work confidently with a diverse range of stakeholders. You will have experience of delivering procurement and commercial activity and be comfortable balancing multiple priorities in a busy environment. You will bring strong planning, organisation and communication skills, excellent attention to detail and the ability to use evidence and data to inform your approach. Experience of negotiation and working within a large, complex and decentralised organisation would be particularly valuable. Please review our job description, paying particular attention to the Essential and Desirable criteria, and ensure these are covered in your application. What we offer: Alongside the exciting opportunity this role presents, we also offer some great benefits: • 41 Days of holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Hybrid working • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity,paternityand adoption pay • Employeeassistanceprogramme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48804": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11306 About us: The Faculty of Life Sciences is an exciting and dynamic environment driving forward ambitious and challenging plans for future development. We are committed to enhancing research and teaching in relation to major global challenges, including disease, climate change, sustainability, technological change and the data revolution, and inequality. Ranked No. 1 in London and No. 10 in the world (QS World University Rankings 2021) the Faculty offers an outstanding and diverse portfolio of Undergraduate and Postgraduate programmes in biological and biomedical sciences, pharmacy, pharmacological and pharmaceutical sciences. The Faculty is made up of five academic Divisions; the School of Pharmacy, Biosciences, the Laboratory for Molecular Cell Biology, the Sainsbury Wellcome Centre for Neurocircuits and Behaviour and the Gatsby Computational Neuroscience Unit. In addition it is home to a specialist teaching facility; the Cruciform Teaching Facilities Unit. Some of the constituent parts of the Faculty have long and distinguished histories that can be traced back to the early 19th century when UCL was founded and we are proud to have nine Nobel Laureates associated with Life Sciences Research is integrated into many of our degrees giving students the opportunity to make an original contribution to their field of study. The Faculty builds on research strengths in cell and developmental biology, computational biology, evolutionary and population genetics, cellular and circuits neuroscience, and structural and molecular biology to improve lives and communities and create real world impact. Biosciences is one of the world’s foremost centres for research and teaching in the biological sciences and one of the largest Divisions within UCL, undertaking a significant amount of research and teaching. The Division has a diverse portfolio addressing all areas of biology from protein interactions to cell function, organism development, genetics, population studies and the environment. Computational modelling approaches are frequently used alongside experimental research programmes and much of our research crosses traditional boundaries, including the relationship of biodiversity to the health of the planet. Activity is underpinned by high calibre science technology platforms and state of the art equipment. Educational activity includes a range of undergraduate programmes, an expanding number of Masters Programmes and a substantial number of postgraduate research students. The UCL Laboratory for Molecular Cell Biology (LMCB) , is an internationally renowned, multidisciplinary, molecular and cell biology department, located in the heart of UCL’s London Bloomsbury Campus. LMCB scientists explore the dynamic control of cells and their environment during development, physiology and disease using a range of model systems and advanced molecular and imaging technologies. Current research themes include cell signalling and decision making, tissue architecture, homeostasis and regeneration, cell-matrix interactions, RNA biology and the physics of living systems. This is an exciting opportunity to join the Lloyd Lab . You will lead your own project while working in a collaborative and innovative team who are committed to excellence in research. About the role: The Lloyd Lab, funded by CRUK, aims to understand the homeostasis and regeneration of the peripheral nervous system and the roles of these processes in cancer. To deliver this role effectively you will need a combination of excellent understanding of molecular, cell biology and imaging, with a special focus on peripheral nerve biology as well the ability to establish effective and collaborative relationships. Interviews are scheduled to take place in late October/early November 2026. This is a fixed-term, part-time (18.25 hours/week) role ending on 30/04/2027. The anticipated start date is: as soon as possible. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. If you have any queries about the role, please contact Professor Alison Lloyd at alison.lloyd@ucl.ac.uk. If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact the FLS HR team at fls.hr@ucl.ac.uk. About you: The successful candidate will be part of a world-leading, collaborative research team studying peripheral nerve biology in health and disease. The candidate should possess: • Strong technical and organisational skills • an extensive knowledge of research techniques and methodologies related to imaging • Experience in mouse work including colony management and in vivo work • experience in data analysis • willingness to contribute to a supportive and collaborative environment among team members and to advocate for and promote an endeavour of equality, diversity, and inclusion among colleagues and other LMCB members. What we offer: The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/policies-advice/ways-working to find out more. Salary Range for Research Assistants on Grade 6B is: £39,148 - £41,833 (including London Allowance). As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our division holds an Athena SWAN Bronze award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48519": {
      "title": "2 x Clinical Lecturer (Teaching) in Implant Dentistry (0.2FTE)",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11230 About us: The Department of Continuing Professional Development (CPD) was established in 1995 and offers comprehensive and structured CPD programmes for general dental practitioners, consultants, specialists and all other members of the dental team. Short educational programmes and longer part-time substantive degree programmes are also offered, together with revision courses and distance learning programmes. This type of education and training is facilitated by the emphasis on small group teaching which is evidence-based. Staff are experienced postgraduate teachers with high levels of clinical expertise. The Department delivers the Dental Manikin (DM) section of the Overseas Registration Examination (ORE) for the General Dental Council, as part of the ORE Consortium which consists of UCL Eastman Dental Institute, the Royal ENT and the Eastman Dental Hospital, Queen Mary University of London and the Royal College of Surgeons of England. About the role: The main purpose of the post is to ensure the development, coordination and smooth running of the prestigious part-time Masters in Implant Dentistry for Dental Practitioners. In addition, the post is involved in the design and delivery of short courses and teaching on other postgraduate programmes (including the supervision of MSc dissertation/research projects) and courses, in the fields of Prosthodontic Dentistry, including related training and mentoring. The MSc in Implant Dentistry runs over 3 years with over 82 contact days including compulsory induction/enrolment and examination days. Teaching takes place throughout the academic year on Tuesdays. We have two part-time posts available at CL8, each at 0.2 FTE, with a starting salary of £12,816.80–£14,402.20 per annum (20% of the full-time salary range of £64,084–£72,011), inclusive of London Allowance. Salary on appointment will be dependent on experience. This role does not meet the requirements for a Skilled Worker visa certificate of sponsorship under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. UCL will not be able to sponsor individuals who require a right to work in the UK. Interviews will be held on Tuesday 24 th November 2026 in the morning . The interview process will include a practical exercise, a short presentation, and an interview. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. UCL Terms and Conditions of Service: Research and Professional Services Staff Find out about the UCL Ways of Working: www.ucl.ac.uk/ways-of-working . If you have any queries about the role, please contact Dr Dev Patel at dev.patel@ucl.ac.uk. If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact Khadija Bouzgan at hr-eastman@ucl.ac.uk. About you: You must be a fully qualified dental practitioner and must be registered with the GDC, or be eligible to register. The post-holder will have experience of delivering postgraduate teaching in Implant and Prosthodontic Dentistry, and will also have experience of delivering continuing professional development courses. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49034": {
      "title": "Accommodation Advisor",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £31,904-£36,433; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00385 About us: UCL is one of the world's leading universities, providing an exceptional experience for students from across the globe. As part of UCL Estates, the award-winning Accommodation team manages more than 26 halls of residence, housing over 6,500 students across London. Our vision is to become the leading accommodation provider within the Higher Education sector by delivering an outstanding student experience. We believe our accommodation is more than just a place to live. It is where students build friendships, develop independence and become part of a thriving community. We're looking for a customer-focused Accommodation Advisor to help us create a welcoming, safe and supportive environment for our residents. About the role: As an Accommodation Advisor, you will be at the heart of the student experience, providing both front-of-house and administrative support across our accommodation sites. You will act as the first point of contact for students, visitors, contractors and stakeholders, delivering an exceptional level of customer service while ensuring the smooth day-to-day operation of our residences. This is a varied and rewarding role where no two days are the same. From welcoming new arrivals and supporting residents throughout their stay, to handling administrative processes and helping deliver community events, you will play a key role in ensuring students feel at home during their time at UCL. Please note: Core coverage is required between 8:00am and 7:00pm Monday to Friday , and 9:00am to 5:00pm on Saturdays , with shifts determined by the line manager. During holiday and summer periods, work will be undertaken on a rota basis across five days out of seven, which may include weekends. Occasional evening, weekend and bank holiday working will be required to support operational needs, with time off in lieu provided in accordance with UCL procedures. Due to peak operational activity, annual leave may be restricted during key accommodation periods. Please note we are likely to have two of these vacancies, one based in Bloomsbury and the other in King's Cross. About you: We're looking for someone who is passionate about customer service, enjoys working with people and thrives in a fast-paced environment. Essential Requirements Experience in a customer-facing role - experience in hospitality, accommodation, or purpose-built student accommodation would be particularly useful. Previous administration experience. Excellent verbal and written communication skills. Ability to remain calm, professional and objective when handling challenging situations. Strong relationship-building and stakeholder management skills. Proficiency in Microsoft Office applications. Excellent organisational skills, with strong attention to detail. Ability to manage competing priorities and meet deadlines. A customer-first mindset with strong empathy and problem-solving abilities. Positive, adaptable and collaborative approach to work. Ability to work independently and use initiative. Flexibility to work a rota and adapt to changing operational requirements. Desirable Requirements Experience within student accommodation, hospitality, retail or residential services. Experience in complaint handling and dispute resolution. Experience supporting marketing, communications or resident engagement activities. Knowledge of accommodation management systems such as StarRez. What we offer: Alongside the exciting opportunity this role presents, we also offer some great benefits: • 41 Days of holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Hybrid working • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity,paternityand adoption pay • Employeeassistanceprogramme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48960": {
      "title": "Accommodation Manager",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Estates, Facilities, Accommodation and Safety; London; £43,981-£54,019​; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00384 About us: Shape an exceptional student living experience at UCL UCL Accommodation is looking for an experienced and inspiring Accommodation Manager to lead the delivery of outstanding residential services across a cluster of student accommodation sites. This is a key leadership role, combining people management, operational delivery, customer experience and commercial awareness. You will lead and develop accommodation teams, build strong relationships with colleagues and external partners, and ensure our residents experience a safe, welcoming and well-managed place to live. About the role: You will lead a team providing clear direction, coaching and support while creating a positive, collaborative and high-performing culture. You will take responsibility for performance, development and service delivery, ensuring teams understand how their work contributes to wider UCL Accommodation objectives. Working across multiple sites, you will oversee operational standards, customer satisfaction, health and safety, financial performance and supplier relationships. You will work closely with teams across UCL, including Estates, Human Resources, Marketing and Communications, Student Experience and Safety Services, as well as external contractors and suppliers. You will also lead and support key operational projects, including summer turnaround, refurbishment and commercial activity, while identifying opportunities to improve services through customer feedback, performance data and best practice. At the heart of the role is creating an environment where students feel safe, respected, supported and part of a community. You will engage with residents and student representatives, respond effectively to feedback and complaints, support community activities and ensure consistent student experience standards across your area. You will be a visible and approachable leader who understands the needs of students and is committed to delivering a responsive, professional and caring service. A full job description is attached to this advert. About you: You will be an experienced people manager with a strong background in a customer-facing environment. You will have a proven ability to motivate and develop teams, manage performance and deliver consistently high standards of customer service. You will bring strong knowledge of health and safety, experience of managing budgets and costs, and the ability to build effective relationships with a wide range of stakeholders and suppliers. You will be confident managing challenging situations and complaints with professionalism, empathy and sound judgement. Highly organised and adaptable, you will be comfortable working at pace across a multi-site environment, balancing competing priorities while maintaining attention to detail. Strong communication skills and good IT literacy, including Microsoft Office, are essential. Experience in student accommodation, hospitality, retail, facilities management or a similar service environment would be advantageous. Please review our job description, paying particular attention to the Essential and Desirable criteria, and ensure these are covered in your application. What we offer: As well as exciting opportunities, UCL offers a range of great benefits. Find out more: Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy Available documents"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49004": {
      "title": "Admissions & Visiting Electives Administrator (0.8 FTE, Fixed-Term maternity cover)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Administration and Business Support; London; £36,433-£42,891​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11330 About Us: Bringing together six UCL divisions and institutes - Cancer Institute, Eastman Dental Institute, Division of Medicine, Division of Infection and Immunity, Division of Surgery and Interventional Sciences and the UCL Medical School – the Faculty of Medical Sciences (FMS) offers a unique mix of pioneering research, best in class researchers and clinicians, cutting-edge facilities and links with world-renowned partner hospitals and institutions. The faculty is the top in the UK for research power in life and medical sciences (REF 2021) and is consistently ranked in the top 10 in QS rankings. The faculty is dedicated to delivering world-class medical education and developing translational research that benefits patients and society in the UK and globally. The Education Administration and Student Experience (EASE) staff across the Faculty work together in a close network of teams that support all of our students. The Medical School’s MBBS programme aims to qualify distinctive ‘UCL Doctors’ – highly capable, and patient-centred clinicians, equipped to practise medicine in a professional, inclusive, and sustainable way, grounded in science and best practice About The Role: The MBBS Admissions Administrator plays a key role in supporting the delivery of the Medical School’s admissions activities, including the coordination and administration of the Multiple Mini Interview (MMI) process, applicant communications and admissions events. The post holder will also support the Visiting Elective Programme, overseeing applications and pre-placement requirements and liaising with clinical supervisors, Trust administrators and relevant UCL teams to facilitate placements. Outside the main admissions period, the role provides administrative support to MBBS Student Support, as well as supporting the delivery of MBBS assessments and other Medical School events. The post holder will work closely with colleagues across the Medical School and wider UCL, contributing to the effective delivery of a responsive and high-quality service throughout the academic year. Appointment details: The post is graded at GRADE 6, the annual salary for which ranges from £36,433 - £42,891 (pro-rata) per annum inclusive of London Allowance. This is a fixed-term role (maternity cover) until October 2027. This role is 0.8 FTE (29.2 hours) and the role holder could work 4 days per week or 5 days a week with shorter days. This role is eligible for hybrid working, with a minimum of 40% on site. The usual on-site days for this role are Mondays and Tuesdays, although these may be subject to change. Flexibility is required between December and February, when the post holder will be expected to work on site as required to support the admissions cycle. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. Application process: A full job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. Please use the personal statement section to explain how you meet each essential and desirable criteria outlined in the person specification. The advert will close on Thursday 22nd 2026 at 23:59 GMT/BST, however we may close the advert early if we receive a high volume of applications. The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please visit ac.uk/ways-of-working to find out more. Contact details: If you have any queries about the role or application process please contact Deborah Caleb ( D.Caleb@ucl.ac.uk ) If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact Deepak Singh ( Deepak.Singh@ucl.ac.uk). About You: You will have experience working in a professional services environment, ideally within higher education or a Medical School, and be confident managing high-volume administrative processes accurately and to time critical deadlines. You will have excellent written and verbal communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to provide a high-quality service and liaise effectively with applicants, students, academic and clinical colleagues, and other stakeholders. You will be highly organised, proactive and able to prioritise your workload, work independently and collaboratively, and remain effective when working under pressure. Strong IT skills are essential, including Microsoft Word, Excel, Outlook and Teams, alongside the ability to manage and manipulate data using spreadsheets and databases and to learn new systems quickly. What We Offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) pro rata Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance The full range of staff benefits can be found here Our Commitment To Equality, Diversity and Inclusion : As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. UCLMS holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48965": {
      "title": "Applications Specialist - Single Cell",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Administration and Business Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11253 About us: Our mission at the UCL GOS Institute of Child Health is to maximise and advocate for the holistic health of all children, young people and the adults they will become, through world-class research, education and public engagement. The UCL GOS ICH, together with its clinical partner Great Ormond Street Hospital for Children, forms the largest concentration of children’s health research outside North America. The 2024-29 GOS ICH strategy focuses on its five scientific programmes. GOS ICH’s activities include active engagement with children and families, to ensure that our work is relevant and appropriate to their needs. GOS ICH generates the funding for our research by setting out our proposals in high quality applications to public, charitable and industrial funding bodies and disseminates the results of our research by publication in the medical and scientific literature, to clinicians, policy makers and the wider public. The Institute offers world-class education and training across a wide range of teaching and life learning programmes which address the needs of students and professional groups who are interested in and undertaking work relevant to child health. GOS ICH holds an Athena SWAN Charter Gold Award. About the role: Applications are invited for an Applications Specialist position within the Sequencing team at UCL Genomics, UCL’s largest genomics facility based in the Zayed Centre for Research into Rare Disease in Children. UCL Genomics provides cutting-edge technologies, expert guidance, and comprehensive support to enable high-quality genomic research. The successful candidate will play an important role in delivering a high-quality genomics service provision within the Single Cell and Spatial Transcriptomic streams. The postholder will initially be responsible for supporting collaborators from experimental design through sample QC and processing. This will include generating single cell and spatial transcriptomic libraries, QC and generation of the data using Next Generation Sequencing (NGS). The role also includes the associated administration using LIMS, organisational and project planning activities required to support the efficient operation of the facility. This is a varied, predominantly lab-based position offering excellent opportunities to develop expertise across a range of sequencing technologies and contribute to the continuous improvement of a leading genomics core facility. This can involve testing and validating new solutions for scRNAseq and spatial transcriptomic assays from various solution providers. You will work closely with other Applications Specialists and colleagues to deliver reliable, high-quality data and an excellent service provision to our users. As single cell and spatial technologies are rapidly evolving, the responsibilities and scope of the role may develop over time. Collaboration is central to this role and a large proportion of the work involves supporting both internal and external users, and the postholder will work closely with researchers, principal investigators, clinicians, and industry professionals as well as other facility staff to ensure projects are delivered in a timely manner and to a high standard. For candidates appointed at grade 7, additional responsibilities will include leading the expansion and strategic development of the spatial transcriptomics stream, contributing to more complex operational and administrative tasks, and delivering presentations to internal and external audiences. This post is available initially until December 2027 with the possibility of extension. About you: We are seeking a highly motivated and enthusiastic individual with a strong interest in genomic technologies and considerable practical experience in molecular biology techniques such as PCR/qPCR and sample QC. Knowledge of scRNAseq and spatial transcriptomic assays would be essential to flourish within this role. The successful candidate should be confident in following detailed laboratory protocols. Experience of operating NGS sequencing platforms and performing scRNAseq or spatial transcriptomic assays would be highly desirable, although full training will be provided. We are particularly interested in candidates who are keen to develop their expertise within a collaborative genomics facility and contribute to the delivery of a high-quality service provision. As a key member of the team, we are seeking an effective team player who can work collaboratively with others but is also able to work independently and manage their own workload. A proactive approach, attention to detail, good communication skills and a commitment to providing an excellent service will be important attributes for success in this role. Candidates appointed at grade 7 will lead on the expansion and strategic development of the Spatial Transcriptomics stream. and will be expected to be able to deliver and organise presentations in internal facility, project and departmental meetings as well as external symposia and conferences as required. At GOS ICH we aim to always have a balanced interview panel that is representative of gender and ethnic diversity. A job description and full person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. To apply for the vacancy please click on the ‘Apply Now’ button below. If you have any queries relating to your application please contact ICH Human Resources at ich.hr@ucl.ac.uk quoting the job the reference. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Hybrid Final Salary Pension Scheme Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48653": {
      "title": "Archaeologist: Imaging (INTERNAL ONLY)",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Research and Research Support; East Sussex; £29,882-£31,904​; UCL SLASH (B03) Reference: B03-03250 About us: Archaeology South-East is part of the UCL Institute of Archaeology. It is responsible for the delivery of a comprehensive range of professionally accredited archaeological services, providing an interface between the academic activities of the Institute and other communities of interest. The UCL Institute of Archaeology is the largest university-based archaeological institution in Britain, with more than 600 students, from many different countries, age groups and backgrounds, and more than 70 academic and administrative staff. ASE handles hundreds of research and project commissions every year. Much of this work is development-led archaeology, taking place on construction sites in southern and eastern England, with staff working from offices at Portslade (near Brighton, East Sussex) and Witham (near Colchester, Essex), and on the London UCL Bloomsbury campus. This body of professional research is used to contribute to UCL teaching and ASE also provides training and opportunities to gain practical experience to UCL students. About the role: The main purpose of this role is to contribute to photographic and imaging services across Archaeology South-East's commercial, research, publication and public engagement activities, whilst also contributing to archaeological fieldwork and post-excavation projects. The role includes on-site and office-based tasks, working under supervision as part of a team, and the work programme will vary according to project needs. It is anticipated approximately 50% of the role will be dedicated to photography and imaging activities, including publication-quality artefact photography, conservation photography, fieldwork photography and specialist imaging techniques, with the remainder of time spent undertaking archaeological fieldwork and post-excavation work. About you: This post is for internal applicants only. Candidates should have at least 12 weeks of service with UCL prior to applying. You will have experience working in UK commercial archaeology and have a degree (or equivalent qualification) in archaeology or a related discipline. Candidates are also expected to have: • Working knowledge of some periods/topics relevant to fieldwork in British archaeology. • Experience of photographic, digital imaging and visual content creation, including publication standard images. • Experience of using industry-standard software for image processing and digital content production, including video editing applications and associated media workflows and proficient use of Adobe Photoshop and Adobe Premier Pro. • Competent and safe use of hand-tools in excavating archaeological features and removing spoil from areas of investigation. • Methodological working in the handling and processing of archaeological finds and records. • The ability to work as part of a diverse team. To Apply outline in the questionnaire section of this application your reasons for applying and why you think you are a strong candidate for the post. There is the opportunity to tell us why you have applied for this role, and the skills and experience you bring that are required in the person specification and job description (Downloadable from Available Documents). Please provide a clear statement in response to each of the selection criteria, which shows how your qualifications, experience and training fit each of them. It would be very helpful if you could order this statement in line with the selection criteria in the “Person Specification” at the end of the Job description. Your responses to all the selection criteria form an essential part of the selection process. An answer to any of the criteria such as 'Please see attached CV' will not be considered acceptable. If you have any queries regarding the vacancy or the application process, please contact Samantha Clacher (HR Administrator) at Archaeology South-East (Units 1 & 2 Chapel Place, Portslade, East Sussex BN41 1DR; e-mail ase-hr@ucl.ac.uk). What we offer: In addition to a salary of £29,882 - £31,290 UCL/ASE employees are eligible for: • 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Hybrid Final Salary Pension Scheme • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Paid travel time (when applicable) • High quality accommodation when working away • Subsistence payments of £20 per night • H&S training relevant for your role • High quality PPE and site welfare provided • Annual appraisal and funded CPD opportunities • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48542": {
      "title": "Assistant Project Manager",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Estates, Facilities, Accommodation and Safety; London; £43,981-£54,019​; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00355 About us: Estates Development, UCL UCL is a global leader in education and research, with more than 13,000 staff and 50,000 students from over 150 countries. As we look ahead to the next five years, we are investing in our estate to create high-quality, sustainable and inclusive spaces that support our academic mission. Our Estates Development team is responsible for delivering UCL’s Capital Programme, supporting the Estates Masterplan and Academic Strategy. From improving and modernising existing buildings to creating new and adaptable spaces, our projects play an important role in shaping the future of UCL. About the role: We are looking for two office / site based Assistant Project Manager to join our Estates Development team and provide project management support across a varied portfolio of capital projects. Working alongside Project Managers and wider UCL teams, you will help ensure projects are delivered effectively, in line with UCL’s governance requirements, project processes and standards. This is an excellent opportunity for someone looking to develop their project management career within a complex and dynamic organisation. You will gain experience across the construction project lifecycle and contribute to projects that have a tangible impact on the UCL community. Your responsibilities will include: Supporting the delivery of capital projects from pre-project activities through to completion and post-project review. Organising and supporting project and programme meetings, including preparing minutes and maintaining action logs. Supporting the development and approval of business cases and project documentation. Supporting the appointment and management of consultants and contractors. Preparing and maintaining project risk registers and supporting change control processes. Raising and managing purchase orders, contractor work permits and other project requirements. Supporting contract administration and ensuring project processes and governance requirements are followed. Supporting project delivery by attending site visits to monitor project progress. Attending site to support project handover. Support project delivery by attending construction sites to undertake health and safety inspections. Working collaboratively with colleagues across UCL to coordinate project activities and help achieve successful outcomes. About you: You will have experience of working in project management, project support or a related construction environment, together with an understanding of the project lifecycle. You will be organised, proactive and comfortable managing multiple priorities. With excellent attention to detail, you will be confident coordinating meetings, maintaining project information and producing clear and concise reports and correspondence. We are looking for someone with: A degree or equivalent qualification, or significant relevant experience in project management or a project support role. An understanding of the project lifecycle, from pre-project activities through to completion and review. Experience of delivering or supporting projects or elements of projects through to successful completion. Experience supporting project or programme meetings, including maintaining action and decision logs. The ability to coordinate and plan multiple tasks and project activities. An understanding of project planning, including estimating effort, scheduling resources and identifying dependencies. Excellent organisational, planning and communication skills. Strong attention to detail, literacy and numeracy skills. Good working knowledge of Microsoft Office. The ability to build effective working relationships and work collaboratively as part of a project team. The ability to work independently, exercise sound judgement and handle confidential or sensitive information with discretion. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48436": {
      "title": "Associate Director Data and Digital",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; IT and Digital; London; See advert text; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00397 About us: UCL Estates provides and manages the environment in which students and staff live, learn and work. Through Estates team, we deliver sustainable facilities, high-quality buildings and essential services that support one of the world's leading universities. The Data & Digital Team plays a critical role in driving digital transformation, improving data maturity and enabling smarter, data-driven decision-making across the Estates function. About the role: We are seeking an experienced and visionary Associate Director, Data & Digital to lead the development and delivery of UCL Estates' Data & Digital Strategy. This newly created senior leadership role will drive digital transformation across a complex estate, ensuring systems, information and data support operational excellence, regulatory compliance and an outstanding campus experience. You will lead the evolution of UCL's Smart Campus approach, bringing together technologies including IWMS, Common Data Environments, Buildings Information Management, IoT, AI and future digital solutions to improve asset management, sustainability, operational performance and user experience. Working closely with Estates senior leaders and Information Services Division, you will define and deliver a strategic digital roadmap that aligns with wider university priorities and supports long-term organisational goals. As the lead for Data & Digital, you will oversee the delivery of key digital programmes, act as Product Owner for major technology initiatives, build and develop a high-performing team, and ensure successful adoption of new systems and ways of working. You will also be responsible for securing investment through robust business cases and ensuring that the benefits of digital transformation are realised across the organisation. Alongside digital leadership, you will own the Estates Data Strategy, driving improvements in data governance, standardisation, quality and reporting. You will champion data literacy, promote a culture of accountability and continuous improvement, and ensure reliable, accessible data is available to support strategic and operational decision-making. This is a key senior role within UCL, which is likely to command a salary of £95,000+ A full job description is attached to this advert. About you: You will be a strategic digital and data leader with significant experience delivering large-scale transformation programmes within complex organisations. You will have a strong track record of acting as the bridge between operational teams and digital delivery functions, ensuring technology solutions meet business needs and deliver measurable outcomes. You will bring extensive experience working with enterprise systems such as IWMS and Common Data Environments, alongside a deep understanding of digital transformation, data governance and organisational change. Comfortable operating at both strategic and operational levels, you will be skilled at influencing senior stakeholders, building consensus and driving engagement across diverse teams. An effective people leader, you will have experience managing and developing teams while leading through influence across matrix organisations. You will possess strong business acumen, excellent communication skills and the ability to translate complex digital and data challenges into clear, practical solutions. You will be passionate about creating data-driven organisations, improving digital maturity and delivering innovative services that enhance operational performance and customer experience. Experience developing business cases, leading agile delivery programmes and implementing change in large estates, facilities or infrastructure environments will be essential to success in this role. A degree or equivalent experience, together with a commitment to equality, diversity, inclusion and continuous improvement, will be highly valued. Please read our job description, paying particular attention to the Essential and Desirable criteria, and ensure you address these in your application. We reserve the right to close our advert after two weeks if we receive an unexpectedly high level of interest, so would recommend you apply at the earliest opportunity. What we offer: As well as exciting opportunities, UCL offers a range of great benefits. Find out more: Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48571": {
      "title": "Associate Lecturer (Teaching)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL SLASH (B03) Reference: B03-03271 About us: The Department of Philosophy at UCL is one of the leading philosophy departments in the UK. In the 2021 REF, 60% of our overall profile received the top grade (4*), the highest of any UK philosophy department. We offer several undergraduate degree programmes. Undergraduate first-year modules usually involve two hours of lectures per week; second-year modules usually involve a one-hour lecture plus a one-hour seminar per week; third year modules usually involve two hours of seminars per week. We offer taught master’s programmes, and a prestigious research master’s and PhD. Graduate-level modules usually involve two hours of seminars per week. The department has great advantages as a centre of research activity. The extraordinary number of research seminars, talks and lectures that take place in the various London colleges, the Institute of Philosophy in the University of London, the Institute of Classical Studies, the Aristotelian Society and the Royal Institute of Philosophy, all offer a wealth of opportunities. About the role: The post is offered on a one-year fixed term contract, full-time basis, with a start date of January 2027. Main purpose of the job The successful candidate will be expected primarily to provide teaching, supervision, and administrative duties associated with the following modules: ‘Philosophy of Space and Time’ ‘Formal Epistemology’ ‘Science and Pseudoscience’. Duties will also include supervision of dissertations. The appointee may also be required to carry out other specific tasks and duties, commensurate with the spirit of the role, as requested by the Head of Department. Closing date 15th October 2026, 11:59pm BST About you: Candidates must hold a PhD in Philosophy or be soon to be awarded one. Applicants must demonstrate expertise in the areas to be taught, with an aptitude for teaching and be committed to fostering a positive learning environment for students. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave plus 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts; • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48972": {
      "title": "AV & IT Support Officer (Internal position / Open to UCL Employees Only)",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; IT and Digital; London; £36,433-£42,891​; Vice-President (Operations) (B01) Reference: B01-05756 About us: UCL is a world-leading teaching and research university, often ranked in the top ten in the world with an annual turnover of well over £1 billion. Part of UCL’s vision is to take on the hardest global challenges. The Information Services Division (ISD) is the primary provider of IT services to UCL. We support and enhance learning, teaching, research and administrative processes by providing information- and technology-related services to over 50,000 staff and students of UCL and associated institutions. Our ambition is to be the leading IT services group in the HE sector and we are growing our team’s capability in experience/UX, agile development, security, cloud, service management and partnering. We are modernising our technology foundations, digitising the processes of the university to transform experience for students and staff, and partnering across the university to drive differentiation in UCL education and research. About the role: The purpose of the AV and IT Support Officer role is to provide prompt, in-depth, customer-focused 2nd line support to staff, students and honorary contract holders. The post holder will provide field, phone and remote based support of UCL Managed Desktop, desktops/laptops, mobile devices, networking equipment, audio visual equipment, multimedia equipment, video conferencing, specialist equipment and other peripherals used by academic staff, administrative staff and students. The post holder will form part of a medium sized team and will be expected to work within virtual teams across other parts of the ISD. About you: The post holder will possess deep and broad understanding in the following areas: Audio visual fundamentals Signal processing, switching and distribution Fundamentals of cabling conventions, standards and their applications Knowledge of Video Conferencing IT hardware including laptops, desktops, tablets and mobile devices Managed Desktop Services using Active Directory, Microsoft Windows, SCCM, Appsense and Ivanti Operating system support for Windows 10 and OSX Understanding of work place safety including carrying out informal risk assessments Experience of supporting large scale conferences and high profile events Experience of using fault logging system Experience of network switches, patching and Wireless networks Experience of manual handling regulations Additional skills: Excellent problem solving skills including an ability to diagnose complex hardware and software faults Excellent communication skills Be able to work under pressure Professional and supportive towards colleagues, staff, students and others The post holder will also be able to demonstrate the following ways of working indicators Responding to colleague, student and stakeholder needs with the intention to provide an excellent service Building rapport and being comfortable with others across different cultures and background Working methodically, prioritising and managing tasks What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48489": {
      "title": "Business Analyst",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Administration and Business Support; London; £56,431-£66,437​; Vice-Provost (Strategy) (B24) Reference: B24-00093 About us: Since our founding 200 years ago, UCL has blazed its own trail, fostering a vibrant, diverse community of world-class academics, staff, students, and alumni who aren’t afraid to think big and think differently. Together, we question the status quo, challenge assumptions, and tackle some of the world’s most complex problems head-on. Located in the heart of London, UCL is consistently ranked among the top ten universities in the world. With more than 16,000 staff and 50,000 students from 150 different countries, and an annual turnover of more than £1.2 billion a year, we are London’s leading multi-disciplinary university. Alongside our global repoution for excellence in teaching, we are recognised as one of the most successful and influential research institutions in the world. UCL’s strategy sets out clear, cross-university priorities. To turn these bold ambitions into action, we established the Strategic Change Team. The team brings together new and established capabilities and is at the heart of building and delivering a prioritised portfolio of change initiatives. These initiatives will enable UCL to achieve its strategic objectives, deliver cohesive services and implement the enabling digital technologies that support them. We provide leadership, partnership and hands on help to ensure the delivery of fit-for-purpose solutions that create meaningful value for our community. This is your opportunity to bring fresh thinking, make a tangible impact, and help shape transformation across a world-leading institution. About the role: Please note this is a 6 month fixed term contract As a Business Analyst, you will explore and analyse business services and processes to develop and shape requirements into discrete ‘value add’ pieces of work. You'll be working with Agile product teams or programme teams to design and develop suitable solutions and more effective ‘to-be’ service / process models to support the user community in defining their needs. Additionally, you will work with a broad range of stakeholders including users, service owners and product delivery teams to support the business case, project scope definition and full project delivery lifecycle. Your responsibilities will include the following: • Providing information about targeted business services and change initiatives for use in the construction of business cases • Explore how services or processes work currently through interviews and research, looking at current business goals, performance, operations design, organisation structure and use of technologies • Elicit, document, and manage business requirements • Document detailed to-be process models and scenarios (user stories, user cases) in line with agreed project deliverables • Maintain an operations work plan • Support the development of best practice ideas and promote our business analysis services. • Attend events related to business analysis to increase your knowledge and proactively maintain networks with other professionals in the area. Please note this role is not eligible for sponsorship. About you: You have previously worked on projects delivering change, ideally within Agile environments, and understand the business analysis service. With your excellent communication and interpersonal skills, you’ve facilitated workshops and conducted stakeholder interviews to extract relevant information and are able to prioritise and manage your time effectively. You can clearly define and articulate business process concepts to end users and colleagues across the business and process excellent presentation skills. Additionally, you can work effectively in a collaborative team environment sharing knowledge and can take ownership of problems solving them with your analytic skills and logical thinking. You have experience of applying various techniques and delivering analysis outputs including: Requirements elicitation and documentation Business process mapping & User Stories Data and system analysis and documentation What we offer: In return, you’ll have the opportunity to make a significant impact within Student Lifecycle portfolio, working alongside talented colleagues. We offer a collaborative environment where your ideas are valued, your growth is supported, and your work directly contributes to the university’s bold strategic goals. Our people really are our greatest asset and we invest in them so that they can fulfil their potential. Equity, diversity and inclusion is important to us. We are committed to creating an environment where everyone is encouraged to give their best in a place where their unique experiences, perspectives and skills are seen as valuable assets. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Flexible working options • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department is working towards an Athena SWAN award. We are committed to advancing gender equality within our department. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/ Work at UCL pages: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48698": {
      "title": "Clinical Practitioner",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Administration and Business Support; London; £36,433-£42,891​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11222 About us: UCL is one of Europe’s largest medical research centres and was one of the top-rated universities in the UK for research strength in the most recent Research Excellence Framework. The Lungs for Living Research Centre combines a large cohort of clinician-scientists working on translational research within early lung cancer detection and clinical intervention. The work has been extensively recognised in field of cancer research and comprises a wide scope including lung cancer screening, diagnostics, intervention, as well as genomics and biomarker discovery. Recent studies include SUMMIT, the largest ever lung screening study with 25,000 participants, which has been instrumental in the establishment of screening in the UK, and ASCENT, a translational study examining 500 surgically resected lung tumours for hallmarks growth. The ALPINE study is a pioneering observational cohort study investigating novel biomarkers in participants undergoing Targeted Lung Health Checks (lung cancer screening service at University College London Hospitals, in collaboration with the North Central London Cancer Alliance). The study aims to recruit 10,000 participants and generate matched biospecimens, CT scans, and clinical data to advance early lung cancer detection strategies. About the role: We are looking for a candidate with excellent interpersonal skills, who is motivated in optimising clinical research pathways and who will thrive when given responsibility over the delivery of an ambitious study. The Clinical Trial Practitioner (CTP) will play a critical role in participant recruitment, consent, sampling, coordination, and data collection, ensuring the study is conducted to the highest ethical and regulatory standards. The CTP will support the delivery of the ALPINE study by working closely with clinicians, researchers, radiologists and administrative teams. There is a lot of potential for the CTP to develop their career skillsets with a wealth of training opportunities. Sample collection will necessitate clinical procedural skill such as buccal swabbing and venipuncture, and there will be provisions for training in these areas. This role is crucial in ensuring efficient participant engagement, collection of high-quality clinical data, and ensuring that the study runs smoothly. Their work has the potential to make a huge difference to the lives of thousands of people who are at risk of developing lung cancer and transform the landscape of early cancer detection. This post is not suitable for UCL sponsorship and is available for 5 months in the first instance. About you: The ALPINE study team are looking for a motivated Clinical Trial Practitioner to assist in delivering a unique study exploring early biomarkers for chronic lung disease and lung cancer. Your day-to-day responsibilities will include: Identify and recruit participants for the ALPINE study, ensuring eligibility criteria are met. Conduct informed consent discussions and obtain written consent from participants in line with ethical guidelines. Coordinate and perform the collection of biological samples, including blood, buccal swabs, and breath condensate. Ensure accurate and timely data entry onto study databases (e.g., REDCap, RADVenture) while maintaining compliance with data protection regulations. Act as a liaison between participants, clinical teams, and research staff to facilitate smooth study execution. Work closely with radiologists, pathologists, and oncologists to track participant outcomes and follow-up assessments. Ensure adherence to GCP, HTA, and ethical protocols, maintaining compliance with research governance frameworks. Monitor participant progress, ensuring follow-up appointments and procedures are completed as per study protocol. Attend and contribute to study team meetings, providing updates on recruitment progress and data quality. Maintain accurate records of study-related activities, ensuring audit readiness. Assist in the preparation of study reports, ethics applications, and regulatory submissions as required. What we offer: This is an exciting opportunity to join a multidisciplinary team working across the Division of Medicine. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nurser On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48337": {
      "title": "Clinical Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11247 About us: The Department of Neuroinflammation focuses primarily on research into multiple sclerosis (MS), employing a broad range of basic and clinical methodologies, including clinical therapeutic trials. The department is internationally recognised as a centre of excellence for MS research, building on the legacy of Professor Ian McDonald, whose contributions are memorialised in the widely accepted McDonald Criteria for the diagnosis of MS. Based within the department, the Queen Square Multiple Sclerosis Centre (QSMSC) operates across multiple domains of MS research and integrates research with clinical care across UCL, UCLH, and the National Hospital for Neurology and Neurosurgery (NHNN). About the role: We are seeking a Clinical Research Fellow to support the clinical delivery of the REFINE-MS trial, a multi-centre, phase 3 study led by Professor Olga Ciccarelli and Professor Max Parmar at UCL. The trial aims to identify the optimal dosing frequency of ocrelizumab in patients with stable relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis (RRMS). You will play a key role in patient recruitment, clinical assessments, and data collection, working closely with colleagues at UCLH and the MRC Clinical Trials Unit. Responsibilities will also include coordinating blood sample analysis, contributing to data interpretation, and supporting public and patient engagement activities. This academic post offers opportunities to participate in additional research projects and the clinical care of people living with MS. An honorary contract will be sought from UCLH NHS Foundation Trust, and other Trusts where the postholder may provide input, for which a DBS check will be required. The post is available from 01 November 2026 and funded by the National Institute for Health and Care Research - Efficacy and Mechanism Evaluation (NIHR EME) for two years in the first instance. If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online, or have any queries regarding the application process, please contact the Institute of Neurology HR Team ( ion.hradmin@ucl.ac.uk ). Informal enquiries regarding the role can be addressed to Professor Olga Ciccarelli (email: o.ciccarelli@ucl.ac.uk ). A full job description and person specification for this role can be accessed below. To apply, please upload a current CV, complete the online application form, and use the supporting statement section or upload a cover letter to outline how you meet the essential and desirable criteria for the role. Please do not upload any additional attachments as these will not be considered by the selection panel. About you: You will be a registered medical practitioner with a licence to practise in the UK (i.e., GMC registration) and have completed the MRCP or hold an equivalent qualification from an international institution. You will have a good understanding of the biology of neurological conditions and/or clinical assessments (e.g., EDSS, patient-reported outcome measures), with experience of working with Multiple Sclerosis patients, and the ability to provide clinical supervision. Excellent communication, interpersonal, and organisational skills, along with the ability to work independently and use your own initiative, are essential for this role. This role meets the eligibility requirements for a skilled worker certificate of sponsorship or a global talent visa under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore, UCL welcomes applications from international applicants who require a visa. What we offer: The role is offered in the range of £52,748 - £82,026 per annum including London Allowance, on the CL7 or Resident Doctors Pay Scale, dependent on NHS contract and/or stage of clinical training. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits, some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan On-site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible, and job share basis wherever possible. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: The Institute prides itself on operating in an all-inclusive environment irrespective of personal, physical, or social characteristics. Teamwork is highly valued, individual strengths are recognised and celebrated, and we are committed to advancing the careers of everyone. 12% of Institute staff are actively working on Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) initiatives; visit our EDI website for more information about our initiatives and priorities. The Institute also holds an Athena SWAN Silver award , in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality. As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce; these include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, LGBTQI+ and gender diverse people in all roles, and women in Grade 9 and 10 roles."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48212": {
      "title": "Clinical Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Clinical; London; £39,254 - £55,025; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11220 About us: This is an exciting opportunity for a Clinical Research Fellow within the Centre for Medical Imaging (CMI). CMI has expertise in multimodality gastrointestinal, oncological, musculoskeletal and neurological imaging, as well as medical physics, statistical methodology, health economics, modelling, and health behaviour. The academic department is based in the Imaging Division of University College London Hospitals NHS Foundation Trust (UCLH), one of the largest NHS trusts in the United Kingdom, with a secondary office in Charles Bell House. While we are most proud of our research, we are especially pleased with the convivial and enabling atmosphere within the Centre. This engenders an enjoyable and stimulating environment so that all employees can achieve the very best of their potential. CMI aims to remain in the UK at the forefront of clinical imaging research worldwide so that we can ultimately improve patient outcomes. The UCL PROMIS and PRECISION studies have revolutionised the diagnosis and management of prostate cancer. Multi-parametric MRI (mpMRI) is now routinely used across the NHS for the diagnosis of prostate cancer. mpMRI has transformed care by enabling targeted biopsy, reducing unnecessary procedures, and improving early detection. However, mpMRI remains imperfect. Its negative predictive value is strong, but its positive predictive value is limited — meaning that many men with MRI-visible lesions still undergo unnecessary biopsy. Furthermore, mpMRI produces a wide diagnostic spectrum of lesions whose true biological behaviour is not always clear. These limitations highlight three major research gaps: Diagnostic gap – improving accuracy to avoid unnecessary biopsies; Screening gap – creating a better first-line test for population screening; Prognostic gap – identifying which Gleason 3+4 cancers will progress. CMI is running a set of imaging trials, including the ASPIRE , TRANSFORM , and HistoMRI, together with several AI prostate studies. These are designed to address critical gaps. The Clinical Research Fellow will contribute directly to this next generation of imaging research, helping to refine diagnostic accuracy, develop screening-appropriate MRI tools, and advance MRI-based prognostic assessment for men on active surveillance. About the role: We are seeking a Clinical Research Fellow (CRF) to conduct prostate cancer clinical imaging research. The CRF will support the design, delivery, and analysis of prostate cancer imaging trials within the Centre for Medical Imaging. The CRF will lead clinical trial assessment workflows, oversee patient recruitment and safety, contribute to protocol development, and ensure high-quality research delivery. The postholder will be encouraged to pursue a higher degree (PhD/MD) as part of this programme. This is an exciting opportunity for a CRF to work at the frontiers of clinical translation of MR technologies to rapidly and directly benefit on the care of cancer patients. This role is available for 2 years. About you: You will have a medical degree and full registration with the GMC and Clinical experience relevant to imaging, oncology or urology. You will have Knowledge of clinical research governance and Good Clinical Practice (GCP) with an ability to prioritise and manage a varied clinical research workload. Strong organisational skills and attention to detail in clinical documentation. It is essential that you are able to work effectively both independently and in a multidisciplinary team, with excellent written and verbal communication skills in English, with an ability to explain complex clinical concepts to patients and colleagues. You must display commitment to high-quality clinical research and patient-centred care. What we offer: This is an exciting opportunity to join a multidisciplinary team working across the Division of Medicine. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49066": {
      "title": "Clinical Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Clinical; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11368 About us: We are looking for a highly motivated and committed clinical research fellow. The post will principally support the quarantine phase of the international Mucosal Immunity in Human Coronavirus Challenge (MUSICC) project delivered at the Royal Free Hospital supported by the UCL Institute of Infection, Immunity and Transplantation. In addition to research activities, the post holder will join the Royal Free Hospital infection service which is a world-leading department providing outpatient and inpatient infectious diseases clinical care, and is the national referral centre for contact High Consequence Infectious Diseases (HCID). The post is ideally suited to, but by no means limited to, a doctor early in infection training seeking research experience, or an IMT doctor considering application to infection training. About the role: The post holder will act as the main study doctor supporting the safe and compliant delivery of the SARS-CoV-2 Omicron EG.5.1 human challenge study (COVHIC003) as part of the Royal Free-UCL Controlled Human Infection Model research team. This will include volunteer screening, consent, inoculation of SARS-CoV-2 challenge agent, clinical monitoring during the quarantine periods, and contribution to study documentation. The successful applicant will also join the Fink laboratory group at the UCL Institute of Infection, Immunity and Transplantation (IIT) based in the state-of-the-art Pears Building at the Royal Free Campus. The post holder will also have the opportunity to contribute to UCL MBBS infection and immunology teaching. The post holder will also join the clinical infection service at the Royal Free Hospital receiving training and supervised clinical experience in infectious diseases. About you: The post-holder will: Act as delegated study doctor for the SARS-CoV-2 human challenge study, supporting safe and compliant delivery of viral challenge, quarantine and follow-up procedures in accordance with the study protocol and Good Clinical Practice (GCP). Work collaboratively with the wider multidisciplinary human challenge study team, including project manager, nursing, and laboratory colleagues. Contribute to the preparation and review of study documentation, including study electronic data capture (using REDCap), protocols, participant information sheets, consent forms and case report forms. Participate in the general clinical infection service at the Royal Free Hospital, receiving structured training and supervised clinical experience in infectious diseases. Have the opportunity to allocate time for working within the IIT research laboratory, gaining exposure to translational immunology and infection research techniques. Have the opportunity to allocate time for UCL MBBS undergraduate teaching. Please see the attached job description and person specification for full details. Please also read the attached Candidate Guidance document. All applications must include a supporting statement, telling us, using examples, how you meet the essential criteria listed in the job description. Applications without a supporting statement will be rejected. If you have any queries about the role or application process, technical issues, or need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online, please contact the staffing team at hr.ii@ucl.ac.uk . The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues in being successful and happy at UCL by sharing expectations around how we work. Please visit www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/policies-advice/ways-working to learn more. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48857": {
      "title": "Departmental Officer",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £36,433-£42,891​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07863 About us: The UCL Department of Electronic & Electrical Engineering is looking to recruit a Departmental Officer to join our Professional Services team. The post holder will be a key member of the team providing a reception and facilities management service in the department as well as administrative support to staff and students. About the role: The position comprises administrative support, reception and facilities management, and events, in addition to being responsible for a range of tasks which support staff and students in a large and busy department. The post holder will be required to establish good working relationships with departmental academic and professional services staff with whom they will work closely. The postholder will provide a welcoming front of house service to staff, students and all visitors to the department. This roles involves dealing with multiple tasks and customers at the same time and is key to the smooth running of the department. The post is available from 1 st December and is open-ended. This is an on-site role with an expectation of 100% on campus attendance, Monday – Friday. Attendance may vary depending on event requirements. About you: Experience of working within a higher education and/or service environment is essential, as is experience of working in a busy customer focused and customer facing environment. The post holder should possess excellent communication skills (written and oral) and good interpersonal skills and the ability to deal with a variety of people, both in in UCL and external agencies. They must be efficient, organised and take great care in attention to detail. A professional, friendly and helpful attitude is essential. The ideal candidate will be able to work independently as well as part of a small team. Experience of working in a reception and/or in an HR supporting role is desirable. Application details: To apply for the role, click the 'Apply Now' button at the bottom or top of the page Please upload a cover letter outlining how you meet the criteria in the person specification (document linked under 'Available documents') Applications close on 19th October at 23:59 A full job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page Informal enquiries regarding this post and the application process can be addressed to Rebecca Thomas at becca.thomas@ucl.ac.uk. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48974": {
      "title": "Deputy Director of Operations",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Professional Services Leadership; London; £68,284-£78,386​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11275 About us: UCL is a world leading, global university with a community of more than 51,000 students from 150 countries and over 18,000 staff which pursues academic excellence, breaks boundaries, and makes a positive impact on real world problems. We are consistently ranked among the top ten universities in the world and are one of only a handful of institutions rated as having the strongest academic reputation. We are ambitious for change and are committed to taking ever bolder and more impactful interventions towards a becoming a more diverse, equitable and inclusive university. Faculty of Brain Sciences We are a global leader in research and education focused on the mind and brain. Our vision is to solve the greatest health and wellbeing challenges in brain sciences to reduce the global burden of disease and transform society. Established in 2011, the Faculty brings together world-class expertise in psychology, psychiatry, prion diseases, ophthalmology and vision, language sciences, audiology, deafness and hearing, neurology, and basic neuroscience. It comprises six academic departments: Division of Psychology and Language Sciences Division of Psychiatry Queen Square Institute of Neurology Institute of Ophthalmology Ear Institute Institute of Prion Diseases With over 1,900 staff and 4,000 students, we are one of UCL’s most research-intensive Faculties. For any questions relating to the role, please contact Geoff Dunk (Director of Operations) on g.dunk@ucl.ac.uk for an informal discussion. This is an internal recruitment advert, available to eligible UCL workers only . About the role: The Deputy Director of Operations will make an impact and support with establishing effective professional services provision across the Faculty of Brain Sciences. The role holder will work closely with the Director of Operations to establish functional service level provision for our academic departments and work to establish consistency in our ways of working, professional development and best practice amongst Faculty professional services teams. In addition to managing large teams as the need arises anywhere in the Faculty, the role holder will be tasked with initiating and autonomously managing a portfolio of short, medium and long-term projects to improve the efficiency and effectiveness of operations, and embedding strong identification with the Faculty and institution amongst staff. The Deputy Director of Operations will take a lead on identifying and supporting organisational change in any area of the Faculty, embedding strategic thinking and best practice across the whole of the Faculty. Close collaboration across the six academic departments in the Faculty is vital. This role has key responsibility to engage senior staff before and during change, ensuring all are equipped to support their staff in turn. The role holder will work in tandem with the Director of Operations on large projects, sometimes extending beyond the boundaries of the Faculty, and through involvement in strategic institutional projects relevant to Life and Medical Sciences or the university as a whole. The Deputy Director of Operations will report to the Director of Operations, but will work closely with the Dean and Deputy Dean, Vice-Deans and other senior academic and professional services colleagues across the Faculty and wider institution. The postholder will be a role model for the UCL Ways of Working, Dignity at Work and take an active role in Equality, Diversity and Inclusivity activities. A full job description and person specification for this role can be accessed below. To apply, please upload a current CV, complete the online application form, and use the supporting statement section or upload a cover letter to outline how you meet the essential and desirable criteria for the role. Please do not upload any additional attachments as these will not be considered by the selection panel. About you: The role holder will be a senior operations manager who can thrive in a complex organisation and is motivated to make a positive impact across UCL. Experience of working in a large, complex academic environment is essential. You will be confident in providing high-quality operational leadership including leading and managing organisational change processes, translating strategic plans into operational processes, and managing business administration such as estates, budget and people planning. Alongside your experience, you will demonstrate excellent analytical skills, with the ability to interpret complex data insights and communicate insights clearly to diverse audiences. You will be a confident problem-solver who can develop and implement practical solutions, drawing on sound judgement and an evidence-based approach. You will be highly organised and able to manage a complex workload with competing priorities. Strong interpersonal and communication skills are key: you will be able to communicate complex ideas in writing and in person and will have experience of leading and managing teams, enabling team members to deliver to the best of their ability. We are looking for someone who is resilient and professional when faced with challenges, able to work independently but who also thrive as part of a team. Most importantly, you will contribute to a collaborative and inclusive culture, working closely with colleagues across the Faculty and able to build and maintain effective working relationships. This role does not meet the eligibility requirements for a Skilled Worker Visa certificate of sponsorship under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore UCL will not be able to sponsor individuals who require right to work in the UK to carry out this role. What we offer: Starting salary offered at a range between £68,284-£85,203 per annum, inclusive of London Allowance. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48932": {
      "title": "Deputy Head of Operations (6months FTC) - Internal Applicants Only",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Professional Services Leadership; London; £54,931-£64,644; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07630 About us: For two centuries, the Department of Civil, Environmental and Geomatic Engineering (CEGE) has hosted pioneers of scientific approaches to complex problems. Our teaching and research are designed to bring positive change to human society and the natural environment. We do so by collaborating across disciplines and working with and for people. Our students are not passive learners: they get to address real-world challenges, whether local or global, in partnership with industrial stakeholders. Our success is built on the vast diversity in expertise and backgrounds of our staff and students, as well as our world-leading facilities. CEGE is based on the Bloomsbury campus and features extensive teaching and research laboratories and workshop facilities. The department also operates a structural and environmental testing facility for large-scale experiments and a large-scale robotics laboratory at UCL Here East in Stratford, East London. In Dagenham, CEGE hosts two unique research environments: the Person-Environment-Activity Research Laboratory (PEARL) , which investigates how people interact with their surroundings, and the Controlled Active Ventilation Environments (CAVE) laboratory , focused on innovative ventilation systems. About the role: We are seeking a Deputy Head of Operations (Internal Applicants Only) to support the Head of Operations in translating the department’s academic vision and strategic priorities into effective operational delivery. This is a broad senior role spanning planning, finance, people, space, governance, compliance, risk, communications and service improvement. You will work closely with the Head of Operations, Head of Department and senior leadership team, providing advice, analysis and operational leadership to support effective decision-making and the smooth running of the department. A particular focus of this fixed-term role will be providing continuity and resilience during a period of organisational change, including supporting transition and knowledge retention through EASE changes and Service Simplification. You will work across the department’s Bloomsbury, Here East and Dagenham East sites, build strong relationships across UCL’s matrix environment, and deputise for the Head of Operations as required. This is a fixed-term role until 28 th February 2027 in the first instance. This is an Internal recruitment advert (eligible UCL workers only) and also available as an internal secondment role (current UCL staff only). We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible and job share basis wherever possible. This role is eligible for hybrid working with a minimum of 40% on-site. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff . About you: As a Deputy Head of Operations , you will be an experienced operational leader who is comfortable working across a broad portfolio and navigating a large, complex organisation. You will combine strategic thinking with practical delivery, using sound judgement, evidence and organisational awareness to identify priorities, manage risk and maintain momentum. You will bring strong communication and relationship-building skills, with the confidence to work effectively with senior academic and professional services colleagues and to operate successfully within a matrix environment. You will be collaborative and inclusive in your approach, while also being comfortable making decisions, managing competing demands and providing clear advice where there may not be an obvious answer. Experience of organisational change, service improvement and operational planning will be particularly valuable. You will also have a strong commitment to creating a positive and inclusive working culture, supporting colleagues through change and helping teams work effectively across organisational boundaries. What we offer: A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. To apply for the vacancy please click on the ‘Apply Now’ button below. If you have any queries regarding the vacancy or the application process, please contact Kati Massey at k.massey@ucl.ac.uk If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact the HR team at cege-hr@ucl.ac.uk . The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please visit UCL Ways of Working to find out more. In the event we get a high number of applications, we may close the advert early before the published closing date. As a minimum we will keep all adverts open for 2 weeks. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) (pro-rata) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit UCL Staff Benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Bronze award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here ."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48762": {
      "title": "Detection and Response Lead",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; IT and Digital; London; £68,284-£76,244; Vice-President (Operations) (B01) Reference: B01-05648 About us: Information Services Division (ISD) is the primary provider of IT services to UCL. We support and enhance learning, teaching, research and administrative processes by providing information- and technology-related services to over 50,000 staff and students of UCL and associated institutions. Our ambition is to be the leading IT services group in the Higher Education (HE) sector and we are growing our team’s capability in experience/UX, agile development, security, cloud, service management and partnering. We are modernising our technology foundations, digitising the processes of the university to transform experience for students and staff, and partnering across the university to drive differentiation in UCL education and research. UCL boasts a track record of ground-breaking research that transforms the lives of people around the world. Our teams provide dedicated, specialist support throughout the research and innovation lifecycle to help sustain and advance UCL’s position as a world-class, research-led university. About the role: Within ISD, this post is in the Information Security Group, which leads on all aspects of security at UCL. The role sits within Security Operations, which is responsible for security monitoring, incident response, vulnerability management and threat intelligence. You will lead UCL’s Detection and Response function, with responsibility for security monitoring, alert triage, investigations and security incident response. You will provide calm and accountable leadership during security incidents, develop the team’s capabilities, and ensure that effective processes, tooling, metrics and escalation arrangements are in place. Working closely with colleagues across Security Operations and the wider university, you will identify and address gaps in UCL’s detection and response capability, maintain and improve incident response plans and playbooks, and coordinate exercises to test UCL’s readiness. You will also build trusted relationships with senior stakeholders, service providers, government bodies and law enforcement, translating complex technical issues into clear operational and risk-based advice. About you: As the ideal candidate you will have a mix of the following skills and experience: Significant experience leading or managing security operations, SOC, detection and response, or incident response services in a complex organisation. Strong knowledge of security monitoring, alert triage, investigation workflows, incident declaration, incident response coordination and post-incident improvement. Experience working with relevant security tooling and platforms, such as SIEM, SOAR, EDR, NDR, identity protection, CSPM, CTEM and specialist incident response or forensic tools. Experience developing and improving incident response plans, playbooks, runbooks, exercises and operational process documentation. The ability to provide calm, decisive and evidence-based leadership during security incidents, including making timely decisions when information is incomplete. Experience leading, developing and supporting multidisciplinary technical teams, setting clear objectives and building capability, resilience and a culture of continuous improvement. Strong stakeholder management and communication skills, with the ability to explain technical security issues, operational impacts and risks to technical and non-technical audiences. Experience using metrics, evidence and operational data to improve services, prioritise risk and report outcomes to senior stakeholders. The ability to work collaboratively across internal teams, external suppliers and specialist partners to resolve telemetry, tooling, process and capability gaps. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48654": {
      "title": "Diary Secretary (3 days/6mths FTE)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Administration and Business Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; President and Provost (A01) Reference: A01-00100 About us: UCL’s Office of the President & Provost supports the President & Provost across the full range of their responsibilities as the academic leader of the university. The team provides high-level logistical, administrative, executive and policy support, enabling the President & Provost and their leadership team to deliver strategic priorities and lead change across UCL. The office operates in a fast-paced, high-profile and highly sensitive environment , working closely with colleagues across UCL and with external stakeholders. The President & Provost represents UCL nationally and internationally and engages with governments, organisations and other stakeholders across the higher education sector. The team works collaboratively with UCL’s Business Operations Service (BOS) and the wider network of Executive and Personal Assistants. Together, they provide coordinated, professional and effective support to senior leaders across the university. The environment requires professionalism, discretion, confidentiality, flexibility and excellent relationship-building skills , particularly when working with senior colleagues and high-profile internal and external stakeholders. About the role: This is a part-time job-share role, working 0.6 FTE from Monday to Wednesday from the office , providing high-level diary support to the President & Provost. You will be responsible for managing the President & Provost’s complex and demanding diary, ensuring that their time is prioritised effectively and that commitments are carefully balanced. You will act as a guardian of the President & Provost’s time , using initiative and sound judgement to manage competing requests, identify potential diary clashes or risks, and find practical solutions. You will need to understand priorities, build a strong working relationship with the President & Provost and have the confidence to challenge requests or say no when appropriate. The role also involves coordinating meetings, events, travel, accommodation, venues and catering, as well as supporting overseas visits and ensuring the President & Provost has the information and logistical support needed to fulfil their commitments. You will maintain accurate office tools and systems and contribute to improving processes and ways of working. Alongside diary management, you will provide ad hoc support and a professional, welcoming front-of-house service to visitors, staff and stakeholders. You will work closely with Executive and Personal Assistants across UCL’s leadership teams and contribute to a collaborative wider executive support function. This is an opportunity to work at the heart of a high-profile university office, supporting senior leadership in a fast-paced, complex and highly sensitive environment , while building strong relationships across UCL and contributing to the smooth running of the President & Provost’s Team. This role requires you to work on site from Monday to Wednesday of every week. If you have any questions about the role or the application process please contact Passant Khalifa (Senior Recruiter) at p.khalifa@ucl.ac.uk About you: You are a highly organised, proactive and experienced professional who enjoys working in a fast-paced environment where priorities can change quickly. You are confident managing a complex executive diary and understand that effective diary management is about much more than scheduling meetings — it requires judgement, diplomacy, curiosity and a clear understanding of priorities. You are comfortable working with senior stakeholders and have the confidence to build trusted relationships while remaining appropriately professional and discreet. You can communicate tactfully and assertively, including when you need to challenge a request, manage competing demands or protect senior leadership time. You have excellent attention to detail and can anticipate potential issues before they arise, while remaining calm and solutions-focused when unexpected changes occur. You are someone who takes ownership, uses initiative and looks for ways to improve processes rather than simply following them. You enjoy working collaboratively and understand the importance of clear communication and strong handovers within a job-share arrangement and across a wider executive support team. You are also comfortable working across organisational boundaries and building positive relationships with colleagues at all levels. Discretion and confidentiality are essential to the role, and you will be trusted to handle sensitive information with the highest level of professionalism. You will bring a warm, friendly and welcoming approach to the front-of-house aspect of the role, while being able to operate confidently in a high-profile environment involving senior internal and external stakeholders. Most importantly, you will be a reliable and proactive partner to your job-share colleague, helping to ensure that the office operates smoothly and that the President & Provost’s time is managed effectively. What we offer: As well as exciting opportunities, UCL offers a range of great benefits. 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Find out more: UCL Rewards and Benefits Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women.” Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48798": {
      "title": "Education Administrator",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Administration and Business Support; London; £36,433-£42,891​; School of Education (B16) Reference: B16-02252 About us: About UCL Institute of Education Founded in 1902, UCL Institute of Education has been shaping policy and helping government, organisations and individuals navigate a changing society for the last 120 years. We embrace collaboration and excellence to create a future that is inclusive and just and have been ranked number one for education every year since 2014 in the QS World University Rankings by Subject. The Academic Programmes Office (APO) is a large vibrant and dynamic professional service support team at IOE. The APO provides student and programme support for all accredited academic programmes, (undergraduate, post graduate and teacher training) as well as for all research student activity. About the role: You will support the administration of learning and teaching on Undergraduate and/or Postgraduate Taught programmes at IOE. You will be expected to deliver a high-quality administrative service to students, colleagues and partners working in collaboration with academic and professional colleagues across the Faculty. You should be pro-active in your work and demonstrate flexibility in working with a number of individuals across different programme areas including working with other administrative colleagues as part of a team. This post is available until 31 August 2027 in the first instance. About you: You will have experience of working in a busy office environment, with excellent administration skills and an organised and flexible approach to work, along with experience of working independently and as an active member of a team, supporting colleagues and contributing to the effective operation of the team. You will also have experience of developing, maintaining, reviewing and improving administrative, management and information systems. Your application form should address all the person specification points and should clearly demonstrate how your skills and experience meet each of the criteria. It is important that the criteria are clearly numbered and that you provide a response to each one. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday: 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days (pro rata for part time staff) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme (pro rata for part time staff) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to EDI: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our faculty holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48834": {
      "title": "Education Administrator (0.6FTE)",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £36,433-£42,891​; School of Education (B16) Reference: B16-02259 About us: About UCL Institute of Education Founded in 1902, UCL Institute of Education has been shaping policy and helping government, organisations and individuals navigate a changing society for the last 120 years. We embrace collaboration and excellence to create a future that is inclusive and just and have been ranked number one for education every year since 2014 in the QS World University Rankings by Subject. The Academic Programmes Office (APO) is a large vibrant and dynamic professional service support team at IOE. The APO provides student and programme support for all accredited academic programmes, (undergraduate, post graduate and teacher training) as well as for all research student activity. About the role: You will support the administration of learning and teaching on Undergraduate and/or Postgraduate Taught programmes at IOE. You will be expected to deliver a high-quality administrative service to students, colleagues and partners working in collaboration with academic and professional colleagues across the Faculty. You should be pro-active in your work and demonstrate flexibility in working with a number of individuals across different programme areas including working with other administrative colleagues as part of a team. This post is available on a part time basis at 0.6FTE (21.9 hours per week). For any informal queries about the role please contact Lorraine Fernandes - l.fernandes@ucl.ac.uk About you: You will have experience of working in a busy office environment, with excellent administration skills and an organised and flexible approach to work along with experience of working independently and as an active member of a team, supporting colleagues and contributing to the effective operation of the team. You will also have experience of developing, maintaining, reviewing and improving administrative, management and information systems. Your application form should address all the person specification points and should clearly demonstrate how your skills and experience meet each of the criteria. It is important that the criteria are clearly numbered and that you provide a response to each one. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday: 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days (pro rata for part time staff) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme (pro rata for part time staff) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our faculty holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49065": {
      "title": "Education Administrator Maternity Cover",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Administration and Business Support; London; £36,433-£42,891​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11322 About us: The UCL Faculty of Population Health Sciences (FPHS) brings together eight academic departments whose common aim is to explore and understand the underlying factors that cause ill-health and innovate to improve health worldwide. We reorganised our Education Administration and Student Experience (EASE) team in 2022 and have formed hubs to support our different types of students: Postgraduate Taught (PGT); Postgraduate Research (PGR); Undergraduate/Online; UCL East. Our EASE team is integrated across the Faculty and we work closely to complete annual administrative tasks together. We are continually looking for ways to increase efficiency, reduced duplication, and create a better experience for our staff. We are looking for an excellent Education Administrator to join our highly successful and supportive team. Based in Bloomsbury, the successful candidate will play a key role in supporting our postgraduate online students within the Undergraduate and Online programmes team. About the role: This role will provide specialist administration support to two of our established online programmes; Clinical Trials MSc and Master of Public Health MSc. The successful candidate will be responsible for ensuring effective procedures are adopted and documented for dealing with all aspects of the programmes administration. The position will support every part of the student lifecycle, including induction, attendance monitoring, assessments, student records, and teaching activities. They will play a crucial part in providing a high quality and timely service to students and staff. This role is offered on a part-time (0.85 FTE) basis. This role is eligible for hybrid working with a minimum of 40% on site. This is a maternity cover position offered on a fixed term basis. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. Application Details: This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-service… for more information. Application Process: A full job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. Please use the personal statement section to explain how you meet each of the essential and desirable criteria outlined in the person specification. Contact Details: If you have any queries regarding the vacancy or the application process, please contact Andreas Opitz a.opitz@ucl.ac.uk If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact FPHS.HR@ucl.ac.uk About you: Applicants should have a minimum of five GCSEs at Grade C or above (including Mathematics and English) or have similar relevant work experience. You must have worked in a busy office environment, preferably within higher education. You should be very organised in your work, excellent at performing administrative duties, and be comfortable using a variety of software and systems. You will need good communication skills and have the desire to offer a high level of service. You will need to be able to work well as part of a team, liaise with a variety of people, and establish good working relationships with staff and students in a teaching and learning context. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave pro-rata, 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver Award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48608": {
      "title": "Finance Officer",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Finance; London; £36,433-£42,891​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07816 About us: The Department of Medical Physics and Biomedical Engineering is one of the largest Medical Physics and Biomedical Engineering departments in the UK. The Department is proud to host internationally leading research groups covering a broad range of activities. Our staff and students have a diverse range of interests and expertise, covering many areas of physics, engineering, medicine, physiology, computer science, and mathematics. We also offer a range of undergraduate and postgraduate degrees. Our Research and Finance team is part of a wider Professional Services team within the Department, working alongside HR specialists and others, enabling the success of the Department. UCL is a world leading, global top 10 university. Made up of 11 academic faculties and the central professional service teams, we employ around 17,000 people to deliver ground-breaking research ad exceptional teaching to over 50,000 students. UCL’s Finance function is led by the Vice President Finance, Commercial and Estates (VP FCE), supported by the Executive Directors for Strategic and Operational Finance, and is part of UCL’s Professional Services. The VP FCE reports to the President & Provost and is a member of the University Management Committee (UMC). Operational Finance includes Financial Accounting, Finance Change and Financial Systems and Reporting. Strategic Finance comprises Financial Planning and Analysis (FP&A) and Treasury and Long-term Investments teams. Also within this group are Finance’s business focussed teams, including the new Faculty and Professional Services Finance team, alongside Finance Business Partnering and Management Accounting. Together Strategic Finance is responsible for the management of UCL’s financial performance and strategy and for providing assurance to UCL Council and UMC on UCL’s financial position. About the role: To provide first line finance administrative support to department(s) / division(s), ensuring the accuracy of UCL’s general ledger and projects (excluding research) through general accounting and transaction processing. The post holder will deliver an excellent customer focused service, support day-to-day finance operations such as journals, reconciliations, invoicing and purchasing, and work collaboratively with colleagues across finance to continuously improve financial systems, processes and workflows. About you: Duties and responsibilities Transactional and Financial Operations Review, process and authorise financial transactions including purchase orders, expense claims, journals and sales invoices using UCL financial systems, ensuring compliance with delegated authority limits, UCL Financial Regulations and local financial controls. Maintain accurate financial records and perform routine reconciliations to safeguard the integrity of departmental accounts and the general ledger. Monitor open commitments and outstanding invoices, proactively resolving aged items to minimise financial risk and ensure accurate reporting. Review and validate financial transactions to ensure appropriate budgetary control and adherence to agreed processes. Customer Service and Support Act as the first point of contact for routine financial queries, providing clear guidance and resolving issues or escalating as appropriate. Provide guidance and training to staff on transactional financial processes and use of UCL financial systems to promote consistent and compliant practice. Liaise with central teams, including PS Hub and Procurement, to resolve transactional issues and ensure continuity of service. Promote effective use of UCL financial systems and processes to support adoption of standard ways of working. Compliance, Governance and Service Delivery Ensure all financial activity complies with UCL financial regulations, policies and internal control requirements. Contribute to month end and year end processes by completing reconciliations and required financial returns within agreed deadlines. Deliver finance outputs in line with agreed service standards and timescales to support effective departmental operations. Maintain clear documentation and audit trails to support transparency, assurance and audit requirements. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact Medphys.HR@ucl.ac.uk The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please visit www.ucl.ac.uk/ways-of-working As the Service Simplification HRFRI Programme develops, the role may change and the postholder will be expected to adapt to any changes in processes, systems and ways of working Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Bronze award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48505": {
      "title": "Full Time PhD Studentship- Mapping Spatial Plasticity Dynamics in Colorectal Cancer",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11314 About us: The Cell Communication Lab at the UCL Cancer Institute , led by Prof. Chris Tape, is dedicated to understanding how cancer cells communicate, adapt, and evolve within complex tumour environments. Our research combines cutting-edge experimental and computational approaches to investigate fundamental questions in cancer biology, with a particular focus on cancer cell plasticity and treatment resistance. Working closely with clinical colleagues at UCLH and partners across the Cancer Grand Challenges REWIRE-CAN programme , we bring together cancer biologists, clinicians, computational scientists, and drug developers to accelerate discoveries that can ultimately benefit patients. Our laboratory develops and applies innovative technologies including patient-derived organoids, single-cell analysis, spatial transcriptomics, and high-dimensional data science. We foster a collaborative, inclusive, and intellectually stimulating environment where researchers are encouraged to think creatively, work across disciplines, and contribute to impactful translational cancer research. About the role: This fully funded four-year PhD project will investigate how cancer cell plasticity varies across different regions of colorectal tumours. The successful candidate will explore a fundamental question in cancer biology: whether all tumour cells possess the ability to switch between drug-sensitive and drug-resistant states. You will establish and analyse patient-derived organoid models from multiple tumour regions, perform functional perturbation experiments, and study responses to chemotherapy. In parallel, you will use spatial transcriptomic technologies and advanced computational approaches to map plasticity dynamics within tumour tissue. Throughout the studentship, you will receive training in a range of state-of-the-art technologies, including organoid culture, single-cell mass cytometry, spatial transcriptomics, and high-dimensional data analysis. Working at the interface of discovery science and clinical research, you will collaborate with leading researchers across UCL and an international network of scientists within the REWIRE-CAN consortium. How to Apply Submit the following by 24th October 2026- (using the apply button.) CV including: Contact details of two referees (one academic). A short statement explaining how your experience aligns with the project and person specification. Academic transcripts and certificates (PDF format). Include official English translations if applicable. About you: You are a highly motivated and curious scientist with a strong interest in cancer biology and a desire to address important biomedical research questions. You enjoy problem-solving, are resilient when faced with experimental challenges, and are excited by the opportunity to work in a collaborative team-science environment. You will have, or be expecting to achieve, a First or Upper Second Class degree (or equivalent) in a relevant subject. A strong background in cell biology or cancer biology is desirable, along with an enthusiasm for learning new experimental and computational techniques. Experience in complex cell culture systems, such as organoids, single-cell technologies, or data analysis using Python and/or R would be advantageous. Most importantly, you will be passionate about advancing our understanding of cancer biology through innovative research and collaborative working. What we offer: This is a full time fully funded 4year PhD studentship , successful candidates will receive a non-taxable annual stipend of £27,800pa covering tuition fees at Home rate and meet UCL PhD admissions criteria. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people. Our division holds an Athena SWAN Gold award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48719": {
      "title": "Governance and Benefits Analyst",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Strategy, Project and Change Management; London; £43,981-£54,019​; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00382 About us: UCL Estates provides and manages the environment in which students and staff live, learn and work. Through our Estates team, we deliver sustainable facilities, high-quality buildings and essential services that support one of the world's leading universities. The Programme and Performance team provides the insight, assurance and reporting that helps ensure Estates achieves its strategic and operational objectives. About the role: We are seeking a Governance & Benefits Analyst to support effective governance and benefits management across a diverse portfolio of programmes and projects. Reporting to the Assistant Director - Performance, you will coordinate governance processes, manage committee workflows, monitor actions and ensure high-quality reporting for senior stakeholders. You will also play a key role in benefits management, working with project teams to identify, define, track and report benefits throughout the project lifecycle. The role involves maintaining benefits registers, analysing performance data, supporting post-implementation reviews and providing clear insight to support decision-making and continuous improvement. A full job description is attached to this advert. About you: You will have experience working in governance, benefits management, performance reporting or a similar analytical role within a complex organisation. Comfortable engaging with stakeholders at all levels, you will be able to manage competing priorities, build effective relationships and communicate complex information clearly and confidently. With strong organisational skills, excellent attention to detail and a proactive approach, you will enjoy taking ownership of tasks and delivering high-quality outcomes. You will be proficient in Microsoft Office and SharePoint, with the ability to analyse data and present meaningful insights. Experience with Power BI and working within a large public-sector or complex environment would be advantageous. Above all, you will be committed to continuous improvement, collaboration and delivering best practice. Please read our job description, paying particular attention to the Essential and Desirable criteria, and ensure you address these in your application. We reserve the right to close our advert after two weeks if we receive an unexpectedly high level of interest, so would recommend you apply at the earliest opportunity. What we offer: As well as exciting opportunities, UCL offers a range of great benefits. Find out more: Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=47676": {
      "title": "Group Leader Research Fellowship Sponsorship Scheme at UCL",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11176 About us: The Research Department of Structural and Molecular Biology (SMB) is a world leading academic centre that promotes multi-disciplinary research at the interface of structural, computational and chemical biology, pioneering multi-disciplinary approaches aimed to bridge across the scales of biology, and methods to integrate disparate data across the disciplines. Our research portfolio encompasses several foci, including (i) gene expression & RNA biology, (ii) protein folding, membranes & trafficking, (iii) microbiology & synthetic biology, (iv) metabolism, cancer & biological chemistry and (v) evolution, computational biology with AI & deep learning ( https://www.ucl.ac.uk/biosciences/structural-and-molecular-biology/ ). About the role: We invite Expressions of Interest from early career researchers who wish to be sponsored for external fellowships that will enable them to establish an independent research group at SMB, via a tenure-track like process. Such fellowships include: Wellcome Trust Career Development Award, MRC Career Development Award, CRUK Career Development Fellowship, ERC Starting Grant, Royal Society URF, Royal Society Dorothy Hodgkin Fellowship, EPSRC Open Fellowship and similar awards. Our research fellows make vital contributions to our research department. They bring energy and prestige, novel and original research questions and approaches and new collaborative opportunities, as they widen or deepen our current research portfolio. We run two selection rounds per year. The deadline for this round is Monday 12 October 2026, 23:59 BST . We welcome applications with research focus on Structural and Molecular Biology with particular interest in the areas of AI-enabled Molecular Design and Engineering Biology , Microbiology , and RNA and Disease . About you: Prospective candidates will have an exceptional track record appropriate for their career stage and have to demonstrate their potential to attract competitive fellowships. Following application, selected candidates will be invited to give a research seminar and attend an interview with the Fellowship Panel via an online platform on Monday 9 November 2026 . Successful candidates will be invited to visit our Research Department, with opportunities to meet staff, students and current fellows informally and engage in short meetings with selected group leaders. Eligibility: candidates should confirm their eligibility for specific fellowships schemes and will be required to provide evidence of eligibility to work in the UK before commencing any fellowship offered. To apply, please submit: 1) a short CV with names of referees; 2) a two-page research vision/draft fellowship application; 3) a cover letter explaining how your proposal is suitable for the fellowship scheme(s) you are interested in, a timeline for application(s), and how you see this research programme fitting into the SMB Research Department. Further information can be found here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/biosciences/structural-and-molecular-biology/research-fellowships What we offer: Successful candidates will receive substantial support for the preparation of their Fellowship applications including mock interviews. Candidates that are successful in securing a fellowship are supported by and embedded into a closely-knit network of colleagues. They receive laboratory and office space, access to state-of-the-art equipment and expertise and access to research students from a range of 4-year PhD programmes. In addition, we provide bespoke mentoring and continued professional training. We expect fellows to develop into strong candidates for permanent positions within the department, or elsewhere. In line with this ethos, a large proportion of our past fellows have progressed to permanent positions at SMB. For informal enquiries, please contact the SMB fellowship coordinators at smb-fellowship@ucl.ac.uk . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48671": {
      "title": "Head of Advancement Data",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £54,931-£64,644; Vice-President (Advancement) (C16) Reference: C16-00303 About us: We are UCL: a diverse community with the freedom to challenge and think differently. The world urgently needs solutions to diverse problems. UCL was founded 200 years ago on the generosity of a collective of people who believed in an institution that could drive forward those solutions. The world needed collective support then, and it needs it again now. In response, UCL has launched Here, it will happen , our biggest philanthropic and engagement campaign yet. Here, it will happen sets out our ambitions to solve global health, societal, climate and education challenges in UCL’s next century. Here, we have the determination to double down on our discoveries, translate our innovations into impact and inspire the citizens of the future. There has never been a more exciting time to join UCL Advancement. We are a team of exceptionally ambitious, creative and passionate individuals who work together to achieve remarkable things with a global impact, and we would love for you to be part of this exciting journey and our success. Here, it will happen . About the role: As Head of Advancement Data, you will lead the strategy and development of UCL Advancement's data and technology ecosystem, ensuring our CRM, systems and data services enable world-class fundraising and alumni engagement. You'll oversee our data platforms, champion robust governance and data quality, and lead the continuous improvement of our systems to ensure colleagues have confidence in the information they rely on every day. Working closely with stakeholders across Advancement, you'll build trusted relationships, helping colleagues embrace new ways of working and demonstrating the value of high-quality, reliable data. You'll also lead a talented team responsible for delivering user-focused services, systems enhancements and innovative data solutions that support UCL's ambitious philanthropic goals. We are happy to consider applications to work on a part-time/flexible basis wherever possible (minimum 0.8 FTE – equivalent of 4 days per week, stated salary to be pro rata). This role is eligible for hybrid working with a minimum of 20% on site. To find out more about the role, please contact Rob Mynors, Associate Director, Data Intelligence & Solutions, by email: r.mynors@ucl.ac.uk . Please use the attach cover letter upload option to tell us how you meet the essential and desirable criteria in the person specification. For more information about UCL Advancement, please visit: www.ucl.ac.uk/giving About you: You'll be an experienced CRM or data systems leader with a strong track record of managing enterprise CRM platforms and improving data quality within a complex organisation. You'll ideally have hands-on experience of Blackbaud CRM; however, we'll also welcome applicants with significant experience of Raiser's Edge or another comparable fundraising CRM, alongside the aptitude and enthusiasm to quickly develop expertise in Blackbaud CRM. You'll understand that successful data management is as much about people as technology. You'll be confident building relationships across an organisation, influencing stakeholders, and bringing colleagues on the journey by demonstrating the value of accurate, trusted data and effective governance. You'll have experience leading data governance initiatives, driving continuous improvement, and ensuring data quality, integrity and compliance. Alongside your technical knowledge of CRM systems, databases, SQL and system integrations, you'll be an engaging people leader who enjoys developing high-performing teams and delivering services that put users at the heart of everything you do. We are pleased to accept applications from individuals who possess transferable skills and experience drawn from sectors outside of Higher Education. What we offer: We know that our people are our greatest asset and in return we provide an empowering environment where personal and professional growth is invested in. We offer agile working and flexibility around working hours. Working with some of the greatest intellects in the world, UCL also offers a generous pension scheme and holiday allowance, plus closure days and bank holidays. Visit www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48674": {
      "title": "Head of Advancement Data Intelligence & Analytics",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £54,931-£64,644; Vice-President (Advancement) (C16) Reference: C16-00304 About us: We are UCL: a diverse community with the freedom to challenge and think differently. The world urgently needs solutions to diverse problems. UCL was founded 200 years ago on the generosity of a collective of people who believed in an institution that could drive forward those solutions. The world needed collective support then, and it needs it again now. In response, UCL has launched Here, it will happen , our biggest philanthropic and engagement campaign yet. Here, it will happen sets out our ambitions to solve global health, societal, climate and education challenges in UCL’s next century. Here, we have the determination to double down on our discoveries, translate our innovations into impact and inspire the citizens of the future. There has never been a more exciting time to join UCL Advancement. We are a team of exceptionally ambitious, creative and passionate individuals who work together to achieve remarkable things with a global impact, and we would love for you to be part of this exciting journey and our success. Here, it will happen . About the role: As Head of Advancement Data Intelligence and Analytics, you will lead the team responsible for transforming data into strategic insight that drives fundraising and alumni engagement across UCL Advancement. Working closely with senior leaders, you will shape the data intelligence strategy, delivering predictive analytics, performance reporting and actionable recommendations that support evidence-based decision-making and maximise philanthropic impact. This is an exciting opportunity to lead and evolve UCL Advancement's data intelligence capability. Alongside setting the strategic direction, you'll oversee the development of high-quality dashboards, predictive analytics and reporting solutions that empower colleagues across Advancement to make informed, evidence-based decisions and maximise fundraising and alumni engagement outcomes. We are happy to consider applications to work on a part-time/flexible basis wherever possible (minimum 0.8 FTE – equivalent of 4 days per week, stated salary to be pro rata). This role is eligible for hybrid working with a minimum of 20% on site. To find out more about the role, please contact Rob Mynors Associate Director, Data Intelligence & Solutions, by email: r.mynors@ucl.ac.uk . Please use the attach cover letter upload option to tell us how you meet the essential and desirable criteria in the person specification. For more information about UCL Advancement, please visit: www.ucl.ac.uk/giving About you: You'll be an experienced analytics or business intelligence professional with a track record of turning complex data into meaningful insight that supports strategic decision-making. You'll be comfortable working with senior stakeholders, understanding business needs and translating them into high-quality reporting and analytics solutions. You'll have strong technical skills in SQL and/or DAX, with experience designing and building robust dashboards and reporting solutions from the ground up, rather than simply maintaining existing reports. You'll be confident working with large CRM datasets, developing analytical models and creating visualisations that enable users to make better, evidence-based decisions. Experience of Python or R for advanced analytics would be advantageous, but is not essential. You'll also bring experience leading or mentoring others, fostering a collaborative, high-performing team and promoting a culture where data, insight and continuous improvement are central to success. Excellent communication and stakeholder management skills are essential, as you'll be equally comfortable discussing technical concepts with analysts as presenting clear recommendations to senior leaders. We are pleased to accept applications from individuals who possess transferable skills and experience drawn from sectors outside of Higher Education. What we offer: We know that our people are our greatest asset and in return we provide an empowering environment where personal and professional growth is invested in. We offer agile working and flexibility around working hours. Working with some of the greatest intellects in the world, UCL also offers a generous pension scheme and holiday allowance, plus closure days and bank holidays. Visit www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48365": {
      "title": "Head of Commercial Contracts - IT&T",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Commercial and Procurement; London; See advert text; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00398 About us: UCL is one of the world’s leading multidisciplinary universities, with more than 18,000 staff and over 51,000 students from more than 150 countries. Alongside our global reputation for teaching and research, we operate a significant and complex commercial environment, spending more than £900 million each year with third-party suppliers. Commercial & Procurement Services plays an important role in ensuring this investment delivers maximum value for UCL. Our IT&T commercial activity supports the technology, infrastructure, systems and services that underpin UCL’s teaching, research and professional services. As we continue to strengthen our in-house commercial capability, we are looking for an experienced commercial contracts professional to help shape how some of our most important and business-critical IT&T supplier relationships and contracts are managed. About the role: As Head of Commercial Contracts – IT&T, you will provide senior commercial leadership across a portfolio of strategic, high-value and business-critical IT and technology supplier contracts. Reporting to the Director of Supply Chain – IT&T, you will lead the commercial and contract management of key suppliers, ensuring contracts are actively managed throughout their lifecycle and continue to deliver value for money, strong service performance and appropriate protection from commercial, operational and supply-chain risk. You will lead supplier governance and strategic supplier relationships, oversee complex commercial negotiations and contractual change, and use financial and performance insight to identify opportunities to improve value, manage cost and strengthen outcomes. You will also lead the resolution of supplier performance and commercial issues, ensuring appropriate contractual remedies and escalation routes are used where required. Working closely with Category Management, Finance, Legal, operational stakeholders and suppliers, you will help establish consistent, data-led commercial contract management practices and strengthen UCL’s approach to supplier performance, governance and commercial assurance. This is a senior and specialist role. Salary is likely to be £75,000-£95,000. A full job description is attached to this advert. About you: You will be an experienced senior commercial or contract management professional with significant experience of managing, reviewing, negotiating and improving complex contracts within a large, complex or regulated organisation. You will bring strong knowledge of contract lifecycle management, supplier governance, commercial and financial management, risk and value-for-money delivery. Importantly, you will have a good understanding of contractual terms and mechanisms relevant to IT and technology services, which could include software, IT services, infrastructure or managed services contracts. You will be confident leading complex supplier negotiations, managing contractual change and resolving commercial disputes or performance issues. Strong analytical capability will enable you to interrogate commercial and financial information, challenge costs and supplier performance, and use data to provide clear insight to senior decision-makers. You will also be a credible and collaborative senior stakeholder manager, able to advise, influence and constructively challenge senior colleagues, suppliers and cross-functional teams. With strong commercial judgement and a continuous improvement mindset, you will help strengthen supplier accountability and build a high-performing commercial contract management capability across UCL. Please read our job description, paying particular attention to the essential and desirable criteria, and ensure these are covered in your application. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent.We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49074": {
      "title": "Head of Delivery (Estates)",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £68,284-£76,244; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00327 About us: The UCL Estate comprises over 240 buildings, more than 6.5 million square feet of academic accommodation and around 4500 owned student bedrooms. The location, scale, variety and complexity of the estate are unparalleled in the sector, including world-leading research, teaching, offices and student accommodation within our Bloomsbury and UCL East campus, at Stratford Together, Campus Experience and Infrastructure and Estates Development (CE&I and ED) is responsible for managing all aspects of the UCL Estate from the strategic to the operational, including property asset management, master-planning, capital projects and seamless end to end operational support. The function comprises approximately 350 FM and property professionals, project managers and customer facing operational colleagues This key activity supports the student and campus experience for our 46,000 students and 13,000 staff across our sites. From providing student accommodation, security and catering, to delivering an estates masterplan and all forms of capital projects in a sustainable manner. We are a collective of technical professionals who work together to deliver a resilient, functional, and vibrant estate We have a proud history, and the ambition and drive to innovate. We strive to provide a coordinated and customer-oriented approach to both buildings and services, maintaining and transforming UCL’s Estate, so that it directly contributes to the creation of a truly inspirational environment for our academics, students and staff. We aim to provide the best possible experience, and responsive support, to our students and campus users in respect of the physical estate and all our campus services About the role: The Head of Delivery will set and lead the delivery culture and standards of the Capital Programme’s construction projects in delivery With oversight from supplier selection through pre-construction, construction to handing over to operations and academic occupation, the Head of Delivery will set and lead the delivery standard for capital programme works on behalf of UCL to objectively; Deliver a safe and respectful working environment for the workforce and work site adjacencies seeking industry leading-best practice in delivery Establish a high-quality interface with public realm focused on respecting and not interrupting a high-quality campus experience Ensure rigorous regulatory compliance for the works in delivery, setting the standard for how works are inspected and by whom Set a delivery model and standard for delivering world-class quality Ensure a seamless handover to operational and academic occupants, leading culture of engagement with operational colleagues and end users in the project delivery model Oversee project reporting to ensure rigorous command of works in delivery from a programmatic and resourcing lens, setting the standard for how we monitor and report on construction works in progress Oversee HSE reporting and near-miss and incident investigation, ensure robust and measured responses are actioned within the works in progress and lessons learned cascaded across the programme in delivery Ensure works in delivery meet existing UCL Standards and working closely with the UCL Health and Safety, Engineering and Operations teams to highlight if/where UCL standards do not delivery construction industry best practice and conceive pathways to agree and close any gaps The Head of Delivery will work with all respective Capital Programme Assistant Directors and UPOs, who will retain ultimate responsibility for their projects in delivery The Head of Delivery will assess skills gaps within the Capital Programme team and work will propose relevant training to close any skills gaps About you: With a focus is on delivery assurance and how we put people to work to deliver world-class places and spaces, the Head of Delivery will work with Capital Programme leadership team, the SLT and the Health and Safety and Operations team to set and define the aspired delivery culture for the programme and will review and drive enhancement in existing ways of working. Key considerations will include; Safety Culture and Project delivery leadership behaviours Roles and Responsibilities of our UPOs and PMOs in regard to health and safety and project delivery assurance Competency assessment and training/coaching to raise qualitive performance and confidence across programme Pre-Start checklists and establishing methods to assure project set up Quality of our CPPs and RAMS Training, review standards Quality of our site safety inspections Raising and closing out inspection and audit actions Monitoring and reporting progress on site Incidence response and reporting Quality of our monitoring and reporting Quality of how we put people to work daily Site set-up standards Welfare arrangements Logistics arrangements Interfaces with live academic spaces Communication with stakeholders and project environment neighbourhood What we offer: In return, you’ll have the opportunity to make a significant impact within Student Lifecycle portfolio, working alongside talented colleagues. We offer a collaborative environment where your ideas are valued, your growth is supported, and your work directly contributes to the university’s bold strategic goals Our people really are our greatest asset and we invest in them so that they can fulfil their potential. Equity, diversity and inclusion is important to us. We are committed to creating an environment where everyone is encouraged to give their best in a place where their unique experiences, perspectives and skills are seen as valuable assets As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Flexible working options Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department is working towards an Athena SWAN award We are committed to advancing gender equality within our department. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/ Work at UCL pages: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48721": {
      "title": "Head of Education & Student Experience  (Grand Challenges) and Sustainable  Development Goals (SDGs)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Administration and Business Support; London; £56,431-£66,437​; Vice-Provost (Research, Innovation and Global Engagement) (B09) Reference: B09-01429 About us: University College London (UCL) is London’s Global University. Founded in 1826, we were the first university in England to welcome students of any religion and the first to admit women on equal terms with men. Today, we are among the world’s top 10 universities, with a diverse community of over 50,000 students and 18,000 staff from more than 150 countries. UCL’s Grand Challenges is a flagship initiative within Research, Innovation & Global Engagement (RIGE). It brings together researchers, students and external partners to address some of the most pressing societal challenges of our time, using challenge-led, interdisciplinary approaches that cut across research, education and institutional practice. Alongside this, UCL’s Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) activities provide a unifying framework for showcasing and strengthening UCL’s contribution to the UN SDGs across education, research, operations and global engagement. As UCL’s Strategic Plan 2022–27 continues to be delivered, the Grand Challenges programme and our SDGs activities are evolving together, with a growing emphasis on education, student leadership and real-world impact. This is an exciting moment to join the team and play a central role in shaping how students and early career researchers engage with global challenges and sustainable development at UCL. About the role: Please note this a 12 month secondment opportunity, for internal candidates only. We are seeking a Head of Education & Student Experience (Grand Challenges) and SDGs to provide strategic leadership for UCL’s challenge-led education and student engagement offer, while overseeing delivery of the University’s SDGs activities. This is a leadership role within an expanded Grand Challenges team, bringing together responsibility for education, student experience and Education for Sustainable Development (ESD) under a single, integrated portfolio. You will embed Grand Challenges learning across the curriculum and co-curriculum, lead innovative credit-bearing and extra-curricular pilots, and champion student-led social innovation and enterprise. Working closely with academic leaders, professional services colleagues and partners including Students’ Union UCL, you will influence and support faculties to develop interdisciplinary, challenge-focused learning opportunities. You will also equip early career researchers with the skills and confidence to work in challenge-led and interdisciplinary ways. Alongside this, you will provide strategic oversight of UCL’s SDGs activities, including engagement with national and international networks, stewardship of SDG-related partnerships and innovation programmes, and leadership on how UCL measures, communicates and evidences its contribution to the SDGs. This is a highly collaborative role with significant scope to shape direction, approaches and impact, operating at the intersection of education, research, student experience and global engagement. About you: You will be an experienced and credible leader with a strong track record in interdisciplinary and ESD-aligned education within a complex organisation. You will bring excellent programme and change-management skills, with the ability to work across organisational boundaries and influence senior stakeholders. You will be confident leading multifaceted portfolios, managing budgets and supporting others to deliver ambitious programmes at pace. Strong communication and relationship-building skills will be essential, as will the ability to convene diverse groups and build shared purpose around challenge-led education and societal impact. A good understanding of the Sustainable Development Goals and their relevance to higher education, alongside a clear commitment to inclusion, collaboration and student partnership, will be key to success in this role. Above all, you will be motivated by the opportunity to help shape how students and researchers engage with the world’s most urgent challenges and contribute to meaningful social and environmental change. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48904": {
      "title": "Head of Operations",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £56,431-£66,437​; Vice-President (External Engagement) (B23) Reference: B23-00229 About us: UCL is a radically different university. Founded in 1826 in the heart of London, we were the first university in England to welcome students of any religion and the first to welcome women to university education. Today, UCL has over 50,000 students and 15,000 staff and is one of the world’s top 10 universities. The Office of the Vice-President External Engagement (VPEE) leads UCL’s engagement with internal and external audiences, helping to build affinity with our staff and students, strengthen UCL’s public prominence and reputation, and demonstrate the public value of our research, education, innovation and partnerships. Our work spans brand, integrated marketing, internal communications, public affairs, media relations and events. We are looking for a Head of Operations to coordinate cross-team activity including finance and budgeting, HR processes, and team culture and wellbeing. About the role: The Head of Operations is a key role, that helps and enables leaders and colleagues across all teams in VPEE to work effectively. An expert in financial management, HR operations, you will support and train managers in VPEE on UCL policy and best practices in: staff recruitment, effective onboarding, people processes, and effective appraisal and development conversations. You will ensure a best in sector approach is in place for inducting new staff and introduce tools and activity to measure and increase colleague engagement. From a finance perspective, you will work with the Director of Operations, VPEE budget holders and Finance Business Partner, to coordinate planning and information gathering for UCL’s annual budgeting and reforecasting processes. You will also assist colleagues with procurement processes and develop training and information sessions on Finance processes and systems. Please note, this role is offered on a full-time basis (part-time applications of a minimum of 0.8 FTE would be considered), with hybrid working available - currently a minimum of 2-days per week onsite at our Bidborough House and Torrington Place offices (subject to change). Job Ref: B23-00229 Closing Date: 20-10-2026 – 23:59 (UK time) About you: We are looking for an experienced operations professional with a strong understanding of financial management, operational planning and organisational delivery, ideally with knowledge and experience of the higher education sector and the complex environment in which universities operate. You will understand the opportunities and challenges facing higher education and be able to apply your operational expertise effectively within a university setting. You will have experience of developing budgets, monitoring financial performance and using financial and people data to inform strategic decision-making. You will be confident working with senior colleagues and professional services teams to ensure resources are used effectively and organisational priorities are delivered. You will bring experience of managing change and improving processes, systems and ways of working in support of organisational objectives. You will be comfortable working across a complex organisation, managing competing priorities and bringing together different perspectives to achieve shared outcomes. You will be an experienced people leader who can motivate and develop colleagues and work effectively through both direct and matrix management. You will build strong, trusted relationships with senior colleagues and a diverse range of stakeholders, using your influencing and relationship management skills to achieve positive outcomes. Excellent communication skills are essential, including the ability to present complex information clearly to a wide range of audiences. You will be able to make sense of complex issues, including where information is incomplete, and use your judgement to establish priorities and respond effectively to changing demands. You will bring resilience, adaptability and a commitment to continuous improvement, alongside a genuine interest in delivering excellent professional services. You will demonstrate an active commitment to inclusion, diversity and intercultural sensitivity and will share UCL’s commitment to creating an environment where colleagues, students, partners and the wider community can thrive. Experience of analysing and presenting data to support evidence-based decision-making and proactive problem-solving would also be desirable. What we offer: Standard UCL Benefits 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=47736": {
      "title": "Head of Operations",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Professional Services Leadership; London; £68,284-£78,386​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11104 About us: The UCL Ear Institute is part of the Faculty of Brain Sciences and is internationally recognised as the UK’s premier multidisciplinary centre for research into hearing and deafness. It brings together some of the world's leading academics, clinicians and healthcare scientists working in fields related to hearing, balance and ENT care. In partnership with the Royal National ENT and Eastman Dental Hospitals, the Ear Institute forms Europe's largest single grouping of audio-vestibular researchers and clinical scientists, positioning it at the forefront of innovation and discovery in hearing health. The Ear Institute offers a genuinely collaborative research environment spanning fields as diverse as genetics, biophysics, cell biology, systems and computational neuroscience, human cognition and clinical neuroscience. Alongside its research activities, the Ear Institute delivers specialist undergraduate and postgraduate courses in audiology and hearing sciences; postgraduate research degrees; and a substantial portfolio of continuing professional development and short courses for healthcare professionals. Its thriving, vibrant community includes approximately 75 academic, research, technical and professional services staff, 25 PhD students and over 100 MSc and BSc students. About the role: UCL is seeking an exceptional operational leader to join the Ear Institute as its next Head of Operations. This is an exciting opportunity to join the Institute's Strategic Leadership Team and play a pivotal role in shaping its future. Working in close partnership with the Ear Institute Director, Professor Jennifer Linden, you will provide strategic and operational leadership across all aspects of the Institute's activities, supporting a community delivering research, education and clinical engagement. As Head of Operations, you will be responsible for the effective leadership of the Institute's professional services and operational functions. You will lead a professional services team of 11 staff, with direct line management responsibility for four team members, second-line management oversight of a further four staff through those managers, and matrix management responsibility for the remaining three team members. Working closely with academic, technical and clinical colleagues, you will help ensure the Institute continues to thrive in a complex and highly regulated environment. The role offers a diverse portfolio of responsibilities, spanning strategic planning, financial management, education and student support, human resources and people management, and the oversight of specialist facilities and infrastructure. It includes supporting major research initiatives, driving service improvement, and contributing to Faculty and UCL-wide priorities. As a key member of the Ear Institute's Strategic Leadership Team, you will work in partnership with colleagues to translate academic ambition into operational delivery, foster a positive and inclusive culture, support staff and student success, and ensure the long-term sustainability and success of the Institute The Ear Institute combines the reputation and ambition of a world-leading research institute with the culture of a collegiate and community-focused department. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to work closely with colleagues across the Institute, play a visible role in shaping its future direction and ways of working, and make a meaningful contribution to its research, education and operational success. The Ear Institute is committed to fostering an inclusive, supportive and equitable environment in which staff and students can thrive. It holds an Athena Swan Silver Award in recognition of its commitment to advancing gender equality and creating an inclusive culture for all members of its community. Environmental sustainability is also a core priority. The Ear Institute has achieved Gold accreditation through both Green Impact and the Laboratory Efficiency Assessment Framework (LEAF), and is one of a small number of UCL departments participating in the pilot of Green DiSC (Digital Sustainability Certification). These initiatives reflect the Institute's commitment to ensuring its activities remain environmentally, financially and operationally sustainable. Interviews will be held on 24 November 2026. If you have any questions about the role or would like to have an informal discussion, please contact the Director, Professor Jennifer Linden (j.linden@ucl.ac.uk) About you: We are seeking an experienced and collaborative leader who can operate confidently within a complex academic environment. You will have a strong track record of strategic planning, operational leadership, financial management and delivering organisational change. You will be able to build trusted relationships with a wide range of stakeholders, influence effectively at senior levels and provide clear, evidence-based advice to support decision-making. You will also bring excellent communication skills, strong analytical capability and the ability to balance strategic thinking with operational delivery. Experience of working within higher education, research, healthcare or another complex knowledge-intensive environment would be advantageous. Most importantly, you will be committed to supporting an inclusive and collaborative culture that enables world-leading research and education to flourish. The role is based at the Ear Institute, Gray's Inn Road, London, with hybrid working arrangements in line with UCL's Ways of Working framework. Given the operational complexity of the Institute and its specialist facilities, the postholder is expected to maintain a significant on-site presence. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance F ind out more Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49042": {
      "title": "Head of Publications and Stories",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £56,431-£66,437​; Vice-President (Advancement) (C16) Reference: C16-00314 About us: At UCL Advancement, we build lifelong relationships with the alumni, donors, volunteers and supporters who help make UCL's mission possible. Our work stretches across the university and around the globe, and it's all about giving people meaningful ways to connect with UCL, be part of its community, celebrate what it achieves and help shape its future. That covers alumni engagement, philanthropic partnerships, volunteering and supporter communications. Whatever the channel, our aim is the same: to help our global community feel they belong here, and to inspire them through experiences and stories that show the difference UCL makes in the world. Right now, much of that energy is focused on Here, it will happen – UCL's major philanthropic and engagement campaign and one of Advancement's key strategic priorities. The campaign brings alumni, donors, volunteers, partners and supporters together to tackle some of the most significant challenges facing society. It's a big moment for us, but our work doesn't stop there: we're also here to support Advancement's long-term engagement, communications and fundraising goals. You'll join the Advancement Messaging, Storytelling and Content Team, part of our Alumni and Donor Engagement function. We provide strategic leadership for Advancement's communications, content and audience engagement, and we're organised around three closely connected disciplines: Strategic Messaging – narrative development, messaging, tone of voice, strategic communications planning and high-profile communications. Publications and Stories – editorial strategy, storytelling, publications, multimedia content, design, visual identity and content asset management. This is where you come in. Digital Channels – digital channel strategy, websites, email and CRM communications, social media, audience journeys, optimisation and analytics. The three Heads work as one leadership team, making sure everything we put out into the world is strategically aligned, creatively distinctive, focused on our audiences and genuinely effective at inspiring engagement, advocacy and support across UCL's global community. About the role: If you love finding the story behind the story and turning it into something people want to read, watch and share, this is a role where you can make a real mark. You'll lead our editorial strategy, storytelling programme, publications, multimedia content, design services and content asset management, sharing stories that show what philanthropy, alumni engagement, volunteering and partnership achieve. Working hand in hand with your fellow Heads of Strategic Messaging and Digital Channels, you'll bring Advancement's narratives to life through high-quality, audience-centred content. What you'll be doing Shaping our editorial and storytelling direction Leading an editorial strategy and storytelling programme that bring our priorities, philanthropic impact and community engagement to life for alumni, donors, volunteers and supporters. Managing our story pipeline, so we always have a diverse, sustainable supply of high-quality stories. Translating strategic narratives and messaging into compelling editorial content, publications, multimedia stories and creative assets. Bringing content and creativity to life Leading the planning, commissioning and delivery of our flagship publications and editorial products. Overseeing written, visual, audio and video content that's engaging, accessible and effective across audiences and formats. Leading our design and creative production services, and acting as steward of Advancement's visual identity with the guidance, templates and resources colleagues need. Keeping our content ethical and well governed Overseeing consent, rights management and content governance, so stories, images, video, testimonials and other assets are used ethically, compliantly and effectively. Collaborating and building relationships Working closely with the Heads of Strategic Messaging and Digital Channels so our content reflects agreed messaging and tone of voice, and is optimised across digital channels and audience journeys. Building strong partnerships across UCL, including with the central Brand Team, to stay aligned with institutional standards. Managing editorial, design, multimedia, print and other specialist suppliers, ensuring high standards and value for money. Measuring impact and always improving Using audience insight, performance data and feedback to evaluate our work and drive continuous improvement. Developing KPIs and reporting that show how our content and creative activity contribute to engagement, advocacy and philanthropic goals. Leading and supporting your team Leading, coaching and developing the Publications and Stories team, creating a high-performing, collaborative and inclusive culture. Setting team objectives, KPIs and service standards that focus our energy on the highest-value priorities. Deputising for the Associate Director when needed and contributing to departmental planning and strategic decisions. About you: You're a natural storyteller and an experienced editorial leader who knows how to turn big strategic priorities into content that moves people. You enjoy bringing out the best in a creative team, you're comfortable working with a wide range of stakeholders and partners, and you care about getting the details right – from the quality of the writing to the way content is governed and shared. We don't expect anyone to tick every box on day one. If this role excites you and you meet most of the essential criteria, we'd genuinely love to hear from you. Experience and Skills Experience of leading the development and delivery of high-quality publications, editorial content or publishing programmes within a complex organisation. Experience of translating organisational priorities, strategies or campaigns into compelling stories, publications and audience-centred content. Experience of developing and delivering strategic storytelling programmes that support engagement, fundraising, marketing or organisational objectives. Experience of managing creative, editorial or design professionals and developing high-performing teams. Experience of managing external agencies, suppliers or creative partners to deliver high-quality outputs. Experience of establishing content governance, asset management, information management or consent processes. Understanding of editorial standards, content governance, brand management and accessibility requirements. Excellent communication, leadership and stakeholder management skills. Ability to manage multiple priorities, projects and stakeholders while maintaining high standards of quality and delivery. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with outstanding editorial judgement and attention to detail. Ability to identify, develop and communicate compelling stories that engage audiences and support strategic objectives. Ability to provide strategic communications advice and constructive feedback in a collaborative and supportive manner. Ability to use audience insight, performance data and stakeholder feedback to evaluate and improve content effectiveness. What we offer: As well as the chance to tell the stories of one of the world's leading universities, you'll enjoy a generous package of benefits, including: Flexible, hybrid working – you may normally work remotely for part of the week if you wish. 41 days' holiday (27 days' annual leave, 8 bank holidays and 6 university closure days). A defined benefit career-average pension scheme. Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan. Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay. On-site nursery, on-site gym and an Employee Assistance Programme. Discounted medical insurance and a wide range of staff discounts. Ongoing learning and development opportunities. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London's Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world's talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. If you need any adjustments during the application or interview process, please let us know – we're happy to help."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48375": {
      "title": "Information and Data Manager",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; IT and Digital; London; See advert text; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00381 About us: UCL Estates provides and manages the environment in which students and staff live, learn and work. Through our Estates team, we deliver sustainable facilities, high-quality buildings and essential services that support one of the world's leading universities. The Estates Information Team is a new team, responsible for delivering the Estates Data Strategy and working closely with stakeholders across the organisation to improve information management, data quality and governance. About the role: We are seeking an experienced Information & Data Manager to lead the management, governance and continuous improvement of Estates information and data. Reporting to the Head of Data & Digital, you will play a key role in ensuring that Estates data and information are managed in line with industry best practice, and comply with university policies and procedures. You will be responsible for developing and maintaining information management standards, policies and governance frameworks while promoting a consistent approach to data ownership, quality and accountability across Estates. Acting as a trusted adviser, you will support and educate data owners, stewards and champions, ensuring information is effectively managed throughout its lifecycle. The role will oversee the Estates Common Data Environment and other key information systems, support digital and data transformation initiatives, and ensure quality-assured information is captured and handed over throughout the project lifecycle. Working closely with project delivery teams, operational colleagues, consultants and contractors, you will champion best practice ininformation management and data governance while helping drive a culture focused on data quality and a single source of truth. A full job description is attached to this advert. About you: You will be an experienced information and data management professional with a strong understanding of Estates, facilities management or the built environment. You will have hands-on experience with IWMS platforms, Common Data Environments, Business Information Management tools and information management practices, including an understanding of the practical application of ISO 19650 and data governance frameworks. You will be comfortable developing policies, standards and processes that improve the quality, consistency and accessibility of information across a complex organisation. Strong stakeholder management skills are essential, as you will work with a wide range of colleagues and partners to influence behaviours, establish accountability and embed best practice. You will bring excellent communication and data storytelling skills, with the ability to translate technical concepts into meaningful insights for senior leaders and operational teams. Alongside proven project or programme management experience, you will have a proactive, solutions-focused approach, strong leadership capabilities and the ability to manage multiple priorities in a fast-paced environment. Experience within a large estates environment, knowledge of information management standards, and a track record of delivering data and digital improvements will be key to success in this role. Professional qualifications in information management, construction, facilities management or related disciplines would be advantageous. Please read our job description, paying particular attention to the Essential and Desirable criteria, and ensure you address these in your application. Please note we reserve the right to close our advert after two weeks if we receive an unexpectedly high level of applications, so we would recommend you apply at the earliest opportunity. What we offer: As well as exciting opportunities, UCL offers a range of great benefits. Find out more: Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48982": {
      "title": "Internships and Vacancies Administrator (Fixed-Term Contract)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Student Well-being and Registry; London; £31,904-£36,433; Vice-Provost (Education and Student Experience) (B06) Reference: B06-00213 About us: The UCL Higher Education Development & Support Institute (HEDS) brings together UCL Arena and UCL Careers within the Office of the Vice Provost (Education & Student Experience). Together, we support excellence in education and student experience, while helping students and graduates develop the skills, knowledge and opportunities they need for their futures. UCL Careers provides careers information, internships and vacancies services, guidance and events to current UCL students, researchers and recent graduates. Our work with employers and recruiting organisations helps connect our students and graduates with opportunities and supports their engagement with the labour market. We are looking for an Internships & Vacancies Administrator to join our Careers team on a secondment cover basis until July 2027. This is an opportunity to contribute to a busy, student-focused service, working with employers and colleagues across UCL to support the delivery of our central internship and vacancy services. About the role: As an Internships & Vacancies Administrator, you will provide administrative and customer-facing support across our central internships and vacancies services. You will be a key first point of contact for employers looking to advertise jobs and internships to UCL students and graduates, helping them understand our services and identifying the most appropriate ways for them to engage with our student community. Working as part of a collaborative team, you will manage enquiries through a shared inbox, provide advice to recruiters by email and Teams, and work with colleagues across employer engagement to ensure enquiries are directed appropriately. You will also review and approve employer and vacancy information on our careers management system, ensuring opportunities meet UCL's advertising policies and relevant requirements. You will maintain accurate employer and opportunity information on myUCLCareers (TargetConnect) , support vacancy reporting and help promote internships and other opportunities through our jobs board. You will also provide administrative support across specific services, including the UCL Talent Bank shortlisting service and the Careers Extra Summer Internship scheme. This is a varied role that would suit someone who enjoys working with people, has strong attention to detail and is interested in supporting students and employers within a higher education careers environment. Please note, this role is offered on a fixed-term contract basis until July 2027. This role does not meet the requirements for a Skilled Worker visa certificate of sponsorship under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. UCL will not be able to sponsor individuals who require a right to work in the UK Job Ref: B06-00213 Closing Date: 22-10-2026 – 23:59 (UK time) About you: We are looking for an organised and customer-focused professional who can communicate confidently with a range of audiences, both in writing and verbally. You will have excellent organisational skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities, work accurately and remain calm when working to deadlines. You will have good IT skills, including experience of using Microsoft Office, spreadsheets and databases to manage information, contacts or recruitment-related activity. Strong attention to detail and a commitment to providing a high-quality service are essential. Experience of working in a higher education careers, placements, internships or graduate recruitment environment would be advantageous, as would experience of administering internship or placement schemes or working with recruiters. An understanding of the graduate labour market and experience of liaising with employers would also be desirable. Above all, you will be committed to equality, diversity and inclusion and enjoy working collaboratively as part of a team. You will understand how your work contributes to the wider service and the experience of our students, graduates and employer partners. What we offer: Standard UCL Benefits: 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48725": {
      "title": "Lab Manager (Maternity Cover)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Laboratory Technical and Lab Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11238 About us: Help shape the future of hearing and balance research Are you an experienced laboratory professional looking for an opportunity to lead laboratory operations within a world-leading research environment? The UCL Ear Institute is seeking a proactive and highly organised Laboratory Manager (Maternity Cover) to play a pivotal role in supporting our internationally recognised research and teaching activities. Working at the heart of one of the world's leading centres for hearing, deafness and balance research, you will help ensure our laboratories, facilities and research infrastructure operate safely, efficiently and to the highest professional standards. This is an exciting opportunity for someone with strong laboratory experience who is ready to broaden their leadership and operational management expertise within a complex and dynamic higher education environment. UCL Ear Institute is part of the Faculty of Brain Sciences and is internationally recognised as the UK's premier multidisciplinary centre for research into hearing and deafness. We bring together leading researchers, clinicians, educators and professional services staff to deliver groundbreaking discoveries and innovations that improve lives worldwide. Through our partnerships with the Royal National ENT and Eastman Dental Hospitals, we form Europe's largest grouping of audio-vestibular discovery and clinical scientists. The Institute is home to approximately 75 staff and supports a diverse portfolio of hearing science, auditory neuroscience and clinical research, underpinned by specialist laboratory facilities and shared research infrastructure. We are proud of our strong commitment to sustainability, inclusion and operational excellence, holding Athena Swan Silver accreditation together with Gold accreditation through Green Impact and LEAF. About the role: Reporting to the Head of Operations, with a dotted line to the Institute Director, you will lead the day-to-day management of shared laboratories and associated research facilities across the Institute. You will work closely with academic, technical and professional services colleagues to ensure laboratories remain safe, compliant, well-equipped and fully operational. Key responsibilities include: Managing all shared laboratory facilities and identifying opportunities for service improvement. Overseeing laboratory equipment procurement, maintenance, servicing and calibration. Acting as a key contact for laboratory refurbishment, relocation and infrastructure projects. Leading on laboratory health and safety compliance, audits, inspections, inductions and risk management. Supporting sustainability initiatives, including LEAF and Green Impact activities. Managing laboratory databases, records and inventory systems. Overseeing laboratory-related procurement and expenditure, ensuring value for money and compliance with UCL procedures. Line managing a Cell and Molecular Laboratory Technician. This is a fixed-term maternity cover position for 13 months. The post has been created to provide cover during a period of maternity leave and is expected to end on the return of the substantive post-holder or at the end of the fixed-term period, whichever is sooner. The post is also available as a secondment opportunity for eligible internal applicants, subject to agreement from their current line manager. For an informal discussion, please contact Aisling Muller at aisling.muller@ucl.ac.uk About you: We are looking for an individual who combines strong scientific understanding with excellent operational and relationship-management skills. You will have: A degree (or equivalent experience) in a relevant scientific discipline. Practical experience working within a research laboratory environment. Strong knowledge of molecular and cell biology and laboratory techniques. Experience managing stakeholders across research teams and external partners. A good understanding of laboratory health and safety regulations and compliance requirements. Excellent organisational, communication and problem-solving skills. The ability to manage multiple priorities and work independently in a fast-paced environment. Experience in laboratory management, line management, project management, higher education and the use of university finance systems would be advantageous This maternity cover role offers a unique opportunity to gain leadership experience within a complex research-intensive institute while contributing directly to cutting-edge scientific research and education. You will work closely with senior academic and professional services leaders and benefit from being part of the wider technical and professional services community across UCL's Faculty of Brain Sciences. What we offer: Starting salary offered at £43,981 per annum, inclusive of London Allowance. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits, some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: The Institute prides itself on operating in an all-inclusive environment irrespective of personal, physical, or social characteristics. Teamwork is highly valued, individual strengths are recognised and celebrated, and we are committed to advancing the careers of everyone. 12% of Institute staff are actively working on Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) initiatives; visit our EDI website for more information about our initiatives and priorities. The Institute also holds an Athena SWAN Gold award , in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality. As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce; these include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, LGBTQI+ and gender diverse people in all roles, and women in Grade 9 and 10 roles."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48680": {
      "title": "Learning and Development Partner (18 months fixed-term)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; HR and Wellbeing; London; £56,431-£66,437​; Vice-Provost (Strategy) (B24) Reference: B24-00092 About us: UCL’s Organisational Development (OD) department delivers impactful learning and career development opportunities to over 16,000 colleagues across academic, research, teaching and professional services. Within OD, the Learning and Leadership Development (LLD) team provides a broad portfolio of development opportunities helping build a connected and future-ready UCL. About the role: As Learning & Development Partner (HRFRI), you will lead the design, coordination and delivery of the training and capability workstream for UCL's HR, Finance & Research and Innovation (HRFRI) Programme, ensuring that colleagues have access to the knowledge, skills and support required to transition successfully into new roles, processes and ways of working. Working closely the Senior Instructional Designer (HRFRI) and with Organisational Development colleagues, programme teams, change managers, subject matter experts and key stakeholders across faculties and professional services, you will undertake training needs analysis, develop learning strategies and plans, coordinate learning delivery and evaluate learning effectiveness. You will play a critical role in translating complex business processes, system functionality, and subject matter expertise into engaging, accessible and practical learning solutions for a range of audiences across UCL. You will ensure that learning and capability activities are delivered in a consistent, scalable, and sustainable way, by developing and delivering a coherent blend of digital learning, facilitated training, guidance, and performance support. This will be a highly collaborative role requiring strong consultancy, stakeholder engagement and project management skills. You will work across multiple teams and functions to help colleagues confidently adopt and apply new processes, systems and ways of working. If you have any questions about the application process or the role please contact Passant Khalifa (Senior Recruiter) at p.khalifa@ucl.ac.uk About you: You will be a skilled Learning & Development, Organisational Development or HR professional with significant experience designing and delivering blended learning and capability interventions that support organisational change, transformation programmes or workforce transition initiatives. You will have a proven track record of conducting organisation-wide or programme-level training needs analysis and translating complex requirements into practical, learner-centred solutions, including learning journeys, capability frameworks and development plans. You will be confident working with subject matter experts and senior stakeholders to develop engaging learning experiences that support strategic objectives and business change. You will bring strong project coordination and stakeholder management skills, enabling you to manage multiple priorities, influence effectively and deliver high-quality learning solutions within a complex organisational environment. What we offer: As well as exciting opportunities, UCL offers a range of great benefits. Find out more: UCL Rewards and Benefits . 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women.” Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48736": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Teaching)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Academic; London; £56,431-£66,437​; School of Education (B16) Reference: B16-02222 About us: About UCL Institute of Education Founded in 1902, UCL Institute of Education has been shaping policy and helping government, organisations and individuals navigate a changing society for the last 120 years. We embrace collaboration and excellence to create a future that is inclusive and just and have been ranked number one for education every year since 2014 in the QS World University Rankings by Subject. The department of Curriculum, Pedagogy and Assessment is a world-leading centre for business, citizenship, computing, economics, geography, history, mathematics, religious education, science and social sciences education. A core strength of the Department is in the study, support and improvement of pedagogy, curriculum and assessment through multidisciplinary programmes. The Department hosts top-rated initial teacher education programmes, including Secondary PGCE, innovative Masters programmes and a wide range of continuing professional development courses. The department is also home to four research centres: Centre for Climate Change and Sustainability Education, Centre for Holocaust Education, Centre for Teachers and Teaching Research and the Development Education Research Centre About the role: The department of Curriculum, Pedagogy and Assessment invites applications for the position of Lecturer (Teaching) in Citizenship Education. The successful candidate will join the Citizenship education team at the UCL Institute of Education. The role will involve working on an ongoing project to support teachers in countering disinformation and conspiracy theories in the classroom. The Countering Conspiracy theories in the classroom role will involve supporting the Project Leader, delivering CPD to teachers in schools and ITE and helping to create the online programme. The successful candidate will be expected to play a full role in the continuing development of the Citizenship Education team and there may be opportunities to contribute to the work MA Citizenship Education. The possibility of job share will be considered The post is offered on a part-time basis ( 0.40 FTE / 14.60 hours per week) and is initially funded until 31/08/2027. Due to the nature of the duties, a DBS check will be required. For further information on this post, please contact Jeremy Hayward; email: j.hayward@ucl.ac.uk About you: The successful candidate will have a first degree, or equivalent experience and have Qualified Teacher Status or equivalent experience. You are knowledgeable of scholarship in the area of Citizenship education and/or media literacy/responding to disinformation and have successful teaching experience in schools and the potential to teach successfully at a higher education level (if not already experienced in this sector of education). In addition, you will have the ability to work effectively with others, co-operating with a range of academic and administrative colleagues and helping the team achieve its goals, and have a strong professional and interpersonal skills that enable effective communication and relationship-building. Your application form should address all the person specification points and should clearly demonstrate how your skills and experience meet each of the criteria. It is important that the criteria are clearly numbered and that you provide a response to each one. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday: 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days (pro rata for part time staff) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme (pro rata for part time staff) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our faculty holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48476": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Teaching) in Bioscience Innovation & Enterprise",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; London; £54,931-£64,644; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-10980 About us: UCL Institute of Ophthalmology conducts cutting-edge research, attracting researchers of the highest international calibre to a stimulating research environment. The Institute is committed to a multi-disciplinary research portfolio that furthers an understanding of the eye and visual systems, linked with clinical investigations targeted to specific problems in the prevention and treatment of eye disease. Based on its expertise not only in research, but in real-world use of that research, the Institute has developed two Masters programmes within the translational space, the MSc Bioscience Innovation & Enterprise and the MSc Bioscience R&D. It is also involved in graduate training of research students (MD, MPhil, PhD), and delivers a total of eight highly regarded taught postgraduate programmes. The combination of the Institute’s research with the expertise and resources of Moorfields Eye Hospital, which has the largest ophthalmic patient population in the Western World, facilitates advances at the forefront of vision research and provides an unparalleled clinical and academic setting for education and training in bioscience innovation. There are four Research Priority Pillars at IoO: Visual Neuroscience, Disease Mechanisms, Development and Ageing, Genomic and Translational Medicine These thematic specialties give rise to expertise in translation of innovation into social and economic value. The Institute’s expertise in areas from genetics to digital health have produced many spinouts, licences, partnerships, plus clinical and process innovations. About the role: UCL Institute of Ophthalmology (IoO) invites applications for a Lecturer (Teaching) to teach on, and support growth of, its novel cross-disciplinary MSc Bioscience Innovation & Enterprise programme. This MSc is positioned as teaching scientists the parts of business that are most relevant for real-world value-creation. The Lecturer will have responsibility for the design, development and delivery of science and/or digital health entrepreneurship-related modules and will have responsibility in conjunction with the Programme Director and other Module Leads for the organisation, growth and strategic management of the programme, for continued quality improvements and for the academic experience of the students on the programme. To note, this Lecturer position covers enterprise and innovation within bioscience and digital health, and does not require ophthalmology domain expertise. The post offers an exciting opportunity for someone to play a key role in developing new forms of education at the interface between science, digital health and business, in a highly successful and dynamic research and teaching Institute, as well as benefiting from the opportunities afforded by being part of the larger financial, educational, pedagogic and administrative community of the Faculty of Brain Sciences at UCL. This role is not eligible for sponsorship. About you: You will have a solid knowledge of concepts, practice, trends and dynamics within bioscience entrepreneurship, innovation, technology transfer, digital health, knowledge exchange or aligned fields, including scientific/digital literacy and educational background. The ability to place bioscience entrepreneurship-related theories in context in the practice of new-venture creation at the science-business interface is essential in addition to significant experience of teaching for- or about- biosciences entrepreneurship and innovation and/or digital health innovation. The successful candidate will be able to demonstrate excellence in delivery of taught content and/or outstanding teaching evaluations. Experience of working with students or colleagues from a wide range of cultural backgrounds, including experience of working collaboratively with students to improve the quality of education in response to feedback is essential. Application Process If you have any queries regarding the vacancy please contact Marcus Fruttiger (m.fruttiger@ucl.ac.uk). If you have any queries regarding the application process please email ioo.hr@ucl.ac.uk. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. To apply for the vacancy please click on the 'Apply Now' button below. Please ensure you attach your CV and highest academic qualification. In addition, please ensure you provide a personal statement outlining how you meet the criteria to support your application. Please DO NOT attach research papers. What we offer: Working with some of the greatest intellects in the world, UCL also offers a generous pension scheme and holiday allowance, plus closure and bank holidays as well as enhanced parental pay and more. Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49029": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Teaching) in Business and Sustainability- maternity leave cover",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; London; £56,431-£66,437​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07937 About us: Set within The Bartlett Faculty, The Bartlett School of Environment, Energy and Resources (BSEER) is comprised of four world-class institutes, providing expert knowledge, collaborative research, and teaching. This role is within the Institute for Sustainable Resources, whose mission is to provide evidence, expertise and training to respond to climate change and support sustainable transitions for people and planet. About the role: The UCL Institute for Sustainable Resources is looking for a maternity cover for a Lecturer (Teaching) in Business, Sustainability and Society. The successful candidate will play an important role in the delivery of our MSc programme, Business and Sustainability (BaS), which is committed to transformation across business, environment and society. They will act as interim Deputy Course Leader, significantly contributing to running the programme and associated managerial and administrative tasks, in collaboration with the Course Leader. They will lead/co-lead two taught modules, and contribute teaching on other modules as required. They will also act as supervisor and marker to student Capstone projects. The role will involve being a key contact for students, and working closely with administration team on delivery of the programme. This post is available from 1 st December 2026 and is funded until 30 April 2028 in the first instance; further funding to support the post may become available. UCL welcomes applications from international applicants and has licence to sponsor individuals who require a visa. This is dependent on the post and candidate meeting eligibility requirements for visa sponsorship under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. About you: A track record of managing and delivering postgraduate taught programmes and/ or modules on topics related to business sustainability, and of successful postgraduate research supervision (at Masters level), is essential. An ability to deliver high-quality teaching, excellent communication, and enthusiasm for research and teaching related to business sustainability is also required. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits, some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit Work at UCL | Work at UCL - UCL – University College London to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48870": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Teaching) in Computational Linguistics",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11339 About us: Based within the Division of Psychology and Language Sciences in UCL’s Faculty of Brain Sciences, the Department of Linguistics brings together theoretical, experimental and computational approaches to the study of language, and has a track record of innovative and excellent research in formal theoretical linguistics and the cognitive science of language. UCL is the top-ranked university in the UK for research power in psychology, psychiatry and neuroscience according to the UK’s Research Excellence Framework 2021. The Division is ranked fifth in the world for psychology in the QS World University Rankings by Subject 2026, and UCL is ranked joint eighth in the QS World University Rankings. About the role: The postholder will deliver and develop teaching in computational linguistics, while also undertaking original research and publication, master’s student supervision in areas related to their research expertise, contributions to doctoral supervision where appropriate, and academic service. They will teach the existing Introduction to Computational Linguistics and Advanced Computational Linguistics modules on the MA Linguistics with a Specialisation in Computational Linguistics. They will also design Foundations of Maths and Programming for Computational Linguistics for the same postgraduate programme and teach it from 2027–28. This is a fixed-term role for two years from the start date. The salary range is from Grade 8, SP 40 - 42 (£56,431 - £59,571 per annum, inclusive of London Allowance). This role meets the eligibility requirements for a skilled worker certificate of sponsorship or a global talent visa under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore, UCL welcomes applications from international applicants who require a visa. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research, Teaching and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. About you: The successful candidate will hold a relevant PhD by the start date and can evidence independent research in computational linguistics, desirably with high-quality research outputs commensurate with career stage. They will have mathematical expertise and relevant university teaching experience, with the ability to develop accessible postgraduate teaching and assessment in mathematical and programming foundations. They will organise teaching and administrative work independently and contribute to an inclusive academic environment. Further details The advert will close on 26 October 2026 at 23:59 GMT. A Job Description and Person Specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. If you have any queries about the role, please contact Mario Giulianelli at m.giulianelli@ucl.ac.uk . If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact PALS HR at pals.hr@ucl.ac.uk . What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Athena Swan Status Statement Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48722": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Teaching) in Design - MSci Year 1 Design Module Tutor BARC0140/42",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; London; £56,431-£66,437​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07882 About us: The Bartlett is unrivalled in its breadth and depth of disciplines, programmes and departments. It is made up of 9 departments, which are constructed of 13 schools, Institutes, Centres and Units, providing teaching and research in a wide range of subject areas to over 4,000 students each year from across the world. The Bartlett is ranked #1 in the world for Architecture and the Built Environment and remains #1 in the UK for the eleventh year in a row, in the QS World University Subject Rankings 2025. The Bartlett School of Architecture is a world-leading place to study, teach and research architecture. The School is a key part of UCL, a top-world-ranking university, where architecture has been taught for over 175 years. With over 400 permanent staff, across four London sites, the school enjoys a rich reputation for cultivating innovative ideas in architecture. For further information about the School, please visit www.bartlett.ucl.ac.uk/architecture About the role: The Bartlett School of Architecture is seeking to appoint a part-time Lecturer (Teaching) position in MSci on a 0.38 FTE. • This is a fixed-term role until 27/09/2027 the first instance. • We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible and job share basis wherever possible'. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Academic Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/policies/2021/jun/conditions-service-academic-staff for more information. About you: The successful candidate will deliver high-quality and engaging design teaching across the designated Architecture MSci Year 1 modules, preparing relevant teaching materials and supporting students to develop their architectural design skills, critical thinking and creative practice. They will provide effective academic support, assessment and constructive feedback through individual and group teaching, contribute to marking, portfolio reviews, assessment panels and other academic activities, and work collaboratively with Programme Directors, Module Coordinators and the wider teaching team to support programme delivery and development. They will contribute positively to the wider student experience, including reviews, induction, field trips and summer show activities, while maintaining and developing their subject and teaching expertise through scholarship, professional development and engagement with current research, ARB/RIBA requirements and UCL teaching developments. They will also foster an inclusive, supportive and stimulating learning environment that reflects UCL’s Ways of Working and institutional values. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Application Process • The advert will close on 15th October 2026 at 23:59 GMT, however we may close applications early if we receive a high volume of applications. Early application submission is recommended. As a minimum we will keep all adverts open for 2 weeks. • Interview Date(s) are scheduled from 29th October 2026. • A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. Contact Details • If you have any queries about the role, please contact a.lafita@ucl.ac.uk. • If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact the HR Team at bartlett.arch.hr@ucl.ac.uk. The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please visit www.ucl.ac.uk/ways-of-working to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48892": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Teaching) in European Prehistory",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; London; £56,431-£66,437​; UCL SLASH (B03) Reference: B03-03295 About us: The UCL Institute of Archaeology is recognised as one of the leading academic departments of Archaeology and Heritage in the world. It is the largest department within its field in the UK. It offers a uniquely stimulating environment with more than 50 academic staff pursuing high-quality research across five continents. We are at the forefront of global archaeology, archaeological science, conservation, and heritage studies. To find out more about the Institute of Archaeology, visit our webpage at UCL Institute of Archaeology . About the role: We are seeking an innovative archaeologist to appoint as a full time (36.5 hours per week) Lecturer (Teaching) available from 1 January 2027 to 31 December 2027. We are looking for applicants with research expertise in the European Mesolithic, Neolithic, Bronze Age and/or Iron Age, and in leading-edge methods associated with aDNA and/or palaeoecology. Applicants should also be able to contribute to teaching in regional survey courses on prehistory and the coordination of core teaching in introductory archaeology. Some contribution to the Institute’s active UK field training programmes (either in the field or post-excavation) will also be expected. The successful candidate will be expected to undertake undergraduate and postgraduate teaching, as well as supervise Masters’ and Undergraduate dissertations, and undertake some administrative duties. About you: The successful candidate will have a PhD in Archaeology (relevant to some aspect of European Prehistory) and experience of research-led teaching in Archaeology. They will also have experience of designing, coordinating, and delivering teaching and assessments at undergraduate level in fields relevant to the role. Field and/or collections experience in European Prehistoric Archaeology and willingness to support Institute of Archaeology student fieldwork training and post-excavation supervision and research experience with either aDNA or palaeoecology is also essential. Further details about the post and a person specification can be found in the Job Description (linked below). Queries about the post may be addressed to Professor Kevin MacDonald at kevin.macdonald@ucl.ac.uk . If you have any queries about the application process, please contact Louisa Goldsmith (HR Administrator) at l.goldsmith@ucl.ac.uk . To apply for the vacancy, please click on the Apply Now button. Candidates are asked to also upload the following documents: A full CV (including academic and employment history, and any additional relevant skills and the contact details of two referees, one of whom should be the current or most recent line manager) A cover Letter that states how you meet each of the essential and desirable criteria as listed in the person specification within the job description Evidence of qualifications Please refer to the application process within the job description for guidance on completing your cover letter. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days' annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. This role meets the eligibility requirements for a skilled worker certificate of sponsorship or a global talent visa under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore, UCL welcomes applications from international applicants who require a visa. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Bronze award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48158": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Teaching) in Project Management",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; UCL East; £54,931-£64,644; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-10291 About us: The UCL Global Business School for Health (GBSH) at UCL East campus is an innovative business school in preparing future healthcare leaders to disrupt and reimagine global health systems. GBSH is dedicated to training and developing the next generation of leaders in the global health economy by building on UCL’s proud tradition of cross-disciplinary academic excellence in education and research. GBSH develops talent from all sectors – policy makers, technologists and entrepreneurs – to challenge conventional thinking, to improve health outcomes and address health inequalities world-wide. With a vision for public good, GBSH is dedicated to health as the most important global issue in the 21st Century which is impacting communities and economies worldwide. Its ambition is aligned to the UN Sustainable Development Goal of ‘Universal Health Care for All’. At GBSH, we bring together global perspectives from diverse healthcare systems, industry partners and sector networks. GBSH is an interdisciplinary academic entity that is located within UCL’s Faculty of Population Health Sciences which is committed to innovating and improving health worldwide. About the role: The Lecturer (Teaching) in Project Management will contribute to the delivery, development, and enhancement of undergraduate and postgraduate teaching in project, programme, and portfolio management with appropriate integration of supply chain and operations management perspectives. The postholder will teach across a range of project management subjects, supporting students in developing the knowledge, skills, and behaviours required to lead and deliver projects and manage interconnected activities, resources, suppliers, and stakeholders in complex organisational environments. About you: The role will contribute to the continued development of innovative and practice-informed project management education, integrating contemporary methodologies, digital technologies, leadership approaches, supply chain considerations, and industry best practice. The successful candidate will support UCL Global Business School for Health's commitment to interdisciplinary education and prepare graduates to manage strategic change, transformation, innovation, and organisational improvement across the health sector and wider economy, including the effective management of projects and supply chains in increasingly complex and interconnected organisational settings. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: •41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and closure days) •Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme •Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) •Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan •Immigration loan •Relocation scheme for certain posts •On-Site nursery •On-site gym •Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay •Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service •Discounted medical insurance Visit UCL rewards and benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: For information on UCL’s values, culture, and staff benefits, visit Work at UCL. As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s academic talent, from local to global. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We, therefore, particularly encourage applications from candidates currently under-represented in UCL’s academic, research and teaching workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQ+ people; and women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=47822": {
      "title": "Lecturer - x2",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Academic; London; £56,431-£66,437​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07695 About us: The Department of Medical Physics and Biomedical Engineering is one of the largest Medical Physics and Biomedical Engineering departments in the UK. The Department is proud to host internationally leading researchers covering a broad range of activities, spread over several sites. Our staff and students have a diverse range of interests and expertise, encompassing many areas of physics, biomedical engineering, medicine, physiology, computer science, and mathematics. Our focus is on excellent academic research to identify new technologies and methods for the diagnosis, treatment and management of diseases. Affiliated with 8 major London hospitals, the Department is a key partner of UCL’s Institute of Healthcare Engineering and the Hawkes Institute, and works closely with other departments from the Faculty of Engineering Sciences, including Mechanical Engineering, Electronic and Electrical Engineering, and Computer Science. We offer a range of undergraduate and postgraduate degrees and are home to over 700 students. These programmes include a range of departmental and cross-faculty modules, incorporating problem and scenario-based learning at all stages. This creates a highly engaging learning environment where students put their theoretical knowledge to practical application. More information on the department’s research and teaching activities can be found at: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/medical-physics-biomedical-engineering . About the role: We are looking for two new Lecturers (Assistant Professors) to lead their own research groups within the department, ideally complementary to existing activities. We seek applicants with a programme of research within biomedical engineering and medical physics disciplines aligned with the interests of the department. Apart from offering mentorship and support to their own research teams, the new lecturers will contribute to teaching on our BEng/MEng in Biomedical Engineering programmes, act as a personal tutor for students on our taught programmes, supervise student projects and contribute to the effective running of the department. They will be expected to teach one or more disciplines, which may not align directly with their research areas. This is a permanent position. This role meets the eligibility requirements for a skilled worker certificate of sponsorship or a global talent visa under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore, UCL welcomes applications from international applicants who require a visa. We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible and job share basis wherever possible. This role is eligible for hybrid working with a minimum of 40% on site. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Academic Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/policies/2021/jun/conditions-service-academic-staff for more information. Please upload Cover Letter, Research Proposal, Teaching Proposal and summary of publications. About you: You will have an exciting vision for your research group and be able to communicate how this research group could flourish within UCL. You will be, or have the potential to be, an effective and approachable leader, demonstrating mentorship and support for your team. You will have a PhD level degree or equivalent in Engineering, the Physical Sciences, or relevant field, and must have experience in producing independent, original, impactful and rigorous research contributions in your field. You will be familiar with the research funding process and grant writing. You will have experience of supervising BSc, MSc and PhD level students and of delivering university level teaching at either BSc or MSc level, in a relevant topic. Your application should include: a full CV including publications. a Cover Letter. Please evidence how you meet the essential and desirable criteria in the Person Specification part of the Job Description. a Research Proposal (maximum length 2 pages). Please describe your vision and research topic, how you will build your team and how you will start to fund your research programme. a Teaching Proposal (maximum length 2 pages). Please outline how you would contribute to teaching in our department. Summary of publications (maximum length 1 page). Please include your 6 most important papers, explaining your contribution and impact of each paper. The Cover Letter, Research Proposal, Teaching Proposal and summary of publications should be compiled into a single document. The minimum acceptable font size is 11 and margins in all directions must be at least 2cm. Your application may be rejected if these documents are not provided or the above size restrictions are not respected. (By including a Cover Letter, you can leave blank the 'Why you have applied for this role' field in the online application form, which is limited in the number of characters it will allow.) What we offer: Standard UCL Benefits: 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . The advert will close on 16 th October 2026 at 23:59 GMT. Interview Date(s) are scheduled for: 9 th – 13 th November 2026. A Job Description and Person Specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. If you have any queries about the role, please contact Professor Terence Leung ( t.leung@ucl.ac.uk ) or Head of Department Professor Peter Munro (p.munro@ucl.ac.uk) If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact medphys.hr@ucl.ac.uk . This role meets the requirements for a Skilled Worker certificate of sponsorship under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. UCL welcomes applications from international applicants who require a visa. We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible, and job share basis wherever possible.” UCL Terms and Conditions of Service: Academic Staff Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Bronze award in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48750": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Contemporary Moving Image 0.5FTE",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Academic; UCL East; £56,431-£66,437​; School of Education (B16) Reference: B16-02254 About us: Founded in 1902, UCL Institute of Education has been shaping policy and helping government, organisations and individuals navigate a changing society for the last 120 years. We embrace collaboration and excellence to create a future that is inclusive and just, and have been ranked number one for education every year since 2014 in the QS World University Rankings by Subject. The Department of Culture, Communication and Media (CCM ) is a department of IOE, UCL’s Faculty of Education and Society. It is committed to excellence in teaching, research and consultancy in the areas of: Media and Cultural Studies, Art, Design and Museology; Academic writing; English education: Applied Linguistics; Music Education; Multimodality studies; Learning with Digital Technologies. We are looking to appoint an exceptional Lecturer in Contemporary Moving Image. UCL's BA Media blends creative media production and critical thinking through practice-as-research. Unlike purely practical film production or game development programmes, students critically engage with theories of media and technology, addressing these concepts directly within their practical work. Students explore digital and neural media, interactive storytelling, worldbuilding, fine art, programming, and animatics through making, experimentation, and reflection. About the role: The Lecturer in Contemporary Moving Image will support students in developing experimental work at the frontier of 21 st century moving image practices, while demonstrating a nuanced and critical understanding of the relationship between art-making, technology, and culture. The postholder will bring experience of creating inclusive and equitable learning environments, including the decolonisation of thought and the promotion of diversity within art and technology. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/prospective-students/undergraduate/degrees/media-ba The postholder will play an important role in the delivery of the BA Media. In addition to teaching at undergraduate level, the post-holder may contribute to Masters and PhD programmes in media, and to research, knowledge exchange and consultancy activities. About you: You will be a creative practitioner with a recognised practice in contemporary moving image fields, and demonstrable experience in directing and presenting ambitious public-facing moving image productions. You will teach moving image as an experimental and critical medium, making use of film and video technologies, animation, virtual production, storytelling alongside key competencies in camera and lighting, NLEs (Adobe Premiere Pro, DaVinci Resolve), and direction of projects involving cast and/or crew, from development through to delivery/presentation. With a doctorate (completed or close to completion) or comparable professional practice or/and industry experience, in games development, art and technology or creative computing, you will also have evidence of potential to produce internationally excellent, original and significant practice and research. For further information please contact Dane Sutherland – dane.sutherland@ucl.ac.uk Your application form should address all the person specification points and should clearly demonstrate how your skills and experience meet each of the criteria. It is important that the criteria are clearly numbered and that you provide a response to each one. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday: 27 days annual leave, 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days (pro rata for part time staff) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme (pro rata for part time staff) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our faculty holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48752": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Digital Media Practice - 0.5",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Academic; UCL East; £54,931-£64,644; School of Education (B16) Reference: B16-02209 About us: Founded in 1902, UCL Institute of Education has been shaping policy and helping government, organisations and individuals navigate a changing society for the last 120 years. We embrace collaboration and excellence to create a future that is inclusive and just, and have been ranked number one for education every year since 2014 in the QS World University Rankings by Subject. The Department of Culture, Communication and Media (CCM ) is a department of IOE, UCL’s Faculty of Education and Society. It is committed to excellence in teaching, research and consultancy in the areas of: Media and Cultural Studies, Art, Design and Museology; Academic writing; English education: Applied Linguistics; Music Education; Multimodality studies; Learning with Digital Technologies. About the role: We are looking to appoint an exceptional Lecturer in Digital Media Practice. UCL's BA Media blends creative media production and critical thinking through practice-as-research. Unlike purely practical film production or game development programmes, students critically engage with theories of media and technology, addressing these concepts directly within their practical work. Students explore digital and neural media, interactive storytelling, worldbuilding, fine art, programming, and animatics through making, experimentation, and reflection. You will support students in developing technical fluency in experimental web and mobile creative programming, while demonstrating a nuanced and critical understanding of the relationship between art-making, technology, and culture. The postholder will bring experience of creating inclusive and equitable learning environments, including the decolonisation of thought and the promotion of diversity within art and technology. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/prospective-students/undergraduate/degrees/media-ba In addition to teaching at undergraduate level, the post-holder may contribute to Masters and PhD programmes in media, and to research, knowledge exchange and consultancy activities. About you: You will be a creative practitioner with a practice engaging contemporary digital and computational fields, and demonstrable experience of public-facing work. This is a post for someone who will teach coding (building browser-based, interactive, and generative work using JavaScript and libraries such as three.js, p5.js, and WebGL/GLSL) alongside key tools such as Unreal Engine, Blender, and Stable Diffusion, treated as both creative and critical media. With a doctorate (completed or close to completion) or comparable professional practice or/and industry experience, in games development, art and technology or creative computing, you will also have evidence of potential to produce internationally excellent, original and significant practice and research. For further information please contact Dane Sutherland – dane.sutherland@ucl.ac.uk Your application form should address all the person specification points and should clearly demonstrate how your skills and experience meet each of the criteria. It is important that the criteria are clearly numbered and that you provide a response to each one. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday: 27 days annual leave, 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days (pro rata for part time staff) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme (pro rata for part time staff) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our faculty holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48634": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Quantitative Social Science",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Academic; London; £56,431-£66,437​; School of Education (B16) Reference: B16-02206 About us: Founded in 1902, UCL Institute of Education has been shaping policy and helping government, organisations and individuals navigate a changing society for the last 120 years. We embrace collaboration and excellence to create a future that is inclusive and just and have been ranked number one for education every year since 2014 in the QS World University Rankings by Subject. The Social Research Institute , formerly the Department of Social Science in the UCL Institute of Education, is one of the leading centres for social research in the UK. It is home to seven internationally renowned research units: Centre for Longitudinal Studies Centre for Time Use Research Cohort & Longitudinal Studies Enhancement Resources (CLOSER) Evidence for Policy & Practice Information Centre (EPPI) Quantitative Social Science (QSS) Social Science Research Unit Thomas Coram Research Unit Our strategy is to exploit and promote our key characteristics: All our centres are multidisciplinary, with staff drawn variously from sociology, social policy, demography, economics, psychology, anthropology and social statistics. We are working across centres/units to promote cross-methodological, problem-solving interdisciplinary research on particular themes where we have outstanding scholarship and critical mass, e.g. gender, families, work, inequalities, migration, bio-social interactions. We are tackling major social challenges, e.g. on how societies support family wellbeing, gender equality and economic prosperity. We have a central role in sociological studies at UCL with staff working on empirically and theoretically rich research. Our work is increasingly transgressing the boundaries of academic social science, taking us into life sciences (health, social epidemiology, biosocial research) natural sciences (information science, environmental science) and the humanities (peacebuilding, creative arts). Our staff actively engage with public priorities in policy and practice (as seen in our many Impact Case Studies) and with networks for knowledge exchange. We are active in the collection and curation of data as a public resource and in the development of research methods as well as in substantive research. We have a globally central position in longitudinal research, time use research, and systematic reviews. More than 180 academic, research, teaching, technical and professional service staff work in the department. In addition to the seven research centres, it is home to three undergraduate degrees, three master’s programmes and a thriving doctoral community. About the role: We are now seeking a Lecturer (Assistant Professor) in Quantitative Social Science to contribute to our research and teaching. Relevant fields include sociology and economics. A research agenda on the environment and/or climate change is welcome. Applicants with the ability to teach advanced quantitative methods, particularly causal inference and proficiency in R/Python are especially welcome. The post holder will contribute to and help shape our interdisciplinary research portfolio by applying advanced quantitative social science methods to pressing real-world challenges around the environment and climate change. They will be expected to develop an ambitious research agenda, seek external funding from a diverse range of sources and produce work with demonstrable impact beyond academia. The successful candidate will teach and lead modules on our BSc Social Sciences with Data Science, MSc Social Research Methods with Data Science or MSc. Sociology programmes and may be invited to (co-)lead these programmes over time. We are particularly interested in applicants with a commitment to open and collaborative scholarship, innovative pedagogy, and inclusive curriculum design. Appointees will also be expected to contribute actively to the intellectual and collegial life of the department. The post is available from 01 September 2027. For further information contact Professor Kirstine Hansen, Director, QSS Research Centre and chair of the recruitment panel (k.hansen@ucl.ac.uk). About you: You’ll have a PhD in sociology or economics where advanced quantitative methods have been applied, completed by time of appointment. A research agenda around the environment and/or climate change. A clearly articulated publication strategy and pipeline targeting internationally leading journals in sociology and economics. Your application form should address all person specification points and clearly demonstrate how your skills and experience meet each of the criteria. It is important that the criteria are clearly numbered and that you provide a response to each one. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday: 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days (pro rata for part time staff) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme (pro-rata for part-time staff) • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our faculty holds an Athena SWAN Silver award in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. For a full job description and to apply for this role please visit UCL’s online recruitment portal (https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs)"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48384": {
      "title": "Lecturer/ Associate Professor",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Academic; London; See advert text; UCL SLASH (B03) Reference: B03-03266 About us: The Department of Philosophy at UCL is one of the leading philosophy departments in the UK. In the 2021 REF, 60% of our overall profile received the top grade (4*), the highest of any UK philosophy department. We offer several undergraduate degree programmes. Undergraduate first-year modules usually consist of two hour-long lectures plus an hour-long seminar each week; second-year modules usually involve a one-hour lecture plus a one-hour seminar each week; third year modules usually consist of a weekly two-hour seminar. We offer taught master’s programmes, and a prestigious research master’s and PhD. Postgraduate-level modules usually consist of a weekly two-hour seminar. In collaboration with the Department of Computer Science, we run a BA in Philosophy and Computer Science, and in 2026 introduced an MASc in Philosophy, Logic and Artificial Intelligence. The department has great advantages as a centre of research activity. The many research seminars, talks and lectures that take place in the various London colleges, the Institute of Philosophy in the University of London, the Institute of Classical Studies, the Aristotelian Society and the Royal Institute of Philosophy, all offer a wealth of opportunities. The post is offered on an open-ended, full-time basis, with an ideal start date of 1 September 2027. As London’s Global University, we are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates whose backgrounds are underrepresented in Philosophy. About the role: Main purpose of the job We are looking for a candidate whose main research lies in the philosophy of computer science and/or artificial intelligence, and who will contribute to the teaching and development of our two joint degrees with Computer Science and help build research connections between the departments. We welcome candidates working anywhere at the interface of philosophy and computing, including the philosophy of cognitive science, the foundations of machine learning, and the ethical and social dimensions of AI. We are open to a wide range of approaches. This appointment is intended to complement our existing strengths in logic and the philosophy of mathematics. The appointment will be made at either Lecturer (Grade 8) or Associate Professor (Grade 9) level. Application Applications will be made online. When you apply, you will need to submit: • a CV; • a cover letter, of no more than two sides; • two writing samples, totalling no more than 20,000 words. Important: You will also need to arrange for two referees to submit letters of reference prior to the published closing date . Letters should be addressed to Professor Daniel Rothschild at philosophy.letters@ucl.ac.uk. About you: A PhD in a relevant field, awarded before the start date. Clear evidence of excellence in philosophical research at a level commensurate with the grade of the job applied for (Lecturer or Associate Professor). What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=47450": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Associate Professor in Artificial Intelligence for Biomolecular Design & Molecular Interactions",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Academic; London; £68,284-£76,244; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-10648 About us: The Division of Biosciences at UCL is a major centre for research and teaching in the biological sciences, fostering curiosity-driven research that spans molecules to ecosystems. Within Biosciences, the Research Department of Structural & Molecular Biology (SMB) provides a stimulating and diverse research environment where important and exciting areas of modern biology, biophysics, biochemistry, biotechnology and medicine are investigated at the atomic, molecular, cellular and organism level. We are recruiting a Lecturer (Assistant Professor equivalent) or Associate Professor in AI for Biomolecular Design & Molecular Interactions to expand our strengths in Computational Biology and Bioinformatics as applied to research surrounding Modelling and Design of Life. This role also forms part of AI for Life, a major programme across Biosciences in computational and artificial intelligence-driven life sciences. About the role: An exciting development in AI-driven biosciences is the development of predictive and generative models of biomolecular interactions, spanning protein–ligand, protein–protein, and protein–nucleic acid systems. Next-generation approaches that integrate structural, thermodynamic and sequence-level information to model binding specificity, affinity, and conformational dynamics, and on translating these insights into the rational design of novel molecules and interaction networks, will enable precise control of biomolecular interactions and functions. This is an exciting opportunity to become a Lecturer/Associate Professor at UCL. The successful candidate will take an active role in the Research Department of Structural & Molecular Biology (SMB), developing international standard, competitive research programme, conducting teaching and training of undergraduate and postgraduate students, and supporting the Departmental vision of creating an impact on the life of the University and the society. The successful candidate will establish (at Grade 8) or expand (at Grade 9) a research and teaching programme centred on original, AI-driven approaches for biomolecular design, with a focus on molecular recognition and interaction engineering. We are particularly interested in candidates with a track record of developing innovative approaches combining elements of structural biology, biophysics, quantitative biology and machine learning, to understand and design interactions between proteins, small molecules, and nucleic acids. This new research programme will be fully embedded within research activities of SMB, providing opportunities of fruitful interactions between experimentalist and theoreticians, shaping and expanding new research directions. The postholder will play a pivotal role in SMB’s teaching activities, contributing teaching in relevant areas (e.g. computational biology, protein-nucleic acid/small molecule interactions, protein/biomolecular design) at both undergraduate and postgraduate levels, including a new cross-division MRes AI for Bioscience. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Academic Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/policies/2021/jun/conditions-service-academic-staff for more information. Interviews will be provisionally scheduled for early January 2027 For informal queries about the post, please contact Professor Franca Fraternali (f.fraternali@ucl.ac.uk). For queries about the application process, or to request reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for the job online, please contact the FLS HR team on fls.hr@ucl.ac.uk About you: You will have a PhD or equivalent qualification in a relevant field and an innovative research agenda in AI for Biomolecular Designs and Molecular Interactions, Computational Biology applied to Molecular Biosystems or in a closely related area with clear quantitative and data science strength. You will have experience of research-led teaching at undergraduate and/or postgraduate level, with a strong interest in interdisciplinary working, external engagement and translational impact. You will also show the potential to build collaborative partnerships, attract research funding, and contribute to an inclusive and ambitious academic environment. A full list of essential and desirable criteria is provided in the accompanying person specification at the bottom of this page with further details of how to apply. What we offer: The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/policies-advice/ways-working to find out more. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity, and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know that diversity and inclusion are fundamental drivers of creativity, innovation, and excellence, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world's talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong, thrive, and reach our full potential. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian, and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women’ Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity, and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48789": {
      "title": "Maintenance Operative (CHP)",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Estates, Facilities, Accommodation and Safety; London; £36,433-£41,833; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00322 About us: UCL is a radically different university. Founded in 1826 in the heart of London, we were the first university in England to welcome students of any religion and the first to welcome women to university education. Today UCL has over 50,000 students and more than 18,000 staff and is one of the world’s top universities. We are ranked second in the UK for research power and first in London (REF 2021). UCL Estates plays a vital role in supporting this community, managing a large and complex estate that underpins world-leading research, teaching and public engagement. The Maintenance Operations team ensures that buildings, systems and infrastructure are safe, reliable and operating effectively across the university. About the role: We are looking for a Maintenance Operative specialising in Combined Heat and Power (CHP) and steam system operation to join our Estates Maintenance Operations team. This is a hands-on technical role focused on the delivery of both planned and reactive maintenance across critical plant and infrastructure. You will work on systems including CHP plant, district heating, steam and hot water systems, as well as associated controls and building services equipment. A BOAS qualification would be desirable. You will be responsible for diagnosing faults, responding to breakdowns and carrying out repairs to ensure minimal disruption to services. The role will involve working across multiple disciplines, including mechanical, electrical and plumbing maintenance, supporting the safe and efficient operation of a complex estate. You will also be involved in planned maintenance programmes, testing and inspection activities, and preparing plant and equipment for statutory compliance. Working closely with colleagues, supervisors and contractors, you will play a key role in maintaining high standards of safety, performance and customer service. About you: You will have completed a recognised apprenticeship or equivalent training in a mechanical and or electrical discipline, with practical experience working in maintenance operations, ideally within a complex environment. You will have strong fault-finding skills and be confident working with plant, equipment and systems such as CHP, heating, and associated controls. You are comfortable interpreting drawings, manuals and system data, including using building management systems to monitor and diagnose issues. You will be a collaborative team player who can also work independently, managing your own workload and responding effectively to changing priorities. You take a methodical approach to your work, maintaining high standards of accuracy, safety and compliance at all times. Strong communication skills are important in this role. You will be able to engage effectively with colleagues, contractors and building users, providing clear updates and maintaining a strong focus on customer service. You will also bring a proactive and flexible approach, with the willingness to work across different sites and adapt to the needs of a busy operational environment, always contributing positively to the wider team. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49071": {
      "title": "Management Accountant (12- Month Secondment)\n\nInternal Applicants only",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Finance; London; £56,431-£66,437​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11324 About us: Are you an experienced finance professional looking to broaden your strategic financial management experience in a complex and research-intensive environment? The UCL Cancer Institute is seeking a proactive and collaborative Management Accountant to join its Professional Services team on a 12-month secondment. This role offers an excellent opportunity to work at the heart of one of the world's leading cancer research institutes, supporting senior decision-making and contributing to the effective financial management of a dynamic and diverse organisation. The UCL Cancer Institute is the hub for cancer research at UCL and brings together world-leading scientists and clinicians working to improve outcomes for cancer patients through innovative research and collaboration. The Institute has strong links with UCL's partner hospitals and includes the Cancer Research UK & UCL Cancer Trials Centre, one of the largest cancer trials centres in the UK. About the role: Reporting to the Institute Manager, the Management Accountant will play a key role in supporting the financial management of the UCL Cancer Institute. Working closely with senior leaders, budget holders and colleagues across UCL Finance, you will provide high-quality financial advice, management information and business planning support. Responsibilities include leading the Institute's budgeting, forecasting and long-term financial planning processes, monitoring income and expenditure, and producing clear financial reports to support decision-making. You will provide financial advice to academic and professional services colleagues, support business case development and financial modelling, and ensure compliance with UCL financial regulations and procedures. The role is also responsible for managing month-end and year-end processes, maintaining robust financial controls, and supporting the effective management of a diverse portfolio of accounts. In addition, you will line manage and develop two Finance Administrators, helping to ensure a responsive and high-performing finance service. This is an excellent opportunity for a proactive finance professional to develop their experience in a complex and dynamic environment while contributing to the delivery of world-leading cancer research. You will also play an important role in identifying opportunities for process improvement and enhancing financial services across the Institute. This vacancy is available as an internal secondment opportunity. Applicants interested in applying on a secondment basis should discuss the opportunity with their line manager and obtain agreement in principle before submitting an application. Any appointment will be subject to formal approval from the individual's substantive department About you: We are seeking a qualified or part-qualified finance professional with experience of budgeting, forecasting and financial reporting in a complex organisation. You will have strong analytical, organisational and communication skills, with the ability to build effective relationships and explain financial information clearly to a wide range of stakeholders. You will be customer-focused, proactive and detail-oriented, with experience of supporting or developing staff and managing competing priorities. Experience within Higher Education and familiarity with Oracle, MyFinance or Axiom planning tools would be advantageous. What we offer: UCL is one of the world's leading universities and offers staff an exceptional working environment, including: Opportunities for professional development and career progression. Generous annual leave entitlement. Competitive pension scheme. Family-friendly policies. Employee wellbeing support. Flexible and hybrid working arrangements where operationally appropriate. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48889": {
      "title": "Moving Image Practice 1: Camera, Sound and Edit Postgraduate Teaching Assistant (Arts and Sciences)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; UCL East; £36,433-£42,891​; UCL SLASH (B03) Reference: B03-03305 About us: University College London (UCL) is a multi-faculty college of the University of London with a population of over 52,000 students, from more than 150 different countries. Degree programmes are provided in Arts and Humanities, Social and Historical Sciences, Education, Architecture, Building, Environmental Design and Planning, Laws, Life Sciences and Clinical Sciences (including Medicine), Mathematical and Physical Sciences, and Engineering Sciences. UCL is among the world’s top universities, as reflected in performance in a range of rankings and tables, and is ranked 9th in the world, 5th in Europe and 2nd in London in the QS World University Rankings 2025. As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and minority ethnic backgrounds, disabled people, LGBTQI+ people, and women. The UCL Arts and Sciences (UASc) department is the home for the Bachelor of Arts and Sciences (BASc) which is the UK's leading interdisciplinary degree, covering arts, social sciences and sciences degree. BASc combines the breadth of interdisciplinary study across the arts and sciences with the opportunity to specialise in one or two key subject areas. There is also postgraduate taught sister programme to UCL’s Bachelor of Arts and Sciences (BASc), the new MASc in Creative Health which combines the breadth of interdisciplinary study across the arts and sciences, with the opportunity to specialise in the role of non-clinical, community-led, socially engaged approaches to public health. UCL Arts and Sciences also launched a new undergraduate degree, the Bachelor in Creative Arts and Humanities (BACAH) , with its first intake 2023/24. This degree is homed at UCL East and brings together critical humanities thinking with practices of writing, performance and moving images. BACAH further develops the UASc departments’ interdisciplinarity by bringing together critical humanities thinking with critical practice. The Department has a doctoral programme in Interdisciplinary Societies, Arts and Sciences which accepted its first students in the 2023/24 academic year and is aiming to grow to 20 doctoral students. About the role: The Department of Arts and Sciences wishes to make an appointment for a Postgraduate Teaching Assistant to teach on the BASC0062 Moving Image Practice 1: Camera, Sound and Edit module running in Term 2 of the 2026/27 academic year. This appointment is required to work on the BA Creative Arts and Humanities degree based at the UCL East campus in Stratford. The purpose of this job is to support teaching and learning in our BASC-coded modules, working with the academic modules leads through the delivery of small group teaching for students taking this module in the Arts and Sciences Department. Main duties of the post are tutorial class teaching, regular meetings with the course lecturer, provision of feedback to students, attendance reporting, coursework assessment and examination marking. PGTAs will be given training in support of their roles. PGTAs are expected to be able to commit be present on the UCL East campus for the full duration of the term and during the examination period as required. We will not be offering any online seminar classes in 2026/27. This role does not meet the eligibility requirements for a Skilled Worker Visa certificate of sponsorship under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore UCL will not be able to sponsor individuals who require right to work in the UK to carry out this role. About you: Applicants must be in at least the second year of their PhD studies at the start of any contract with us (4 January 2027). PhD students from outside UCL can also apply, but priority for appointments will go to current UCL PhD students. Please check the job description and person specification for additional knowledge or experience required for the module. All contracts are inclusive of teaching hours, preparation hours, office hours and marking. Previous experience of undergraduate teaching is desirable. Please note that we can only consider applications from students still completing their PhD and registered as a student at the time a contract would begin (see date above). Applicants with a completed and awarded PhD are not eligible to apply. The deadline to apply is 23.59 (BST) on Sunday 25 October 2026. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. If you have any queries about the role, need reasonable adjustments, a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact Cristy Meadows, Head of Operations, UCL Arts and Sciences at uasc-operations@ucl.ac.uk . What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49079": {
      "title": "Notarial Practice Private International Law Convenor",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; London; £56,431-£66,437​; UCL SLASH (B03) Reference: B03-03312 About us: UCL’s Faculty of Laws is a dynamic, highly respected and friendly community, which enjoys global recognition for the quality of its teaching and research. For almost 200 years, UCL Laws has been one of the leading centres of legal education in the world. Recognised as offering an outstanding educational experience to our students, we combine a strong theoretical foundation in the law with practical teaching from world-leading academics and practitioners. About the role: We are looking to appoint an outstanding candidate to lead the Private International Law module of the Notarial Practice Course, who will work closely with the Course Director in managing teaching and learning materials, maintaining a Moodle page, leading workshops, preparing assessments to monitor students' progress, and marking formative and summative assessments. The module is taught partly through online materials and partly through classes held in London. This role is expected to start in early January 2027, and is fixed-term until 31 July 2027. We anticipate this part-time role (0.2 FTE) will work on average one day per week, but exact working pattern and contracted hours will be agreed with the successful candidate. The successful candidate will be appointed at Grade 8 on UCL's salary scale, and the full-time equivalent salary (£57,570-£67,776 per annum, inclusive of London Allowance) will be pro-rated for part-time appointments (£11,514-£13,555.20 per annum, inclusive of London Allowance). About you: The successful candidate will hold, or be close to completion of, a PhD in Private International Law or a related subject area, with evidence of successful teaching in this area at Higher Education level. As an excellent communicator with strong interpersonal skills, the role holder will be an engaging educator, work effectively as a member of a team, and be able to convey complex ideas clearly and to support students’ learning through constructive and developmental feedback. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. To apply for the vacancy, please click on the ‘Apply Now’ button below. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. To apply for this vacancy, please click on the 'Apply Now' button. You will be required to upload your CV and supporting statement as part of your application; please note that the online application portal may prompt you to upload other documents, and we ask you not to do so at this stage. The deadline for applications is 25 October 2026, and interviews will likely take place in week(s) commencing 16 or 23 November 2026. If you have any queries regarding the vacancy or the application process, please contact Dr David Foster, Notarial Practice Course Director at david.foster@ucl.ac.uk. If you have any queries about accessibility or reasonable adjustments please contact lawshr@ucl.ac.uk. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48887": {
      "title": "One Desk HR Manager (ELIGIBLE FOR UCL INTERNAL CANDIDATES ONLY)",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £56,431-£66,437​; Vice-President (Operations) (B01) Reference: B01-05752 About us: The Professional Services (PS) Hub brings together key customer-facing and transactional services across HR, Finance, IT and Business Operations, with a strong focus on delivering high-quality, efficient services and an excellent customer experience. Within the Hub, One Desk provides a single point of contact for UCL students, staff, suppliers and partners, responding to thousands of enquiries every day through telephone, email, live chat and other digital channels. Our team provides timely, accurate and helpful advice while continually looking for ways to improve our services and the customer journey. About the role: As One Desk Manager, you will provide operational and people leadership to Team Leaders, Senior Advisors, Advisors and Assistants, ensuring the team is well supported, appropriately resourced and equipped to deliver an exceptional customer experience. You will be responsible for monitoring and improving performance against agreed SLAs and KPIs, using data and customer insight to identify opportunities for improvement. You will oversee quality monitoring across calls, chats and tickets, providing coaching and feedback to support high standards of service. If you have any queries about the role or the application process please contact Passant Khalifa (Senior Recruiter) via p.khalifa@ucl.ac.uk About you: We are looking for an experienced people manager with a strong customer service focus and the ability to lead teams in a busy, complex environment. You will have experience managing a team that provides high-quality advice and guidance, alongside a good working knowledge of HR legislation and customer-focused practices. You will bring excellent interpersonal, communication and organisational skills, with the ability to prioritise competing demands while maintaining accuracy and service quality. You will be confident using data and KPIs to monitor performance, identify trends and drive continuous improvement. You will also have experience working with HR, Finance and Payroll systems, as well as customer service technologies such as ticketing and live chat platforms. Experience within a large, complex organisation would be advantageous. Above all, you will take pride in delivering an excellent service, sharing knowledge and experience, building strong relationships and making timely, evidence-based decisions. A customer service qualification or training, and Lean Six Sigma or similar certification, would be desirable. What we offer: As well as exciting opportunities, UCL offers a range of great benefits. Find out more: UCL Rewards and Benefits . 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women.” Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49026": {
      "title": "Operations Administrator",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Administration and Business Support; London; £36,433-£42,891​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07831 About us: The Centre for Advanced Spatial Analysis (CASA) is an interdisciplinary research department focusing on the science of cities, applying it to city planning, policy and architecture in the pursuit of making our cities better places to live in. It is part of The Bartlett, UCL's Faculty of the Built Environment. CASA was established in 1995 to lead the development of a science of cities drawing upon methods and ideas in modelling, sensing the urban environment, visualisation and computation. It seeks to examine and offer solutions to the problems of resource efficiency and effective planning, and governance shared by all cities. Further information can be found on our website at https://www.ucl.ac.uk/bartlett/casa/ About the role: The Operations Administrator will work alongside colleagues who provide departmental professional services. The postholder will act as first point of contact for internal and external routine departmental operations queries, and deliver a wide range of administrative duties including, servicing committee meetings, event organisation, conference travel arranging, routine financial administrative support, support with maintaining a safe and compliant office environment, support with departmental facilities, access and sustainability processes, and deliver day-to-day office and space management activities. A proactive, flexible and customer-focussed approach is always required to ensure a high level of service to staff, students and visitors. This is a fixed-term or secondment post available from 11th January to 10th July 2027. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research, Teaching and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. Find out about the UCL Ways of Working: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/organisational-development/career-development-and-progression/ways-working. We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible and job share basis wherever possible. For any queries about the role please contact Susan Francis (s.francis@ucl.ac.uk). A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. To apply for the vacancy please click on the ‘Apply Now’ button below. About you: The successful candidate will be educated to A level standard or have experience working in a similar administrative role in a Higher Education Institution, or similarly complex organisation. The successful candidate should also demonstrate excellent administrative skills and a proactive approach to providing a comprehensive administrative service. They should be able to take direction and to work independently, have excellent communication skills and attention to detail, as well as a have high level organisational and time management skills. We are looking for someone who takes initiative and is willing to take on new tasks, who is a problem solver and who works well in a team. For full list of essential and desirable criteria, please see a job description and person specification at the bottom of this page. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days); Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave; Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE); Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan; Relocation support for eligible posts; On-site nursery and gym; Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay; Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme); Discounted medical insurance. Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/ Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy (https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/hr-policies-procedures-and-advice/z-policies/prevention-bullying-harassment-and-sexual-misconduct) and Personal Relationships Policy (https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/hr-policies-procedures-and-advice/z-policies/personal-relationships-policy)"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48937": {
      "title": "Operations Administrator - 0.6 FTE",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £36,433-£42,891​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11346 About us: Join one of the country’s leading Clinical Trials Units. The UCL Institute of Clinical Trials and Methodology brings together five expert research units: UCL Innovative Clinical Trials Unit (InCTU, formerly MRC Clinical Trials Unit at UCL), the Comprehensive Clinical Trials Unit at UCL, the MRC Centre of Research Excellence in Clinical Trial Innovation, and virtual partners:- Cancer Research UK/UCL Cancer Trials Centre, the PRIMENT CTU Across the Institute we design and deliver high-quality clinical trials, meta-analysis and epidemiological studies, across a range of diseases, that inform clinical practice globally. We are committed to improving local, national and global health. Key areas of focus are continuing to deliver innovations in trial methodology to improve outcomes for patients, and training and developing the next generation of trialists. We work with academic collaborators in more than 60 countries worldwide and a significant number of commercial partners. Within the Institute, the Innovative CTU leads research addressing internationally important questions, primarily in infectious diseases, cancer and neurodegenerative diseases. A key goal is to deliver swifter and more effective translation of scientific research into patient and public health benefits. We achieve this by carrying out challenging and innovative studies. We work closely with MRC Centre of research excellence in Clinical Trial Innovation to develop and implement methodological advances in study design, conduct and analysis. InCTU is a UKCRC registered trials unit. We are seeking to recruit an Operations Administrator to support the work of this research unit. About the role: An enthusiastic committed and professional individual is sought for the role of Operations Administrator. You will provide work closely with the Director of the Innovative Clinical Trials Unit as well as two other members of the leadership team. You will work within a multi-disciplinary team and will be expected to provide administrative support across a wide range of core InCTU activities as the need arises, developing their understanding of the research environment in which the post will operate as well as promoting a positive working environment and being a team player. Key Programme tasks will include, organising and minuting meetings and events, monitoring project milestones, assistance with procurement, expense and budgetary management, diary management and travel arrangements as required. This post is funded for 2 years in the first instance. About you: No two days are ever the same in the role we offer. This is a development level role for a highly organised, flexible candidates who is prepared take on a variety of tasks and manage their portfolio of work working directly with Senior Staff. You should have strong communication skills (written and verbal) and be willing to work with personnel across the Unit ranging from Professorial and Clinical down to Trial Managers and operational staff. In return we offer a supportive Team environment and the opportunity to develop your skills and ambitions. Please attach a covering letter, with your CV, explaining how you meet the criteria in the person specification that is included in the Job Description. If you have any queries regarding the vacancy or the application process, to arrange an informal discussion regarding the post please contact Nav Kaur (n.kaur@ucl.ac.uk) ICTM HR (ictm.hr@ucl.ac.uk) What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: · 41 Days holiday pro rata (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) · Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme · Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) · Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan · Immigration loan · Relocation scheme for certain posts · On-Site nursery · On-site gym · Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay · Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service · Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Bronze award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48835": {
      "title": "PA & Administrative Assistant to Vice-Deans and DESE",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £36,433-£42,891​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11105 About Us: The Faculty of Medical Sciences is an exciting and dynamic environment driving forward ambitious and challenging plans for future development. This is a pivotal post within the Faculty team and provides an excellent opportunity for the right applicant to develop a career in university administration. We're a recognised world leader in Medical Education, helping students to become leaders in their fields. The faculty is the top in the UK for research power in life and medical sciences (REF 2021) and is ranked sixth in the world for Medicine and number one in London (QS World University Rankings by Subject 2023). The Faculty is dedicated to delivering world class medical education and developing translational research that benefits patients and society in the UK and globally. Bringing together six UCL divisions and institutes the Faculty of Medical Sciences (FMS) offers a unique mix of pioneering research, best in class researchers and clinicians, cutting edge facilities and links with world renowned partner hospitals and institutions. About the Role: The role requires interaction with many people within and external to UCL. A professional attitude, willingness to help, and an ability to remain tactful and assertive are required in order to adequately execute the duties outlined. This permanent post which will be based at the Bloomsbury Campus. Appointment details: The post is graded at GRADE 6, the annual salary for which ranges from £36,433 - £42,891 (pro-rata) per annum inclusive of London Allowance. Application process: A full job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. Please use the personal statement section to explain how you meet each essential and desirable criteria outlined in the person specification. The advert will close on 18 October 2026 at 23:59 GMT/BST, however we may close the advert early if we receive a high volume of applications. Interviews are expected to take place on Tuesday 3 rd and Wednesday 4 th The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please visit ac.uk/ways-of-working to find out more. Contact details: If you have any queries about the role or application process please contact Marion Atkinson ( Marion.Atkinson@ucl.ac.uk ). If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact Deepak Singh ( Deepak.Singh@ucl.ac.uk). About You: An enthusiastic, proactive and resourceful individual is sought for this part-time post. The post holder will provide dedicated executive support, including diary management, arranging meetings and committee servicing to the Faculty Vice-Deans and DESE. The post holder will assume general and specific administrative support duties to progress the operational activities of the FMS Faculty Office, and where appropriate LMS Faculties. You will be educated to A level standard, or NVQ Level IV administration, or have substantial relevant experience. You will have an experience of working in an administrative position, incorporating an EA/PA role, in a large or complex organisation, experience in event management, servicing high-level committees, working groups and similar including meeting preparation as well as minute taking. Experience of organising own work and taking responsibility for meeting targets, drafting emails, letters, papers and presentations. The more extensive list of skills is included within Job Description and Person Specification attached to this job. What We Offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) pro rata Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance The full range of staff benefits can be found here Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. UCLMS holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48784": {
      "title": "PGT Senior Employer Engagement Officer",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; Vice-Provost (Education and Student Experience) (B06) Reference: B06-00210 About us: UCL is a global university with a distinctive commitment to innovation, collaboration and positive change. Our community brings together students and staff from around the world, creating an environment where different perspectives, ideas and experiences can thrive. The Higher Education Development & Support Institute (HEDS) brings together UCL Arena and UCL Careers, supporting excellence in education, student experience and graduate outcomes. UCL Careers provides careers education and support to current students and graduates, alongside recruitment solutions and employer engagement activity. UCL School of Management is home to world-leading research and education in business and management, with more than 2,000 students, over half of whom are from outside the UK. The School is currently undertaking an ambitious expansion as it delivers a strategy to double in size over the next five years. This is an exciting opportunity to join the School of Management Careers team and play a key role in developing connections between our postgraduate students, employers and alumni. About the role: As Senior Employer Engagement Officer, you will lead the development of employer and alumni relationships that create valuable opportunities and experiences for postgraduate students across the UCL School of Management. You will build and maintain strong relationships with a diverse range of stakeholders, including employers, alumni, academic colleagues, Programme Directors, senior managers and professional services teams. You will use your understanding of stakeholder needs, labour market insight and student feedback to identify opportunities, secure engagement and develop activities that deliver meaningful outcomes. A key part of the role will be designing and delivering a programme of employer and alumni activity, which could include employer-led events, skills workshops, mentoring opportunities, internships and other experiences that connect students with the world of work. You will work collaboratively with colleagues and external partners to shape content, agree objectives and ensure activities are delivered to a high standard. You will also take responsibility for project managing activity from initial idea through to delivery and evaluation. This will involve coordinating stakeholders, managing competing priorities, monitoring engagement and using qualitative and quantitative feedback to assess impact and identify opportunities for improvement. Working as part of the wider School of Management Careers team, you will contribute to the development of the School's Employer Engagement Strategy, share labour market intelligence and help strengthen connections across the School and wider UCL Careers community. The role offers an opportunity for someone who enjoys working with people, building partnerships and turning ideas into well-delivered activity. You will have scope to develop new approaches, respond to changing employer and student needs, and contribute to the continued development of careers provision within a growing Business School. Please note this role is offered on a full-time basis with hybrid working available. As a student-facing role, attendance on campus (Canary Wharf School of Management) is required for currently a minimum of one day per week during term-time but may be more according to workload and business need. Job Ref: B06-00210 Closing Date: 25-10-2026 – 23:59 (UK time) Interviews: Interview are likely to take place onsite at our Canary Wharf campus from 20th November. About you: We are looking for a collaborative and proactive professional with experience of building and managing effective relationships with a wide range of stakeholders. You will be confident engaging with people at different levels and able to establish credibility, understand different perspectives and influence others to achieve shared outcomes. You will have experience of working in a stakeholder-facing, partnership, engagement or professional services environment, with the ability to manage multiple relationships and projects simultaneously. You will be comfortable taking an idea from initial concept through to delivery, coordinating others, managing deadlines and evaluating the impact of your work. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills are essential, alongside the confidence to present, facilitate discussions and engage stakeholders both individually and in group settings. You will be able to use evidence and data to inform your approach, make recommendations and identify opportunities for continuous improvement. Knowledge or experience of graduate recruitment, employer engagement, careers or relevant employment sectors such as professional services, consulting, technology, financial services, entrepreneurship or innovation would be valuable. We would also welcome applications from individuals who have developed strong stakeholder management and partnership experience in another professional services, higher education or business environment. You will be organised, adaptable and comfortable working in a busy environment where priorities can change. Most importantly, you will be committed to providing a responsive, high-quality service and to developing inclusive opportunities and positive outcomes for our students. What we offer: Standard UCL Benefits: 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49072": {
      "title": "Post Graduate Teaching Assistant",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; London; £36,433-£41,833; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07405 About us: The Centre for Advanced Spatial Analysis is offering a range of teaching opportunities for postgraduate research students to assist on its Masters’ level courses as Post Graduate Teaching Assistants. Both full and part time students are eligible to apply. Please see the Job Description, at the bottom of this page, for full details. Students may apply to assist on any of the modules, and only one application is required. Please specify which module/s you are applying for when completing application form. About the role: Post holders can be expected to take on some or all of the following duties: • Teaching assistance through the facilitation of seminars and/or workshops; • Class preparation and meeting with lecturers/module organisers; • After-class contact time with students, including time-tabled office hours; • Marking students' work; • Giving feedback to students. Each post is unique and the duties will vary depending on arrangements of teaching in each module. Please note that PGTA roles are dependent on student numbers and these are to be confirmed for individual modules. In the event of student numbers being lower than expected, the work may be not required or reduced. PGTAs will be notified if work is withdrawn on this basis. Please note that this is an internal opportunity for UCL students. Only if there is no suitable UCL PhD student available, applications from external PhD students at other universities would be considered. For any queries about the role or about the application process please contact Sandra Starosta (s.starosta@ucl.ac.uk). To apply please click on the ‘Apply Now’ button below and complete application form. Please remember to attach your CV. About you: You must be registered as a PhD student at the start of the academic year 2026/2027 to be eligible to apply. Any PGR student wishing to undertake teaching or assessing responsibilities at UCL must have attended the Arena One: Gateway workshop. You must have the right to work in the UK. Other requirements are: • Working towards a postgraduate research degree (PhD/EngD). • A desire to gain teaching experience in a built environment discipline. • Disciplinary knowledge and, if applicable, specialist skills of the subject area of the programme unit/module. • Ability to communicate clearly, both orally and in writing, with students and staff. • Excellent organisational and time-management skills. • Ability to be flexible in terms of research workload and other commitments. • Ability to work independently for short periods and as part of a team, recognising when advice / input needs to be sought. • Available for the three hour mandatory UCL Arena One Gateway Workshop, if not previously attended. • Commitment to UCL’s policy of equal opportunities and the ability to work harmoniously with colleagues and students of all cultures and backgrounds. • The ability to meet deadlines. What we offer: The appointments will be made on UCL’s salary structure at grade 6. PGTAs will be paid for training, preparation time, attending lectures and meetings, office hours and marking. The estimated total teaching hours across each module is listed in the Job Description, at the bottom of this page. Payments will be spread evenly for the duration of each module. Hours of work vary across modules. Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more about the benefits on offer. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/ Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy (https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/hr-policies-procedures-and-advice/z-policies/prevention-bullying-harassment-and-sexual-misconduct) and Personal Relationships Policy (https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/hr-policies-procedures-and-advice/z-policies/personal-relationships-policy)"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48954": {
      "title": "Post Graduate Teaching Assistant",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; London; £36,433-£41,833; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-06449 About us: About UCL & the Faculty of Engineering Join a top-tier global university ranked #1 in the UK for research strength, home to 30 Nobel laureates and a vibrant community from over 150 nationalities. As part of the UCL Faculty of Engineering - the UK’s largest and second most impactful in Europe - you will be embedded in an environment defined by academic excellence, world-class industry partnerships, and state-of-the-art facilities across Central London and the Queen Elizabeth Olympic Park. About the Department (CEGE) Ranked in the top 50 worldwide, the Department of Civil, Environmental & Geomatic Engineering delivers pioneering, people-focus research and teaching. Building on a 200-year legacy of innovation, our multidisciplinary team tackles real-world global challenges alongside industrial partners. We thrive on diversity, sector-leading inclusivity, and cutting-edge experimentation in our world-class living laboratories, including PEARL and CAVE. Driven by engineering for people, breaking boundaries across disciplines, empowering a diverse community, and continuing a legacy of pioneering leadership. Learn more about our Values. About the role: We seek Postgraduate Teaching Assistants to support teaching and learning activities for the upcoming academic year 2026-2027. As a PGTA, you will work closely with academic module leads to support the delivery of teaching for students enrolled in modules within the Civil, Environmental & Geomatic Engineering Department (CEGE). For more details, please review the Job descriptions for the module(s) you are interested in in the attachment below. Further information on each module is also available via UCL’s Module Catalogue . This role is offered on a 15 guaranteed hours contract for the 2026-2027 academic year. UCL PhD students will have priority, but students from other universities may also apply, subject to agreement with their Supervisor. PGTAs are employed at Grade 6 on the UCL non-clinical salary scale , on an hourly basis, in line with the UCL PGTA Code of Practice . About you: In order to be eligible, you need to: Be a registered Post Graduate Researcher (PGR) student at UCL or another UK university, or have recently completed a PGR degree; Have the right to work in the United Kingdom; Have the approval of your primary PhD supervisor. All PGTAs must enroll and complete UCL’s dedicated PGTA training programme, ‘Arena One’. Further in-house induction and informal training will be provided. Further Information: For more information about your experience as a PGTA, please visit : Getting started as a PGTA at UCL For queries about the application process, please contact the UCL CEGE HR team at cege-hr@ucl.ac.uk . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Bronze award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. If we receive many applications, we may close the vacancy before the published closing date.We will keep the advertisement open for at least two weeks and recommend submitting your application early. For more information about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion, please read further."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48731": {
      "title": "Post Graduate Teaching Assistant",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; London; £36,433-£41,833; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07534 About us: The Department of Medical Physics and Biomedical Engineering at UCL is one of the UK’s largest and most diverse departments. We are proud to host internationally renowned research groups across multiple sites, with staff and students working at the intersection of physics, engineering, medicine, physiology, computer science, and mathematics. As a global leader in Medical Physics and Biomedical Engineering research and education, our department fosters close collaborations with clinicians, practitioners, and scientists in major teaching hospitals. This enables our researchers to remain at the forefront of healthcare engineering and allows our students to learn about the latest innovations directly from leading experts in the field. Our mission is to advance cutting-edge research that develops new technologies and methods for diagnosing, treating, and managing diseases through medical imaging, physiological monitoring, and implantable devices. Additionally, we offer a wide range of undergraduate and postgraduate degree programs. The department holds an Athena Swan Bronze Award, recognizing our ongoing commitment to supporting and advancing women’s careers in STEM (Science, Technology, Engineering, and Mathematics). About the role: We seek Postgraduate Teaching Assistants to support teaching and learning activities for the upcoming academic session. The roles are varied and may include: • Facilitating laboratory practical sessions • Supporting small-group project work and tutorials • Assisting with marking and providing feedback • Helping to prepare teaching materials Specific responsibilities will vary depending on the assigned module(s) and departmental needs. About you: We invite applications from highly motivated individuals who are current or prospective doctoral (PhD) students at UCL. Students must be enrolled on an MPhil programme to be eligible to apply. Applicants must: • Be registered UCL research students at the time of appointment. • Have the right to work in the UK. Some roles do not require specialist skills, and training can always be provided, however some of the skills (though not all) that we are looking for are listed below – having a range of them increases the chance of being appointed: • Analogue Electronics to 1st year undergrad level and/or degree level electrical engineering • Experience using CAD software (Solidworks, Fusion 360) and experience of 3D printer use • Experience in technical drawing • Experience of measuring bio-impedance signals • Matlab and/or Simulink experience. • Arduino type programming experience • Proficiency in Python programming • Design and DIY skills • Physics background with knowledge of optics, waves, thermodynamics, solid state physics, nuclear medicine • Mechanical Engineering background – in particular, knowledge of materials and dynamics, using an Instron machine Before confirming your appointment, you must provide valid right-to-work documentation, such as a current passport and any applicable visa (sharecode). What we offer: PGTA roles are offered under an average-hour contract, meaning your total agreed hours will be evenly distributed over the duration of the contract. This arrangement ensures that you receive consistent, prorated monthly payments throughout your employment period. All new PGTAs will start at the first spinal point of Grade 6. PGTAs with prior experience from the previous academic session will advance by one spinal point. At UCL, employed PGTAs are entitled to sick leave and pay, as set out in the Conditions of Service for Research, Teaching and Professional Services Staff . PGTAs qualify for sick pay, which is automatically included in their normal monthly salary as they are on an Averaged Hours contract. Additionally, annual leave pay is also included automatically. HR Services will automatically calculate the payment due for any leave accrued for the hours worked monthly for Averaged Hours contracts. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London's Global University, we recognize that diversity enhances creativity and innovation.We strive for our community to reflect the diversity of global talent. We are committed to providing equal opportunities, fairness, and inclusion, ensuring everyone feels they belong here.Therefore, we particularly encourage applications from underrepresented candidates in UCL's workforce, including individuals from Black, Asian, and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled individuals, and those from the LGBTQI+ community. If we receive many applications, we may close the vacancy before the published closing date.We will keep the advertisement open for at least two weeks and recommend submitting your application early."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48657": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL SLASH (B03) Reference: B03-03281 About us: The Department of Greek and Latin at UCL is looking to appoint a Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Papyrology to work on the AHRC-funded project Christian Books from Oxyrhynchus . The appointment is expected to commence on 1 December 2026 and will end on 31 October 2030 . The post is part-time at 0.8 FTE . About the role: This Postdoctoral Research Fellowship forms part of the AHRC-funded project Christian Books from Oxyrhynchus , led at UCL by N. Gonis in collaboration with colleagues at the University of Birmingham and the University of Oxford. The project investigates unpublished Oxyrhynchus papyri containing fragments of the Septuagint, the New Testament, and other early Christian literature, with the aim of advancing our understanding of the history of these texts and providing new insights into the production and use of Christian books in Roman and late antique Egypt. The Research Fellow will contribute to this wider programme of research by working on papyri containing fragments of the Septuagint. Main purpose of the job, duties, and responsibilities To contribute to the research programme of Christian Books from Oxyrhynchus under the direction of the Project Lead and Co-leads. To carry out high-quality independent research. To edit unpublished Oxyrhynchus papyri containing fragments of the Septuagint. To contribute to the revision of editions by other scholars. To participate in regular project meetings. To assist in organising project events. To help maximise the scholarly impact and visibility of the project. To participate in research-support and outreach activities as directed by the line manager. In addition to the above the post-holder is expected to: Actively follow UCL policies, including those relating to equal opportunities, and give consideration within their role to how they can advance equality of opportunity and foster good relations between people who share a relevant protected characteristic and those who do not share it. Maintain an awareness and observation of Fire and Health and Safety Regulations. Carry out their duties in a resource-efficient manner and actively support UCL’s Sustainability Strategy, policies, and objectives within the remit of their role. Carry out any such other duties as are within the scope, spirit and purpose of the post as requested by the line manager. About you: You will have completed a PhD in a relevant field and possess specialist expertise in Greek literary papyrology. You should have an excellent command of Hellenistic Greek, the ability to decipher fragmentary and damaged papyrus texts, and a reading knowledge of modern scholarly languages relevant to the discipline. Familiarity with the society and culture of late Roman Egypt, the textual history of the Greek Bible, Biblical Hebrew, and/or other languages relevant to Hebrew Bible scholarship would be advantageous. You should also possess excellent written and interpersonal communication skills in English, strong attention to detail, and the ability to work both independently and collaboratively within a research environment. Further requirements can be found in the job description. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit UCL rewards and benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48426": {
      "title": "Principal Research Infrastructure Developer (Lead Cyber Security Developer)",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; IT and Digital; London; £68,284-£78,386​; Vice-President (Operations) (B01) Reference: B01-05695 About us: UCL is a multi-disciplinary university with a population of over 13,000 staff and 42,000 students from 150 different countries. For more information see www.ucl.ac.uk/about . The UCL Centre for Advanced Research Computing (ARC) is UCL’s institute for infrastructure and innovation in digital research. We are an innovative hybrid: a professional services department that delivers reliable and secure infrastructure and services to UCL research groups, and a laboratory for research and innovation. We are a home for the research technology professionals - research software engineers, HPC systems engineers, dev-ops specialists, data scientists and data stewards - who support and collaborate in the delivery of UCL research. Further information about working in ARC can be found on our website https://www.ucl.ac.uk/arc . About the role: We are seeking a highly motivated cyber security expert with experience (or a strong interest) in Research Computing/HPC environments. This post will help develop secure research infrastructure alongside providing support and guidance on cyber security best practice to staff across our Research Infrastructure, Data and Software Engineering domains. . You’ll play a vital role in not only shaping our policies and processes, but developing tooling to help secure our hardware and software. Salary range is £68,284 to £78,386 (including London Allowance) depending on experience. This role is eligible for hybrid working with a minimum of 20% on site. Key responsibilities: Research and deploy cyber security tooling Liaise with UCL’s central Information Security Group to ensure ARC’s infrastructure meets central requirements Audit infrastructure deployments to ensure compliance with security requirements Provide guidance and recommendations on security best practice to all teams across ARC Develop tooling and processes to ensure new platforms comply with ISO 27001 and CAF Contribute to documentation, knowledge sharing, and continuous service improvement About you: The successful candidate will need to demonstrate the following criteria. Essential: Experience working in Linux environments Background in cyber security Background in research computing, HPC, or a related technical field Strong problem-solving skills Ability to communicate security recommendations effectively to technical members of staff who aren’t security experts Experience auditing/monitoring cyber security compliance Desirable: Experience with Kubernetes Experience with DevOps tooling (Terraform, GitHub Actions) Familiarity with private/public cloud platforms Experience with secrets management in a large organisation Please see the attached job description and person specification for full details. If you have any queries regarding the vacancy or application process, please contact Mrs. Jodie Jackson, via e-mail (jodie.jackson@ucl.ac.uk). What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits. Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. We are committed to advancing gender equality within our department. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48849": {
      "title": "Project Manager (ASAP)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £56,431-£66,437​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11207 About us: The Department of Clinical and Movement Neurosciences at UCL Queen Square Institute of Neurology is a world-leading centre for research into movement, movement disorders and neurological disease. Bringing together clinical and basic neuroscience, the Department uses advanced imaging, computational, cellular and molecular approaches to understand brain function and develop new approaches to neurological disorders. With strong links across UCL, the National Hospital for Neurology and Neurosurgery and the Royal Free London, we offer a highly collaborative, multidisciplinary research environment with excellent facilities and opportunities for impactful translational research. About the role: We are seeking a Project Manager to join a newly funded international research programme supported by Aligning Science Across Parkinson’s (ASAP). The programme brings together researchers from five leading institutions: the MRC Laboratory of Molecular Biology, Van Andel Institute, University College London, the University of Cambridge, and the University of Manchester. The project will investigate the structure, composition and biological behaviour of alpha-synuclein filaments across Parkinson’s disease, Parkinson’s disease dementia, dementia with Lewy bodies, multiple system atrophy and related neurodegenerative conditions. Using a range of cutting-edge approaches, including cryo-electron microscopy, mass spectrometry, biochemical assays, cell models, human organoids, and mouse models, the team will explore how different filament structures arise and whether they can drive disease-associated pathology. You will coordinate the day-to-day delivery of this multidisciplinary programme, working closely with researchers across the partner institutions. You will coordinate meetings and milestones, support project reporting and facilitate the exchange of samples, protocols and data between teams. You will also have the opportunity to contribute to research data generation and analysis, as well as the preparation of presentations and scientific manuscripts. This is an exciting opportunity to play a central role in a collaborative, international programme addressing important questions in Parkinson’s disease and related neurodegenerative disorders. The post is available immediately and funded by ASAP to 31 May 2029 in the first instance. If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online, or have any queries regarding the application process, please contact the Institute of Neurology HR Team ( ion.hradmin@ucl.ac.uk ). Informal enquiries regarding the role can be addressed to Dr Zane Jaunmuktane ( z.jaunmuktane@ucl.ac.uk ). A full job description and person specification for this role can be accessed below. To apply, please upload a current CV, complete the online application form, and use the supporting statement section or upload a cover letter to outline how you meet the essential and desirable criteria for the role. Please do not upload any additional attachments as these will not be considered by the selection panel. About you: You will have a postgraduate qualification and/or research experience in neuroscience, neurodegeneration or a related biomedical discipline and experience of managing and coordinating complex research projects or programmes, with the ability to work effectively across multiple teams and institutions. You will have excellent communication and stakeholder management skills, and be proactive and resourceful, with strong problem-solving skills and the ability to manage competing priorities, monitor progress, and deliver to deadlines. An interest in research and a commitment to supporting high-quality research are essential. This role meets the eligibility requirements for a skilled worker certificate of sponsorship or a global talent visa under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore, UCL welcomes applications from international applicants who require a visa . What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits; v isit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible, and job share basis wherever possible. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce; these include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, LGBTQI+ and gender diverse people in all roles, and women in Grade 9 and 10 roles."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48805": {
      "title": "Projects Health and Safety Manager",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Estates, Facilities, Accommodation and Safety; London; See advert text; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00375 About us: Our estate is one of the most diverse and complex in the higher education sector, encompassing listed and historically significant buildings, state-of-the-art research facilities, laboratories, teaching spaces, student accommodation and new-build developments. This unique environment presents a range of health and safety challenges and opportunities, making UCL an exceptional place for safety professionals to develop and apply their expertise. The Campus Experience & Infrastructure and Estates Development (CE&I and ED) team is responsible for the strategic planning, development, maintenance and operation of the UCL estate. Together, these functions oversee everything from long-term estate strategy, property and asset management, and capital development programmes through to the day-to-day delivery of facilities and operational services. UCL Safety Services provides expert advice, assurance and leadership to support the university's commitment to a safe and healthy environment for all. We work in partnership with faculties, departments and professional services to embed a positive safety culture, ensuring that health and safety is integrated into decision-making, planning and day-to-day activities across the institution. About the role: As our Projects Health and Safety Manager, you will play a pivotal role in ensuring CDM requirements are embedded throughout the design and construction lifecycle. You will provide expert health and safety leadership across a diverse capital projects programme, including minor works, fit-outs, refurbishments, heritage buildings, infrastructure projects, public realm improvements and major new developments. You will lead on the development and implementation of policies, frameworks and procedures to support duty holders, improve safety performance and champion best practice. You will work collaboratively with project teams, designers, contractors and health and safety colleagues across the organisation to ensure a consistent, high‑quality approach to compliance. Regular site monitoring, audits and reviews will underpin your responsibility to maintain safe, legally compliant working environments. Salary range: £68,284 - £78,386 per annum (including London Allowance), depending on experience, pro-rata for part-time staff. About you: To do this job you'll need: Chartered Member of IOSH (CMIOSH) with evidence of ongoing CPD. NEBOSH Diploma, health and safety degree-level qualification, or equivalent. Extensive, up-to-date knowledge and practical experience of CDM 2015 in construction and project delivery. Experience of implementing and auditing health and safety management systems, ideally aligned to ISO 45001. Up-to-date knowledge of UK health and safety legislation, including CDM 2015, the Building Safety Act 2022, fire safety legislation, and other statutory requirements. Experience of leading incident investigations, including root cause analysis and embedding lessons learned. Excellent stakeholder engagement, communication, and influencing skills. Experience of commissioning or delivering health and safety training and supporting competency development. Confident user of Microsoft 365 and digital reporting tools, including Excel, Word, Outlook, and Power BI. Specialist knowledge of construction and M&E risks, including fire, asbestos, legionella, and gas. Experience of producing clear health and safety performance reports for senior management. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion. Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48934": {
      "title": "Recruitment and Selection Advisor",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Student Well-being and Registry; London; £36,433-£41,833; Vice-Provost (Education and Student Experience) (B06) Reference: B06-00207 About us: UCL is a global university, committed to transforming education, research and innovation to create positive change. Through the Higher Education Development & Support Institute (HEDS), we bring together expertise in education development and careers to enhance the student experience and support students and graduates to achieve their ambitions. UCL Careers provides careers education and guidance to current students, graduates and staff, alongside recruitment solutions for employers. Our core service supports students in navigating the recruitment and selection process, from developing effective CVs and cover letters through to preparing for interviews and securing opportunities in the UK and globally. As part of HEDS, UCL Careers works closely with faculties, departments, employers and other partners to ensure our careers and employability provision reflects the changing graduate labour market and the needs of our diverse student community. About the role: We are looking for a Recruitment & Selection Advisor to join our team in our careers team. This is an excellent opportunity for a careers and employability professional who is passionate about helping students and graduates develop the confidence and skills they need to succeed in the recruitment process. You will provide expert, practical advice to students and graduates applying for internships, graduate roles, jobs and further study. This will include delivering one-to-one appointments, providing constructive feedback on CVs, cover letters, application forms, motivation statements and professional profiles, and helping individuals understand how to tailor their applications effectively to different opportunities. You will also design and deliver engaging small-group workshops on employability and recruitment, including topics such as CV tailoring, cover letter writing and application forms. You will use your knowledge of recruitment and selection to make these sessions practical, interactive and relevant to the needs of UCL students and graduates. A strong understanding of graduate recruitment and selection practices will be important in this role. You will draw on your experience of how employers recruit and assess candidates, alongside current labour market and sector knowledge, to help students understand what employers are looking for and how to navigate recruitment processes successfully. You will work with a diverse student population across UCL's faculties, supporting undergraduates, taught Masters students and research students, including PhD students. You will also contribute to research into recruitment practices and graduate labour market trends, sharing insights with colleagues and incorporating them into student advice, workshops and online careers resources. The role offers the opportunity to collaborate widely across UCL Careers, faculties and departments, as well as with employers and alumni. You may also contribute to careers events, sector-focused activities and wider Careers projects. Please note, the activities in this role follow the main employer recruitment season and therefore in the Autumn term you will be expected to undertake approximately 5 hours of one to one student application advice appointments per day. This will decrease in the Spring and Summer terms in line with student demand. Please note, this is an internal recruitment advert, available to eligible UCL workers. The role offers hybrid working with currently a minimum of 2-days per week in our Bloomsbury offices (subject to change). Please draft your application and include all essential/desirable criteria in your supporting statement detailing how you meet each individual criterion. Job Ref: B06-00207 Closing Date: 28-10-2026 – 23:59 (UK time) Interviews: Interviews are due to take place from 24th November About you: You will have professional experience in careers and employability, graduate recruitment and selection, or a closely related area, with a strong understanding of the challenges students and graduates face when entering the labour market. You will have experience of advising students, graduates or other early-career individuals on a one-to-one basis, with the ability to quickly build rapport and provide clear, specific and constructive feedback. You will be confident discussing applications and helping individuals identify how they can strengthen and tailor their CVs, cover letters and other application materials. You will also have experience of planning and delivering small-group workshops or training sessions on employability, ideally covering areas such as CV writing, CV tailoring, cover letters, applications, interviews or other aspects of graduate recruitment. You will be able to create an engaging and inclusive learning environment that encourages participants to develop their own skills and confidence. Your experience will include an understanding of graduate recruitment and selection, enabling you to provide credible, labour-market-informed advice on what employers are looking for and how recruitment processes operate across different sectors. Experience of engaging with employers, undertaking labour market research or keeping up to date with recruitment trends would be particularly valuable. You will be an excellent communicator who can deliver advice and feedback with diplomacy and tact, including when working with students from diverse backgrounds and those whose first language may not be English. You will be comfortable managing a busy workload, particularly during peak graduate recruitment periods, while maintaining a high standard of service. Above all, you will take a facilitative and educational approach, helping students and graduates to develop the knowledge, skills and confidence to navigate recruitment and selection processes independently. You will be collaborative, organised and committed to delivering an inclusive careers and employability service that makes a meaningful difference to our students and graduates. What we offer: Standard UCL Benefits: 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48802": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11340 About us: Our mission is to maximise and advocate for the holistic health of all children, young people and the adults they will become, through world-class research, education and public engagement. The UCL GOS ICH, together with its clinical partner Great Ormond Street Hospital for Children, forms the largest concentration of children’s health research outside North America. The 2024-29 GOS ICH strategy focuses on its five scientific programmes. GOS ICH’s activities include active engagement with children and families, to ensure that our work is relevant and appropriate to their needs. GOS ICH generates the funding for our research by setting out our proposals in high quality applications to public, charitable and industrial funding bodies and disseminates the results of our research by publication in the medical and scientific literature, to clinicians, policy makers and the wider public. The Institute offers world-class education and training across a wide range of teaching and life learning programmes which address the needs of students and professional groups who are interested in and undertaking work relevant to child health. GOS ICH holds an Athena SWAN Charter Gold Award. Please only attach relevant documents to your application (qualifications, cover letters, supporting statements) and avoid attaching large files e.g. research papers, thesis, publications etc. About the role: The Fisher Laboratory focuses on engineering of gdT cells to deliver immunotherapeutic solutions. This research assistant position is funded by the Bone Cancer Research Trust. It is intended to allow completion of an existing project which focuses on testing Ewing Sarcoma immunotherapy using 3D models. The salary offered in this role is £39,807 per annum and is funded until 14/07/2027 in the first instance. About you: The successful candidate will have experience in the culture and engineering of gdT cells and their assessment in 3D culture systems. As this is a short position, experience in this area is essential. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48445": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £36,433-£42,891​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11244 About us: Join the Developmental Diversity (DevDiv) lab, a collaboration between the Department of Clinical, Educational & Health Psychology and the Institute of Cognitive Neuroscience at UCL, the UK’s top-ranked university for research in Psychology, Psychiatry and Neuroscience. We investigate how genetic, neural, cognitive and environmental factors shape social, emotional, and wellbeing outcomes for people with neurodevelopmental conditions. You will join a supportive research environment that works in partnership with individuals, families, clinicians and educators to advance understanding, inform policy and practice, and improve lives. About the role: You will support the CHEER project (Characterising Humour Enjoyment, Expression and Role in children with and without neurodevelopmental conditions associated with intellectual disabilities), funded by the Baily Thomas Charitable Fund. This novel strengths-based study explores what types of humour are understood, enjoyed and used by children with and without fragile X syndrome or autism, and whether humour can support social engagement. Working with Dr Katherine Ellis, Dr Sarah White, Dr Jo Moss and Prof Sophie Scott, you will contribute to and develop expertise in the design, collection, management, analysis and dissemination of research data from children and their families. The advert will close on 16/11/2026 at 23:59 GMT, however we may close applications early if we receive a high volume of applications. Early application submission is recommended. Interview Date(s) are expected to be in the first week of December 2026 A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. Salary is £39,807 per annum. UCL does not believe that this role is eligible for sponsorship and cannot provide individual immigration advice. Applicants who believe they may be eligible for a visa for this role should visit https://www.gov.uk/browse/visas-immigration/work-visas to check their individual circumstances. About you: The ideal candidate will hold a master’s in psychology or a related discipline or have equivalent research experience. They will have experience working with children with intellectual disability and their families, collecting research data, and managing research datasets. Candidates should be highly organised, able to work independently and collaboratively, and possess excellent communication skills. A keen interest in developmental psychology, intellectual disability and neurodevelopmental conditions is essential. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Athena Swan Status Statement Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48692": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11290 About us: We have an exciting opportunity for a Research Assistant to join a dynamic and collaborative team, committed to improving the lives of autistic people and people with learning disabilities. We are located in UCL’s Clinical, Educational, and Health Psychology department. The department has a strong track-record in training early-career researchers, and this will be an excellent opportunity for the right applicant to develop in their career. We are currently the top-ranked university for research in Psychology, Psychiatry and Neuroscience, research environment is ranked equal 1st in the UK with a 100% world leading rating (REF 2021). About the role: The post-holder will work across the following project workstreams, as well as liaising with a range of stakeholders and organisations. They will support completion of systematic reviews on access to and experience of psychological therapy services for adults with a learning disability and autistic adults. The successful applicant will lead on analysis of qualitative interviews with adults with a learning disability, autistic adults, and clinicians about their experiences in psychological therapy services and potential solutions to problems. They will also support stakeholder meetings to produce outputs and plans to improve services for adults with a learning disability and autistic adults. This is a fixed-term role available for 12 months in the first instance with a salary offer of £39,807 per annum (including London allowance). An acceptable DBS check is required to carry out this role. About you: We are looking for a motivated and enthusiastic individual with a BSc in Psychology (or related subject). The right candidate will have strong social skills (to build rapport with participants and diverse team members), as well as an excellent attention to detail and planning/organisational skills. The successful candidates should have excellent knowledge and understanding of the issues facing autistic people and/or people with a learning disability. Please see the Job Description and Person Specification for more information. For information about the application process, please contact: Pals.hr@ucl.ac.uk What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be under-represented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48695": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11274 About us: Our mission is to maximise and advocate for the holistic health of all children, young people and the adults they will become, through world-class research, education and public engagement. The UCL GOS ICH, together with its clinical partner Great Ormond Street Hospital for Children, forms the largest concentration of children’s health research outside North America. The 2024-29 GOS ICH strategy focuses on its five scientific programmes. GOS ICH’s activities include active engagement with children and families, to ensure that our work is relevant and appropriate to their needs. GOS ICH generates the funding for our research by setting out our proposals in high quality applications to public, charitable and industrial funding bodies and disseminates the results of our research by publication in the medical and scientific literature, to clinicians, policy makers and the wider public. The Institute offers world-class education and training across a wide range of teaching and life learning programmes which address the needs of students and professional groups who are interested in and undertaking work relevant to child health. GOS ICH holds an Athena SWAN Charter Gold Award. Please only attach relevant documents to your application (qualifications, cover letters, supporting statements) and avoid attaching large files e.g. research papers, thesis, publications etc. About the role: We are seeking a highly motivated Research Assistant to join the Chatzeli group at the UCL Great Ormond Street Institute of Child Health (GOS ICH). This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to a multidisciplinary project funded by the Wellcome Trust, investigating how branching morphogenesis scales across species. The Research Assistant will work closely with the PhD students, collaborators and postdocs in the Chatzeli lab to aid their experiments and allow smooth running of the lab. Working with both mouse and human developing salivary glands, we will uncover how organs grow, branch and pattern themselves over different timescales and sizes. You will be involved in establishing and maintaining organoid and whole organ cultures, imaging whole organs, and supporting the quantitative analysis of branching behaviour using advanced microscopy and computational tools. The role also involves supporting computational analysis of transcriptomic datasets and assisting with the day‑to‑day running of the laboratory and animal maintenance. This project sits at the interface of developmental biology, quantitative imaging, and computational analysis, offering an excellent opportunity for someone interested in interdisciplinary research. The salary offered is £39,807 per annum and is funded for 2 years in the first instance. About you: You will have a degree in a relevant biomedical or life sciences and practical experience in laboratory research (e.g. biomedical science, genetics, biochemistry, biology). You will have experience working in a laboratory setting. Experience with mouse models, organoids, primary tissue culture and imaging is advantageous. A strong interest in developmental biology, quantitative imaging and computational analysis will be advantageous for this role. Excellent organisational skills and attention to detail are crucial, along with enthusiasm, curiosity and teamwork. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48664": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11319 About us: The mission of the UCL GOS Institute of Child Health is to maximise and advocate for the holistic health of all children, young people and the adults they will become, through world-class research, education and public engagement. The UCL GOS ICH, together with its clinical partner Great Ormond Street Hospital for Children, forms the largest concentration of children’s health research outside North America. The 2024-29 GOS ICH strategy focuses on its five scientific programmes. GOS ICH’s activities include active engagement with children and families, to ensure that our work is relevant and appropriate to their needs. GOS ICH generates the funding for our research by setting out our proposals in high quality applications to public, charitable and industrial funding bodies and disseminates the results of our research by publication in the medical and scientific literature, to clinicians, policy makers and the wider public. The Institute offers world-class education and training across a wide range of teaching and life learning programmes which address the needs of students and professional groups who are interested in and undertaking work relevant to child health. GOS ICH holds an Athena SWAN Charter Gold Award. About the role: A Research Assistant position is available in the laboratory of Professor Julien Baruteau at the Great Ormond Street Institute of Child Health. The successful candidate will join a GOSH Charity-funded project developing mRNA therapy for neuronopathic Gaucher disease, a severe and currently incurable form of Gaucher disease characterised by progressive neurological involvement. The project aims to develop a therapeutic mRNA approach capable of restoring glucocerebrosidase (GCase) activity in the brain and ultimately providing a new treatment strategy for patients with neuronopathic disease. The project is already progressing well, with optimisation of the therapeutic mRNA construct underway and promising initial in vitro results. Over the course of the 12–15-month appointment, the post-holder will play a key role in advancing the programme towards in vivo proof-of-concept. Their responsibilities will include: • Completing the in vitro optimisation and characterisation of the therapeutic mRNA construct. • Investigating mRNA delivery to the mouse brain and evaluating expression and biodistribution. • Maintaining and managing a mouse model of neuronopathic Gaucher disease. • Performing in vivo studies to assess the therapeutic efficacy of the optimised mRNA construct. • Contributing to experimental design, data analysis, interpretation and dissemination of findings. The post-holder will benefit from close scientific interaction with Professor Ahad Rahim at the UCL School of Pharmacy and Professor Stephen Hart at UCL Great Ormond Street Institute of Child Health, providing an exciting multidisciplinary environment spanning RNA therapeutics, gene therapy, neuroscience and rare disease research. About you: We are seeking a highly motivated scientist who is enthusiastic about developing innovative therapies for children affected by rare neurological diseases. Candidates should have recently obtained, or be about to obtain, an MSc in Neuroscience, Molecular Biology, Biomedical Sciences or a closely related discipline. Experience with molecular biology techniques and rodent models is expected, together with strong skills in scientific communication, literature review and scientific writing. Previous hands-on experience in mRNA research and technology is essential. This is an excellent opportunity to join a dynamic translational research programme and contribute to the development of a potentially transformative therapeutic approach for neuronopathic Gaucher disease. Post is available for 12 -14 Months. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/ If you have any queries regarding the application process, please contact Madhur Sharma on ich.hr@ucl.ac.uk quoting job reference"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48348": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11256 About us: The UCL Institute for Global Health (IGH) is at the heart of UCL’s Grand Challenge of Global Health. The Institute’s vision is for a world where international policy on global health is informed by world class research. IGH aims to support the development of robust solutions to aspects of the world’s major global health challenges through scholarly outputs, education, public engagement, translational research and ultimately by influencing public policy and professional practice. We are seeking a collaborative and self-motivated Research Assistant for an exciting opportunity to contribute to the racism, intersectionality and discrimination workstream of the National Institute for Health and Care Research (NIHR) Inequalities Challenge: Maternity Disparities Consortium. The research carried out by the Consortium will focus on inequalities before, during and after pregnancy. The programme is underpinned by an innovative and transformative capacity building programme of career development awards and opportunities, linked and embedded within the consortium’s research areas to support the next generation of researchers working to address maternity inequalities. In this research Consortium we aim to provide evidence to reduce these inequalities. We will work with colleagues across the Consortium and country to improve care before, during and after pregnancy. We aim to both support staff directly and to ensure accountability mechanisms are in place to encourage organisations to tackle discrimination. About the role: The postholder will be linked primarily to the racism, intersectionality and discrimination work. This cross-cutting workstream will support each theme in three distinct ways: 1. systematic application of an intersectionality action matrix during study design and conduct to reach intended populations, alongside systematic application of anti-racism principles in intervention refinement/ implementation; 2. work collaboratively with themes to develop tailored educational resources to ensure anti-discrimination approaches are taken in intervention delivery for specific intervention components; 3. design an accountability framework that is comprehensive and adaptable between settings to reflect theme priorities for addressing racism, discrimination and intersectionality in intervention design and delivery. The position is based in London at the Institute for Global Health, University College London (UCL), with opportunities for hybrid work in line with UCL policy. The role will also involve some travel to meet the needs of the project. This is a full-time role (i.e. 36.5 hours per week) and it is available until 31 May 2028 in the first instance. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. Salary range: £39,807 to £42,891 per annum (including London Allowance), depending on experience. If you have any queries about the role, please contact Prof Delan Devakumar on d.devakumar@ucl.ac.uk. If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact the IGH HR Team on IGH.HR@ucl.ac.uk. About you: The successful candidate must hold a BSc/BA or equivalent. They must also hold a MSc in anthropology, epidemiology, global health, medical science, public health, social science or statistics, or other relevant field. The ideal candidate will have: • Proven ability and commitment to carry out high quality original research, leading to publication in peer-reviewed journals. • Quantitative and statistical software skills using standard statistical software (e.g. Stata, R) • Qualitative skills using standard qualitative analysis software (e.g. Nvivo) • Advanced organisational skills / project management and a methodical and accurate approach to work, with attention to detail and ensuring deadlines are met • Excellent written and verbal communication, including documenting work and communicating technical and/ or scientific information to a wide range of scientific and lay audiences What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48287": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11265 About us: The mission of the UCL GOS Institute of Child Health is to maximise and advocate for the holistic health of all children, young people and the adults they will become, through world-class research, education and public engagement. The UCL GOS ICH, together with its clinical partner Great Ormond Street Hospital for Children, forms the largest concentration of children’s health research outside North America. The 2024-29 GOS ICH strategy focuses on its five scientific programmes. GOS ICH’s activities include active engagement with children and families, to ensure that our work is relevant and appropriate to their needs. GOS ICH generates the funding for our research by setting out our proposals in high quality applications to public, charitable and industrial funding bodies and disseminates the results of our research by publication in the medical and scientific literature, to clinicians, policy makers and the wider public. The Institute offers world-class education and training across a wide range of teaching and life learning programmes which address the needs of students and professional groups who are interested in and undertaking work relevant to child health. GOS ICH holds an Athena SWAN Charter Gold Award. About the role: We are seeking Research Assistant to contribute to an experimental programme investigating skeletal muscle responses to mechanical and metabolic stress. The postholder will be responsible for conducting and supporting animal studies including surgical procedures, animal monitoring, endpoint assessments, tissue collection and processing. The role will also involve a range of histological and molecular analyses, including tissue sectioning and staining, RT-qPCR, ELISA and RNAscope. The postholder will analyse experimental data and undertake statistical analyses under the supervision of the research team. Post is available for one year in the first instance from 1/12/26. About you: The successful candidate will have an MSc in a relevant biological or biomedical science and experience of working in a research laboratory. Previous experience of animal experimentation, particularly mouse handling and tissue collection, and/or molecular or histological techniques would be highly desirable. You will be organised, reliable and meticulous, with the ability to follow experimental protocols accurately and maintain high standards of record keeping and data quality. You will be able to work independently while also contributing effectively as part of a multidisciplinary research team. A willingness to learn new techniques, good analytical skills and a genuine interest in skeletal muscle biology and experimental research are essential. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/ If you have any queries regarding the application process, please contact Madhur Sharma on ich.hr@ucl.ac.uk quoting job reference"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48843": {
      "title": "Research Assistant - Clinical & Affective Neuroscience",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11341 About us: UCL (www.ucl.ac.uk) is a welcoming university situated at the heart of London. UCL is a top-ranked university in the UK for research in psychology; psychiatry & neuroscience. UCL’s Institute of Cognitive Neuroscience is an inter-disciplinary research institute, bringing together different disciplines with common interests in the human mind and brain. The ICN has over 120 staff, including a vibrant body of postdoctoral and postgraduate (PhD) researchers, plus clinical fellows, organised into distinct research groups. The post-holder will be part of the Clinical and Affective Neuroscience Group About the role: This is an exciting opportunity to join a team led by Prof. Sarah Garfinkel and Prof. Camilla Nord (University of Cambridge). We are recruiting a research assistant to contribute to the running of a clinical trial. The post-holder will be responsible for conducting behavioural, interoceptive and emotion-based tasks and will contribute to the recruitment, tracking and retention of participants undergoing a mindfulness- and decentring-based intervention for depression and anxiety. This work aims to understand the interoceptive mechanisms that help us perceive, understand and regulate our emotions. About you: We are looking for an RA with a background in psychology and/or cognitive neuroscience, with experience in behavioural and psychophysiological testing and in clinical research and/or working on a clinical trial. Ability to code (python/R) and experience working with individuals with mental health conditions (anxiety/depression). Someone who enjoys teamwork, highly organised, self-directed, interested in the biological basis of emotion. Valid right to work in the UK covering at least one year (we are unable to sponsor a visa for this post). What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48812": {
      "title": "Research Assistant in Neuroimaging",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11246 About us: The Department of Imaging Neuroscience is an internationally recognised centre for research into the human brain in health and disease. Home to the Functional Imaging Laboratory (FIL), the Department combines cutting-edge neuroimaging technologies, including 7 T and 3 T MRI, MEG and wearable OPM systems, with world-leading research in cognitive neuroscience. The Department hosts major collaborative research programmes and works closely with clinicians, scientists and researchers across UCL and internationally to advance understanding of brain function and develop new approaches to the diagnosis and treatment of neurological and psychiatric disorders. About the role: We are seeking a Research Assistant to support high-quality human neuroimaging research across the Department’s range of imaging platforms. You will contribute to the safe and effective collection of data across a variety of cognitive neuroscience studies, with a particular focus on supporting study participants and the safe operation of the MRI environment. Your responsibilities will include consenting study participants, operating neuroimaging equipment following appropriate training, managing and curating data from imaging sessions, carrying out routine equipment checks and supporting quality assurance procedures. You will work closely with researchers and colleagues across the Department to ensure that data are collected accurately, safely and to a high standard. This role offers an excellent opportunity to develop practical experience in human neuroimaging within a world-renowned research environment. You will receive training in the use of the Department’s scanners and related procedures and gain experience across a range of neuroimaging platforms and research studies. The post is available immediately and is funded for two years in the first instance. If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online, or have any queries regarding the application process, please contact the Institute of Neurology HR Team ( ion.hradmin@ucl.ac.uk ). Informal enquiries regarding the role can be addressed to Daniel Bates (email: daniel.bates@ucl.ac.uk ). A full job description and person specification for this role can be accessed below. To apply, please upload a current CV, complete the online application form, and use the supporting statement section or upload a cover letter to outline how you meet the essential and desirable criteria for the role. Please do not upload any additional attachments as these will not be considered by the selection panel. About you: You will have an Honours degree in neuroscience, psychology, radiography, physics, engineering or a related discipline, together with hands-on experience of working with a neuroimaging modality, such as MRI, MEG or EEG. You will have an understanding of scientific research and MRI safety principles, as well as experience of working with databases and a strong working knowledge of data protection and GDPR. You will have excellent interpersonal, communication and IT skills and be able to work effectively and cooperatively within a multidisciplinary research environment. You will be a confident problem-solver who can learn new skills quickly, work resourcefully and use your own initiative while maintaining a high level of care and consideration for research participants. What we offer: Starting salary offered in the range £39,807 - £42,891 per annum, inclusive of London Allowance. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits, some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan On-site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible, and job share basis wherever possible. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: The Institute prides itself on operating in an all-inclusive environment irrespective of personal, physical, or social characteristics. Teamwork is highly valued, individual strengths are recognised and celebrated, and we are committed to advancing the careers of everyone. 12% of Institute staff are actively working on Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) initiatives; visit our EDI website for more information about our initiatives and priorities. The Institute also holds an Athena SWAN Gold award , in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality. As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce; these include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, LGBTQI+ and gender diverse people in all roles, and women in Grade 9 and 10 roles."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48978": {
      "title": "Research Assistant, Early Psychosis Informatics into Care (EPICare)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11209 About us: The UCL Division of Psychiatry, located within the Faculty of Brain Sciences, is a world-renowned centre of excellence in mental health research. Our mission is to improve mental health through cutting-edge research and education. By leveraging insights from basic science, we address clinical problems with the aim of improving patient outcomes and public health. Moreover, we take pride in delivering innovative teaching that is directly relevant to clinical practice and informed by our research. Join our team and help us drive forward the field of mental health research and education The EPICare programme is a large NIHR- and Mental Health Mission–funded study to develop, implement and evaluate the first national digital psychosis registry and NHS Trust-based clinician dashboard in Early Intervention in Psychosis (EIP) services across England. The programme aims to provide timely insights into EIP service care, and patient outcomes to support clinical decision-making and improvements in EIP care. EPICare is organised across seven work packages spanning scientific evaluation, programme management, public and patient involvement, data integrity and research capacity development. We are seeking a Research Assistant to support activity during the evaluation stage of EPICare (work packages 5 and 6) which is supported by an NIHR Programme Grant for Applied Research. The post is funded at Grade 6B for 12 months at 0.4 FTE as part of this four-year NIHR Programme Grant. The programme will assess how accurately and reliably demographic, treatment and clinical outcome data are captured in the digital registry and examine the clinical effectiveness of the registry by investigating variations in psychosis incidence, inequalities in care, and the real-world effectiveness of treatments on patient outcomes. It will also evaluate whether the clinical dashboard improves data quality and adherence to NICE standards, and whether both the registry and dashboard are cost-effective. Within this quantitative research support role, the research assistant will lead data quality, integrity and management activities with the support of the Programme Manager, Chief Investigator and the investigator team, including preparing large datasets for analysis and developing and implementing data management procedures to support reproducible, high-quality analyses. The postholder will also assist in delivering EPICare platform training for clinical staff and provide administrative support to the Programme Manager. The role is based at UCL with occasional travel to study sites. This post suits a candidate with strong data management and organisational skills who is motivated to contribute to high-quality applied mental health research. For more information about EPICare, please visit: www.epicaremh.org About the role: Duties and responsibilities The Research Assistant will support the management of the EPICare registry dataset, working closely with the Programme Manager (Melanie Lafanechere), Chief Investigator (Prof. James Kirkbride) and wider research team. They will clean, process and prepare data for analysis; collaborate with team members to resolve data quality issues; be involved in the development, implementation and maintenance of data management documentation; and support programme evaluation activities. The postholder will also support the development of EPICare platform training materials and delivery of training to clinical staff, providing support to stakeholders across the participating NHS pilot Trusts. Additionally, the Research Assistant will provide administrative support to the Programme manager and EPICare team, coordinating meetings and communicating with multiple stakeholders. The postholder will be expected to communicate with stakeholders across academic, clinical, digital, governance and lived experience groups to help ensure the smooth running of the EPICare programme. Appointment details: The post is graded at Grade 6B, with an annual salary ranging from £39,807 to £42,891 pro rata, including London Allowance. The appointment is available from 1 November 2026 and is funded for a fixed duration of 12 months at 0.4FTE About you: You will have a strong academic background (Bachelor’s and Master’s degrees) in psychology, epidemiology, public health or a related field and an interest in applied mental health research. You will have excellent organisational skills, attention to detail and strong written and verbal communication skills. You should have experience of conducting data analysis using statistical software, managing and maintaining study documentation and coordinating meetings. You will be comfortable working both independently and as part of a multidisciplinary research team to meet agreed deadlines, and should uphold the highest ethical, governance and professional standards in all aspects of the research. The ideal candidate will also have a good understanding of governance requirements pertaining to sensitive health data, GDPR and Good Clinical Practice, and may have experience working with large datasets similar to the EPICare registry or within mental health research or services. Application process: A full job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. In your personal statement, please explain how you meet each of the essential and desirable criteria outlined in the job description. You should include the following documents in your application: (1) Maximum of 2-page CV, (2) Maximum of 2-page personal statement Applications will be anonymised, therefore please ensure your personal statement is fully completed. Please also ensure to include your degree class within your personal statement if applicable Please do not upload any additional documents (transcripts, your publications, or other reports). Interviews will be held in mid-October (exact date TBC, online via Microsoft Teams) Contact details: If you have any queries about the role, please contact Melanie Lafanechere at m.lafanechere@ucl.ac.uk If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact dop.hr@ucl.ac.uk What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance The full range of staff benefits can be found here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/pay-benefits/staff-benefits Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. The Division of Psychiatry prides itself for operating in an all-inclusive environment. Teamwork is highly valued, individual strengths are recognised and celebrated, and there is a commitment to advancing the careers of everyone, regardless of gender or role. We aim to provide a family friendly environment where both women and men feel able to take the time they need for family. The Athena SWAN Charter recognises commitment to advancing women's careers in science, technology, engineering, maths and medicine (STEMM) employment in academia and the Division is delighted to have an Athena Swan Silver Award since 2022. All staff are invited to contribute to EDI initiatives within the Division to contribute to improving working conditions and opportunities for all. Mentoring is a crucial part of supporting career progression and mentoring schemes are available for staff in the Division. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/psychiatry/about/equality-diversity-and-inclusion https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48425": {
      "title": "Research Data Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; IT and Digital; London; £43,981-£54,019​; Vice-President (Operations) (B01) Reference: B01-05696 About us: UCL is a world-leading teaching and research university, often ranked in the top ten in the world with an annual turnover of well over £1 billion. Part of UCL’s vision is to take on the hardest global challenges. The UCL Centre for Advanced Research Computing (ARC) is UCL’s new institute for infrastructure and innovation in digital research - the supercomputers, datasets, software and people that make computational science and digital scholarship possible. We are an innovative hybrid: a professional services department that delivers reliable and secure infrastructure and services to UCL research groups, and a laboratory for research and innovation in the application of advanced computational and data intensive research methods, working in partnership with academics from all fields. We are a home for the research technology professionals - research software engineers, HPC systems engineers, dev-ops specialists, data engineers, data scientists and data stewards - who support and collaborate in the delivery of UCL research About the role: Would you like to work with complex data and bridge cutting-edge AI with domain research? Research Data Scientists (also known as AI Engineer, Data Scientist, Machine Learning Specialist, Computational Statistician): Conduct collaborative research to extract knowledge and insight from datasets using a variety of mathematical, statistical and computational approaches. Develop software and scripts to implement these analyses in robust and reproducible ways. Work with research infrastructure developers to deploy these to production. You will, in all subject areas, provide data science expertise and services to world-leading research teams, training researchers in best practices, and working with scientists and scholars to build workflows, analyses and software to meet new research challenges. With such a varied job we don’t expect our candidates to know it all from the start. It is an excellent opportunity to develop new skills, spending time to study both the research areas we support and the specialist technologies applied About you: We are specifically looking for candidates with hands-on experience in Agentic AI and Large Language Models (LLMs) , along with a strong foundation in mathematics and a deep understanding of deep learning architectures . The successful applicant will also have experience of analysing, researching, and solving complex computational data analysis problems. You will be able to communicate well with researchers, and present complex or technical information to a range of stakeholders. A relevant degree or equivalent professional experience is required according to seniority. We are looking for candidates who have addressed complex data science problems in at least one field, but more importantly, who can build an understanding of new scientific questions, and see how to translate research needs to technology requirements. We are deeply committed to data science best practices and their application in research contexts. You should share our enthusiasm for reproducible, reliable computational research, and be able to help to spread that to our research community. In addition, ARC aims to help you develop your own research ideas in data science and connected areas. You will be able to set up research initiatives, bid for funding, exchange/collaborate within ARC themes across a range of novel digital research. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49050": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11259 About us: The mission of the Department of Neuroinflammation is to transform the lives of people living with diseases underpinned by neuroinflammation, including multiple sclerosis and Alzheimer’s disease, for the better, by identifying, understanding, and correcting or preventing the underlying mechanisms. Our vision is to build upon the excellence and breadth of our research, seamlessly integrating basic and clinical science. The Physics group, led by Professor Claudia Wheeler-Kingshott, develops quantitative MRI approaches to investigate neurological disease, particularly multiple sclerosis. The group has access to a 3T Philips Ingenia Cx MRI scanner and develops applications spanning microstructure, physiology and functional measurements across the central and peripheral nervous systems. The group has close collaborations with the University of Pavia in Italy, including research into brain digital twin technologies. Professor Wheeler-Kingshott also leads international research exploring ultra-low-field MRI and other accessible neuroimaging technologies in the UK, Italy and Uganda. About the role: We are seeking an enthusiastic and motivated researcher to join Professor Wheeler-Kingshott’s Physics group within the Department of Neuroinflammation and contribute to a UKRI-funded project investigating the physics of brain digital twins and their potential application in low-resource settings. You will work with Dr Antonio Ricciardi and Professor Wheeler-Kingshott to understand the limits of this technology using the 3T MRI scanner and EEG signal recordings. You will also learn about fast neural electrical impendence tomography (fnEIT) developed by Professor David Holder, which in future may be a better option for detecting brain function in low resource settings. The post is available from 01 November 2026 and funded for 1 year in the first instance. If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online, or have any queries regarding the application process, please contact the Institute of Neurology HR Team ( ion.hradmin@ucl.ac.uk ). For informal enquiries about the role please contact Professor Claudia Wheeler-Kingshott (email: c.wheeler-kingshott@ucl.ac.uk ). A full job description and person specification for this role can be accessed below. To apply, please upload a current CV, complete the online application form, and use the supporting statement section or upload a cover letter to outline how you meet the essential and desirable criteria for the role. Please do not upload any additional attachments as these will not be considered by the selection panel. About you: You should hold a PhD in physics, mathematics, engineering, neuroscience or related discipline, or be close to obtaining, with demonstrable experience in quantitative MRI acquisition/processing and demonstrable experience/understanding of computational modelling. Experience/understanding of virtual brain modelling platforms and a good knowledge of neuroanatomy and advanced neuroimaging analyses such as imagine and signal manipulation for parameter’s extraction is essential, as is knowledge of machine learning / artificial intelligence applications in neuroscience, and strong programming skills, including Python and MATLAB. This role meets the eligibility requirements for a skilled worker certificate of sponsorship or a global talent visa under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore, UCL welcomes applications from international applicants who require a visa. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits, some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan On-site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible, and job share basis wherever possible. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: The Institute prides itself on operating in an all-inclusive environment irrespective of personal, physical, or social characteristics. Teamwork is highly valued, individual strengths are recognised and celebrated, and we are committed to advancing the careers of everyone. 12% of Institute staff are actively working on Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) initiatives; visit our EDI website for more information about our initiatives and priorities. The Institute also holds an Athena SWAN Gold award , in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality. As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce; these include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, LGBTQI+ and gender diverse people in all roles, and women in Grade 9 and 10 roles."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48957": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11291 About us: A Research Fellow post is available to work with Senior Research Fellow Dr Paul Nderitu at the UCL Institute of Ophthalmology at UCL, ranked the best place in the world to study ophthalmology (CWUR Rankings by Subject 2017). UCL Institute of Ophthalmology conducts cutting-edge research, attracting principal investigators, post-doctoral fellows and MD/PhD students of the highest international calibre to a stimulating research environment. About the role: The successful Research Fellow will have an exciting opportunity to play a leading role in a project that will develop state-of-the art AI clinical support tools for the detection and prediction of two of the most common causes of sight-loss; diabetic eye disease (diabetic retinopathy) and sight-threatening age-related macula degeneration (wetAMD). The successful candidate will gain experience in developing AI models using longitudinal multimodal retinal imaging data from Moorfields Eye Hospital, a world-leading institution for eye care. The post is funded from the 1 January 2027 until 31 December 2027, in the first instance. The salary for this role will be on Grade 7, between spine points 32-34 (£46,261 - £48,679 per annum, inclusive of London Allowance.) Appointment as a Research Fellow at Grade 7 is dependent upon having been awarded a PhD. If this is not the case, an initial appointment will be made as a Research Assistant at Grade 6b with payment at Grade 7 backdated upon award of a PhD to the date of final submission of the PhD thesis (with corrections). About you: The successful candidate will hold an PhD in a relevant topic, such as computer science. They will have significant experience in computer vision, deep learning tools (e.g. PyTorch), large-data analysis, statistical analysis and familiarity with manuscript writing and working in cluster or cloud-based environments. Experience with longitudinal data and/or healthcare datasets, particularly in ophthalmology, foundation models, and domain adaptation techniques, will be advantageous. The applicant should also have excellent communication skills and a commitment to conducting high-quality research that will benefit healthcare. Application Process If you have any queries regarding the vacancy please contact Dr Paul Nderitu (p.nderitu@ucl.ac.uk) If you have any queries regarding the application process please email: ioo.hr@ucl.ac.uk. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. To apply for the vacancy please click on the 'Apply Now' button below. Please ensure you attach your CV and highest academic qualification. In addition, please ensure you provide a personal statement outlining how you meet the criteria to support your application. Please DO NOT attach research papers. What we offer: Working with some of the greatest intellects in the world, UCL also offers a generous pension scheme and holiday allowance, plus closure and bank holidays as well as enhanced parental pay and more. Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48899": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07906 About us: The Department of Medical Physics and Biomedical Engineering is one of the largest Medical Physics and Biomedical Engineering departments in the UK. The Department is proud to host internationally-leading research groups covering a broad range of activities and spread over several sites. Our staff and students have a diverse range of interests and expertise, covering many areas of physics, engineering, medicine, physiology, computer science, and mathematics. Our focus is on excellent academic research in identifying new technologies and methods for diagnosis, treatment and management of diseases using medical imaging, physiological monitoring, and the development of implanted devices. We also offer a range of undergraduate and postgraduate degrees. The Advanced X-Ray Imaging (AXIm) group is a multi-PI group, hosted within the Department of Medical Physics and Biomedical Engineering, that develops novel X-ray image formation techniques and deploys them with the latest developments in source and detector technology. We have an opportunity to recruit a Postdoctoral Research Fellow to the AXIm group. The post is part of an international collaboration with cutting-edge x-ray industry from the Republic of Korea, with options to travel to Seoul and engage directly with the industrial collaborators. The aim of the project is to develop novel x-ray microscopy methods that go beyond current capabilities. AXIm has recently achieved world-first results in the 3D imaging of intact, unstained biological tissue where every single cell and cell nucleus is visualized. This project will significantly expand these capabilities into much larger volumes and across different materials. About the role: Applications are invited for a Research Fellow position to join a collaborative effort with leading x-ray industry in the Republic of Korea and other partners to develop innovative, laboratory-based x-ray microscopy that goes beyond the currently available capabilities. The post offers a unique opportunity to conduct research and development at an international leading university and contribute to the developments of advanced x-ray methods for the life and physical sciences. UCL’s AXIm group is internationally recognised as a leading group in the development of x-ray phase contrast and other cutting-edge x-ray imaging methods. The appointed person will be formally part of the team led by Prof Sandro Olivo and be co-supervised by him and Dr Michela Esposito, who leads the microscopy activities. The postholder will be required to design, build and commission an innovative x-ray instrument, and to validate it on cutting-edge scientific and industrial applications. The role will also involve preparing and presenting findings of research activity to colleagues for review purposes, contributing to the drafting and submitting of papers to appropriate peer-reviewed journals, preparing progress reports on research for funding bodies amd other stakeholders, and contributing to the overall activities of the research team and department as required. For more information about this role, please contact Prof Sandro Olivo, a.olivo@ucl.ac.uk , or Dr Michela Esposito, michela.esposito@ucl.ac.uk About you: Applications are invited for a Research Fellow position to join a collaborative effort with leading x-ray industry in the Republic of Korea and other partners to develop innovative, laboratory-based x-ray microscopy that goes beyond the currently available capabilities. The post offers a unique opportunity to conduct research and development at an international leading university and contribute to the developments of advanced x-ray methods for the life and physical sciences. UCL’s AXIm group is internationally recognised as a leading group in the development of x-ray phase contrast and other cutting-edge x-ray imaging methods. The appointed person will be formally part of the team led by Prof Sandro Olivo and be co-supervised by him and Dr Michela Esposito, who leads the microscopy activities. The postholder will be required to design, build and commission an innovative x-ray instrument, and to validate it on cutting-edge scientific and industrial applications. The role will also involve preparing and presenting findings of research activity to colleagues for review purposes, contributing to the drafting and submitting of papers to appropriate peer-reviewed journals, preparing progress reports on research for funding bodies amd other stakeholders, and contributing to the overall activities of the research team and department as required. For more information about this role, please contact Prof Sandro Olivo, a.olivo@ucl.ac.uk , or Dr Michela Esposito, michela.esposito@ucl.ac.uk A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. We may close applications early if we receive a high volume of applications. The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please visit www.ucl.ac.uk/ways-of-working If you have any queries about the role, please contact a.olivo@ucl.ac.uk . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Bronze award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48912": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11039 About us: The Division of Biosciences at UCL is one of the world's foremost centres for research and teaching in the biological sciences. We have an outstanding international reputation and a community of over 500 staff and more than 300 PhD students engaged in cutting edge research and collaboration. Further information can be found on our website https://www.ucl.ac.uk/biosciences This is an exciting opportunity to join the Amoyel lab in the Department of Cell and Developmental Biology at UCL on a Wellcome Trust-funded project. The lab studies how local and systemic environmental inputs influence reproduction. We use a variety of approaches, from genetics and imaging, RNA sequencing and metabolomics, to mathematical modelling. We use the Drosophila testis as a model system, exploiting the many tools available in flies, as well as developing new tools when needed. Our research aims to bridge scales to understand how cell behaviour is coordinated both at the local level and with organismal physiology and how this is underpinned by changes in cell biology. The lab aims to be an inclusive and supportive environment; all applicants are welcome and informal enquiries are encouraged. About the role: The post holder will investigate how diet impacts on male fertility in Drosophila. They will join a team within the lab studying systemic physiology and its regulation by diet, and in turn, how stem cells in the gonad respond to these changes. Initially, they will contribute to ongoing work in which we seek to identify inter-organ signals that regulate the response of testis stem cells to nutrient levels. Over time, the successful candidate would be expected to develop independent lines of research and original contributions within this broad topic. This role is an open-ended contract with a funding end date of 28/02/2028 in the first instance. Appointment at Grade 7 is dependent upon having been awarded a PhD; if this is not the case, initial appointment will be at Grade 6B (salary £38,357 - £41,005 per annum) with payment at Grade 7 being backdated to the date of final submission of the PhD Thesis. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. Successful candidates will be expected to adhere to the Wellcome Trust’s policies on the Responsible Conduct of Research ( https://wellcome.org/research-funding/guidance/policies-grant-conditions/responsible-conduct-research ), as well as other Wellcome Trust policies ( https://wellcome.org/research-funding/guidance/policies-grant-conditions ), including promoting a positive research environment and a commitment to continuing personal development. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. If you have any queries about the role, please contact Marc Amoyel at marc.amoyel@ucl.ac.uk . If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact the HR Administrator. About you: You will be expected to lead a research project, through designing and conducting experiments in consultation with the supervisor. You will be able to work independently and as part of a team, to present your work to colleagues and to a wider audience, and to critically evaluate your work and the scientific literature. You must have a PhD in a relevant field (eg developmental or stem cell biology, cell biology or metabolism), or be working towards obtaining one. You should have a track record in producing high quality research, and be in a position to apply for research fellowships. Experience with Drosophila work is desirable but not essential, but you should have experience of biochemical or ‘omics techniques and a good understanding of metabolic pathways and their influence on cell behaviour. What we offer: The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/policies-advice/ways-working to find out more. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know that diversity and inclusion are fundamental drivers of creativity, innovation, and excellence, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world's talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong, thrive, and reach our full potential. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian, and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women’. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity, and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/ ."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48847": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11301 About us: The Institute of Ophthalmology is committed to a multi-disciplinary research portfolio that furthers an understanding of the eye and visual system linked with clinical investigations targeted to specific problems in the prevention and treatment of eye disease. The combination of the Institute’s research resource with the resources of Moorfields Eye Hospital, which has the largest ophthalmic patient population in the Western World, opens the way for advances at the forefront of vision research and provides an unparalleled setting for research and training in Ophthalmology. About the role: Inherited retinal diseases (IRDs) are the commonest cause of blindness amongst working age adults. There are over 360 causative genes, but for the majority of patients, no treatments are available. Professor Mariya Moosajee and her team are working to advance our understanding of IRDs by generating human disease models using induced pluripotent stem cell technology to derive patient retinal organoids. These can be studied to identify disease pathophysiology and to test new treatments. Working in close collaboration with Professor Mariya Moosajee, the Post Doctoral Fellow will focus on the development of non-viral approaches, using mRNA and non-viral gene therapy, as potential treatment options for two genes of interest MYO7A and GUCY2D-retinopathy. The Post Doctoral Fellow will use a range of techniques to facilitate the group’s work, including: stem cell culture, generation of iPSC colonies and differentiation into target retinal cells, histology, electron and confocal microscopy, molecular biology such as western blots, and immunocytochemistry. Additional skills may be required for testing in vivo models, and necessary training will be provided where necessary. Candidates with a PhD is essential, experience in the aforementioned techniques, and who are bright, independent and highly motivated, but willing and able to work effectively as part of a collaborative team are encouraged to apply. About you: The candidate must be a good communicator, both in lay and scientific terms, with strong writing skills. They must be competent with literature searches, published papers, and have background understanding of genetic eye disease. The candidate should be organised, hardworking and enjoy writing. What we offer: Working with some of the greatest intellects in the world, UCL also offers a generous pension scheme and holiday allowance, plus closure and bank holidays as well as enhanced parental pay and more. Visit h ttps://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Application Processs If you have any queries regarding the vacancy please contact Professor Maria Moosajee, (email: m.moosajee@ucl.ac.uk ). If you have any queries regarding the application process please email: ioo.hr@ucl.ac.uk . A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. Please ensure you attach your CV, highest academic qualification . Also, please ensure you provide a personal statement outlining how you meet the criteria to support your application. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality. The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please visit www.ucl.ac.uk/ways-of-working to find out more."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48702": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11205 About us: The Department of Epilepsy at the UCL Queen Square Institute of Neurology is dedicated to advancing the understanding and treatment of epilepsy and other neurological disorders. Research within the Department spans basic and translational science, bringing together researchers with expertise across a range of neurological conditions and experimental approaches. Based within the department, the Jepson laboratory uses Drosophila models to investigate the mechanisms underlying neurodevelopmental and neurodegenerative disorders, including dystonia, epilepsy, neuropathies and amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS). About the role: We are seeking a Research Fellow to join an exciting research project investigating the role of DEAF1 in neurodevelopment. Working with Dr Gabriel Aughey in the Jepson laboratory and in collaboration with Dr Jenna Carpenter, you will contribute to research aimed at informing the development of a potential gene therapy for a severe neurodevelopmental disorder caused by mutations in the DEAF1 gene. Using Drosophila as a model system, you will help generate and characterise new disease models and investigate the cell types and developmental windows in which restoring DEAF1 function may be beneficial. You will contribute to genetic and behavioural experiments, as well as transcriptomic studies and data analysis, to improve our understanding of how DEAF1 disruption affects the development and function of the nervous system. The role offers an excellent opportunity to gain experience in a range of genetic, molecular and behavioural approaches while working as part of a collaborative project that brings together Drosophila and human cell models. The role is available from 01 October 2026 and funded by for one year in the first instance. If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online, or have any queries regarding the application process, please contact the Institute of Neurology HR Team ( ion.hradmin@ucl.ac.uk ). Informal enquiries regarding the role can be addressed to Dr Gabriel Aughey ( g.aughey@ucl.ac.uk ). A full job description and person specification for this role can be accessed below. To apply, please upload a current CV, complete the online application form, and use the supporting statement section or upload a cover letter to outline how you meet the essential and desirable criteria for the role. Please do not upload any additional attachments as these will not be considered by the selection panel. About you: You will have a PhD in Neuroscience, Genetics or a related field, with experience in Drosophila husbandry, maintenance, genetics, and behavioural assays. You will also have experience of molecular biology techniques and confocal and/or live imaging methods. Knowledge of neuroscience and neurological disease research, alongside experience of collaborative and interdisciplinary working, is essential, as are strong organisational, communication, literacy and numeracy skills and the ability to prioritise competing demands and work effectively as part of a research team. This role meets the eligibility requirements for a skilled worker certificate of sponsorship or a global talent visa under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore, UCL welcomes applications from international applicants who require a visa. What we offer: Appointment as Research Fellow is dependent upon having been awarded a PhD; if this is not the case, initial appointment will be as Research Assistant (salary £39,807 - £42,891 per annum) with appointment as Research Fellow being backdated to the date of final submission of the PhD thesis. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits, some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan On-site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible, and job share basis wherever possible. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: The Institute prides itself on operating in an all-inclusive environment irrespective of personal, physical, or social characteristics. Teamwork is highly valued, individual strengths are recognised and celebrated, and we are committed to advancing the careers of everyone. 12% of Institute staff are actively working on Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) initiatives; visit our EDI website for more information about our initiatives and priorities. The Institute also holds an Athena SWAN Gold award , in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality. As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce; these include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, LGBTQI+ and gender diverse people in all roles, and women in Grade 9 and 10 roles."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48613": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07841 About us: The UCL School of Management is home to UCL’s business and management research and education programmes. The School has forged a reputation for world-leading research and teaching in the areas of innovation management, the creation and growth of technology-intensive organisations, business analytics, and entrepreneurship. The research undertaken at the UCL School of Management has been rated as ‘world-leading’ and ‘internationally excellent’ in the Research Excellence Framework 2021 (REF), placing us 2nd in the UK for Business and Management. The School is accredited by both the AACSB and AMBA, and is a member of the Chartered Association of Business Schools. The School is home to over 2,300 students, with more than 55% from outside the UK, who study across a range of undergraduate, postgraduate, executive and PhD programmes. These programmes prepare our students for leadership roles in the next generation of tech-intensive organisations and new ventures, as well as in finance and consulting firms. This is an exciting time to join the UCL School of Management, which, since 2025, has been undergoing a rapid expansion to deliver a new strategy that will see staff and student numbers double over the coming years. This growth includes new degree programmes, additional staff, and enhanced facilities at our Canary Wharf campus, supporting our mission to educate socially conscious entrepreneurial leaders equipped to tackle the world’s greatest challenges. Further information about the School can be found here: www.mgmt.ucl.ac.uk/ About the role: UCL School of Management Operations and Technology group is seeking a motivated, independent, and highly skilled Postdoctoral Research Fellow to contribute to a research agenda in the following two potential directions: • Led by Dr. Kenan Arifoglu, Dr. Aras Selvi: research on end-to-end synthetic data generation and decision making under uncertainty. The work will develop new methods for generating privacy-preserving data that remain useful for the operational decisions ultimately made from them, rather than treating data generation and decision making as separate stages. A particular focus will be on high-stakes healthcare settings, where sensitive data must support reliable planning, capacity, and resource-allocation decisions under uncertainty despite privacy restrictions. • Led by Dr. Yi-Chun Akchen and Dr. Mengzhenyu Zhang: projects on examining how firms, platforms, and market designers can use information and assortment strategically. Two related research projects are possible. The first project studies information disclosure combined with mechanism design in markets with private information. The second studies dynamic information and assortment policies in markets where customers arrive over time and do not observe the history of previous decisions. Both contribute to a broader understanding of how limited, carefully designed communication and menus can improve allocation, coordination, and revenue. This post is a fixed‑term, 12‑month contract. The role is based at the School’s Canary Wharf campus, and the start date is flexible, with appointments possible from January 2027. About you: The successful candidate will need to evidence the following in their application. They must hold a PhD, or be close to completing a PhD, in Operations Research, Operations Management, Economics, Computer Science, or a related discipline. If the PhD has not been formally awarded before the employment start date, the post holder will be appointed at the Research Assistant Grade 6B level. They must demonstrate clear interest in one of the two research agendas presented as core to this role, as set out in the Job Description. This interest must be evidenced in the Cover Letter, where applicants are required to specify which agenda they intend to pursue and how they plan to use the 12‑month appointment to meet the duties and responsibilities of the post. They must also demonstrate advanced knowledge and experience in optimisation methods or game theory, mechanism design, economic theory, and strong mathematical and analytical skills, including experience with formal proofs. This expertise should be evidenced through the CV and writing samples. In addition, applicants must show that they can propose a research plan that fits within one of the two research directions described in the Job Description, and this should be clearly articulated in the Cover Letter. Please see the attached Job Description and Person Specification for full details. When completing your supporting statement, please tell us, using examples, how you meet the essential criteria listed in the Job Description. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits, some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and, for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity, and Inclusion here."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48880": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11327 About us: The mission of the Department of Clinical and Experimental Epilepsy is to transform the lives of people living with epilepsy for the better by identifying, understanding, and correcting or preventing the underlying mechanisms leading to the epilepsies and associated comorbidities in each individual person. Our vision is to build upon the excellence and breadth of our research, seamlessly integrating basic and clinical science. Based within the department, the Experimental Epilepsy Group conducts research spanning the fundamental mechanisms of vesicle fusion and synaptic transmission, ion channel biology, experimental models of epilepsy, neural circuit function, neurological diseases modelled in rodents and flies, and advanced therapies for epilepsy and related disorders. Professor Kirill Volynski’s group investigates the molecular mechanisms underlying neurotransmitter release, using quantitative imaging and computational approaches to study presynaptic function in health and disease. About the role: We are seeking a Research Fellow to join Professor Kirill Volynski’s laboratory and contribute to an interdisciplinary research programme investigating the molecular and cellular mechanisms underlying neurodevelopmental disorders caused by mutations in synaptic vesicle release machinery genes, known as SNAREopathies. You will investigate the functional consequences of disease-linked variants using advanced electrophysiological and optical approaches, including quantitative fluorescence imaging of neurotransmitter release at individual synapses. The research will draw on complementary expertise in synaptic physiology, molecular biology, biophysics and structural biology. The role involves collaboration with Professor Dimitri Kullmann at UCL Queen Square Institute of Neurology and Dr Shyam Krishnakumar at Yale University, providing an opportunity to work across complementary research environments and contribute to research that will advance understanding of SNAREopathies and inform potential therapeutic and gene therapy approaches. The post is available from 01 November 2026 and is funded by an MRC grant awarded to Professor Volynski for one year in the first instance. If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online, or have any queries regarding the application process, please contact the Institute of Neurology HR Team ( ion.hradmin@ucl.ac.uk ). We expect to hold interviews on Thursday, 22nd October. A full job description and person specification for this role can be accessed below. To apply, please upload a current CV, complete the online application form, and use the supporting statement section or upload a cover letter to outline how you meet the essential and desirable criteria for the role. Please do not upload any additional attachments as these will not be considered by the selection panel. About you: You’ll have a PhD in a relevant discipline within neuroscience or biophysics (or be close to obtaining) and a track record of experimental laboratory research. Experience of quantitative optical microscopy and/or patch-clamp electrophysiology, molecular biology, and working with neuronal cultures is essential. Knowledge of scientific computer programming and a strong publication record are desirable. This role meets the eligibility requirements for a skilled worker certificate of sponsorship or a global talent visa under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore, UCL welcomes applications from international applicants who require a visa. What we offer: Appointment as Research Fellow is dependent upon having been awarded a PhD; if this is not the case, initial appointment will be as Research Assistant (salary £39,807 - £42,891 per annum) with appointment as Research Fellow being backdated to the date of final submission of the PhD thesis. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits, some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan On-site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits; visit This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: The Institute prides itself on operating in an all-inclusive environment irrespective of personal, physical, or social characteristics. Teamwork is highly valued, individual strengths are recognised and celebrated, and we are committed to advancing the careers of everyone. 12% of Institute staff are actively working on Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) initiatives; visit our EDI website for more information about our initiatives and priorities. The Institute also holds an Athena SWAN Silver award , in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality. As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce; these include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, LGBTQI+ and gender diverse people in all roles, and women in Grade 9 and 10 roles. As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. 12% of Institute staff are actively working on EDI initiatives; visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/ion/equality-diversity-inclusion for more information about what we’re doing. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce; these include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, LGBTQI+ and gender diverse people in all roles, and women in Grade 9 and 10 roles."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48764": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11357 About us: The UCL Cancer Institute is the hub for basic biological and translational cancer research at University College London, one of the world's leading universities. The Institute draws together over 400 talented scientists who are working together to translate research discoveries into developing more effective therapies for cancer patients. The UCL Cancer Institute is part of the CRUK City of London Centre, which draws together the expertise present in four leading centres for cancer research, UCL, Barts, KCL and the Francis Crick Institute. Further Information about the UCL Cancer Institute can be found on our website https://www.ucl.ac.uk/cancer/ About the role: The Treatment Resistance Team, led by Prof Gerhardt Attard, focuses on identifying and overcoming treatment resistance in prostate cancer. The group has been integrating liquid and solid biopsy multiomic analysis to discover novel markers for patient stratification and to track tumour clone dynamics in patients progressing through multiple lines of treatment. The overall aims are to improve the management and detection of prostate cancer patients and to discover novel drivers of resistance in multiple cancer types. Appointment at Grade 7 is dependent upon having been awarded a PhD; if this is not the case, initial appointment will be at Research Assistant Grade 6B, with payment at Grade 7 being backdated to the date of final submission of the PhD thesis. Applications should include a CV and a Cover Letter: In the Cover Letter please provide evidence of the essential and desirable criteria in the Person Specification part of the Job Description. (By including a Cover Letter, you can leave blank the 'Why you have applied for this role' field in the application form, which is limited in the number of characters it will allow.) The role will be available for 18 months in the first instance with the possibility of extension. About you: You will hold a PhD, or be close to completing one, in a relevant biological or biomedical discipline, with substantial hands-on experience in next-generation sequencing workflows, including whole genome library generation, targeted sequencing and sequencing data analysis. You will have experience of nucleic acid extraction, managing research samples and databases, and working with genomic datasets using programming languages such as R and/or Python. Knowledge of prostate cancer biology and familiarity with GCP and HTA requirements are also essential. You will be a highly organised and motivated researcher, capable of working independently while also contributing effectively as part of a collaborative research team. You will be comfortable managing multiple projects simultaneously, maintaining accurate records, and communicating scientific findings clearly through both written reports and presentations. We are looking for a candidate who is committed to high-quality research, excellent research practice and UCL's values. Experience of long-read sequencing and single-cell sequencing workflows would be advantageous, as would a willingness to contribute to the wider life of the department and institution. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance For rewards and benefits at UCL please visit: Staff Benefits | UCL People & C ulture - UCL – University College London Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: Equality, Diversity & Inclusion: Think differently, do differently. | Office of the President and Provost (Equality, Diversity & Inclusion) - UCL – University College London"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48357": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11300 About us: University College London Interaction Centre (UCLIC) is a world leading Centre of Excellence in Human-Computer Interaction teaching and research, studying interactions between people and technology, drawing on the best scientific traditions in Computer Science and Human Sciences, and working collaboratively with the research community and industry. UCLIC is unique in this space since it straddles Computer Science and Psychology. We are proud of our work in EDI and outreach, promoting equity, diversity and inclusion across our community and aiming to inspire, empower and engage people from all backgrounds. With Athena Gold and Silver awards in Computer Science and Psychology respectively, we are committed to creating a fair, inclusive and supportive environment for all staff and students. About the role: This exciting research position is part of XR-SENSE: Next-Generation Visuohaptic Media Representation, Coding and Interactive Rendering for Immersive and Inclusive XR , a major EU Horizon-funded project bringing together leading research and industry partners across Europe. XR-SENSE aims to develop the next generation of immersive technologies, creating realistic, scalable, inclusive and affect-aware visuo-haptic experiences. The project combines advanced sensing, streaming and rendering technologies with human-centred affective computing and interaction models. The successful candidate will play a key role in developing affect recognition and modelling approaches to enable personalised XR experiences . They will work within a highly interdisciplinary European consortium, collaborating with researchers and industry partners across multiple countries and contributing directly to the development and evaluation of innovative immersive technologies. This post-doctoral researcher position is full time (1 FTE) for 2 years starting in November 2026 or soon after. About you: You will be a collaborative and motivated researcher with a PhD, or equivalent experience, in Affective Computing, Machine Learning, Computer Vision, body gesture recognition or a related discipline. You will have experience developing affect recognition models, analysing multimodal sensor data, and designing human-participant studies, alongside a strong track record of peer-reviewed research. You will bring expertise in computer vision and machine learning for analysing human affective behaviour and non-verbal signals, as well as experience using LLMs for verbal data analysis. You will be an effective communicator and team member, able to work independently, manage competing priorities and contribute effectively to a multidisciplinary research environment What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits such as: .41 Days holiday including bank holidays .Hybrid working .Final Salary Pension Scheme .Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan .On-Site nursery .On-site gym .Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay .Employee assistance programme: .Staff Support Service .Discounted medical insurance. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. To apply for the vacancy please click on the ‘Apply Now’ button. If you have any queries regarding the vacancy or the application process, please contact nadia.berthouze@ucl.ac.uk Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Support Staff."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48514": {
      "title": "Research Fellow / Senior Research Fellow in Artificial Intelligence for Cancer Digital Twins",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11142 About us: This post is based in Professor Jasmin Fisher’s laboratory at the UCL Cancer Institute . The UCL Cancer Institute is a world-leading centre for cancer research, bringing together more than 400 scientists and clinicians who work collaboratively to understand cancer and translate discoveries into improved diagnostics, treatments and patient outcomes. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/cancer/ . We are seeking a highly motivated and talented Senior Research Fellow in Artificial Intelligence to join an ambitious and multidisciplinary research programme focused on understanding cancer using AI to generate digital tumour twins. This programme aims to transform cancer research and precision oncology by developing a new generation of transparent, interpretable and trustworthy AI technologies that integrate machine learning, mechanistic modelling, formal verification and large-scale biomedical data. Cancer is a complex, multi-scale disease involving interacting processes across the molecular, cellular, tissue and organ levels. At the same time, advances in genomics, molecular profiling, pathology, imaging and clinical data collection have generated unprecedented volumes of multimodal cancer data. A major challenge is to integrate these diverse data sources into coherent computational frameworks that can generate biological insights, support clinical decision-making and accelerate therapeutic discovery. The Fisher laboratory addresses this challenge through the development of tumour digital twins, which are executable mechanistic models that capture the biological behaviour of individual tumours and can be used to predict disease progression and treatment response. A central goal of the programme is to develop novel neurosymbolic AI methodologies that combine the power of large language models, and machine learning with formal reasoning, mechanistic modelling and formal verification to automatically construct and validate transparent, interpretable biologically grounded models directly from large-scale cancer datasets and scientific knowledge. About the role: The successful candidate will play a central role in the design and development of a neurosymbolic AI platform for cancer. They will contribute to the integration and analysis of large-scale multi-omics and clinical cancer datasets, the development of AI models and the creation of explainable and verifiable computational frameworks for tumour digital twins. Working at the intersection of artificial intelligence, formal methods and cancer research, the postholder will collaborate closely also with Professor Mateja Jamnik and her group in the Department of Computer Science and Technology at University of Cambridge, alongside biologists, clinicians and computer scientists from partner organisations. The research will contribute to fundamental advances in our understanding of cancer biology while supporting the development of innovative approaches for patient stratification, therapeutic target discovery, treatment optimisation and precision oncology. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to publish in leading journals, present their work at major international conferences and contribute to the development of transformative AI technologies with significant scientific and clinical impact. This is an exceptional opportunity for an ambitious AI researcher who is passionate about applying cutting-edge AI technologies to challenging real-world problems in biomedicine. The position offers the chance to work at the forefront of trustworthy AI, neurosymbolic reasoning, computational biology and cancer systems medicine, within a world-leading research environment committed to scientific excellence and translational impact. Appointment at Grade 7 and Grade 8 is dependent upon the successful award of a PhD . Candidates who have not yet been awarded their PhD may be appointed initially at Research Assistant Grade 6B (with progression to Grade 7 and backdated salary adjustment upon submission of the final corrected PhD thesis. Appointment at Grade 8 is contingent on the candidate’s previous experience. For more details about the Fisher Lab, please visit: www.ucl.ac.uk/cancer/fisher-lab This post is funded for 3 years in the first instance, with a probationary period of 9 months. The position is available from 1st October 2026, and early availability would be advantageous. Interviews will be held in November 2026. Applications should include a CV and a Cover Letter: In the Cover Letter, please provide evidence of the essential and desirable criteria in the Person Specification part of the Job Description. (By including a Cover Letter, you can leave blank the 'Why you have applied for this role' field in the application form, which is limited in the number of characters it will allow.) About you: You will have a PhD, or be close to completing one, in Computational Biology, Bioinformatics, Cancer Biology, Systems Biology, Genomics or a related discipline, with experience analysing large-scale biomedical datasets, including genomic, transcriptomic and multi-omics data. You will have strong programming skills in R, Python and MATLAB, alongside expertise in statistical analysis, data integration and reproducible research practices. You will be an innovative and highly motivated researcher with excellent analytical and problem-solving skills, capable of working independently while contributing effectively to a multidisciplinary team. Experience in cancer biology, computational oncology, bioinformatics or related fields is essential, and a strong publication record demonstrating high-quality research outputs is expected. You will be an excellent communicator with the ability to present complex scientific concepts to a range of audiences and collaborate effectively with biologists, clinicians, computational scientists and AI researchers. Organised, proactive and committed to scientific excellence, you will be excited by the opportunity to contribute to pioneering research at the forefront of computational cancer biology, tumour digital twins and precision medicine. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance For rewards and benefits at UCL please visit: Staff Benefits | UCL People & C ulture - UCL – University College London Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: Equality, Diversity & Inclusion: Think differently, do differently. | Office of the President and Provost (Equality, Diversity & Inclusion) - UCL – University College London"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48522": {
      "title": "Research Fellow / Senior Research Fellow in Software Development for Cancer Digital Twins",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11143 About us: This post is based in Professor Jasmin Fisher’s laboratory at the UCL Cancer Institute . The UCL Cancer Institute is a world-leading centre for cancer research, bringing together more than 400 scientists and clinicians who work collaboratively to understand cancer and translate discoveries into improved diagnostics, treatments and patient outcomes. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/cancer/ . We are seeking a highly motivated and talented Senior Research Fellow in Software Development to join an ambitious and multidisciplinary research programme focused on understanding cancer using AI to generate digital tumour twins. This programme aims to transform cancer research and precision oncology by developing a new generation of transparent, interpretable and trustworthy AI technologies that integrate machine learning, mechanistic modelling, formal verification and large-scale biomedical data. Cancer is a complex, multi-scale disease involving interacting processes across the molecular, cellular, tissue and organ levels. At the same time, advances in genomics, molecular profiling, pathology, imaging and clinical data collection have generated unprecedented volumes of multimodal cancer data. A major challenge is to integrate these diverse data sources into coherent computational frameworks that can generate biological insights, support clinical decision-making and accelerate therapeutic discovery. The Fisher laboratory addresses this challenge through the development of tumour digital twins, which are executable mechanistic models that capture the biological behaviour of individual tumours and can be used to predict disease progression and treatment response. A central goal of the programme is to develop a novel neurosymbolic AI platform that combine the power of large language models and machine learning with formal reasoning, mechanistic modelling and formal verification to automatically construct and validate transparent, interpretable biologically grounded models directly from large-scale cancer datasets and scientific knowledge. About the role: The successful candidate will play a central role in the design and development of a neurosymbolic AI platform for cancer. They will contribute to the integration and analysis of large-scale multi-omics and clinical cancer datasets, the development and fine-tuning of AI models and the creation of explainable and verifiable computational frameworks for tumour digital twins. Working at the intersection of artificial intelligence, formal methods and cancer research, the postholder will collaborate closely also with Professor Mateja Jamnik and her group in the Department of Computer Science and Technology at University of Cambridge, alongside biologists, clinicians and computer scientists from partner organisations. The research will contribute to fundamental advances in our understanding of cancer biology while supporting the development of innovative approaches for patient stratification, therapeutic target discovery, treatment optimisation and precision oncology. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to publish in leading journals, present their work at major international conferences and contribute to the development of transformative AI technologies with significant scientific and clinical impact. This is an exceptional opportunity for an ambitious research software development engineer (RSDE) who is passionate about applying cutting-edge formal verification and AI technologies to challenging real-world problems in biomedicine. The position offers the chance to work at the forefront of trustworthy neurosymbolic AI, formal reasoning, computational biology and cancer systems medicine, within a world-leading research environment committed to scientific excellence and translational impact. Appointment at Grade 7 and Grade 8 is dependent upon the successful award of a PhD . Candidates who have not yet been awarded their PhD may be appointed initially at Research Assistant Grade 6B (with progression to Grade 7 and backdated salary adjustment upon submission of the final corrected PhD thesis. Appointment at Grade 8 is contingent on the candidate’s previous experience. For more details about the Fisher Lab, please visit: www.ucl.ac.uk/cancer/fisher-lab The position is available from 1st October 2026, and early availability would be advantageous. Interviews will be held in November 2026. This post is funded for 3 years in the first instance, with a probationary period of 9 months. Applications should include a CV and a Cover Letter: In the Cover Letter, please provide evidence of the essential and desirable criteria in the Person Specification part of the Job Description. (By including a Cover Letter, you can leave blank the 'Why you have applied for this role' field in the application form, which is limited in the number of characters it will allow.) About you: You will hold a PhD in Computer Science, Software Engineering, Machine Learning, Mathematics, Physics or a related quantitative discipline, with a strong track record in software development and/or research. You have extensive experience designing, building and maintaining robust, scalable software systems, and are proficient in modern software engineering practices, including version control, automated testing, documentation and CI/CD workflows. You have experience developing and deploying AI-enabled applications, including machine learning, large language models and generative AI technologies, and are comfortable working with cloud platforms, distributed computing environments and data-intensive systems. You are an analytical problem-solver with excellent communication skills, able to explain complex technical concepts to both specialist and non-specialist audiences. Most importantly, you thrive in collaborative, multidisciplinary environments and are motivated by the opportunity to develop trustworthy, transparent AI technologies with real-world impact. You bring a proactive approach, a commitment to technical excellence and reproducibility, and a genuine enthusiasm for working at the intersection of artificial intelligence, software engineering and cancer research. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance For rewards and benefits at UCL please visit: Staff Benefits | UCL People & C ulture - UCL – University College London Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: Equality, Diversity & Inclusion: Think differently, do differently. | Office of the President and Provost (Equality, Diversity & Inclusion) - UCL – University College London"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48583": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in  Evolution and Development",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11225 About us: The Division of Biosciences at UCL is one of the world's foremost centres for research and teaching in the biological sciences. We have an outstanding international reputation and a community of over 500 staff and more than 300 PhD students engaged in cutting edge research and collaboration. Further information can be found on our website https://www.ucl.ac.uk/biosciences We are seeking an enthusiastic researcher with a background in evo-devo/functional genomics to join the group of Dr. Sean Stankowski. The position is well funded through a UKRI Future Leaders Fellowship. About the role: The Research Fellow will play a lead role in an ambitious project to reconstruct the evolutionary pathway between different reproductive modes in Littorina. ( https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.adi2982 ). Specifically, they will lead a strand of the project focused on understanding the molecular and developmental basis underlying a recent transition from egg-laying to live-bearing in this system. They will take responsibility for designing and implementing the experimental strategy using various approaches, potentially including bulk and single cell RNAseq, in situ hybridisation, chromatin accessibility assays, and proteomics. The project will be supported by a research technician. The researcher will work as part of a team and in collaboration with project partners from UCL (Paola Oliveri), the Sanger Institute (Dr. Joana Meier), the Institute of Science and Technology Austria (Dr. Nick Barton), The University of Gothenburg (Dr. Kerstin Johannesson and Dr. Erical Leder) and the University of Göttingen (Dr. Daniel Jackson). This role is an open-ended contract of three years with the possibility of extension. A PhD is an essential criterion for this role in relevant subject area; Where the successful candidate is close to completion and awaiting their formal PhD award, the initial appointment will be made at Research Assistant Grade 6B (salary £39,107–£42,891 per annum). The appointment will transfer to Grade 7 following confirmation of the PhD award, with the Grade 7 salary backdated to the date of final thesis submission. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. If you have any queries about the role, please contact Sean Stankowski at s.stankowski@ucl.ac.uk. If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact the HR Administrator (fls.hr@ucl.ac.uk) stating the advert reference number in the subject line. About you: Applicants must hold a PhD in a relevant field and have a strong theoretical understanding of molecular and developmental biology, and experience using approaches such as single-cell and spatial transcriptomics, in situ hybridisation, differential expression analyses, chromatin accessibility assays, and proteomics. The ideal candidate will have a demonstrated ability to design and conduct research independently, and be organised, creative, and collaborative. Experience in integrating multiple data types to address biological questions, is highly desirable. An interest in the developmental and genetic basis of reproductive differences , experience working on molluscs would be advantageous . What we offer: We will support your professional development to help you achieve your long-term goals and will provide access to training courses and international and domestic conferences. The position will include time to develop and peruse your own research interests, and includes opportunities to work with and visit project partners at other research institutions. VISA The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/policies-advice/ways-working to find out more. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefit s to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our division holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48955": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in AI for Neonatal Retinal Imaging",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11293 About us: A Research Fellow post is available to work with Senior Research Fellow Dr Siegfried Wagner at the UCL Institute of Ophthalmology, ranked the best place in the world to study ophthalmology (CWUR Rankings by Subject 2017). UCL Institute of Ophthalmology conducts cutting-edge research, attracting principal investigators, post-doctoral fellows and MD/PhD students of the highest international calibre to a stimulating research environment About the role: This is an exciting opportunity for a motivated researcher with a background in machine learning, computer science or a related quantitative discipline to engage in high-impact, translational research in medical AI. The post holder will play a central role in building the world's largest, most diverse neonatal retinal imaging dataset and in developing a self-supervised foundation model for neonatal retinal images. The role combines large-scale data curation and annotation with methodological development in foundation models, applied to a globally important, underserved area of paediatric ophthalmology: retinopathy of prematurity (ROP) and associated systemic disease in preterm infants. The post is funded from the 1 December 2026 until 30 November 2028 in the first instance. Salary will be on Grade 7, Point 32 (£46,261 per annum, inclusive of London Allowance.) Appointment as a Research Fellow at Grade 7 is dependent upon having been awarded a PhD. If this is not the case, an initial appointment will be made as a Research Assistant at Grade 6b with payment at Grade 7 backdated upon award of a PhD to the date of final submission of the PhD thesis (with corrections.) About you: The successful candidate will hold an PhD in a relevant topic, such as computer science. They will have demonstrable experience in AI for healthcare or medical imaging; the development, training and fine-tuning of deep learning models; working with large-scale imaging or medical datasets; and a strong publication record in relevant journals. Knowledge of software engineering standards such as Python, and experience with neonatal/paediatric retinal imaging and disease will be advantageous. The applicant should also have excellent communication skills and a commitment to conducting high-quality research that will benefit healthcare. Application Process If you have any queries regarding the vacancy please contact Mrs Polly Rawlinson (p.rawlinson@ucl.ac.uk) If you have any queries regarding the application process please email: ioo.hr@ucl.ac.uk. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. To apply for the vacancy please click on the 'Apply Now' button below. Please ensure you attach your CV and highest academic qualification. In addition, please ensure you provide a personal statement outlining how you meet the criteria to support your application. Please DO NOT attach research papers What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer a generous pension scheme and holiday allowance (27 days), bank holidays (8 days), plus closure (6 days) and more. Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewardand-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49053": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Algal Biomineralisation and Excitability",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11372 About us: The Division of Biosciences at UCL is one of the world's foremost centres for research and teaching in the biological sciences. We have an outstanding international reputation and a community of over 500 staff and more than 300 PhD students engaged in cutting edge research and collaboration. Further information can be found on our website https://www.ucl.ac.uk/biosciences This is an exciting opportunity to join the McClelland lab, which studies how marine algae build mineral structures and how electrical signalling at the cell membrane shapes their physiology. You will join a small, interdisciplinary team that combines algal cell biology with electrophysiology, advanced imaging, structural biology, geochemistry and mathematical modelling. Ideas and methods move freely between disciplines. About the role: You will investigate biomineralisation and electrical excitability in marine algae, applying specialist expertise in one of three areas: electrophysiology (patch clamp and sharp electrode), fluorescence lifetime imaging (FLIM), or cryo-electron microscopy (cryo-EM). You will design and carry out experiments, analyse and publish results, and present your work within the group and at conferences. Alongside the core project, you will be encouraged to develop your own research ideas. You will get extensive support to apply for independent fellowship or grant funding, including mentoring and feedback on applications. Salary: £ 46,261 – £ 54,019 per annum, Grade 7. Depending on experience. This is a fixed-term post until 30 th September 2027, for up to 12 months depending on the start date. Appointment at Grade 7 is dependent upon PhD award. If not yet awarded, initial appointment will be at Grade 6B, with backdating to Grade 7 upon submission of PhD thesis. Visa sponsorship This role meets the requirements for a Skilled Worker certificate of sponsorship or Global Talent visa under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. UCL welcomes applications from international applicants who require a visa. For guidance on eligibility, see Gov.uk Skilled Worker Visa guidance Flexible working and conditions We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible, and job share basis wherever possible. UCL Terms and Conditions of Service: Research and Professional Services Staff About you: You will hold a PhD (or have submitted your thesis) in a relevant discipline, with extensive hands-on experience of algal biology, including culturing algae and manipulating them in experiments. You will also have extensive demonstrable expertise in at least one of: • electrophysiology, including patch clamp and sharp-electrode recording; • fluorescence lifetime imaging microscopy (FLIM); • cryo-electron microscopy (cryo-EM). You will have a publication record appropriate to your career stage. You will be able to work independently while contributing to a collaborative, interdisciplinary team, and you will communicate your work clearly to specialist and non-specialist audiences. Experience with calcifying or silicifying marine algae, quantitative data analysis or programming (e.g. Python, MATLAB), or writing funding applications would be an advantage. Key Applicant Information Closing date: 25/10/26 at 23:59 GMT (applications may close early if volumes are high, so early submission is recommended). Interviews scheduled for: TBC Job description and person specification available at the bottom of the advert. Queries about the role: contact Dr Harry McClelland [h.mcclelland@ucl.ac.uk]. Please use the Subject line: PDRA enquiry-xxxxx (replacing xxxxx with your name). Queries about the process, accessibility, or reasonable adjustments: contact the Faculty HR Team on fls.hr@ucl.ac.uk What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits: As well as exciting opportunities, UCL offers a range of great benefits. Find out more: UCL Rewards and Benefits . ‘Why UCL?’ the impact UCL has had in the world and the impact people can make working with us. The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – visit UCL Ways of Working to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women.” Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy Our Faculty holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity, and Inclusion here: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion ."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48760": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Bioinformatics",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07896 About us: UCL’s Department of Computer Science (CS) is a top-ranked Computer Science Department in the UK. In the 2021 Research Excellence Framework (REF) evaluation, UCL Computer Science was ranked second in the UK for research power and first in England. The Bioinformatics Group has a world-class reputation in the area of bioinformatics and particularly the applications of AI in protein bioinformatics. About the role: The post sits within the Department of Computer Science and the Jones group, contributing to a BBSRC-funded project developing next-generation protein language models and domain-centric machine-learning frameworks for large-scale sequence-structure analysis and functional prediction. The role involves designing, implementing, and benchmarking computational models; developing and maintaining open-source software and web resources; running and analysing experiments; and documenting, presenting, and publishing research outcomes in line with project objectives. The post also includes contributing to project planning and reporting, supporting group activities, representing the work at scientific meetings, and helping with supervising students where appropriate. Funding is available for this post until 31/01/2031 in the first instance. About you: The ideal candidate is a research-focused computational scientist with a PhD and a strong background in bioinformatics, computational biology, or a closely related quantitative discipline, with experience in protein sequence or structure analysis, machine-learning method development, and modern software engineering practices. They should be proficient in Python and Linux-based development, familiar with deep learning approaches in structural biology, and comfortable working with large-scale datasets and model benchmarking. Strong analytical, organisational, and communication skills are essential, along with the ability to collaborate effectively within a team, contribute to publications and presentations, and support the development of web-based bioinformatics tools. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. To apply for the vacancy please click on the ‘Apply Now’ button below. If you have any queries regarding the vacancy or the application process, please contact Professor David Jones (d.t.jones@ucl.ac.uk). What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday: 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days (pro rata for part-time staff). Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme (pro rata for part-time staff). Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan. On-Site nursery Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay. Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service. Discounted medical insurance. Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London's Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world's talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/ . Our department holds an Athena SWAN Gold award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49014": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Biopharmaceutical Economics & Sustainability Modelling",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07923 About us: The “Next-Gen Biopharma Manufacturing 5.0” Prosperity Partnership is a 5-year collaborative programme between UCL Biochemical Engineering and AstraZeneca, jointly funded by EPSRC and AstraZeneca. It aims to transform antibody manufacturing using AI, automation, digital twins and advanced analytics to improve production of therapies for cancer, obesity and autoimmune conditions. Delivered through the UCL–AstraZeneca Centre of Excellence, the programme will appoint six postdoctoral researchers to work across UCL and AstraZeneca sites, combining computational and experimental approaches. This Research Fellow position sits at the heart of this transformation. Your economic and sustainability research will directly inform and guide the strategic decisions that shape how we reimagine biopharmaceutical manufacturing. By developing robust models that evaluate cost, environmental impact and resource efficiency, you'll provide the evidence base that drives innovation from concept to implementation. Your work will bridge cutting-edge science and real-world application, ensuring that the next generation of manufacturing technologies are not only technically advanced but economically viable and environmentally responsible. UCL Biochemical Engineering is a global leader in bioprocess engineering research and education, with a mission to develop innovative biomanufacturing solutions for health and a sustainable bioeconomy. The department combines world-class facilities, strong industrial links, and an interdisciplinary research culture. AstraZeneca is a global, science-led biopharmaceutical company with a focus on the discovery, development, and commercialisation of medicines in Oncology, Rare Diseases, and BioPharmaceuticals. About the role: We are seeking a highly motivated postdoctoral researcher to join this ambitious Prosperity Partnership between UCL and AstraZeneca and work in a cross-disciplinary team across both UCL (London) and AstraZeneca (Cambridge) sites. This role will evaluate the economic and environmental impact of next-generation biomanufacturing technologies, supporting more sustainable and cost-effective development pathways for advanced biologics through data-driven decision-making. You will lead the development of integrated techno-economic and life cycle assessment models, incorporating uncertainty analysis and multi-objective optimisation to evaluate and optimise the cost, carbon footprint and resource impacts of emerging bioprocess strategies. These decision-support tools will be applied to real-world case studies in collaboration with AstraZeneca, generating insights that inform strategic choices and guide industrial implementation. This role is funded for 2 years in the first instance, with potential for extension. About you: You will hold (or be near completion of) a PhD in a relevant discipline such as biochemical engineering, chemical engineering, industrial engineering, environmental sustainability, or a related field. Essential to this role is demonstrated expertise in both techno-economic analysis and life cycle assessment, supported by strong quantitative modelling and analytical skills. While prior experience in the pharmaceutical or biopharmaceutical sector would be beneficial, it is not essential—you will have the opportunity to develop industry-specific knowledge through close collaboration with AstraZeneca colleagues and exposure to real manufacturing and process development challenges. What matters most is that you bring solid foundations in sustainability assessment and economic modelling, along with the ability to apply these methods to complex industrial systems. You will have experience working on interdisciplinary research projects and be motivated to deliver practical, real-world impact. Excellent communication skills and a collaborative approach are essential, as is the ability to work independently and take ownership of your research. Experience developing decision-support tools, familiarity with LCA software, and skills in optimisation and uncertainty analysis would be advantageous. A commitment to UCL's values and to promoting equality, diversity and inclusion is essential. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, UCL also offer some great benefits. Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. Our department holds an Athena Swan Gold award in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity, and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48775": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Brain Vascular Biology and Brain Cancer Biology",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11358 About us: The UCL Cancer Institute is a £40 million investment in central London based at University College London (UCL), one of the world’s top universities and a founding member of the Francis Crick Institute. The Institute draws together over 400 scientists working together to develop world-class basic and translational cancer research Information about the UCL Cancer Institute can be found on our website https://www.ucl.ac.uk/cancer/ The Group of CNS Angiogenesis and Neurovascular Link / Brain Vasculature and Perivascular Niche headed by neurosurgeon-neuroscientist Associate Professor Dr Thomas Wälchli is one of the laboratories of the University College London (UCL) Cancer Institute in the Department of Oncology of the University College London (UCL). The overarching vision of the research of Associate Professor Dr Thomas Wälchli, MD-PhD, FMH neurosurgery is to understand how the brain vasculature is regulated during brain development, in the adult healthy brain, and in various vascular-dependent brain diseases such as brain tumours and brain vascular malformations. The UCL Cancer Institute is a £40 million investment in central London based at University College London (UCL), one of the world’s top universities and a founding member of the Francis Crick Institute. The Institute draws together over 400 scientists working together to develop world-class basic and translational cancer research. About the role: The Group of Brain Vasculature and Perivascular Niche focuses on vascular growth and the development of blood vessels ( angiogenesis ) in the brain , including endothelial cells and perivascular cells of the neurovascular unit/perivascular niche, and on brain vascular heterogeneity in development, health and disease: The main aims are: i) to understand the cellular and molecular underpinnings that govern the growth of the developing, adult, and diseased human brain vasculature at single-cell resolution, ii) to uncover the single-cell transcriptomic, genomic and epigenomic landscapes and spatial biology of the developing, adult and diseased human brain vasculature, iii) to elucidate how developmental programs regulate vascular growth/angiogenesis in the human brain tumour vasculature (patho-fetal axis / onco-fetal axis), iv) to unravel the onco-fetal programs of vascular growth/angiogenesis and immunosuppression/immunomodulation, and v) to translate these fundamental insights about the onco-fetal axis in the human brain (tumour) vasculature into clinical settings with the ultimate goal to identify novel therapeutic strategies for human brain tumours (and for other vascular-dependent diseases such as human brain vascular malformations). The role will be available for one year in the first instance with the possibility of extension. Appointment at Grade 7 is dependent upon having been awarded a PhD; if this is not the case, initial appointment will be at Research Assistant Grade 6B, with payment at Grade 7 being backdated to the date of final submission of the PhD thesis. Applications should include a CV and a Cover Letter: In the Cover Letter please provide evidence of the essential and desirable criteria in the Person Specification part of the Job Description. (By including a Cover Letter, you can leave blank the 'Why you have applied for this role' field in the application form, which is limited in the number of characters it will allow.) About you: The successful candidates : Have a PhD in bioinformatics/single-cell genomics/computer science (AI), neuroscience, vascular biology, developmental biology, cancer biology, immunology, biology/biomedicine, pharmacy, biomedical engineering, or related fields with ample research experience Have strong skills in working in the fields of bioinformatics/single-cell genomics and/or spatial multi-omics, neuroscience, vascular biology, developmental biology, cancer biology and immunology and/or experience in biotech, intellectual protection, etc. are an extra-added value Have a proven publication record with at least one first author publication in peer-reviewed international journals. Have experience in: (i) integrating single-cell and spatial multi-omics; (ii) computational programming in R, Python (and other common computer languages); (iii) competence/interest in analysing and integrating complex single-cell and spatial multi-omics- and clinical datasets are essential . will be an ambitious researcher, motivated to apply his/her skills to diverse (medical) research projects and enjoy working in a collaborative, dynamic, vibrant and fast-paced team environment. Have excellent organizational and supervisory skills, you can work in a team as well as independently. Have a strong ability to multi-task, to meet timelines, to work accurately; you are stress-resistant. Have outstanding written and verbal communication skills in English. Are able to identify priorities, and to find solutions for complex or difficult tasks. Have excellent organizational and record-keeping skills Have experience in presenting complex scientific concepts and content both in writing and verbally Have a strong commitment to enhance our fundamental understanding of human brain vascular biology and to improving outcomes for brain tumor patients Demonstrate excellent collaborative interpersonal skills with an ability to work co-operatively in a highly inter- and multidisciplinary setting. Have the ability to assist other members of the team, supervise MSc and PhD students and collaborate with other research groups. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance For rewards and benefits at UCL please visit: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48779": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Climate Change and Public Health",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11251 About us: The UCL Institute for Global Health (IGH) is at the heart of UCL’s Grand Challenge of Global Health. The Institute’s vision is for a world where international policy on global health is informed by world-class research. IGH aims to support the development of robust solutions to aspects of the world’s major global health challenges through scholarly outputs, education, public engagement, translational research, and ultimately by influencing public policy and professional practice. The Lancet Countdown: tracking progress on health and climate change, is an independent, international research collaboration, that works to track the evolving links between health and climate change globally, regionally and nationally, in partnership with The Lancet and the World Health Organization. Its aim is to inform a robust response to climate change that protects and promotes human health. We are looking for an experienced Research Fellow in Climate Change and Public Health to join our team. About the role: The successful candidate will have experience in climate change and global health and/or epidemiology, with strong quantitative skills and the ability to apply this expertise across climate change and health research. This will include supporting the development and evaluation of indicators of the health impacts of climate change, policies and actions to address climate change, and use of findings to inform real-world impact. The postholder will support the delivery of the health research and academic initiatives of the Lancet Countdown, including the collation of health and epidemiological evidence for the annual Global Lancet Countdown Report, Regional Reports, special issues, and additional academic and research outputs. They will also support the development of research to quantify the impacts of climate on health, and the health opportunities of climate change mitigation and adaptation action. This role offers the opportunity for an early career researcher to develop a research portfolio in the intersection of climate change and health, in support of the overall Lancet Countdown aims and their personal academic career development. This is a full-time role (i.e. 1.0 FTE, 36.5 hours per week) and it is available until 30 April 2029 in the first instance. Please note that the successful candidate must be living in the UK or willing to relocate to the UK for the duration of the contract. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. Salary range: £46,261 - £54,019 per annum (including London Allowance), depending on experience. If you have any queries about the role, please contact Dr Jennifer Israelsson (Senior Research Fellow) on j.israelsson@ucl.ac.uk . If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact the IGH HR Team on IGH.HR@ucl.ac.uk . About you: It is essential that the successful candidate has a PhD or equivalent working experience in the intersection of climate change, and global health, epidemiology, or a closely related discipline. They will also have: Research experience in climate change and global health, epidemiology, or a closely related discipline Experience in epidemiological modelling, ideally environmental epidemiological models Intermediate proficiency in R or similar coding language Experience working with multidisciplinary academic teams Experience communicating scientific and technical evidence to non-academic audiences Track record of leading or contributing to academic peer-reviewed publications Excellent communications skills including ability to express ideas clearly through multiple modes Strong analytical and critical thinking skills What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48811": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Data-Parallel Proof Checking for Monoidal Theories",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07928 About us: UCL's Department of Computer Science (CS) is a top-ranked Computer Science Department in the UK. In the 2021 Research Excellence Framework (REF) evaluation, UCL Computer Science was ranked second in the UK for research power and first in England. The post is held within the Programming Principles, Logic and Verification (PPLV) research group. About the role: The post is held in connection with the ARIA Safeguarded AI programme project “Data-Parallel Proof Checking for Monoidal Theories”, led by Professor Fabio Zanasi within the Programming Principles, Logic and Verification (PPLV) group at UCL. The post holder will strengthen the Metacat proof-checking core and develop a reinforcement-learning theorem-proving prototype, while continuing research on monoidal theories and diagrammatic proof systems, contributing both software and theoretical outputs to the Safeguarded AI platform. About you: Applicants should hold, or be close to completing, a PhD in Computer Science, Mathematics, or a closely related field, with research expertise in category theory / monoidal categories and string diagrams, and strong software development skills applicable to building and maintaining research-grade proof-checking or verification software. Experience with proof assistants, automated reasoning, or reinforcement learning is desirable, as is a track record of scientific publication and presentation at international venues. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. To apply for the vacancy please click on the 'Apply Now' button below. If you have any queries regarding the vacancy or the application process, please contact Professor Fabio Zanasi (f.zanasi@ucl.ac.uk). What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday: 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days (pro rata for part time staff). Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme (pro rata for part time staff). Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan. On-Site nursery Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay. Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service. Discounted medical insurance. Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London's Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world's talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Gold award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=47954": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Defect-Mediated Charge Transport",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07825 About us: The London Centre for Nanotechnology (LCN) is an interdisciplinary enterprise between University College London, King’s College London and Imperial College London. In bringing together world-class infrastructure and leading nanotechnology research activities, the Centre aims to attain the critical mass to compete with the best facilities abroad. The LCN has strong relationships with the broader nanotechnology and commercial communities, and is involved in much major collaboration. As the world’s only such facility to be located in the heart of a metropolis, the LCN has superb access to corporate, investment and industrial partners. It is at the forefront of training in nanotechnology, and has a strong media presence aimed at educating the public and bringing transparency to this emerging science LCN and UCL Physics and Astronomy are at the forefront of research into complexity in materials science, particularly in the area of emergent properties in strongly correlated systems. Particularly, how charge moves these compounds is of paramount importance, but how it interacts with the defects found in real materials is poorly understood and difficult to reveal. Single crystals can offer unparalleled insights into material properties, charge transport and defect chemistry. The Facility for Advanced Crystals for Emerging Technologies (FACET) at the Research Complex at Harwell has recently opened to allow access to these powerful scientific platforms for device physics applications. About the role: The post holder will be required to carry out research on charge transport in energy materials – specifically iron oxide (a candidate photocatalyst, battery electrode and supercapacitor material) and nickel oxide (an emerging supercapacitor electrode material and hole transport layer for photovoltaics) – with the goal to elucidate the effects of dopants on their antiferromagnetic structure and electron small polaron hopping. In particular, they will apply advanced neutron and X-ray scattering techniques at national facilities to reveal short-range correlated disorder, invisible to long-range structure probes. This project will use a state-of-the-art, laser-heated, high-pressure image furnace to synthesise metal oxide single crystals of extremely high purity. These will be precisely oriented and a suite of transport measurements used to determine the nature of hopping transport. Finally, three-dimensional delta-pair-distribution-function analysis will isolate correlated magnetic and atomic disorder to build a full picture of charge transport in these important materials. The role is a fixed term position, post duration is until 28.02.2029. About you: The ideal candidate will be a motivated and collaborative researcher who is either completing or has recently obtained a PhD in Chemistry, Physics, Materials Science, or a closely related discipline. You will have a strong academic background and expertise in at least one key area of inorganic crystalline materials research, such as synthesis and characterisation, with knowledge of techniques including crystal growth, charge transport measurements, X-ray or neutron scattering, or powder X-ray diffraction being advantageous. The successful candidate will demonstrate the ability to conduct high-quality independent research, critically evaluate scientific information, and analyse and interpret complex data to produce impactful research outcomes. They will have a track record of contributing to peer-reviewed publications and be confident communicating complex scientific concepts to both specialist and non-specialist audiences. Strong interpersonal, written and verbal communication skills are essential, together with the ability to work effectively within a multidisciplinary research environment and build positive working relationships with academic and professional services colleagues. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Bronze award, in recognition of our long-term commitment and 'beacon' status in advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48990": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Disruptive Additive Manufacturing Microstructures via Beam-Shaping",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07903 About us: University College London (UCL) UCL is a multi-disciplinary university with a population of over 13,000 staff and 42,000 students from 150 different countries. Degree programmes are provided in Arts and Humanities, Built Environment, Brain Sciences, Engineering Sciences, Education, Laws, Life Sciences, Mathematical & Physical Sciences, Medical Sciences, Population Health Sciences and Social and Historical Sciences. For more information, please visit http://www.ucl.ac.uk/about UCL Mechanical Engineering UCL Mechanical Engineering has been pioneering the development of engineering education, having taught the core discipline for over 165 years. UCL was home to the UK’s first ever Professor of the Mechanical Principles of Engineering, Eaton Hodgkinson, in 1847. It was also where Sir Alexander Blackie William Kennedy introduced organized laboratory practicals in university education training; a world-leading educational innovation at the time. About the role: The Multiscale X-ray Imaging Lab (MXI Lab) at the Research Complex at Harwell, Oxfordshire, uses bespoke in situ replicators and ultra-fast synchrotron imaging to reveal the physics of additive manufacturing, working with Diamond Light Source and the ESRF. You will lead the adaptation of our Blown Additive Manufacturing Process Replicator (BAMPR-2) to apply non-Gaussian beam shaping and ultrasound to directed energy deposition (DED), and use synchrotron imaging to show how this transforms solidification microstructures and reduces defects. You will hold a PhD (or equivalent experience) in materials science, physics or a related engineering discipline, with in-depth knowledge of X-ray imaging and analysis, physical metallurgy and advanced powder processes. Role specific details: This post is funded in the first instance for 18 months. If you have any queries about the role, please contact ruikang.xue@ucl.ac.uk A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. About you: You will be accountable for the scientific goals of the project: adapting the in situ DED rig, with a Design Engineer, for a non-Gaussian ring beam and ultrasonic excitation; running laboratory and synchrotron imaging campaigns; and analysing the resulting large datasets to quantify melt pool hydrodynamics, solute transport and microstructure evolution. You will also coordinate the MXI Lab’s DED researchers, mentor PhD students, contribute to beamtime proposals, and publish your findings. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian, and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people and LGBTQI+ people."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48854": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Heritage and Environmental Risk- Internal UCL Candidates Only",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07879 About us: BSEER brings together four world-leading institutes to generate sustainable solutions and real-world impact across the built environment, energy, resources and heritage. Our Institute for Sustainable Heritage (ISH) delivers ground-breaking, cross-disciplinary research and teaching to sustain cultural heritage for future generations. About the role: The main purpose of this job is to support ISH’s contribution to the Centre of Excellence in Green Heritage Science. The Green Heritage Science Centre of Excellence (GreenHer) is a new European research hub coordinated by the University of Ljubljana, bringing together institutions across Slovenia and Italy to advance conservation, sustainable heritage management, participatory engagement, and digital heritage. To enable this, the post holder will be required to support the implementation of pilot research projects in collaboration with Slovenian and Italian collaborators on the project and will also be responsible for coordinating and developing collaborative bids for research funding. This post is available from 1 December 2026 and is funded for 36 months in the first instance; further funding to support the post may become available. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. UCL welcomes applications from international applicants and has licence to sponsor individuals who require a visa. This is dependent on the post and candidate meeting eligibility requirements for visa sponsorship under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. About you: You will hold a PhD (or equivalent research experience) in heritage science, archaeology, conservation, sustainability or a related discipline, with a track record of designing and delivering successful research projects. Experience applying Living Labs or other participatory research methodologies would be an advantage. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits, some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48275": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Human Genetics (Rare Cardiovascular Disease)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11243 About us: UCL established the Institute of Health Informatics (IHI) in August 2014 in the Faculty of Population Health Sciences. The aim of IHI is to conduct high quality data-intensive research to improve health at local, national, and international levels. The Faculty of Population Health Sciences has established itself as UCL’s largest research-based faculty, encompassing eight institutes. More information available at www.ucl.ac.uk/population-health-sciences/. About the role: Institute of Health Informatics is looking to appoint a Research Fellow in Human Genetics (Rare Cardiovascular Disease) to join our welcoming and vibrant Institute. This position presents a unique opportunity to join a team of experienced health data scientists, informaticians, statisticians and clinical epidemiologists and contribute significantly to the statistical analysis and subsequent presentation of results from large scale national and rich local health record data resources. The Research Fellow in Human Genetics will work across partners to integrate clinical and genetic data within relevant secure data environments for research; carry out data management tasks to prepare data for analyses, including data extraction and data quality assurance; and generate efficient and shareable code which can be used in different datasets; amongst other duties. A full range of duties can be found on the attached job description. The post is full-time (36.5 hours per week) and the post is funded until 30 September 2029. Further funding may become available. Appointment at Grade 7 (£46,261 - £54,019 per annum, inclusive of London Allowance) is dependent upon having been awarded a PhD; if this is not the case, initial appointment will be at Research Assistant Grade 6B (£39,807 – £42,891 per annum, inclusive of London Allowance) with payment at Grade 7 being backdated to the date of final submission of the PhD thesis. For an informal discussion please contact Professor Tom Lumbers at t.lumbers@ucl.ac.uk For any queries regarding the recruitment process please contact Anita Gorasia at a.gorasia@ucl.ac.uk. Please only submit a CV and complete the questionnaire. No additional information is required or will be considered at this point. About you: If you believe you meet the requirements why not come and be part of this unique and exciting opportunity and be part of something where you feel included, valued and proud. The Research Fellow in Human Genetics must have (PhD in Statistics, Epidemiology, Genetics, Computer Science or a related numerate discipline, or relevant working experience as well excellent programming and data visualisation skills in Python and R; and ability to write code that clearly expresses its purpose, and is readily used by others maintained and extended. The role holder will have experience with large-scale data processing, including data collection, cleaning, and preprocessing (e.g. SQL, PySpark), and analysis. Please review the job description before applying, paying particular attention to the essential / desirable criteria, and ensure your experience in these areas is addressed in the questionnaire section of the application. What we offer: We will consider applicates to work on a part-time, flexible and job share wherever possible. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are:, 41 Days holiday (pro rata for part time staff) (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days), cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, on-site gym and employee assistance programme Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: The Institute prides itself on operating in an all-inclusive environment irrespective of personal, physical, or social characteristics. As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=47847": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Mass Spectrometry",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07792 About us: The Chemistry Department at University College London is the oldest in England, ranked 3 rd in the UK for research excellence in REF 2021. Our department is situated in Bloomsbury, at the heart of London, and we offer an exciting and vibrant environment in which to study at one of the UK's top universities. The Department has an annual research income of around £15 million and is excellently equipped for chemical biology, nanomaterials and biophysical research and characterisation as a result of >£4M of investments in laboratories for synthetic and biological chemistry. This includes peptide synthesisers, extensive HPLC equipment, a suite of 5 high field NMRs and a mass spectrometry suite. The UCL Mass Spectrometry Science Technology Platform (MS-STP: academic lead Prof Kostas Thalassinos) provides access to a world-class portfolio of mass spectrometry and sample preparation technologies supporting structural biology, proteomics, single-cell, and spatial biology research. The facility houses a Waters Select Series Cyclic IMS, Xevo G2-XS QTof with Acquity UPLC M-Class, Waters BioAccord System, Orbitrap Eclipse Tribrid Mass Spectrometer, and an Orbitrap Astral Zoom platform enabling next-generation single-cell proteomics. In addition, the hub hosts a CellenOne system for automated single-cell isolation and preparation, and a Syncell Microscoop platform for subcellular proteomics, allowing proteome measurements to be performed from defined cellular structures and regions. http://www.ucl.ac.uk/chemistry https://www.ucl.ac.uk/science-technology-platforms/mass-spectrometry About the role: We are seeking to appoint a Postdoctoral Researcher in Mass Spectrometry to contribute to a multidisciplinary project investigating cyclic antimicrobial peptide–lipid interactions at molecular resolution. This project is part of an EPSRC Open Plus Fellowship led by Professor Alethea Tabor in collaboration with Professor Kostas Thalassinos. The overall goal of the project is to develop and apply advanced structural mass spectrometry methodologies, especially XL-MS, to understand how cyclic antimicrobial peptides interact with their target lipids, and to support the development of new antimicrobial strategies against multidrug-resistant bacteria. In this project, you will lead the development and application of structural mass spectrometry approaches alongside complementary biophysical techniques using model lipid bilayers and contribute to the development of computational models of peptide–lipid complexes. You will work in a highly collaborative, multidisciplinary environment embedded within both research groups, partnering closely with a Research Fellow in Chemical Biology and a PhD student who will design and synthesise bespoke photoreactive peptides for these studies. You will also engage with collaborators across UCL and partner institutions including Queen’s University Belfast, University of Nottingham, Utrecht University, and Uppsala University, spanning expertise in biophysics, microbiology, NMR, and lipid synthesis. Strong teamwork, communication skills, and the ability to deliver high-quality results in a timely manner will be essential for success in this role. The appointment will be for a fixed term of 2 years initially, with potential for extension subject to funding and/or project requirements. About you: You will have a PhD (or be close to completion) in Mass Spectrometry, Chemistry, Biochemistry, Biophysics or a related discipline, with experience of structural mass spectrometry techniques and computational analyses. Expertise in handling membrane proteins or peptides and studying biomolecular interactions in model membranes, and experience in cross-linking mass spectrometry, would be advantageous. You will be able to work effectively both independently and as part of a multidisciplinary team, demonstrating strong organisational, communication, and problem-solving skills. The ability to manage your time, deliver high-quality results to agreed deadlines, and engage constructively with collaborators is essential. You will also be willing to contribute to the wider research environment, including supporting student supervision, participating in teaching and public engagement activities, and contributing to the safe and effective operation of laboratory facilities. A track record of contributing to publications in peer-reviewed journals, or clear potential to do so, is expected. To apply for the vacancy please click on the ‘Apply Now’ button below. Please upload a CV, list of publications and covering letter stating why you are interested in the role and how you meet the criteria. For informal enquiries please contact Alethea Tabor ( a.b.tabor@ucl.ac.uk ) or Kostas Thalassinos ( k.thalassinos@ucl.ac.uk ). Any queries on the application process can be directed to Rezwana Akhter ( r.akhter@ucl.ac.uk ), as we wish to encourage online applications. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. At UCL Chemistry we are dedicated to the Athena SWAN Charter for women in science, recognising the commitment to advancing women’s careers in science, technology, engineering, maths and medicine (STEMM) employment in higher education and research. The department was successfully awarded a Bronze Award in 2016 and Silver Award in 2020, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48822": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Molecular Beam Epitaxy of III-V/Si Materials and Devices (2 positions)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07884 About us: We are seeking to appoint two outstanding Research Fellows to join our internationally recognised semiconductor photonics research programme within the Department of Electronic & Electrical Engineering at University College London (UCL). The successful candidates will contribute to the EPSRC Programme Grant Quantum Dot on Silicon Systems for Communications, Information Processing and Sensing (QUDOS). The programme brings together world-leading researchers from UCL, Cardiff University, the University of Cambridge and the University of Southampton to develop next-generation integrated photonic technologies based on monolithically integrated III-V compound semiconductors on silicon. The QUDOS Programme Grant is a major EPSRC investment aimed at developing high-performance quantum dot materials, photonic integrated circuits and optoelectronic devices on silicon for future communication, sensing and information-processing technologies. Building on pioneering demonstrations of directly grown telecommunications-wavelength lasers on silicon, the programme seeks to establish scalable monolithic integration technologies that combine the superior optical performance of compound semiconductors with the manufacturing advantages of silicon platforms. The research has the potential to transform photonic integration in a manner analogous to the impact that silicon integrated circuits have had on modern electronics, enabling unprecedented functionality, scalability and cost-effectiveness. About the role: The Research Fellows will play a leading role in the epitaxial growth and development of advanced III-V and silicon-based semiconductor materials and optoelectronic devices using molecular beam epitaxy. The role will involve conducting independent research, developing new device structures, operating and maintaining advanced MBE facilities, and contributing to the broader research objectives of the QUDOS programme. The roles are available immediately until 30 th April 2027 in the first instance. Further funding to support the posts may be available. Subject to project requirements, an extension may be possible through a subsequent Programme Grant, Boundless light: Fibre optic systems unconstrained by glass properties (BOUNDLESS). About you: Applicants should have a PhD in a relevant area of physical sciences or engineering. A first degree in theoretical physics, applied mathematics, materials sciences or electrical engineering is essential. You should strong experience on the operation and maintenance of III-V solid-source MBE and/or Group-IV MBE facility, as well as a strong track-record on the research outcomes of MBE-growth of III-V compound semiconductor materials and/or Group-IV materials. The successful candidate will have in-depth knowledge of the mechanism of QD lasers and strong experience on the heteroepitaxial growth of QD lasers. The ability to analyse and write up data in the form of journal papers and reports is desirable. If the successful candidate has not yet been awarded their PhD, appointment will be made as a Research Assistant (Grade 6B). Payment at Grade 7 will be backdated to the date of final submission of the PhD thesis including corrections, once the PhD has been awarded. Application details: To apply for the role, click the 'Apply Now' button at the bottom or top of the page Applications close on 16th October at 23:59 A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page Informal enquiries regarding this post can be addressed to Prof Huiyun Liu huiyun.liu@ucl.ac.uk. For questions regarding the application process please contact Rebecca Thomas at becca.thomas@ucl.ac.uk. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48579": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Molecular Quantum Sensing for Biomedical Applications",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07874 About us: Ben Miller’s research group at UCL’s London Centre for Nanotechnology develops quantum-sensing approaches for biological applications with a particular interest in nanoparticle probes and sensitive optical detection. The group brings together materials chemistry, spin physics and biological experimentation. Current projects explore organic sensing materials and fluorescent nanodiamonds for biological and diagnostic applications. The post is funded through the UK Quantum Biomedical Sensing (Q-BIOMED) Research Hub and involves collaboration with researchers in chemistry, physics and biomedicine, including Professor Vijay Chudasama’s group in UCL Chemistry. The London Centre for Nanotechnology (LCN) is an interdisciplinary enterprise between University College London, King’s College London and Imperial College London. In bringing together world-class infrastructure and leading nanotechnology research activities, the Centre aims to attain the critical mass to compete with the best facilities abroad. The LCN has strong relationships with the broader nanotechnology and commercial communities, and is involved in much major collaboration. As the world’s only such facility to be located in the heart of a metropolis, the LCN has superb access to corporate, investment and industrial partners. It is at the forefront of training in nanotechnology, and has a strong media presence aimed at educating the public and bringing transparency to this emerging science. About the role: The post holder will develop diagnostic and other biomedical sensing methods using the optical and spin properties of molecular quantum materials in nanoparticle form. Specific objectives involve the development, characterization and integration of molecular nanocrystals into diagnostic tests. The work will involve preparing and characterising nanoparticles, developing coatings and functionalisation methods, and testing their behaviour in biological environments. This will include improving particle dispersibility and stability, attaching biological recognition molecules, and evaluating optical and spin properties and performance in model assays. There will be scope to propose and pursue new materials, chemistries and biomedical applications. Working with Ben Miller and collaborators, the postholder will develop their ideas into experiments, use the results to guide subsequent work and lead the preparation of research papers. They will also contribute to intellectual property arising from the research. The is a fixed term role for 12 months, with the possibility to extend subject to securing further funding. About you: The ideal candidate will hold a PhD in chemistry, physics, materials science, biochemistry, biophysics or a related discipline. You will have practical experience of working with nanoparticles and modifying or functionalising their surfaces. A background in synthetic chemistry would be welcome, especially where this has involved surface modification, coatings or bioconjugation. You will have a strong publication record, and be able to demonstrate your own contributions. You will enjoy working closely with colleagues and collaborators, contributing your expertise to connect new sensing materials with biological applications. You should be comfortable using microscopes and experienced or motivated to learn relevant optical and biological methods. Experience of bioassay development or optical characterisation of molecular materials or nanoparticles would be advantageous. Previous experience in quantum sensing is not essential What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Bronze award, in recognition of our long-term commitment and 'beacon' status in advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https: //www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49059": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Next-Generation Urban Climate Modelling",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07907 About us: The UCL Department of Risk and Disaster Reduction (RDR) inspires innovation and evidence-based practice focused on improving disaster risk reduction (DRR) through our research, education, and partnerships. This creates a better life for many people by reducing the negative impacts of disasters. The RDR is an exciting cross-UCL department, which leads research, knowledge exchange and teaching in the fields of risk and disaster reduction and humanitarian action. By providing a focus for UCL’s activities the RDR, with its breadth of disciplinary emphasis, promotion of novel multidisciplinary research and translation into practice, aims to assume a role of leadership both in the UK and internationally. The Department has 30 members of academic and teaching staff, 12 members of research staff ranging from research assistants to professorial research fellows and 9 members of professional services staff. The Department is in the Faculty of Mathematics and Physical Sciences (MAPS), but works across all UCL’s faculties, and contributes to UCL’s Grand Challenges. The RDR strives to produce world-leading research in disaster risk reduction through a multidisciplinary perspective for the long-term benefit of humanity. The RDR has established the Humanitarian Institute, Centre for Digital Public Health in Emergencies, Centre for Gender and Disaster, and jointly with UCL Department of Science and Technology Studies the Warning Research Centre. The RDR conducts research around the themes of disaster risk reduction and resilience, cascading crises, natural hazards, climate change risk and adaptation, humanitarian crisis response, GIS and remote sensing, law and economics, conflict and migration, catastrophe modelling, warning, digital health in emergencies, and inclusivity including gender responsive resilience, in order to integrate education, research, innovation and enterprise for the long-term benefit of humanity. The RDR aims to develop leaders and experts in disaster risk reduction research, education, and practice. We run PhD and MRes programmes in Risk and Disaster Reduction, MSc programmes in Risk, Disaster and Resilience and Risk and Disaster Science, and an undergraduate BSc programme in Global Humanitarian Studies, all with a diverse and cross-disciplinary international intake. We have about 25 PhD students, 90 masters students and will have about 180 students across the three years of the BSc programme from 2023-2024 academic year. The RDR seeks to empower all to break down barriers to global DRR knowledge. This knowledge exchange is a core part of the mission of the RDR, which it seeks to fulfil by publishing high-impact reports, engagement with the media and building long-term partnerships. RDR members have joined and led several field missions and contributed to multidisciplinary reports. These reports have been drawn on by the UK Cabinet Office and other government bodies in advice to ministers. RDR staff contributed substantially to the UCL-Lancet Commission on Migration and Health. The RDR has close links with London First, the business representative grouping, private companies, London Resilience and UK Government, including the Cabinet Office, and is developing links with intergovernmental organizations such as the International Migration Organization, Plan International, UN Women and other NGOs. UCL RDR runs regular events to engage the public and potential partners in issues relating to risk and disaster reduction and humanitarian action, such as its Annual Conference and Humanitarian Summit. About the role: The Research Fellow is responsible for the scientific integrity and the institutional memory of SUEWS through a period of substantial technical change. They will establish the evidence that the model is correct. A working benchmark already tests each release against observations; the postholder takes it from one reference site to a set spanning contrasting climates, decides what counts as a pass, and sets out in one place, for the first time, what the model assumes, where those assumptions hold and where they do not. They will also capture reasoning that has never been written down, through a structured interview programme with the model's developers and long-standing users: why particular parameterisations were chosen, what was tried and abandoned, and how the science was actually done. That material becomes the training and onboarding route through which new contributors join the project. The postholder will work closely with the PI and with the Research Software Engineer at Reading, and is expected to publish from this work and present it internationally. About you: You will hold a PhD in a discipline relevant to urban climate, environmental modelling or scientific computing, and be able to move confidently between observational data and model output. You will be confident working with scientific code and data, and will have a track record of publishing your own research. We care more about whether you can tell a right result from a wrong one than about how much code you have written, and we expect and welcome the use of AI coding assistants. You will write clearly for readers of very different backgrounds, from model developers to local authority users, and you will be comfortable drawing knowledge out of experienced colleagues and recording it faithfully. We are interested in capability rather than a particular career path: urban climate expertise reaches this field from architecture, public health, engineering and computing as often as from meteorology, and we welcome applications from any of those routes. Please note that this post leads validation, documentation and knowledge transfer rather than core code development, which is led by the Research Software Engineer at the University of Reading. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days per annum) per annum • Additional 5 days' annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance. To find out more, visit www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: We know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL's workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48390": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Reactor Design, Operation and Monitoring",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07844 About us: UCL Department of Chemical Engineering is one of the top research and teaching departments in the UK and has world-class standing. The department offers undergraduate and postgraduate programmes, and has an extensive research portfolio across a wealth of areas, from molecular scale to complex systems. About the role: The successful candidate will conduct experimental and theoretical/computational research for the Sustainable Open-loop Large-scale Inter-seasonal Thermochemical Heat Storage (SOLIS) project. The research will implement reactor design (up to pilot scale) with heat integration, and multiphase flow analysis. The post holder will lead the design and construction of a pilot scale reactor, collaborate with other researchers within the SOLIS project and International Partners, to extend the impact of the research to other problems related to sustainable development. This is a fixed term role until from 1st January 2027 to 30th of September 2029 in the first instance. Salary is £46,261-£48,679 per annum inclusive of London allowance. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. About you: The successful candidate will hold a PhD in chemical or mechanical engineering, materials science, physical sciences or related. The post holder will have experience in reactor design & construction, P&ID, experimentation and analysis, as well as experience in either fluidization, powder technologies and reaction engineering. Hands on experience on reactor assembling and risk assessment is essential. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. If you have any queries about the role, please contact massimiliano.materazzi.09@ucl.ac.uk . If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact chemeng.safety@ucl.ac.uk . The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please visit www.ucl.ac.uk/ways-of-working to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Gold award, in recognition of our long-term commitment and 'beacon' status in advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48554": {
      "title": "Research Fellow – Extragalactic Observations of the High-Redshift Universe",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07866 About us: The Astrophysics Group is one of the largest in the UK, comprising about 130 members of academic, research and support staff, of which 21 are academic staff members. Our research areas cover galaxy formation and evolution, cosmology, gravitational waves, massive stars, star formation and stellar evolution, interstellar and circumstellar processes, exo-planetary and planetary science, atmospheric physics and instrumentation. UCL's Department of Physics & Astronomy has a strong track record of investment and scientific leadership in facilities such as JWST, DES, DESI, 4MOST, Euclid, Ariel, Rubin-LSST, PFS, JCMT, Litebird as well as participation at co-I level of instruments on large explorer mission concepts such as Cassini and JUICE with participation in many other mission and instrument concept designs. UCL is one of the world's top universities based in the heart of London. It is a modern, outward-looking institution. UCL's staff and former students have included 33 Nobel prize winners. It is a truly international community: more than one-third of our student body – more than 50,000 strong – come from 150 countries and nearly one-third of staff are from outside the UK. The Department of Physics &; Astronomy is located in the heart of the historical area of Bloomsbury. Scientific research and study has been a strong feature of UCL since its inception in 1826 and the Department is currently one of the top ranked Physics departments in the country, and indeed the world. We have forged an internationally renowned reputation for being at the forefront of world leading research and providing high class teaching. Those joining us can expect to be part of a dynamic community of Physicists and Astronomers whose enthusiasm for science and innovation is exceptional. About the role: This position is for a 3-year PDRA (starting date between 1st January and 1st April 2027, by mutual agreement) in the Astrophysics Group of UCL’s Department of Physics & Astronomy, working in Dr. Guido Roberts-Borsani’s group on galaxy formation and evolution in the first billion years with the James Webb Space Telescope, as part of the OMEGA project funded by a European Research Council (ERC) Starting Grant. The successful applicant will carry out high-impact research on the interstellar medium, stellar, and black hole properties of high-redshift galaxies, using JWST spectroscopy from the PI's approved programmes (in particular DAJE-JWST and OMEGA-JWST) to advance our understanding of galaxy properties and chemical enrichment from cosmic reionization to cosmic dawn. Depending on the project's needs and the candidate's expertise, the research will address one or more of OMEGA's core themes: the ionisation structure of the interstellar medium and robust metallicity measurements; the physical origins of carbon, nitrogen, and oxygen (CNO) abundance ratios; the burstiness and modes of star formation at high redshift; and the pace at which early galaxies built up their chemical reservoirs. In addition to producing first-author publications, the PDRA is also expected to develop their own research ideas and telescope proposals, engage and coordinate with the PI’s existing collaborations, contribute to the social and intellectual life of the wider Astrophysics Group, and support the supervision of PhD students working on related themes. All applications require the following documents compiled as a single PDF (submitted as the mandatory CV attachment in the application portal): Cover letter CV Research statement (max 3 pages) List of publications All applications also require 2 letters of reference to be sent separately by the referees via email to Guido Roberts-Borsani (g.robertsborsani@ucl.ac.uk) For any enquiries related to the position, please contact Guido Roberts-Borsani (g.robertsborsani@ucl.ac.uk) or Kay Nakum (k.nakum@ucl.ac.uk). About you: The successful candidate will hold (or be close to completing) a PhD in astrophysics, with a strong track record of observational extragalactic research and publications in leading peer-reviewed journals. They will have experience analysing spectroscopic and/or photometric data from JWST (experience with other major observatories such as HST, Keck, VLT, or ALMA, is a bonus), and strong working knowledge of ISM physics of high-redshift galaxies. Programming and data-analysis skills are essentials, as is the ability to plan and prioritise their own research, work well under tight deadlines, coordinating between international collaborations, and deliver high-quality results. Experience presenting published works at conferences and preparing successful telescope proposals is considered an advantage. What we offer: Starting salary offered in the range £46,261 – £54,019 per annum, inclusive of London Allowance. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women.” Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion. Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48756": {
      "title": "Research Fellow –Cancer Metabolism Data Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11355 About us: The Jamal-Hanjani Lab focuses on the biological determinants of cancer metastasis and is a multi-disciplinary group of passionate and determined individuals who will provide the successful candidate with a stimulating and conducive environment to participate and grow as a scientist. The research is conducted across the TRACERx and PEACE consortia, and the CRUK Lung Cancer Centre of Excellence and Francis Crick Institute. These collaborative environments will provide the successful candidate with opportunities to learn from a large network of talented professionals. This project involves working closely with the Karen Vousden lab at the Francis Crick which focuses on how changes in metabolism affect cancer development and progression, the cross talk between tumour and normal cells in the cancer microenvironment and how cancer affects the whole body, including the influence of primary tumours on distant organs and the interplay between cancers and systemic conditions such as obesity, cachexia and aging. The TRACERx (TRAcking lung Cancer Evolution through therapy (Rx)) study TRACERx is a UK-wide lung cancer evolution programme which started in 2014 and has to date recruited over 820 patients. It is the first longitudinal cancer genomics study that forms the first national UK consortium focused on deciphering cancer evolution over space and time. TRACERx involves multi-region sequence analyses of lung cancers (5000+ exomes and whole genomes in over 800 patients) from pre-invasive through to primary and metastatic disease setting, in order to understand cancer evolutionary life histories with detailed clinical annotation. The co-primary end points of the study are to investigate the relationship between intratumour heterogeneity and clinical outcomes, and to determine the impact of cancer therapies on intratumour heterogeneity. TRACERx has enabled additional analyses in the areas of immunology, circulating biomarkers, cancer cachexia and body composition, radiomics and tumour microenvironment. CANCAN Cancer Grand Challenge CANCAN is an international team of scientists with close collaborations tackling the challenges posed by cancer-associated cachexia, a debilitating wasting syndrome characterised by extensive weight loss. Supported by Cancer Grand Challenges, a global initiative addressing key issues in cancer research, it is funded by CRUK and the NIH (https://www.cancergrandchallenges.org/cancan). The team focuses on understanding the mechanisms underlying cancer-associated cachexia and developing therapeutic strategies that could reverse it. About the role: We are seeking a collaborative and self-motivated post-doctoral fellow to work on cancer metabolism in a vibrant and highly collegiate environment. The candidate will be expected to develop and make use of bioinformatics techniques to investigate how metabolic dysregulation relates to the development of cancer cachexia and poor outcomes. The project will focus on the analysis of blood mass spectrometry metabolomics data and leverage existing genomic and transcriptomic sequencing data from the TRACERx lung cancer evolution and PEACE research autopsy programmes. Appointment at Grade 7 is dependent upon having been awarded a PhD; if this is not the case, initial appointment will be at Research Assistant Grade 6B, with payment at Grade 7 being backdated to the date of final submission of the PhD thesis. Applications should include a CV and a Cover Letter: In the Cover Letter please provide evidence of the essential and desirable criteria in the Person Specification part of the Job Description. (By including a Cover Letter, you can leave blank the 'Why you have applied for this role' field in the application form, which is limited in the number of characters it will allow.) The role will be available for one year in the first instance with the possibility of extension. About you: You will hold a PhD (or be close to completion) in a relevant discipline and have a strong track record of independent research, evidenced by high-quality publications and experience analysing complex biological datasets. You will bring expertise in metabolomics data analysis, ideally using mass spectrometry data, alongside experience working with genomic and transcriptomic datasets and applying statistical methods to generate meaningful biological insights. You will be proficient in computational approaches and programming languages such as R or Python, with the ability to analyse, interpret and communicate complex data effectively to a range of audiences. Experience of collaborating with clinicians and laboratory scientists, mentoring junior researchers, and contributing to multidisciplinary research projects will be highly advantageous. We are looking for a collaborative, self-motivated researcher with excellent organisational skills, strong attention to detail, and a commitment to delivering high-quality research. You will thrive in a team environment, enjoy working across disciplines, and be confident representing the research group within national and international collaborations. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance For rewards and benefits at UCL please visit: Staff Benefits | UCL People & C ulture - UCL – University College London Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: Equality, Diversity & Inclusion: Think differently, do differently. | Office of the President and Provost (Equality, Diversity & Inclusion) - UCL – University College London"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48546": {
      "title": "Research Fellow:  3DTreePrint Project",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; UCL SLASH (B03) Reference: B03-03278 About us: University College London (UCL) is one of the world's top universities. Based in the heart of London, it is a dynamic, outward-looking institution. At its establishment in 1826, UCL was radical and responsive to the needs of society, and this ethos – that excellence should go hand-in-hand with enriching society – continues today. The UCL Department of Geography is a leading UK and global department, with a heritage of excellence in research, renowned for its innovative and interdisciplinary research to understanding complex global challenges. It brings together expertise in physical and human geography to explore critical issues such as climate change, sustainable development, and social justice. The department fosters an interdisciplinary approach, equipping students and researchers with the skills to critically engage with the complex interactions between people, places, and environments. About the role: This post sits within ARIA's The Resilient Climate and Ecosystems opportunity seed route. ARIA (Advanced Research + Innovation Agency) is a UK research funding agency that supports ambitious research at the edge of the possible. Its opportunity spaces identify underexplored areas where new scientific and engineering capabilities could lead to major breakthroughs. This post sits within the ARIA's The Resilient Climate and Ecosystems opportunity seed route. Opportunity seeds provide funding of up to £500,000 for individual research teams to pursue high-potential, early-stage ideas aligned with an opportunity space but outside the scope of a larger programme. The seed mechanism is intended to give research teams the flexibility to explore new technical pathways that might otherwise fall between conventional funding routes. We are seeking a research fellow who will join the team and will work on the 3DTreePrint project led by Dr Martin Mokroš at UCL Geography. The 3DTreePrint project pairs low-cost LiDAR with automated workflows to make detailed 3D measurement of trees affordable, repeatable and scalable, building “3D structural fingerprints” to track ecosystem resilience over time. Main partners of the project are Forest Research, Forestry England, Earthwatch Europe and Polish Academy of Sciences. The full job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. This is a full time, fixed term post with funding available until 31 May 2028 . The appointment is available to start on the 1st of December 2026 . Funding is available at Grade 7, spine point 32 (including London Allowance). Informal enquiries may be made to Dr Martin Mokroš (m.mokros@ucl.ac.uk) If you have any queries regarding the application process, or if you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online, please contact the department’s research administration team: geog.research@ucl.ac.uk About you: The successful candidate will have a PhD (awarded or close to submission) in computer science, robotics, geomatics, remote sensing, forest science or a closely related discipline, with a substantial computational component. You will also need to demonstrate : • Substantial experience of processing point-cloud data from terrestrial and/or mobile laser scanning in forest, woodland or other structurally complex vegetated environments. • Advanced programming skills in Python and/or C++, including the ability to read, adapt, extend, debug and test existing open-source codebases rather than only applying them as supplied. Please see the full job description and person specification at the bottom of this page full more information. This role is eligible for hybrid working with a minimum time in site to be determined between successful candidate and line manager. We will consider applications to work on a flexible and job share basis wherever possible . This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave plus 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: The Department is committed to the values of equality, diversity and inclusion and we have an active Equality, Diversity and Inclusion Committee, and networks for BME, LGBTQ, and disabled/neurodiverse staff and students. The Department recognises the impor tance of family - friendly working, and we try, as far as possible, to organise meetings and events with staff responsibilities as parents and carers in mind. In 2022, the Department was awarded an Athena SWAN Silver award in recognition of our advancement of gender equality: representation , progression, and success. Above all the Department aims to be a supportive environment for both its staff and students. More information can be found here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/social - historical - sciences/geography/about/equality - diversity - and - inclusion"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48690": {
      "title": "Research Fellow: UCL Atmospheric Chemistry Research Group",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; UCL SLASH (B03) Reference: B03-03294 About us: University College London (UCL) is one of the world's top universities. Based in the heart of London, it is a dynamic, outward-looking institution. At its establishment in 1826, UCL was radical and responsive to the needs of society, and this ethos – that excellence should go hand-in-hand with enriching society – continues today. The UCL Department of Geography is a leading UK and global department, with a heritage of excellence in research, renowned for its innovative and interdisciplinary research to understanding complex global challenges. It brings together expertise in physical and human geography to explore critical issues such as climate change, sustainable development, and social justice. The department fosters an interdisciplinary approach, equipping students and researchers with the skills to critically engage with the complex interactions between people, places, and environments. About the role: This post will be part of a team of researchers at UCL and MIT investigating the ability to improve estimates of pollutant emissions from rocket launches to better constrain the atmospheric impacts of this emerging pollution source. The position is funded by a Leverhulme Research Project Grant awarded to Prof Marais in June this year ( https://www.leverhulme.ac.uk/listings?field_grant_scheme_target_id=15 ). The Research Fellow will have the exciting opportunity to learn to use and adapt an existing plume model to simulate pollution from rockets during launch. This will then provide the synthetic observations needed to validate top-down emission inversion estimates from space-based remote sensing instruments. The Research Fellow will also gather datasets of launch locations and timings, and of rocket characteristics to (i) mine satellite data for coincidences between launches and instrument overpasses or scans, (ii) interpret key factors affecting pollutant emissions from launches, and (iii) maintain UCL’s world-leading online tracker of rocket launch emissions ( https://orbitalemissions.org/ ). The full job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. This is a full time, fixed term post with funding available for 2.5 years. The appointment is available to start from 1st January 2027. Funding is available at Grade 7, spine point 32-33 (including London Allowance). Informal enquiries may be made to Prof Eloise Marais ( e.marais@ucl.ac.uk ) If you have any queries regarding the application process, or if you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online, please contact the department’s research administration team: geog.research@ucl.ac.uk About you: The successful candidate will have a PhD in a relevant research field and will need to demonstrate : • Fundamental science knowledge in a related discipline • Experience conducting independent research • Computer coding in a Unix environment Please see the full job description and person specification at the bottom of this page full more information. This role is eligible for hybrid working with a minimum time in site to be determined between successful candidate and line manager. We will consider applications to work on a flexible and job share basis wherever possible . This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave plus 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: The Department is committed to the values of equality, diversity and inclusion and we have an active Equality, Diversity and Inclusion Committee, and networks for BME, LGBTQ, and disabled/neurodiverse staff and students. The Department recognises the impor tance of family - friendly working, and we try, as far as possible, to organise meetings and events with staff responsibilities as parents and carers in mind. In 2022, the Department was awarded an Athena SWAN Silver award in recognition of our advancement of gender equality: representation , progression, and success. Above all the Department aims to be a supportive environment for both its staff and students. More information can be found here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/social - historical - sciences/geography/about/equality - diversity - and - inclusion"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48713": {
      "title": "Research Impact Officer",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11272 About us: Ranked fourth in the world for public health (ShanghaiRanking's 2025), the UCL Faculty of Population Health Sciences (FPHS) is a global leader in public health research working across disciplines to unravel complex health challenges, while educating the next generation of public and global health leaders. Our exciting cross-disciplinary community encompasses eight institutes, schools and centres. About the role: The Faculty of Population Health Sciences and the UCL Research, Innovation and Global Engagement (RIGE) Impact Team Impact Team are recruiting 2 x Research Impact Officers to support our internationally renowned research teams to develop impact case studies. The roles will focus on Research Excellence Framework 2029 (REF2029) preparations in the fields of Public Health, Health Services and Primary Care. Application Details: This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-service… for more information. Application Process: As a minimum we will keep all adverts open for 2 weeks, however we may close applications early if we receive a high volume of applications. Early application submission is recommended. A full job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. Please use the personal statement section to explain how you meet each of the essential and desirable criteria outlined in the person specification. Contact Details: If you have any queries regarding the vacancy or the application process, please contact Sarah Clegg s.clegg@ucl.ac.uk If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact fphs.hr@ucl.ac.uk About you: If you believe you meet the requirements, come and be part of this exciting opportunity to showcase the real-world impact of world-leading research in population health sciences. Work alongside senior academics, help tell compelling stories of change, and demonstrate how research is improving lives, influencing policy, and shaping society. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan and expenses Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver Award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48920": {
      "title": "Research Nurse in Infection and Sexual Health",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11376 About us: The UCL Institute for Global Health (IGH) is at the heart of UCL’s Grand Challenge of Global Health. The Institute’s vision is for a world where international policy on global health is informed by world class research. IGH aims to support the development of robust solutions to aspects of the world’s major global health challenges through scholarly outputs, education, public engagement, translational research and ultimately by influencing public policy and professional practice. This post is based in the UCL Centre for Clinical Research in Infection and Sexual Health. The Centre has a research programme covering a wide range of studies from observational cohort studies, qualitative and mixed methods studies to intervention trials. Historically most of the work has focussed on HIV and other sexually transmitted infections including viral hepatitis. However, our portfolio of research extends to other infectious diseases research particularly TB, COVID-19, and Mpox. The centre collaborates with other centres in IGH to provide the support needed to conduct clinical research. We work closely with the Centre for Population Research in Sexual Health and HIV on research concerning digital health and pathways for care in sexual health. In the case of TB, our work is closely linked with the Centre for Molecular Epidemiology and Translational Research, the Centre for Pragmatic Global Health Trials and the MRC Clinical Trials Unit at UCL. These centres host a number of infectious disease studies (including TB) as part of a programme of transdisciplinary global health research. We have an exciting opportunity for an enthusiastic and highly motivated Research Nurse to join our dynamic team and gain broad experience in a clinical research setting. About the role: The postholder will be part of the clinical research team, currently comprised of six research nurses, five research assistants, doctors, and other non-clinical members. The post holder will work collaboratively with Principal Investigators, co investigators and all staff involved with clinical research across the Centre and affiliated NHS Trusts. The post holder will ensure oversight of studies, conduct risk assessments, contribute to education, training and clinical management within the clinical research team and support nurse revalidation. There is the need for visibility, flexibility, expert communication, and teaching skills to engender understanding and support for the research processes. Research nurses are responsible for the oversight of a portfolio of studies, and lead in all communication to support recruitment, the reporting of clinical events, the collection of quality controlled data and effective communication with external sponsors. The postholder will also contribute to networking across the trust as well the wider community to expand on recruitment into research. Research nurses in the team may be allocated to any of the portfolio of 15-20 current studies. They will have an honorary NHS contract and are able to remain on the NHS pension scheme if already a member. The role is full time (i.e. 36.5 hours per week) and is available immediately for 12 months in the first instance. An acceptable enhanced DBS check is required to carry out this role. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. Salary range: £43,981 - £54,019 per annum (including London Allowance), depending on experience. If you have any queries about the role, please contact Ebun Taiwo (Senior Research Nurse) at e.taiwo@ucl.ac.uk . If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact the IGH HR Team on IGH.HR@ucl.ac.uk . About you: The post holder must have a current professional registration with the Nursing and Midwifery Council (NMC) and evidence of commitment to continuous professional development. They must have completed Good Clinical Practice training or commit to undertake this on appointment (training will be provided). They must also have experience of clinical research/clinical trials and/or gathering and analysing research data. Knowledge of sexual health, infectious diseases and blood-borne virus infection and understanding of data protection and confidentiality issues are essential. The ideal candidate will have experience of working in sexual health, infectious diseases and/or HIV/BBV and they will have excellent organisational, time management and prioritisation skills. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48561": {
      "title": "Research Software Engineer in Bioinformatics",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; IT and Digital; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07809 About us: The UCL Bioinformatics Group is well-known for developing a widely-used suite of web-based tools based on a number of cutting edge protein structure prediction methods. Our web services have been online since 1998 and the methods available allow users to predict a variety of protein structural features, including secondary structure and natively disordered regions, protein domain boundaries and 3D models of tertiary structure. Recently we have been developing new services to assist users in prediction gene function structural domain segmentation - which we believe are vital and unique tool for biologists. Our flagship service, The PSIPRED Workbench, currently unites all our services e.g. predictive methods, online tutorials and common look and feel. Through prior BBSRC BBR grants, we have successfully integrated our suite of tools into a single web portal. In our upcoming BBSRC grant we wish to extend this integration philosophy across multiple resources. The BBSRC funded IntegraTED project is aimed at developing a new Bioinformatics web resource hosted at UCL. Over the past 30 years the UCL Bioinformatics Group (dept of Computer Science) and the CATH Project (dept of Structural & Molecular Biology) have developed world leading web resources in structural bioinformatics. In this project we are looking to bring together three of our most important resources to develop a single, highly integrated and streamlined web platform. The aim is to provide protein structure and function researchers access to an unrivalled online research platform. The three resources involved are; the PSIPRED workbench, an online suite of machine learning based tools for analysing protein structure, The CATH-Gene3D structural classification of protein domains and The Encyclopaedia of Domains (TED) our new resource cataloguing all detectable structural domains in AlphaFoldDB. About the role: In this project you will take the lead on the UCL Computer Science Bioinformatics Group’s portion of the IntegraTED project. There are three principle objectives in this portion of the project. In the first objective we wish to increase the reliability and performance of the existing ML workflows that underpin the PSIPRED workbench. One target here is to integrate novel bioinformatics foundation models to accelerate methods already available via the PSIPRED Workbench. The Second major objective is to work extensively on a new web interface for the IntegraTED resource. Here you will work closely with the CATH team on our planned unified web and data portal. The third major objective is to work on the TED data generation pipeline. The successful applicant for this project will work on all of these theme areas with the possibility of working on novel ML applications in this space and will importantly take the lead on developing and maintaining the PSIPRED Workbench. This role is funded until 31st August 2029 in the first instance. About you: The ideal applicant will be a developer with a strong interested in building Scientific Software and prior experience of software development within academia. You should have the ability and enthusiasm to acquire new skills across the ML-Ops and Bioinformatics fields. We expect good software development skills in a least one language regularly used in scientific computing (i.e. Julia, Python, etc) and you should have an interest in good practice in software development (e.g. source control, testing, etc). We focus mainly on Python but adaptability across languages is a bonus. Eventually, you will be expected to lead the development of large long standing software engineering project, so an interest in developing skill in application design is a must. What we offer: In return, you’ll have the opportunity to make a significant impact within Student Lifecycle portfolio, working alongside talented colleagues. We offer a collaborative environment where your ideas are valued, your growth is supported, and your work directly contributes to the university’s bold strategic goals. Our people really are our greatest asset and we invest in them so that they can fulfil their potential. Equity, diversity and inclusion is important to us. We are committed to creating an environment where everyone is encouraged to give their best in a place where their unique experiences, perspectives and skills are seen as valuable assets. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Flexible working options Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department is working towards an Athena SWAN award. We are committed to advancing gender equality within our department. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/ Work at UCL pages: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48334": {
      "title": "Research Technical Officer",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Laboratory Technical and Lab Support; UCL East; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11236 About us: UCL Division of Biosciences is one of the world’s foremost centres for research and teaching in the biological sciences and one of the largest Divisions within UCL, undertaking a significant amount of research and teaching. The Division has a diverse portfolio addressing all areas of biology from protein interactions to cell function, organism development, genetics, population studies, to ecology and the environment. Computational modelling approaches are frequently used alongside experimental research programmes and much of our research crosses traditional boundaries, including the relationship of biodiversity to the health of the planet. Activity is underpinned by high calibre science technology platforms and state of the art equipment. Educational activity includes a range of undergraduate programmes, an expanding number of Masters Programmes and a substantial number of postgraduate research students. We are now recruiting a Research Technical Officer position to provide technical support for the Agritech Platform and People & Nature Laboratory to support our cross-disciplinary research, teaching and external engagement based the new UCL East campus About the role: In this pivotal role, this position will provide technical support for teaching, research and public engagement activities related to biodiversity, environmental change and human health. The successful candidate will support a variety of complex projects, including those involving (1) vertical farming, greenhouse, bee labs and roof garden experiments; (2) eDNA and environmental genetics; (3) sensor technologies; (4) field-based ecological research and (5) public engagement through citizen science. Working closely with other technical and academic staff, the postholder will play a crucial role in developing and supporting the small technical team, physical spaces, digital assets, field sites, equipment, environmental genetics protocols and procedures at the UCL East Agritech Platform, the People & Nature Lab and wider engagement activities across the Queen Elizabeth Olympic Park. Being both a research and student-facing post, the post-holder will play a crucial role in delivering an excellent student experience and is ideally suited to someone who thrives in an interactive learning environment and is skilled in developing innovative practical-made solutions, while also maintaining safe and organised workspaces. We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible and job share basis wherever possible. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/humanresources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. If you have any queries about the role, please contact Dr Reshma Tilwani (reshma.tilwani@ucl.ac.uk). If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact the HR Administrator. About you: You will have an undergraduate degree, or equivalent experience in a relevant Biological or Agricultural Sciences subject area, and have technical awareness of greenhouses, vertical farms, bee labs and a diverse range of ecological laboratory, data science and field techniques and equipment directly relevant to the UCL East Agritech Platform and People & Nature Lab. You would also have experience of supervising staff and in leading a team to deliver technical support provision, and experience of running greenhouses, vertical farms and diverse laboratory, data science and fieldwork environment and adherence to health and safety requirements. Experience in teaching, mentoring, and demonstrating skills or crafts to staff/students and the wider public would also be an advantage. What we offer: The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/policies-advice/ways-working to find out more. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our division holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48238": {
      "title": "Research Technician",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £36,433-£42,891​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11226 About us: The Division of Biosciences at UCL is one of the world's foremost centres for research and teaching in the biological sciences. We have an outstanding international reputation and a community of over 500 staff and more than 300 PhD students engaged in cutting edge research and collaboration. Further information can be found on our website https://www.ucl.ac.uk/biosciences We are seeking an enthusiastic researcher with a background in evo-devo/functional genomics to join the group of Dr. Sean Stankowski. The position is well funded through a UKRI Future Leaders Fellowship. About the role: The Research Technician will contribute to research projects focused on understanding the genomic basis of adaption and speciation, primarily in intertidal snails from the genus Littorina . ( https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.adi2982 ). They will take the lead in implementing protocols developed by the Darwin Tree of Life project ( https://www.sanger.ac.uk/programme/tree-of-life/ ) to extract high molecular weight DNA and prepare long read sequencing libraries (ONT/PacBio), and assist with DNA/RNA extraction and DNA library prep for Illumina sequencing projects. Other tasks may include assistance with snail rearing, general organisation in the lab, and training of staff and students. Please note, this role is available on a full time (36.5 hours per week) basis for up to 2-years, or part time basis (18.25 - 27.4 hours per week) for 3-years, with possibility of extension. The researcher will work as part of a team that includes two Research Fellows, and also in collaboration with project partners from the Sanger Institute (Dra. Joana Meier), the Institute of Science and Technology Austria (Dr. Nick Barton), The University of Gothenburg (Dr. Kerstin Johannesson). A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. Interviews will be held in November 2026. If you have any queries about the role, please contact Sean Stankowski at s.stankowski@ucl.ac.uk . If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact the HR Administrator (fls.hr@ucl.ac.uk) stating the advert reference number in the subject line. About you: The ideal applicant will have experience with the development and customisation of DNA extraction protocols, QC, and experience with long-read and illumina sequencing library preparation. Experience operating an ONT sequencer and the downstream processing of long and short-read sequence data would be a plus. What we offer: We will support your professional development to help you achieve your long-term goals. and will provide access to training courses and international and domestic conferences. The position will include time and funds to facilitate training, and opportunities to work with and visit project partners at other research institutions. The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/policies-advice/ways-working to find out more. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our division holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48876": {
      "title": "Senior Administrator",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; President and Provost (A01) Reference: A01-00098 About us: UCL’s Office of the President & Provost supports the President & Provost across the full range of their responsibilities as the academic leader of the university. The team provides high-level logistical, administrative, executive and policy support, enabling the President & Provost and their leadership team to deliver strategic priorities and lead change across UCL. The office operates in a fast-paced, high-profile and highly sensitive environment , working closely with colleagues across UCL and with external stakeholders. The President & Provost represents UCL nationally and internationally and engages with governments, organisations and other stakeholders across the higher education sector. The team works collaboratively with UCL’s Business Operations Service (BOS) and the wider network of Executive and Personal Assistants. Together, they provide coordinated, professional and effective support to senior leaders across the university. The environment requires professionalism, discretion, confidentiality, flexibility and excellent relationship-building skills , particularly when working with senior colleagues and high-profile internal and external stakeholders. About the role: This is a varied and challenging new role at the heart of the President & Provost's Team. As Senior Administrator, you will play a pivotal role in the smooth running of the team's executive support provision, providing high-level support to the Chief of Staff and Deputy Chief of Staff. You will take a strategic approach to their forward schedules. You will handle sensitive issues and correspondence, prepare briefings, and manage preparation for and follow-up from key meetings, committees and working groups. You will also coordinate their special projects and their engagement with the wider senior leadership team. You will manage projects, away days and events led by the Chief of Staff from start to finish. This includes research and horizon-scanning, stakeholder engagement, logistics (venues, catering, AV and travel), briefing papers and correspondence, agendas, records of decisions and actions, budgets, and evaluation of what worked. You will act as a conduit between the Chief of Staff and the team and maintain a forward look and yearly cycle of activity that maps governance deadlines, reporting cycles and key milestones. That way the team can anticipate peaks and clashes rather than react to them. You will also support the P&P core team's weekly meetings, the wider performance cycle (including senior leadership team objectives and appraisals), and, working with the governance team, the team's engagement with formal governance committees. You will assist the diary secretaries with strategic direction and line manage the Administrator, working in tandem with colleagues across the executive support function. This a chance to support the top level of the institution in a complex, fast-moving and highly sensitive environment, where you can make a real difference to how time and impact are used. This role is based at UCL's Bloomsbury Campus. If you have any questions about the role or the application process, please contact Passant Khalifa (Senior Recruiter) at p.khalifa@ucl.ac.uk . About you: You are a pragmatic, focused and highly organised professional with a can-do attitude and a keen eye for detail. You are used to providing senior administrative and/or secretarial support to leaders in a complex organisation, and you plan ahead by anticipating needs, dependencies and next steps rather than waiting to be asked. You are comfortable challenging a request when it's the right thing to do, and you stay calm and adaptable when priorities shift or something unexpected lands at the last minute. You are confident managing a demanding workload, and you bring analytical and problem-solving skills to it. You can work independently, make sense of complex issues even when information is incomplete, and are comfortable where there is no clear precedent. You draft clear, timely and fit-for-purpose communications, briefings and reports. You are proficient with standard office and productivity tools, including PowerPoint and a project or task management tool such as Microsoft Planner, and use them to produce high-quality materials and coordinate work effectively. Discretion and diplomacy are essential, and you will be trusted with confidential and sensitive information. You communicate tactfully and assertively, build effective relationships across UCL and externally, and enjoy working as a supportive, service-minded team player across different functions and ways of working. You have some experience of, or are comfortable with, line managing and working in tandem with diary secretaries and other executive support colleagues. Experience in line managing a diary secretary or manager, optimising office processes and digital tools, or delivering a welcoming front-of-house experience would be an advantage. What we offer: As well as exciting opportunities, UCL offers a range of great benefits. Find out more: UCL Rewards and Benefits . 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women.” Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48270": {
      "title": "Senior Category Manager (Infrastructure)",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Commercial and Procurement; London; £56,431-£66,437​; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00349 About us: UCL is London’s leading multidisciplinary university and one of the world’s most successful research institutions, with more than 18,000 staff and over 51,000 students from more than 150 countries. Our Commercial & Procurement function manages more than £900 million of third-party expenditure each year and plays an important role in ensuring UCL achieves excellent commercial outcomes while supporting our wider strategic and sustainability ambitions. Within Estates Development, we support the continued investment, development and improvement of UCL’s diverse and complex estate. Effective infrastructure is fundamental to the operation of the university, supporting the environments in which our students learn, our researchers undertake world-leading work. We are strengthening our commercial capability and are looking for an experienced Senior Category Manager – Infrastructure to help shape how UCL procures and manages strategically important infrastructure requirements. About the role: As Senior Category Manager – Infrastructure, you will take ownership of developing and delivering commercial strategies across our maintenance and infrastructure category. Reporting to the Head of Category – Estate Infrastructure, you will work closely with senior stakeholders to understand their priorities and commercial challenges, translating these into effective commercial solutions. You will act as the framework manager for the maintenance and infrastructure contracts, working to ensure suppliers deliver performance and value for our Hard FM category. You will provide contract advice, while managing risk and supplier innovation You will lead complex procurement activity from initial strategy and market engagement through to competition, negotiation and contract award. This will include determining appropriate routes to market, developing contracting and negotiation strategies and leading the delivery of frameworks, competitions and other commercial solutions. You will take a strategic view of the category, using commercial insight and data to identify opportunities to improve value, drive efficiencies and deliver cost savings. You will also act as a trusted commercial adviser to senior stakeholders, providing guidance on procurement, contractual, commercial and compliance matters and ensuring commercial considerations are embedded into infrastructure planning. This is an opportunity to influence how a major organisation approaches infrastructure procurement and to help develop commercial solutions that balance value, quality, risk, sustainability and long-term operational requirements. A full job description is attached to this advert. About you: You will be an experienced procurement and commercial professional with experience of leading complex procurement activity and delivering commercial outcomes. Ideally, your experience will include maintenance, infrastructure, engineering, utilities, or another technically complex environment, although we are particularly interested in the commercial capability and transferable experience you can bring. You will have experience developing and implementing robust procurements and contracts and be a confident negotiator who has delivered complex, large-scale commercial solutions. You will be comfortable operating within a large and complex organisation and working with a broad range of stakeholders. You will have the credibility to influence senior colleagues, constructively challenge requirements and build strong relationships. Strong analytical skills and commercial judgement will be important. You will be educated to degree level or have equivalent professional experience. Further professional development and accredited membership of the Chartered Institute of Procurement & Supply (CIPS) would be advantageous. If you are looking for an opportunity to combine strategic procurement expertise with the challenge of supporting a large, complex and evolving estate, we would be delighted to hear from you. Please read our job description, paying particular attention to the Essential and Desirable criteria, and ensure these are covered in your application. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent.We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48300": {
      "title": "Senior Category Manager (Major Works)",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Commercial and Procurement; London; £56,431-£66,437​; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00348 About us: UCL is London’s leading multidisciplinary university and one of the world’s most successful research institutions, with more than 18,000 staff and over 51,000 students from more than 150 countries. Our Commercial & Procurement function manages an annual third-party spend of more than £900 million and plays a critical role in ensuring UCL achieves excellent commercial outcomes while supporting our wider strategic and sustainability ambitions. Within Estates Development, we support the investment and development of UCL’s diverse estate. We are now looking for a Senior Category Manager – Major Works to help shape and deliver our commercial approach across this vitally important area. About the role: As Senior Category Manager – Major Works, you will take a leading role in developing and delivering procurement and commercial strategies that support UCL’s Estates Development priorities. Working closely with the Head of Category – Estates Development, you will build strong relationships with senior stakeholders, developing a detailed understanding of their requirements and translating these into effective category, sourcing and contracting strategies. You will lead complex procurement activity from strategy through to contract award, developing appropriate routes to market, commercial models, frameworks and competitions. You will provide expert commercial and contractual advice, lead negotiations and ensure solutions deliver strong operational outcomes, value for money and appropriate management of commercial risk. You'll also influence senior stakeholders and help ensure commercial considerations are embedded into the planning and delivery of major works activity. This is an opportunity to work client-side within a large and complex organisation and have a tangible impact on how UCL procures and delivers investment across its estate. A full job description is attached to this advert. About you: You will be an experienced procurement and commercial professional with significant experience of leading complex, high-value procurement activity. You may currently be working as a Senior Category Manager, Procurement Manager, Commercial Manager, Senior Procurement Manager or in a comparable commercial role within construction, infrastructure, property or a major capital programme environment. You will have a strong track record of developing procurement and contracting strategies, running robust competitive procurement processes and negotiating complex commercial agreements. You will be commercially astute and able to identify opportunities, develop compelling cases for change and translate commercial strategy into successful delivery. You will be confident operating in a large, complex organisation and engaging with a diverse range of stakeholders. Experience of working with major works, large-scale construction, infrastructure or capital investment supply chains will be particularly valuable, alongside an understanding of the commercial considerations associated with complex projects and supplier markets. Please read our job description carefully, paying particular attention the the Essential and Desirable criteria, and ensure these are covered in your application. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent.We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49033": {
      "title": "Senior Category Manager - INTERNAL OPPORTUNITY OPEN TO UCL EMPLOYEES ONLY",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Commercial and Procurement; London; £68,284-£78,386​; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00401 About us: UCL is London's leading multidisciplinary university, bringing together more than 13,000 staff and 42,000 students from over 150 countries. Renowned for excellence in teaching and world-leading research, UCL is committed to delivering an ambitious strategic vision for 2034. Our Commercial & Procurement function plays a vital role in supporting this vision, managing over £600 million in annual third-party expenditure. We aim to deliver an outstanding procurement service that achieves value for money, drives commercial excellence and supports UCL's Sustainable Procurement Strategy. We are looking for an experienced commercial leader who shares our commitment to innovation, sustainability and continuous improvement, and who can help transform our procurement approach to deliver lasting value for the university. About the role: As Senior Category Manager, you will lead the Travel, Meetings, Incentives, Conferences and Events (MICE), Contingent Labour, and External Engagement categories, including communications and marketing. You will develop and deliver category strategies that meet stakeholder needs, generate targeted savings and improve commercial and operational performance. Working closely with senior stakeholders across UCL, you will provide expert commercial advice, lead complex sourcing and negotiation activities, and identify opportunities to improve efficiency, manage demand and strengthen supplier relationships. You will also support the Director of Corporate Services in delivering the wider strategic roadmap. Key responsibilities include: Developing and implementing category strategies aligned with UCL's commercial objectives, stakeholder priorities and sustainability commitments. Building strong relationships with senior stakeholders and acting as the principal point of contact for your categories. Leading strategic sourcing, complex negotiations, contract development, supplier frameworks and preferred supplier lists. Identifying and delivering cost savings, efficiencies and innovative commercial solutions. Providing expert advice on commercial, contractual and procurement compliance matters. Using spend analytics and commercial data to identify opportunities, build business cases and support evidence-based decisions. Driving transformation, continuous improvement and new ways of working across procurement and stakeholder teams. Leading, coaching and developing team members, fostering a high-performing and collaborative culture. Supporting the delivery of UCL's wider Corporate Services strategic objectives. This is an opportunity to influence strategic decision-making, deliver measurable commercial benefits and contribute to the long-term success of a world-leading university. About you: You are a commercially astute procurement professional with significant senior-level experience in category management, strategic sourcing and commercial transformation. You can translate complex business needs into effective procurement strategies, deliver successful negotiations and build credible business cases that achieve measurable results. You will bring: Significant commercial and procurement experience, including leadership, transformation and change management. Experience across Corporate Services categories, ideally including contingent labour, travel, MICE and marketing. A proven track record of developing and implementing robust sourcing strategies and contracts, including complex, large-scale commercial solutions. Strong negotiation, influencing and stakeholder-management skills, with the confidence to engage effectively with senior leaders. Experience working within large, complex and decentralised organisations with diverse stakeholder groups. Strong commercial data literacy, including the ability to interpret spend analytics and use tools such as Excel and Power BI to inform decisions. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to present complex information clearly and persuasively. Strong organisational skills, sound judgement and the ability to manage competing priorities. A collaborative, innovative and evidence-based approach to problem-solving and continuous improvement. You will also be passionate about developing people, building high-performing teams and embedding sustainable, value-driven procurement practices. A relevant degree or equivalent professional experience is essential. Further professional development and membership of the Chartered Institute of Procurement and Supply (CIPS) would be advantageous. If you are ready to lead strategic procurement initiatives, influence senior stakeholders and deliver meaningful commercial impact, we welcome your application to join UCL and help shape the future of our university. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent.We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48377": {
      "title": "Senior Category Manager - IT&T",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Commercial and Procurement; London; £56,431-£66,437​; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00372 About us: At UCL, we're proud to be London's Global University. With more than 18,000 staff and over 51,000 students from more than 150 countries, UCL is one of the world's leading multidisciplinary universities, renowned for both groundbreaking research and exceptional teaching. Our Commercial & Procurement Services function manages over £900 million of third-party spend annually and plays a critical role in supporting UCL's ambitious strategic objectives. Through effective sourcing, supplier management and commercial leadership, we help enable world-class education and research while delivering value, sustainability and innovation across the institution. About the role: Reporting to the Head of Software and Infrastructure Category, IT & Telecoms, you'll act as the commercial lead for your category area, partnering with senior stakeholders across UCL to develop and deliver category strategies that support both institutional priorities and commercial objectives. You'll be responsible for identifying opportunities to drive value, improve supplier performance and deliver innovative commercial solutions, while ensuring compliance, sustainability and best practice procurement principles remain at the forefront. Key responsibilities will include: Developing and implementing category strategies aligned to stakeholder and organisational objectives Building trusted relationships with senior stakeholders and serving as the primary commercial contact for your category Leading complex procurement exercises, tenders, framework agreements and supplier competitions Developing and executing negotiation and contracting strategies to secure excellent commercial outcomes Providing expert advice on procurement, commercial, contractual and compliance matters Driving savings, efficiencies and continuous improvement initiatives across the category Identifying opportunities to innovate and introduce new ways of working Supporting the development of high-performing teams and fostering a culture of commercial excellence This role offers significant exposure to senior stakeholders and the opportunity to influence strategic decision-making within one of the world's leading universities. A full job description is attached to this advert. About you: We're looking for a commercially astute procurement professional with a strong track record of delivering complex procurement and category management solutions in large, stakeholder-rich environments. You'll combine commercial expertise with excellent relationship-building skills and the confidence to operate effectively with senior leaders across a complex organisation. You'll bring: Degree-level education or equivalent professional experience Knowledge and understanding of IT infrastructure Significant commercial and senior procurement experience Experience developing and implementing category and sourcing strategies Strong procurement, tendering and contract management expertise Proven success delivering complex negotiations and commercial outcomes Experience working within large, complex and decentralised organisations Strong stakeholder management and influencing skills Excellent written and verbal communication skills The ability to build compelling business cases and drive change through to delivery A collaborative and pragmatic approach to problem solving Strong organisational skills and the ability to manage multiple priorities Advanced Excel skills It would be advantageous if you also have: Experience within communications, marketing or external engagement-related procurement CIPS membership or progression towards professional accreditation Experience leading teams and developing procurement professionals Evidence of ongoing professional development What we offer: As well as exciting opportunities, UCL offers a range of great benefits. 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49077": {
      "title": "Senior Change Manager",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Strategy, Project and Change Management; London; £68,284-£78,386​; Vice-Provost (Strategy) (B24) Reference: B24-00094 About us: UCL is a global top-10 university with more than 16,000 staff, over 50,000 students from 150 countries and a turnover of more than £1.2bn. Our Strategic Change team makes sure the big transformation initiatives across the university are prioritised, delivered well and actually deliver their benefits. Right now, we're investing in an ambitious portfolio of change across research and innovation, with better services for academics, stronger compliance, greater efficiency and a better experience for the people who run those services. About the role: Transformation only works when people come with it. That's your job. You'll lead the people side of change for UCL's entire Research and Innovation portfolio, owning the change approach and coaching senior leaders to land change that lasts. You'll work with leaders and other colleagues across Research, Innovation & Global Engagement (RIGE), our faculties, Strategic Change and other Professional Service teams, and you'll have a seat on the Extended Strategic Change Leadership team. What you'll be doing Owning the change management strategy and plan across the Research and Innovation portfolio, joined up with wider programme planning. Assessing change impacts across UCL and using that data to shape key decisions and keep change load manageable. Advising on, shaping and/or delivering change management on a wide range of programmes and initiatives improving research and innovation service and effectiveness across UCL Coaching sponsors and senior leaders to lead their teams through change, including running workshops and readiness sessions. Applying agile and waterfall change management tools and techniques, from stakeholder mapping and impact assessments to adoption and benefits planning. Designing compelling engagement and communication approaches that ease transition and address resistance. Growing change capability across UCL. About you: You're a credible change leader who knows how to make transformation stick in a large, complex organisation. You can facilitate a challenging leadership discussion in the morning and build an impact assessment in the afternoon, and you stay calm and focused when things are moving fast. When you apply: use your supporting statement to show how you meet each essential criterion below, with examples. We'll score applications against them. Essential A recognised change qualification or certification, such as APMG or Prosci. A track record of embedding change (digital, process, behavioural and/or organisational design) in a large, complex organisation, using both agile and waterfall approaches. Knowledge or understanding of research and innovation operations in higher education. Expert change management skills: stakeholder management, impact and readiness assessment, transition delivery (including training needs analysis) and measurable adoption. Proven team leadership, including matrix management and developing individuals. Strong relationship-building, influencing and coaching skills at all levels, including with senior sponsors. Excellent communication and presentation skills for a wide range of audiences. Strategic thinking that joins the dots at an enterprise level, and the ability to plan, pivot and lead through ambiguity at pace. A collaborative approach that welcomes input from others. The ability to demonstrate UCL's Ways of Working for Grade 9 , and a real contribution to advancing equality, diversity and inclusion. Nice to have Experience of delivering change in higher education or an equivalent public body. Strategies for building resilience in yourself and your team. A habit of cross-team collaboration and encouraging innovative approaches. What we offer: 41 days of holiday (27 days' annual leave, 8 bank holidays and 6 closure days). Hybrid working, with up to 60% of the week remote. An excellent defined benefit pension scheme. Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, on-site nursery and discounted gym membership. Wellbeing support, learning and development, and staff discounts. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London's Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We're committed to being fair and inclusive and to being a place where everyone belongs, and we particularly encourage applications from people likely to be underrepresented in UCL's workforce."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49061": {
      "title": "Senior Clinical Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11371 About us: The mission of the UCL GOS Institute of Child Health is to maximise and advocate for the holistic health of all children, young people and the adults they will become, through world-class research, education and public engagement. The UCL GOS ICH, together with its clinical partner Great Ormond Street Hospital for Children, forms the largest concentration of children’s health research outside North America. The 2024-29 GOS ICH strategy focuses on its five scientific programmes. GOS ICH’s activities include active engagement with children and families, to ensure that our work is relevant and appropriate to their needs. GOS ICH generates the funding for our research by setting out our proposals in high quality applications to public, charitable and industrial funding bodies and disseminates the results of our research by publication in the medical and scientific literature, to clinicians, policy makers and the wider public. The Institute offers world-class education and training across a wide range of teaching and life learning programmes which address the needs of students and professional groups who are interested in and undertaking work relevant to child health. GOS ICH holds an Athena SWAN Charter Gold Award. About the role: Applications are invited for a Senior Clinical Research Fellow to join the Molecular and Cellular Immunology Section at the UCL Great Ormond Street Institute of Child Health (UCL GOSICH). The post is funded for 12 months. This position is to provide clinical trial support to the internationally renowned advanced therapies group based in Molecular & Cellular Immunology section of Institute of Child Health. It will provide an opportunity to work with cutting edge clinical researchers and interact across inter-disciplinary groups. The gene and cell therapy group are involved in a portfolio of first in man trials involving rare diseases, strategies to improve immune responses to infection, leukaemia after haematopoietic stem cell transplantation, somatic gene therapy for primary immunodeficiencies, HIV and inherited skin disorders. The postholder must have paediatric experience and will be involved with the clinical care of gene therapy trial patients. They will work closely with the immunology and gene therapy research teams. About you: Applicants must have appropriate speciality in a paediatric setting and previous experience in clinical trials. Expertise in gene and cell therapy, molecular biology or immunology and experience working in clinical trials is desirable but not essential. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/ If you have any queries regarding the application process, please contact Madhur Sharma on ich.hr@ucl.ac.uk quoting job reference"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49062": {
      "title": "Senior Clinical Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11371 About us: The mission of the UCL GOS Institute of Child Health is to maximise and advocate for the holistic health of all children, young people and the adults they will become, through world-class research, education and public engagement. The UCL GOS ICH, together with its clinical partner Great Ormond Street Hospital for Children, forms the largest concentration of children’s health research outside North America. The 2024-29 GOS ICH strategy focuses on its five scientific programmes. GOS ICH’s activities include active engagement with children and families, to ensure that our work is relevant and appropriate to their needs. GOS ICH generates the funding for our research by setting out our proposals in high quality applications to public, charitable and industrial funding bodies and disseminates the results of our research by publication in the medical and scientific literature, to clinicians, policy makers and the wider public. The Institute offers world-class education and training across a wide range of teaching and life learning programmes which address the needs of students and professional groups who are interested in and undertaking work relevant to child health. GOS ICH holds an Athena SWAN Charter Gold Award. About the role: Applications are invited for a Senior Clinical Research Fellow to join the Molecular and Cellular Immunology Section at the UCL Great Ormond Street Institute of Child Health (UCL GOSICH). The post is funded for 12 months. This position is to provide clinical trial support to the internationally renowned advanced therapies group based in Molecular & Cellular Immunology section of Institute of Child Health. It will provide an opportunity to work with cutting edge clinical researchers and interact across inter-disciplinary groups. The gene and cell therapy group are involved in a portfolio of first in man trials involving rare diseases, strategies to improve immune responses to infection, leukaemia after haematopoietic stem cell transplantation, somatic gene therapy for primary immunodeficiencies, HIV and inherited skin disorders. The postholder must have paediatric experience and will be involved with the clinical care of gene therapy trial patients. They will work closely with the immunology and gene therapy research teams. About you: Applicants must have appropriate speciality in a paediatric setting and previous experience in clinical trials. Expertise in gene and cell therapy, molecular biology or immunology and experience working in clinical trials is desirable but not essential. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/ If you have any queries regarding the application process, please contact Madhur Sharma on ich.hr@ucl.ac.uk quoting job reference"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=47993": {
      "title": "Senior Cloud Engineer",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; IT and Digital; London; £54,931-£64,644; Vice-President (Operations) (B01) Reference: B01-05393 About us: Information Services Division (ISD) is the primary provider of IT services to UCL. We support and enhance learning, teaching, research and administrative processes by providing information- and technology-related services to over 50,000 staff and students of UCL and associated institutions. Our ambition is to be the leading IT services group in the Higher Education (HE) sector and we are growing our team’s capability in experience/UX, agile development, security, cloud, service management and partnering. We are modernising our technology foundations, digitising the processes of the university to transform experience for students and staff, and partnering across the university to drive differentiation in UCL education and research. UCL boasts a track record of ground-breaking research that transforms the lives of people around the world. Our teams provide dedicated, specialist support throughout the research and innovation lifecycle to help sustain and advance UCL’s position as a world-class, research-led university. About the role: We are looking for a Senior Cloud Engineer (Enterprise Storage) to provide technical expertise across our enterprise storage and backup services. The successful candidate will join a team that provides technical expertise across a range of core infrastructure platforms that underpin UCL’s critical managed services. These include virtual infrastructure, public cloud platforms, web platforms, load balancers, enterprise storage and backup solutions. In this role you will work with a broad set of technologies, contributing to the design, implementation and continuous improvement of our storage and data protection platforms, helping ensure high levels of resilience, reliability and performance. You will leverage your experience and passion for IT, storage and data protection to support the wider team in driving platform improvements and modernisation, whilst collaborating with product owners and stakeholders across the university to deliver reliable, scalable services that meet UCL’s business needs. About you: You will have practical experience administering Windows or Linux systems within a service-oriented infrastructure environment, with a strong focus on automation and operational efficiency. You will bring strong technical knowledge of enterprise storage and backup technologies, with experience administering and safeguarding resilient data protection platforms across private and public cloud environments. This will include supporting data protection strategies, platform resilience and disaster recovery capabilities. The successful candidate will be expected to demonstrate the ability to manage problems through to resolution, often with uncertain and incomplete information, with an eye for detail and accuracy and an aptitude for analytical and logical methodologies. Have experience of using their specialist knowledge to advise and support team members in their technical activities in both project and operational environments, with an ability to adapt to changing technologies. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48600": {
      "title": "Senior E-resources Assistant",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Libraries, museums, theatres and open science; London; £36,433-£42,891​; Vice-Provost (Faculties) (B25) Reference: B25-00302 About us: UCL is London’s global university. LCCOS – Library, Culture, Collections and Open Science – provides outstanding staff, services and resources to support the world-class research, education and enterprise that is undertaken in UCL, and connects the world with UCL through innovative programming and engagement activities. LCCOS is acknowledged as an international leader in Open Science and Scholarship. The LCCOS family comprises 4 museums and allied object collections, 13 libraries, the UCL Student Centre, Special Collections facilities, multiple additional learning spaces and a substantial off-site storage facility. We also provide comprehensive services to healthcare staff through our partnerships with 6 NHS Trusts. LCCOS holds the UK Customer Service Excellence (CSE) Award, The National Archives (TNA) Archives Service Accreditation, and Arts Council England Museum Accreditation. The combined staff in UCL LCCOS totals around 450. About the role: The E-Resources Team is responsible for managing a large and varied collection of e-resources (databases, e-journals and e-books). This includes acquisition, access management, discovery and review. The post holder will ensure UCL’s e-resource holdings and linking information in the library management system (Alma) are accurate and up to date, and they will use their excellent communication skills when responding to user enquiries, and when liaising with publishers, subscription agents and UCL IT support to resolve access issues. This role is eligible for hybrid working with a minimum of 40% on site. We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible and job share basis wherever possible. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. This role does not meet the eligibility requirements for a Skilled Worker Visa certificate of sponsorship under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore, UCL will not be able to sponsor individuals who require right to work in the UK to carry out this role. About you: We are looking for candidates who in particular have the following experience and skills: Experience of periodicals/journals or acquisitions work in a library environment. A demonstrable understanding of the issues involved in acquiring and managing electronic resources. Experience of responding to customer enquiries. Ability to work with accuracy and attention to detail. Further details The advert will close on the published date at 23:59 GMT. Interviews will be held at the Institute of Education Library. A job description and person specification can be accessed from the link at the bottom of this page. To apply, please provide: An up-to-date CV, including as a minimum detail of your current employment, salary and notice period, details of any former employment and your education history. A supporting statement, showing how your skills and experience meet each of the criteria marked ‘Application’ in the Person Specification found within the Job Description for this post. Your statement should be provided using the free text box under ‘Questionnaire’. If you have any queries about the role or have any queries about the application process, please contact lccos-jobs@ucl.ac.uk , quoting the job title and reference number. Further information about UCL LCCOS can be found on our website https://www.ucl.ac.uk/lccos/ The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please visit www.ucl.ac.uk/ways-of-working to find out more. What we offer: Annual leave & Work life balance 27 days annual leave + 6 closure days (Christmas and Easter) as well as statutory holidays Hybrid and flexible working Ability to buy 5 additional annual leave days Children & Family Enhanced maternity, adoption, paternity leave Carer’s leave Workplace nursery Other benefits We also offer some great benefits some of which are defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE); cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan; on-site gym etc. As part of the UCL community you can access free lunch hour lectures, exhibitions and museums and collections. On campus UCL has the Bloomsbury theatre hosting a range of performances and a series of bars, cafes and other facilities, which UCL staff can use. Visit Reward and Benefits | Work at UCL - UCL – University College London to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department is working towards an Athena SWAN award. We are committed to advancing gender equality within our department. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48684": {
      "title": "Senior Finance Officer",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; IT and Digital; London; £43,981-£54,019​; Vice-President (Operations) (B01) Reference: B01-05743 About us: UCL is a multi-disciplinary university with a population of over 18,000 staff and 51,000 students from 150 different countries. For more information see www.ucl.ac.uk/about . The UCL Centre for Advanced Research Computing (ARC) is UCL’s institute for infrastructure and innovation in digital research. We are an innovative hybrid: a professional services department that delivers reliable and secure infrastructure and services to UCL research groups, and a laboratory for research and innovation. We are a home for the research technology professionals - research software engineers, HPC systems engineers, dev-ops specialists, data scientists and data stewards - who support and collaborate in the delivery of UCL research. Further information about working in ARC can be found on our website https://www.ucl.ac.uk/arc About the role: We are looking for someone to join our growing department. This role will work with departmental staff as well as with colleagues from across UCL and the wider research community. About you: This role would suit someone with experience of financial administration and a knowledge of accounting and financial procedures, particularly within a Higher Education setting. A relevant financial qualification or significant experience obtained in Higher Education would be essential for this role. The role holder should also be able to demonstrate excellent computer literacy, including efficient use of Excel and budgetary and reporting software (i.e. Axiom, Worktribe). Please attach a cover letter detailing why you have applied for this role, and the skills and experience you bring that are required in the person specification and job description. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits. Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. We are committed to advancing gender equality within our department. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48395": {
      "title": "Senior Finance Officer",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Finance; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07865 About us: The Department of Mathematics at UCL is an internationally renowned department within one of the world’s most prestigious universities. Since its beginning, mathematics at UCL has been enhanced by its many outstanding members of staff. Indeed, two of its students (and later staff) Professor Klaus Roth (1958) and Professor Alan Baker (1970) have gone on to win the Fields Medal. Another former member of staff Professor Tim Gowers won the Fields Medal in 1998 for work he did whilst at UCL. Since 2011, the department has grown considerably. The department today is home to around eighty-five academic staff, including thirty-nine full professors, and a number of honorary staff, supported by a professional services team of twenty-five. Its community also includes several Postdoctoral Research Fellows, around eighty-five PhD students, and fifty-four MSc students. We welcome a regular stream of distinguished visiting academics from home and abroad. The broad range of research interests is reflected in the wide choice of courses available in the third and fourth years of the degree programmes, from spectral theory, groups and geometry to fluid mechanics and from mathematical ideas in biology to cosmology. Research funding in the department from EPSRC, ERC, NERC, Leverhulme Trust and the Wellcome Trust has grown significantly in recent years, more than doubling since 2014. The department has internationally recognised research groups in pure and applied analysis; fluid mechanics; mathematical physics; geometry and topology; algebra, number theory and combinatorics; mathematical modelling in biology, finance, industry and society. There are regular departmental seminar series in financial mathematics and applied mathematics, as well as seminar series joint with other London universities in number theory, geometry and analysis. There are regular London-Paris meetings in analysis and number theory and a London-Brussels meeting in geometry. The department hosts an annual meeting in the dynamics of rotating fluids. About the role: Applications are invited for a full-time Senior Finance Officer position to work with to work alongside colleagues to provide professional services to the Department. This post is available from 4 th January 2027 or as soon as possible thereafter. This post is a 1-year Fixed-Term contract. Interviews will be held the week of the 26th October 2026. We do not offer sponsorship for this role. About you: You will be an experienced finance professional with strong financial management and analytical skills, and the confidence to provide clear, practical advice to a wide range of colleagues. You’ll have experience of managing budgets, forecasting, reconciliations and financial reporting, ideally alongside experience of research funding or working within a complex organisation. You’ll be highly organised and able to manage competing priorities, with excellent attention to detail and a proactive approach to resolving financial queries and issues. You’ll enjoy working collaboratively with academic and professional services colleagues, building effective relationships and translating financial information into clear advice that supports good decision-making. The role also requires strong communication, problem-solving and people-management skills, including experience of supporting and developing colleagues. You’ll be comfortable working independently, taking ownership of your area of responsibility, while also contributing positively to the wider professional services team. You’ll be someone who takes pride in delivering accurate, high-quality work, improving processes where possible and providing a responsive and professional service to colleagues across the Department. For a full spectrum of duties and responsibilities as well as essential, and desired criteria please refer to the Further Particulars document attached to this advert. If you have any queries regarding the vacancy or the application process, please contact Anna Rutherford ( anna.rutherford@ucl.ac.uk ). Informal enquiries may be addressed to Helen Higgins, email: h.higgins@ucl.ac.uk . We particularly welcome female applicants and those from an ethnic minority, as they are under-represented within UCL at this level. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: UCL Department of Mathematics is committed to developing the careers of all its staff by providing a supportive environment in which they can flourish. We believe that inclusive teams produce the best student experience, and we strive for an environment in which all staff can bring their whole selves to work. The Department actively promotes UCL's work-life balance policy which acknowledges that its staff are most productive when they have achieved a balance that enables them to meet their responsibilities outside work. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48769": {
      "title": "Senior Information Security Officer",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; IT and Digital; London; £56,431-£66,437​; Vice-President (Operations) (B01) Reference: B01-05649 About us: Information Services Division (ISD) is the primary provider of IT services to UCL. We support and enhance learning, teaching, research and administrative processes by providing information- and technology-related services to over 50,000 staff and students of UCL and associated institutions. Our ambition is to be the leading IT services group in the Higher Education (HE) sector and we are growing our team’s capability in experience/UX, agile development, security, cloud, service management and partnering. We are modernising our technology foundations, digitising the processes of the university to transform experience for students and staff, and partnering across the university to drive differentiation in UCL education and research. UCL boasts a track record of ground-breaking research that transforms the lives of people around the world. Our teams provide dedicated, specialist support throughout the research and innovation lifecycle to help sustain and advance UCL’s position as a world-class, research-led university. About the role: Within ISD, this post is in the Information Security Group, which leads on all aspects of security at UCL. The role sits within Security Operations, which is responsible for security monitoring, incident response, vulnerability management and threat intelligence. Working under the direction of the Cyber Threat Management Lead, you will lead day-to-day threat intelligence, threat hunting and detection engineering activity. You will help maintain UCL’s intelligence requirements and threat model, produce actionable intelligence, plan and conduct threat hunts, and identify opportunities to improve detection coverage against priority threats. You will work closely with analysts, technical teams, service providers and stakeholders across UCL to turn intelligence, hunt findings and incident evidence into practical improvements to detections, playbooks, tooling and security controls. You will also provide coaching and direction to colleagues, communicate complex threat information clearly, and support evidence-based decisions about UCL’s defensive posture. About you: As the ideal candidate you will have a mix of the following skills and experience: Significant experience in cyber threat intelligence, threat hunting, detection engineering, SOC operations, incident response support or related operational security work. Strong knowledge of intelligence requirements, threat modelling, intelligence cycles, analytical methods and the production of actionable intelligence. Strong knowledge of threat hunting and detection engineering methods, including hunt planning, telemetry analysis, detection coverage, custom detections and false-positive management. Experience using relevant security tooling and platforms, such as SIEM, TIP, SOAR, CTEM, intelligence collection platforms, reporting platforms and related monitoring or investigation tools. Experience using threat intelligence, hunt findings, incident findings or other operational evidence to improve detections, reporting, playbooks, tooling or controls. The ability to analyse complex or incomplete threat information and support timely, data-led decisions. Experience coordinating intelligence, hunting or detection engineering activity across analysts, technical teams, suppliers and stakeholders. Strong written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to explain threats, detection gaps, hunt findings and recommended actions to technical and non-technical audiences. The ability to organise and prioritise multiple activities, maintain accurate documentation, and ensure actions are clearly assigned, tracked and completed. Experience providing day-to-day leadership, coaching, knowledge sharing or technical direction to colleagues. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48668": {
      "title": "Senior Information Security Officer",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; IT and Digital; London; £56,431-£66,437​; Vice-President (Operations) (B01) Reference: B01-05674 About us: Information Services Division (ISD) is the primary provider of IT services to UCL. We support and enhance learning, teaching, research and administrative processes by providing information- and technology-related services to over 50,000 staff and students of UCL and associated institutions. Our goal is to be the leading IT services group in the HE sector and we are growing our team’s capability in experience/UX, agile development, security, cloud, service management and partnering. We are modernising our technology foundations, digitising the processes of the university to transform experience for students and staff, and partnering across the university to drive differentiation in UCL education and research. Portfolio and Product Delivery manage the delivery of technology led change, partnering with colleagues across UCL. We collaborate as cross functional agile teams and have core capabilities in experience, development & test, analysis and agile delivery management. About the role: Within ISD, this post is in the Chief Information Security Office (CISO) which leads on all aspects of security at UCL. CISO is divided into two teams covering Security Operations and Governance, Risk and Compliance (GRC). You will work closely with stakeholders across UCL to provide expert advice on information security matters, support risk assessments and assurance activities, lead the development and improvement of policies and standards, and promote compliance with UCL's security framework in a pragmatic and solution-oriented manner. The role also includes providing leadership on GRC initiatives, mentoring colleagues, and helping to shape and strengthen UCL's overall security posture. About you: As the ideal candidate you will have a mix of the following skills and experience: Experience operating as part of an Information Security or Governance, Risk and Compliance team, providing advice, support and services to a wide range of stakeholders. Experience leading or contributing to security risk assessments, assurance reviews, audits, compliance activities or security governance initiatives. Strong knowledge of information security governance, risk management and compliance principles, with the ability to apply risk-based thinking to complex situations. Experience developing, reviewing or implementing information security policies, standards, guidance or frameworks. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with experience producing reports and presenting information to both technical and non-technical audiences. Experience building effective relationships with stakeholders at all levels and providing pragmatic, customer-focused security advice. Relevant degree or equivalent professional qualifications and experience. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48820": {
      "title": "Senior OD Consultant (Employee Experience & Transformation)",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; HR and Wellbeing; London; £68,284-£78,386​; People and Culture & PS Hub (B30) Reference: B30-00257 About us: UCL’s Organisational Development team supports more than 19,000 colleagues across academic, research, teaching and professional services roles. As a centre of expertise within People & Culture, we ensure that UCL has the learning, talent and organisational structures needed to deliver its strategy and People Plan. The Organisational Capability & Transformation team shapes the strategic capabilities UCL needs to achieve its academic mission and long-term ambitions. We bring together expertise in workforce capability and planning, employee experience and engagement, talent management, digital transformation and organisational change. Working collaboratively across People & Culture, Information Services, Faculties and Professional Services, we deliver institution-wide initiatives that strengthen organisational performance, improve colleague experience and build future workforce capability. UCL is deeply committed to equality, diversity and inclusion. Creating equity of opportunity is central to our approach to talent, learning, leadership development and organisational change. About the role: As Senior OD Consultant (Employee Experience & Transformation), you will lead the development of evidence-based approaches to employee experience, engagement and colleague voice across UCL. You will take strategic ownership of UCL’s Staff Experience Survey and wider employee-listening activities, translating colleague feedback and organisational data into clear insights and practical recommendations. You will develop a coherent listening strategy using approaches such as pulse surveys, focus groups and employee panels, helping UCL build a culture of continuous listening, learning and improvement. Working closely with senior leaders, People & Culture colleagues and transformation teams, you will provide expert organisational development and change consultancy across major university-wide programmes. You will lead people and organisational change workstreams, assessing change readiness, identifying risks and developing strategies that support successful adoption and sustainable impact. You will also help strengthen UCL’s Employee Value Proposition and improve the colleague experience across key stages of the employee lifecycle, including onboarding, career development, performance, learning, leadership and organisational change. A further focus of the role will be providing strategic leadership for the employee experience roadmap within UCL’s Thrive digital ecosystem. You will work with digital and product teams to translate employee insight into development priorities and explore how emerging technologies, including AI-enabled solutions, could enhance employee experience and workforce capability. As a trusted adviser, you will use evidence, organisational insight and specialist OD expertise to challenge constructively, influence decisions and shape strategic priorities across UCL. About you: You will be an experienced organisational development, employee experience or change professional with a strong record of delivering complex, organisation-wide initiatives. You will have expertise in employee engagement and listening, using qualitative and quantitative insight to develop clear, practical recommendations. An effective programme and stakeholder manager, you will be confident advising and influencing senior leaders, building relationships across multidisciplinary teams and supporting sustainable organisational change. You will also bring a strong commitment to inclusion, colleague voice and equity of opportunity, alongside an interest in how digital solutions and emerging technologies can improve employee experience and organisational capability. What we offer: As well as exciting opportunities, UCL offers a range of great benefits. Find out more: UCL Rewards and Benefits . 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women.” Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48636": {
      "title": "Senior Optical Instrumentation Development Technician",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Laboratory Technical and Lab Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07800 About us: The Biomedical Optics Research Laboratory (BORL) within the Department of Medical Physics and Biomedical Engineering at University College London (UCL) is one of the world's leading centres for the development and clinical translation of optical technologies for monitoring and imaging human physiology and metabolism. The laboratory brings together physicists, engineers, neuroscientists and clinicians to develop innovative solutions that address major biomedical and healthcare challenges. The post will be based within Professor Ilias Tachtsidis' research programme, where he leads the MultiModal Spectroscopy Group and the MetaboLight Team, and is a senior member of BORL. Professor Tachtsidis is internationally recognised for pioneering broadband near-infrared spectroscopy (bNIRS), a unique optical technology capable of non-invasively quantifying tissue oxygenation, haemodynamics and mitochondrial metabolism through measurement of the oxidation state of cytochrome-c-oxidase. His multidisciplinary research spans optical technology development, neuroscience, computational modelling and clinical translation, with applications ranging from neonatal brain health and neurodevelopmental disorders to acute brain injury and neurological disease. About the role: Applications are invited for a Senior Optical Instrumentation Development Technician to join the Department of Medical Physics and Biomedical Engineering at University College London (UCL). The successful candidate will join the internationally renowned Biomedical Optics Research Laboratory (BORL) and work within the programme led by Professor Ilias Tachtsidis, who leads the MultiModal Spectroscopy Group and the MetaboLight Team and is a senior member of BORL. Professor Tachtsidis' work is internationally recognised for pioneering broadband near-infrared spectroscopy (bNIRS), a unique technology capable of non-invasively monitoring tissue oxygenation, haemodynamics and mitochondrial metabolism. The position is funded through the newly established Medical Research Council (MRC) Centre of Research Excellence for Human Neural Development, a landmark partnership between King's College London, University College London and the University of Manchester. The Centre aims to transform our understanding of early human brain development through the integration of advanced imaging technologies, experimental neuroscience, artificial intelligence and clinical research. This exciting technical role will focus on the development, integration, testing and deployment of next-generation optical neuroimaging technologies designed to characterise early brain health across multiple biological scales, from vascular function and oxygen delivery to cellular metabolism and mitochondrial activity. The successful candidate will contribute to the development of advanced hyperspectral near-infrared spectroscopy (NIRS) systems and associated instrumentation platforms for laboratory and clinical applications. The role will involve substantial hands-on work in electronic circuit and PCB design, software development for instrument control and data acquisition, optical system integration, rapid prototyping, 3D printing, testing, calibration and technical documentation. The post holder will work closely with engineers, researchers and clinicians to develop robust and reliable instrumentation suitable for deployment in research and clinical environments, including collaborations with King's College Hospital, University College London Hospitals NHS Foundation Trust and Manchester University NHS Foundation Trust. In addition to supporting the Centre's instrumentation activities, the post holder will provide technical support for undergraduate and postgraduate teaching laboratories within the Department of Medical Physics and Biomedical Engineering, including Biomedical Engineering and Medical Physics programmes. This post offers a unique opportunity to contribute to world-leading biomedical technology development within one of the UK's foremost biomedical optics groups. About you: This post offers a unique opportunity to contribute to world-leading biomedical technology development within one of the UK's foremost biomedical optics groups. This post offers a unique opportunity to contribute to world-leading biomedical technology development within one of the UK's foremost biomedical optics groups. The appointment will be offered on a fixed-term basis for seven years in the first instance, commencing on 1 October 2026, with the expectation that the role will transition into a permanent technical position within the Department, subject to satisfactory performance and departmental requirements. Key Requirements: Essential requirements include: Experience in the design, development and testing of electronic and instrumentation systems. Experience in electronic circuit and PCB design, prototyping and troubleshooting. Experience in software development for instrumentation control, automation and data acquisition. Experience in system integration involving electronic, optical and mechanical components. Experience using CAD software and rapid prototyping methods, including 3D printing. Experience using laboratory electronic test and measurement equipment. Knowledge of electrical safety testing and good engineering practice. Strong technical problem-solving skills and the ability to prepare technical documentation and reports. Ability to work effectively within multidisciplinary technical and scientific teams. Experience in biomedical optics, medical devices, clinical technologies, and knowledge of medical device, electrical safety or laser/light safety standards would be advantageous. For informal enquiries, please contact Professor Ilias Tachtsidis, Department of Medical Physics and Biomedical Engineering, UCL (i.tachtsidis@ucl.ac.uk). What we offer: Standard UCL Benefits: 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . Find out about the UCL Ways of Working : www.ucl.ac.uk/ways-of-working . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Bronxze award in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48569": {
      "title": "Senior Post Graduate Teaching Assistants",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; London; See advert text; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07895 About us: This role is only available for current UCL-affiliated PhD students. The Department of Electronic and Electrical Engineering (EEE) at UCL was established by Professor Sir Ambrose Fleming, inventor of the thermionic valve and hence the founder of the discipline of electronics, in 1885 as the first department of electrical technology in England. That same pioneering tradition of innovation and excellence continues to underpin our work. We push in new directions across research, education and entrepreneurship, building on our long-established links with industry and benefitting from our loyal and supportive alumni community. We are committed to develop individuals and careers, across staff and students, developing and supporting our subject discipline. About the role: We are looking to appoint no more than 10 Senior Postgraduate Teaching Assistants (SPGTAs) in the department. In addition to normal PGTA activities, SPGTAs will also be able to assist with PGTA coordination and training and potentially take on more independent roles in teaching and learning support. The post holder will be invited to participate in improving PGTA experience in the department and help with the formation of a community of practice amongst departmental PGTAs to promote best practice. SPGTA appointment will be at grade 6, starting on spine point 25. About you: A suitable candidate should have already been appointed as a PGTA in the department for at least two academic years (candidates with less than 2 years of departmental PGTA experience may be exceptionally accepted provided that they have extensive prior relevant experience) and they should have experience with multiple aspects of teaching and learning support with demonstrated commitment to excellence in these activities. A suitable candidate should also be able to demonstrate in their application that they have adopted an evidence-based and reflective approach to supporting teaching and learning. Application details: Please upload the following with your application: A CV highlighting your teaching and learning support experience and the area of expertise A personal statement no longer than 400 words in which you should expand on your knowledge, approach and reflections in supporting teaching and learning in higher education A letter of support from an activity lead in the department you have assisted as a PGTA The deadline for receiving applications is 28 October 2026 at 23:59 . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48919": {
      "title": "Senior Programme Officer",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Strategy, Project and Change Management; London; £43,981-£54,019​; Vice-Provost (Research, Innovation and Global Engagement) (B09) Reference: B09-01428 About us: UCL is a world-leading research university with a strong commitment to using its collective expertise to address global challenges and create positive impact locally, nationally and internationally. UCL Grand Challenges is a flagship programme bringing together expertise from across the university to stimulate cross-disciplinary research, education, innovation and knowledge exchange. Its themes include Climate Crisis, Mental Health & Wellbeing, Data-Empowered Societies, Inequalities and Intercultural Communication, supported by significant investment across their five-year lifespan. Based within the Office of the Vice-Provost (Research, Innovation & Global Engagement), the Grand Challenges team works across UCL to turn these ambitions into practical programmes, funding opportunities and collaborative activity. About the role: We are seeking a Senior Programme Officer to join the UCL Grand Challenges Programme Management Office (PMO). Please note this is a fixed-term internal only secondment position until 31st December 2028. Working closely with the Head of Programmes & Operations and colleagues across Grand Challenges, RIGE and the wider university, the role will help translate strategic priorities into effective and consistent ways of working across the programme. The postholder will coordinate a broad range of programme and operational activity, including planning, governance, financial monitoring, risk management, funding administration, data and reporting, and the development of processes and Standard Operating Procedures. There will be a strong focus on continuous improvement, identifying opportunities to make systems and processes more efficient and user-focused. The role will also support the effective operation and development of Grand Challenges’ grant management system, Flexi-Grant, and produce management information, reports and dashboards using tools including Power BI. The postholder will line manage the Programme Administrator and work with a wide range of stakeholders across UCL. About you: You will have experience of developing processes and delivering projects or funding schemes within higher education or another similarly complex organisation, alongside a good understanding of project, programme or portfolio management. Strong organisational, analytical and stakeholder management skills are essential, with the ability to manage competing priorities and work confidently across different teams and activities. Experience supporting the implementation, adoption or improvement of technology systems and identifying practical improvements to processes and ways of working will also be important. You will bring strong data and digital skills, including demonstrable proficiency in Power BI and experience of workflow and grant management systems such as Asana and Flexi-Grant, or equivalent platforms. Financial acumen, including experience of budget monitoring, forecasting and expenditure tracking, is also required. An effective communicator and collaborative colleague, you will be comfortable developing clear guidance, reports and training materials for different audiences, building positive relationships with stakeholders and working independently while knowing when to seek advice and input. What we offer: 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48297": {
      "title": "Senior Project Manager",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Estates, Facilities, Accommodation and Safety; London; £68,284-£76,244; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00303 About us: The UCL Estate comprises over 240 buildings, more than 6.5 million square feet of academic accommodation and around 4500 owned student bedrooms. The location, scale, variety and complexity of the estate are unparalleled in the Higher Education sector, including world-leading research, teaching, offices and student accommodation within our Bloomsbury and UCL East campus, at Stratford. Together, Campus Experience and Infrastructure and Estates Development (CE&I and ED) is responsible for managing all aspects of the UCL Estate from the strategic to the operational, including property asset management, master-planning, capital projects and seamless end to end operational support. The function comprises approximately 350 FM and property professionals, project managers and customer facing operational colleagues. From providing student accommodation, security and catering, to delivering an estates masterplan and all forms of capital projects in a sustainable manner. We are a collective of technical professionals who work together to deliver a resilient, functional, and vibrant estate. About the role: Lead the transformation of one of London's most complex and historic estates. We are seeking an inspirational Senior Project Manager to lead a diverse portfolio of projects across UCL's Bloomsbury Capital Programme. Covering projects from £2m to £100m+, the portfolio includes major redevelopments, new-build projects, adaptation and reuse of historic and listed buildings, world-class research and teaching facilities, commercial developments and strategic estate investments. The role offers exposure to projects across Bloomsbury, UCL East at Stratford and other strategic locations supporting UCL's long-term ambitions. This is an opportunity to shape the places that support world-leading education, research and innovation whilst helping deliver one of the most ambitious and long-term capital investment programmes in the Higher Education sector. Working across a highly operational estate, you will lead projects that leave a lasting legacy for generations of students, researchers, staff and partners. The role combines strategic leadership, technical project delivery and team development. You will lead multidisciplinary project teams through all stages of the project lifecycle, bringing together stakeholders, consultants, contractors and specialist advisers to deliver successful outcomes in challenging and demanding environments. You will be responsible for managing multiple projects simultaneously, balancing competing priorities whilst maintaining a relentless focus on safety, quality, value, programme and user experience. You will work closely with senior leaders, academic stakeholders and professional services colleagues, translating technical information into clear recommendations and guiding projects through governance and approval processes. Success in this role will require an individual who is as comfortable challenging design assumptions and identifying better solutions as they are leading on-site construction activity. You will provide visible leadership throughout the project lifecycle, helping identify risks and opportunities early and ensuring that issues are resolved before they become barriers to delivery. Alongside project delivery, you will play an important role in developing capability within the wider team. You will help create an environment where project professionals can thrive, learn from experience and work collaboratively to deliver outcomes that exceed expectations. About you: Things you'll need in order to do this job include: Significant experience delivering major projects within occupied and operational environments where business continuity, user engagement and stakeholder management are critical to success. A strong track record of successfully delivering projects through design, procurement, construction and handover. Experience delivering projects through RIBA stages and equivalent project delivery frameworks. Demonstrable experience leading multidisciplinary project teams and managing external consultant and contractor organisations. The ability to lead confidently in complex and sometimes ambiguous situations, maintaining momentum and delivering at pace in a demanding environment. Strong construction and project delivery knowledge, with the ability to identify emerging risks and opportunities early and take decisive action before issues impact successful delivery. Excellent communication and influencing skills, with the confidence to engage senior stakeholders, governance groups and project teams. Experience balancing strategic objectives with practical delivery challenges. A collaborative and inclusive leadership style that inspires those around you, builds capability and brings out the best in project teams, consultants and contractors. A proactive, solution-focused mindset with the resilience, energy and drive to thrive in a fast-paced delivery environment. Strong commercial awareness, including experience administering major construction contracts such as NEC and JCT. Experience managing substantial project budgets and providing robust financial forecasting and reporting. Working knowledge of statutory and regulatory requirements including planning, building regulations, Building Safety legislation and CDM responsibilities. A passion for creating environments that support education, research and innovation. Relevant professional qualification or experiential equivalent is essential. The ability to work on-site in London at least 4 days per week. The following qualifications and experience, although not essential, would be highly advantageous: Experience delivering projects valued at £20m+. Experience of delivering teaching laboratories, research laboratories and other similar highly serviced functional spaces. Experience working within complex urban environments, occupied estates or major institutional portfolios. Experience managing projects with complex stakeholder groups and demanding operational requirements. Experience delivering projects within higher education, healthcare, science, cultural, commercial or public sector environments. Chartered status through a recognised professional body (for example CEng, ChPP, MCIOB, MRICS or equivalent). Please review the JD, paying particular attention to the Essential and Desirable criteria, and ensure these are addressed in your application. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48861": {
      "title": "Senior Recruiter - (APPLY IF YOU ARE A UCL INTERNAL & ELIGIBLE CANDIDATE ONLY)",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; HR and Wellbeing; London; £56,431-£66,437​; People and Culture & PS Hub (B30) Reference: B30-00264 About us: UCL’s People & Culture team supports the university in attracting, recruiting and retaining the people who make its teaching, research and professional services possible. Our Talent Acquisition team works with hiring managers across UCL to deliver effective recruitment and a positive experience for candidates. About the role: We are seeking a Senior Recruiter to join our Talent Acquisition team. Reporting to the Talent Acquisition Manager, you will work with hiring managers to understand their recruitment needs, advise on approaches to attracting candidates and support campaigns from planning through to appointment. You will help ensure recruitment activity is well coordinated, inclusive and responsive to the needs of UCL’s varied teams. This is a Grade 8 role with hybrid working. About you: You will bring recruitment experience, strong stakeholder management skills and the ability to manage several priorities at once. You will be comfortable advising hiring managers, communicating clearly with candidates and colleagues, and using sound judgement to resolve problems as they arise. A collaborative approach, attention to detail and a commitment to delivering a positive recruitment experience will help you succeed. What we offer: As well as exciting opportunities, UCL offers a range of great benefits. Find out more: UCL Rewards and Benefits . 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women.” Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=47591": {
      "title": "Senior Research Fellow (Cellular Myologist)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £56,431-£66,437​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11064 About us: The Department of Neurodegenerative Disease is internationally recognised for research into the mechanisms underlying neurodegenerative diseases and dementia, spanning genetic, molecular, cellular and neuropathological processes. Based within the department, the Genetic Therapy Accelerator Centre (GTAC) brings together laboratory and clinical expertise in neuromuscular and neurological disorders, with a focus on developing novel RNA and AAV-based therapies. The Centre fosters cross-institutional collaborations and supports researchers through access to specialist expertise, cellular models, UCL facilities and industry partners. About the role: We are seeking a highly motivated Senior Research Fellow (Cellular Myologist) to join the GTAC Laboratory under the direction of Professor Francesco Muntoni, working across the UCL Queen Square Institute of Neurology and UCL Great Ormond Street Institute of Child Health. You will develop and lead your own research programme in neuromuscular biology, working across GTAC and the Dubowitz Neuromuscular Centre (DNC). You will independently design and conduct research, contribute to collaborative projects, secure research funding and present findings at seminars and conferences. You will also contribute specialist expertise to multidisciplinary projects across neuroscience, support the day-to-day running of the Centre and supervise students and research staff. The role is available from 01 September 2026 and is funded until 01 December 2029 in the first instance. If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online, or have any queries regarding the application process, please contact the Institute of Neurology HR Team ( ion.hradmin@ucl.ac.uk ). Informal enquiries regarding the role can be addressed to Professor Francesco Muntoni ( f.muntoni@ucl.ac.uk ). A full job description and person specification for this role can be accessed below. To apply, please upload a current CV, complete the online application form, and use the supporting statement section or upload a cover letter to outline how you meet the essential and desirable criteria for the role. Please do not upload any additional attachments as these will not be considered by the selection panel. About you: You will have a PhD in a relevant discipline and significant postdoctoral experience in cell and molecular biology, with expertise in neuromuscular translational research and skeletal muscle biology, and strong experience in the effective management of research team. Specialist knowledge of relevant research methodologies, including in vivo and in vitro models, gene expression manipulation (e.g. siRNA, CRISPRi/a, splicing modulation, AAV and lentiviral vectors) and cell-based assays (e.g. fluorescent reporters and immunocytochemistry), is essential. Experience with animal models and/or genetic therapies, including AAV, RNA or cell therapy, is also required. You will be able to manage independent experimental research effectively, write grant applications to support your research programme, work flexibly under pressure, meet deadlines and maintain excellent attention to detail. This role meets the eligibility requirements for a skilled worker certificate of sponsorship or a global talent visa under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore, UCL welcomes applications from international applicants who require a visa. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits, some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan On-site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible, and job share basis wherever possible. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: The Institute prides itself on operating in an all-inclusive environment irrespective of personal, physical, or social characteristics. Teamwork is highly valued, individual strengths are recognised and celebrated, and we are committed to advancing the careers of everyone. 12% of Institute staff are actively working on Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) initiatives; visit our EDI website for more information about our initiatives and priorities. The Institute also holds an Athena SWAN Gold award , in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality. As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce; these include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, LGBTQI+ and gender diverse people in all roles, and women in Grade 9 and 10 roles."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49010": {
      "title": "Senior Research Fellow in Climate Change and Health",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £56,431-£66,437​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11191 About us: The UCL Institute for Global Health (IGH) is at the heart of UCL’s Grand Challenge of Global Health. The Institute’s vision is for a world where international policy on global health is informed by world-class research. IGH aims to support the development of robust solutions to aspects of the world’s major global health challenges through scholarly outputs, education, public engagement, translational research, and ultimately by influencing public policy and professional practice. The Lancet Countdown: tracking progress on health and climate change, is an independent, international research collaboration, that works to track the evolving links between health and climate change globally, regionally and nationally, in partnership with The Lancet and the World Health Organization. Its aim is to inform a robust response to climate change that protects and promotes human health. We are looking for an experienced Senior Research Fellow in Climate Change and Health to join our team. About the role: The successful candidate will be responsible for leading the coordination and timely delivery of the health research and academic initiatives of the Lancet Countdown, including the collation of health and epidemiological evidence for the annual Global Lancet Countdown Report, Regional Reports, special issues, and additional academic and research outputs. This role is designed to provide specialist expertise on public health, global health and/or epidemiology to advance the Lancet Countdown’s academic work, and ensure the Lancet Countdown achieve the high standard of academic rigour and science. They will also support the development of research to quantify the effects of climate on health or the possibilities and health opportunities of climate change mitigation. The postholder will be an expert in climate change and global health and/or epidemiology, with strong quantitative skills and the ability to apply this expertise across climate change and health research, including development and evaluation of indicators of the effects of climate change and policies to address it on public health, and use of findings to inform real-world impact. Their public or global health disciplinary expertise will be essential to ensuring critical appraisal and interpretation of evidence as well as the integration of public and global health principles across all Lancet Countdown outputs. Their leadership and network understanding will be critical in the integration of expertise from across the climate and health fields. The postholder will also be encouraged to develop and expand a research programme at the intersection of climate change and health, in support of the overall Lancet Countdown aims and their personal academic career development. This is a full-time role (i.e. 1.0 FTE, 36.5 hours per week) and it is available until 30 April 2029 in the first instance. Please note that the successful candidate must be living in the UK or willing to relocate to the UK for the duration of the contract. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. Salary range: £56,431 - £66,437 per annum (including London Allowance), depending on experience. If you have any queries about the role, please contact Clare Scully (Head of Operations) on c.scully@ucl.ac.uk . If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact the IGH HR Team on IGH.HR@ucl.ac.uk . About you: It is essential that the successful candidate has a PhD in the intersection of climate change, and global health, epidemiology, or a closely related discipline. They will also have: Postdoctoral experience in the intersection of climate change, and global health, epidemiology, or a closely related discipline Demonstrated experience in quantitative analysis, including the development of models and/or advanced data analysis in climate change and global health, ideally with expertise in epidemiological modelling Excellent technical knowledge of climate change – health intersection Experience managing and working with multidisciplinary academic teams, and participating in cross-disciplinary and international academic projects Experience in leading teams through complex programmes of work, delivering high-quality outputs against tight deadlines Track record of securing research grant income and to developing successful grant applications Track record of academic publications including some as first and/or senior author positions Strong analytical and critical thinking skills What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49069": {
      "title": "Senior School Administrator",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07868 About us: We are The Bartlett, UCL’s global faculty of the built environment. Individually, our schools and sections lead their fields. In partnership, they develop new responses to pressing world issues. As a whole, they represent a world-leading, multidisciplinary faculty, united by the radical spirit of UCL. Our vision is of a world where everything that’s built aims to add to the well-being of people and the environment. The Bartlett is unrivalled in its breadth and depth of disciplines, programmes, and departments. It is made up 12 Schools, Centres, and Institutes, providing teaching and research in a wide range of subject areas to over 4,000 students each year from across the world. Further information about The School can be found online. About the role: University College London (UCL) wishes to appoint a Senior School Administrator. The post holder will be based at The Bartlett School of Sustainable Construction at the Bartlett Faculty of the Built Environment, a world-leading faculty ranked #1 in the world for Built Environment studies and #1 in the UK for its research environment. The post holder will carry out administrative and operational duties within the Bartlett School of Sustainable Construction, consistent with the vision and mission of The School. This is an open-ended, full-time position. About you: The appointee is required to be educated to A-Level or have experience in a similar role, be articulate and able to flex their communication style appropriately for different audiences. The successful applicant will be expected to apply excellent administrative skills and a highly proactive approach to providing a comprehensive administrative service. The applicant will possess strong interpersonal and problem-solving skills including the ability to build successful relationships and collaborate, contributing towards maintaining a positive work environment. The successful candidate will be able to work harmoniously with staff and students of all cultures and backgrounds and possess excellent stakeholder liaison and/or public engagement skills. They will have experience of managing, supervising, or coordinating the work of a team as well as providing excellent support service to a wide range of tasks. Your application will be assessed based on how well the criteria set out in the person specification are met using examples to demonstrate this. Please read the Candidate Guidance document below. A cover letter is required which addresses the criteria in the person specification. For further details including a job description and person specification, please see the attachments towards the bottom of this webpage What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/pay-benefits/staff-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/ We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible and job share basis wherever possible."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49055": {
      "title": "Service Improvement Officer",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; Vice-Provost (Research, Innovation and Global Engagement) (B09) Reference: B09-01408 About us: UCL is a world-leading, research-intensive university, consistently ranked among the top ten in the world, with an annual turnover of well over £1 billion. Our research and innovation continue to grow in scale and complexity, with record levels of research income and impact achieved last year. You'll join Research and Innovation Services (RIS) — the team that supports, advises and provides expertise to UCL's academic community across our extensive and varied portfolio of externally funded projects. RIS brings together four teams: Award Services, Contract Services, Planning, Insight & Improvement, and Compliance and Assurance. Within RIS, Planning, Insight & Improvement leads on short- and long-term business planning, the improvement of end-to-end processes and operational activity, and the stewardship and analysis of research administrative data — all to make research and innovation support better across the university. You'll be joining RIS at a genuinely exciting time. UCL has just launched its five-year strategy, in which RIS plays a key role, and we're investing in a number of strategic programmes across the directorate — bringing in new capacity, expertise and development opportunities that will help shape UCL's research environment and impact for years to come. About the role: Please note this role is currently available until October 2027 in the first instance and is available as a secondment opportunity. This is a great opportunity for a project management professional who enjoys getting close to the detail of how services actually work — and making them work better. You'll use your continuous improvement and business change expertise across a varied portfolio of projects, helping Research and Innovation Services keep pace with UCL's evolving needs. You'll work flexibly across multiple projects at once, and you'll have real scope to identify opportunities for improvement and see your solutions through to delivery. What you'll be doing Build relationships with colleagues across Research and Innovation Services and in academic departments, gathering feedback and spotting opportunities to improve services, processes, systems, policies and data. Log and help prioritise improvements to research and innovation services, processes, systems, policies and data. Lead and deliver small-scale projects that increase the value of the services RIS provides. Support the delivery of RIS workstreams, making sure discovery phases gather enough evidence and data to enable strong service design. Build your continuous improvement toolkit — defining requirements, driving process change, facilitating workshops and carrying out process mapping — with support along the way. Track and communicate the impact of improvements you help deliver. Understand stakeholder needs fully when designing and implementing improvements, including any dependencies or wider impacts. Work with other UCL divisions — including Change & Improvement, the Information Services Division, the Office of the Vice-President (Strategy) and the Research and Innovation Operations change portfolio — to deliver joined-up business change. Stay closely connected to the Planning, Insight and Improvement project portfolio, feeding into wider RIS discussions on service improvement. Identify when external resource or expertise is needed, and work effectively with external suppliers. Build strong working knowledge of UCL's research management systems (Worktribe, MyFinance, Data and Insight Service) and their related processes. Support the development and delivery of UCL's research management systems and interfaces, including aspects of testing on Worktribe Test systems. Develop and deliver training on research management systems and processes, and keep training materials fresh and up to date. Build a network of contacts at other institutions and research funders to keep on top of sector About you: You're a natural problem-solver who enjoys untangling complex processes and finding practical, workable solutions. You'll be comfortable managing multiple projects at once, building relationships with a wide range of stakeholders, and communicating clearly about the changes you're driving. You'll thrive in this role if you can bring: Experience of delivering process or service improvement activity in a previous role. Experience of developing and implementing end-to-end business processes, and understanding their impact on other service functions. Experience of capturing and developing business requirements, with the ability to prioritise effectively. Experience of using data to identify and analyse options for service improvement. Experience of working in a project and/or agile environment, with a good understanding of fundamental project management principles. A proven ability to analyse complex, challenging issues and develop innovative, creative solutions. Good written and oral communication skills, including presentation and report writing. An excellent attitude to customer service. Experience within a Higher Education research services or administration function is a bonus, as is experience advancing research management systems in a complex organisation, or familiarity with tools like Microsoft Project — though none of these are essential. Above all, we're looking for someone who's committed to providing a responsive, helpful service, who makes best practice a genuine priority, and who's happy documenting and sharing solutions so others can build on them too. What we offer: Hybrid working, with the flexibility to work remotely for up to 80% of the week if that suits you. 27 days' annual leave plus 6 university closure days and statutory bank holidays, with the option to purchase up to 5 additional days each year. Membership of one of the UK's leading pension schemes (USS or SAUL, depending on your role). A genuinely exciting moment to join RIS, with new investment, strategic programmes and development opportunities opening up across the directorate. Access to UCL's wider staff benefits, including season ticket loans, cycle-to-work schemes, staff discounts, wellbeing support and a strong culture of learning and development. The chance to shape how one of the world's leading research universities supports its academic community, working across a genuinely varied project portfolio. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London's Global University, we know that diversity fuels creativity and innovation — and we want our community to reflect the full diversity of the world's talent. We're committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where everyone truly belongs. We particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL's workforce, and we're happy to discuss any reasonable adjustments or flexible working arrangements you may need throughout the recruitment process and beyond."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48138": {
      "title": "SOFAIR Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07835 About us: UCL’s Department of Computer Science (CS) is a top-ranked Computer Science Department in the UK. In the 2021 Research Excellence Framework (REF) evaluation, UCL Computer Science was ranked second in the UK for research power and first in England. This 12-month fellowship with the possibility of a multi-year extension unites interdisciplinary academic expertise to pursue fundamentally new foundation models, moving beyond incremental refinement of existing models. The fellow will help advance open, distributed, and trustworthy AI systems, positioning the UK at the centre of global efforts to shape the future of open AI. Ideally the fellow will be interested in innovation and spinouts. SOFAIR - Science of Fundamental AI Research Lab. SOFAIR is a consortium of leading academic researchers from across the UK, with partners Oxford, Cambridge and Edinburgh Universities, and led by UCL. The SOFAIR Lab will pursue fundamentally new AI architectures, moving beyond incremental refinement of existing models. By advancing open, distributed, and trustworthy AI systems, SOFAIR will extend the UK‑LLM initiative, stimulate innovation and spin‑outs, and position the UK at the centre of global efforts to shape the future of open AI. Research Fellow: Postholders will undertake internationally excellent research as members of SOFAIR's interdisciplinary research programme, developing and testing original approaches to fundamental AI and contributing to the Lab's collective research objectives. Senior Research Fellow: In addition to the responsibilities of a Research Fellow, postholders appointed at Senior Research Fellow level will be expected to demonstrate substantial research independence and provide intellectual and strategic leadership. They will initiate and lead research programmes, shape SOFAIR's scientific direction, develop collaborations and funding opportunities, mentor researchers, manage significant research resources, and contribute to the Lab's governance and organisational development. The UCL Centre for Artificial Intelligence hosts the EPSRC AI Hub in Generative Models as well as the UKRI Centre for Doctoral Training (CDT) in Foundational Artificial Intelligence. UCL Department of Computer Science is one of the top research and teaching departments in the UK and has world-class standing. The department offers undergraduate and postgraduate programmes and has an extensive research portfolio across a wealth of areas, from information security to complex systems. See http://www.cs.ucl.ac.uk University College London (UCL) is a multi-faculty college of the University of London with a population of over 40,000 students, from more than 130 different countries. Degree programmes are provided in Arts and Humanities, Social and Historical Sciences, Architecture, Building, Environmental Design and Planning, Laws, Life Sciences and Clinical Sciences (including Medicine), Mathematical and Physical Sciences, and Engineering Sciences. For more information see www.ucl.ac.uk This post is advertised at UCL Grade 7 to Grade 10 dependent on relevant experience Grade 7 - Research fellow salary scale (£43,981-£54,019) inclusive of London Allowance, Grade 8- Senior Research Fellow salary scale (£ 56,431-£62,903) inclusive of London Allowance, Grade 9 - (£68,284- £76,166) inclusive of London Allowance, Grade 10 starting at £80,594 inclusive of London Allowance Funding for this post is available until 15th December 2027 in the first instance. About the role: The role requires the post holder to research non-incremental advances in next generation foundation models. An imperative is that all ideas must be tested at scale to confirm potential utility. The postholder will work as part of an interdisciplinary team to research and implement at significant scale next generation foundation models, reporting to the team on progress and contributing to decisions on the most promising research directions. The postholder must therefore be capable of world-leading research and contribute to the team’s effort to push the frontier forward. Applicants are expected to be familiar with principled approaches to machine learning and current frontier frameworks and have a strong background in the mathematics of machine learning, optimisation and coding (PyTorch). As a member of a newly established national research lab, the postholder will also contribute to SOFAIR’s research culture, cross-institutional collaboration, mentoring, and the development of its research and operational structures. About you: The successful candidate will be expected to conduct research of the highest international standard, working collaboratively with researchers across machine learning, statistics, computer science and related disciplines. The postholder will contribute to the development of innovative theoretical and practical approaches to foundation models, disseminate findings through leading conferences and journals, and help foster an open, collaborative and inclusive research environment. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. To apply for the vacancy please click on the ‘Apply Now’ button below. If you have any queries regarding the vacancy or the application process, please contact David Barber david.barber@ucl.ac.uk What we offer: The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/policies-advice/ways-working to find out more. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know that diversity and inclusion are fundamental drivers of creativity, innovation, and excellence, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world's talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong, thrive, and reach our full potential. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian, and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women’. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Gold award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity, and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/ ."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48896": {
      "title": "Specialist Study Skills Tutor (12-Month Fixed Term Contract)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Student Well-being and Registry; London; £43,981-£54,019​; Vice-President (Operations) (B01) Reference: B01-05767 About us: UCL Student Support and Wellbeing Services provides information, advice and welfare support to our diverse community of students, helping them to thrive and succeed throughout their time at UCL. Within the service, our Disability, Mental Health & Neurodiversity Support Services team provides specialist advice and support to enable students who identify as disabled to fully engage with their studies and make the most of their UCL experience. We are committed to inclusive practice and to removing barriers to learning, recognising the individual needs, experiences and perspectives of our students. About the role: As a Specialist Study Skills Tutor, you will provide specialist study skills support to students with specific learning differences and/or neurodivergence, helping them to develop strategies that enable them to engage confidently and effectively with their learning. You will deliver tailored one-to-one and group sessions, using information from diagnostic assessments, Educational Psychologist reports and other relevant professional reports to understand each student's needs and develop individual learning plans. You will work collaboratively with students to identify and overcome study-related barriers, monitor learning outcomes and evaluate the effectiveness of your support. You will also contribute to the wider Specific Learning Differences provision at UCL, including developing resources, delivering workshops and supporting the team to meet relevant Department for Education requirements and quality standards. You will maintain accurate student records and tutorial documentation, work closely with colleagues across Disability, Mental Health & Neurodiversity Support Services and contribute to the ongoing development of the service. This is a varied role where you will combine specialist knowledge with a student-centred approach, using your expertise to help create an inclusive learning environment for students across UCL. Please note, this role is offered on a fixed-term contract basis for 12-months in the first instance. This is an internal recruitment advert, available to eligible UCL workers. Hybrid working is available with currently a minimum of 2-days per week onsite (subject to change). If you have any queries about the role, please contact Lucy Smith at: lucy.smith@ucl.ac.uk Job Ref: B01-05767 Closing Date: 20-10-2026 – 23:59 (UK time) Interviews: Interviews are likely to place around 30 th October. About you: You will have a Level 5 or above qualification (or equivalent) from a recognised provider or professional training course relevant to specific learning differences/difficulties, together with membership of a relevant professional body such as PASSHE, Patoss or the Dyslexia Guild. You will have sound knowledge of specific learning differences and their manifestations within a Higher Education setting, alongside experience of delivering individual tuition and monitoring and evaluating learning outcomes. You will be confident interpreting diagnostic reports and other relevant assessments and using this information to design and deliver tailored interventions. You will have strong knowledge of study skills strategies appropriate to Higher Education and experience of using assistive technology and specialist software to support students with specific learning differences. You will be able to adapt your approach to work effectively with students with a wide range of disabilities and understand barriers from the student's perspective. You will be an excellent communicator, with the ability to build positive relationships, deliver presentations and workshops to a range of audiences, and work collaboratively with colleagues. You will be organised and able to manage your own workload, maintaining accurate records and meeting deadlines. Most importantly, you will demonstrate a commitment to inclusive practice and the social model of disability, with the patience, empathy and active listening skills needed to understand individual student needs and tailor your approach accordingly. What we offer: Standard UCL Benefits 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48406": {
      "title": "Student recruitment and Admissions Administrator",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; External Engagement, Communications, Marketing and Events; London; £36,433-£42,891​; UCL SLASH (B03) Reference: B03-03276 About us: UCL is London’s Global University – a world-leading institution committed to excellence, innovation, and impact. The Faculty of Social and Historical Sciences (SHS) brings together eight departments and the Institute of Advanced Studies, where humanities and social sciences intersect to tackle global challenges. With over 6,000 students and 1,000 staff, SHS is a vibrant and diverse academic community. This role sits within the faculty’s newly established Marketing, Communications and Student Recruitment team , which plays a vital role in shaping the faculty’s identity, enhancing its visibility, and attracting a diverse and talented student body. About the role: We are seeking to appoint a Student Recruitment and Admissions Administrator to join the Faculty Student Recruitment team. This role plays a key part in delivering an effective student recruitment and admissions service across the Faculty’s portfolio of undergraduate and postgraduate programmes, providing flexible support to ensure a high-quality experience for prospective and incoming students. The post holder will support a range of activities across the applicant journey, including admissions administration, enquiry management, application processing, decision-making support, offer management, and the implementation of student recruitment and admissions initiatives. Working closely with colleagues across the Education, Student Experience and Admissions teams, as well as Programme Directors, they will help ensure a consistent and responsive service for applicants and staff. This role is offered on a part-time basis (14.6 HOURS, 0.4 FTE), fixed term until 30 September 2027. This role does not meet the requirements for a Skilled worker visa certificate of sponsorship under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. UCL will not be able to sponsor individuals who require a right to work in the UK. About you: The successful candidate will be educated to A-level standard (or equivalent) and have experience of admissions within a Higher Education environment, alongside experience working in a high-volume, data-processing service setting. They will be able to quickly learn and apply procedures and regulations, and possess strong IT skills, including proficiency in Microsoft Office applications and experience using databases. A collaborative and proactive team player, the Student Recruitment and Admissions Administrator will have excellent interpersonal skills and a professional, tactful, and diplomatic approach to communication. They will be committed to delivering a high-quality service, with strong organisational skills and the ability to manage competing priorities, meet deadlines, and maintain high standards of accuracy and efficiency when handling large volumes of work in a fast-paced environment. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48409": {
      "title": "Student Recruitment Marketing Manager",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; External Engagement, Communications, Marketing and Events; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL SLASH (B03) Reference: B03-03277 About us: The Faculty of Social and Historical Sciences (SHS) represents areas of knowledge where humanities and social science meet. The interests and methods of the eight component departments (Anthropology, Archaeology, Economics, Geography, History, History of Art, Institute of the Americas, Political Science), together with the interdisciplinary centres offer excellent opportunities for innovative and collaborative research and education. Each of the departments carries out world leading research in its own field, with three of our subject areas appearing in the top 10 in the QS World University Rankings. About the role: We are seeking to appoint a Student Recruitment Marketing Manager to join the Faculty’s Student Recruitment team. This is a key role supporting the recruitment of students to our taught programmes, providing flexible support across the Faculty’s undergraduate and postgraduate portfolio to deliver a high-quality experience for prospective and incoming students. As a Student Recruitment Marketing Manager, you will play a central role in developing and delivering marketing and communications activities that support the Faculty’s student recruitment objectives. You will work closely with colleagues across the Faculty, the wider ESE team, and Programme Directors to ensure a joined-up and effective approach to student recruitment and engagement. This is a varied and rewarding role, offering the opportunity to work across a broad range of activities. Responsibilities include creating and editing marketing and communications content for email campaigns, events and webpages; maintaining web content; and preparing routine communications for prospective students. You will also plan and deliver a range of student recruitment events, including undergraduate and postgraduate open days, offer holder events, and virtual recruitment activities. This role is offered on a full-time basis, fixed term until 30 September 2027. About you: The successful candidate will be educated to A-level standard (or equivalent) and have experience of marketing within a higher education environment. They will have experience working in a fast-paced, service-focused setting, with the ability to learn and apply procedures accurately and work within established regulations. You will be a proactive and organised marketing professional with excellent communication skills, strong attention to detail, and confidence using Microsoft Office applications and databases. Able to manage multiple priorities, you will have experience creating engaging content, coordinating events, and collaborating effectively with a wide range of stakeholders. Most importantly, you will be adaptable, customer-focused, and committed to delivering an outstanding experience for prospective students while supporting the Faculty’s student recruitment objectives. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48492": {
      "title": "UCL INTERNAL ONLY Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11325 About us: The Division of Biosciences at UCL is one of the world's foremost centres for research and teaching in the biological sciences. We have an outstanding international reputation and a community of over 500 staff and more than 300 PhD students engaged in cutting-edge research and collaboration. Further information can be found on our website https://www.ucl.ac.uk/biosciences This is an exciting opportunity to join the Insall lab, working on eukaryotic chemotaxis. You will work in a highly multidisciplinary team including mathematicians, engineers, cell and molecular biologists. About the role: The main purpose of this position in the Insall group is to study and learn equations that define chemotactic steering from a large dataset. The applicant will join an extremely multidisciplinary team, with microscopists, engineers and cell biologists, and will be expected to pitch in to other work while leading the math and machine learning. The is 3 month fixed-term role, due to research funding restriction. Research Fellows - 'Appointment at Grade 7 is dependent upon having been awarded a PhD; if this is not the case, initial appointment will be at Grade 6B (salary £39,148 - £41,833 per annum) with payment at Grade 7 being backdated to the date of final submission of the PhD Thesis.' This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact the HR Administrator. About you: We are a successful group in the field of quantitative and mathematical cell biology. The applicant will play a key role in driving the mathematical analysis of large datasets we have accumulated, ending with an equation that will help us understand the mechanisms of steering. Demonstrable knowledge of research techniques and methodologies, coupled with of a publication record in good quality publications. Experienced in high-level mathematical modelling with strong knowledge of the eukaryotic cell migration field What we offer: The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/policies-advice/ways-working to find out more. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and LGBTQI+ people. Our division holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48223": {
      "title": "UCL Wellington Renal-Cardiovascular Clinical Fellowship Scheme",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11279 About us: University College London (UCL) and the Wellington Hospital are recruiting to a Clinical Fellowship scheme which offers candidates an opportunity to study for a PhD in UCL’s Institute of Cardiovascular Science, while working at the UK's largest private hospital providing specialist complex care. Clinically employed at The Wellington Hospital in St John’s Wood London, part of HCA Healthcare UK, these Clinical Fellows will commence a programme to undertake a period of basic and/or clinical cardiovascular research at UCL prior to continuation of specialist training. This is a tremendous opportunity to jointly develop a research career while working between two prestigious organisations. About the role: This Clinical Fellowship is available to Specialist Registrars in renal medicine looking to undertake a PhD with an interest in the cardio-renal syndrome. This research project will be based at The Hatter Cardiovascular Institute which undertakes both pre-clinical and clinical research. Clinical Fellows will register for their higher degrees at UCL (part-time). Their salary will be funded by The Wellington Hospital who will also cover their annual higher degree (PhD) registration fee and an annual allowance for consumables for a period of up to 3 years, within agreed limits. About you: We welcome all applicants who are medically qualified with the appropriate clinical experience, GMC registered and have MRCP. The scheme is open only to UK nationals due to funding restrictions. In addition, they must meet UCL’s entrance requirements for PhD registration. Those applicants who meet both standards will be invited to interview. Applicant must have a strong interest in Cardiovascular Science as well as a Strong understanding of Cardiovascular Physiology and Biology/Cell Biology/ Molecular Biology. What we offer: Applicants can apply either via the UCL or HCA websites and should be aware that relevant data will be shared between the two organisations as part of the recruitment process. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48794": {
      "title": "UCL – Wellington PhD Programme for UK Clinicians",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11362 About us: University College London (UCL) and The Wellington Hospital are recruiting to a Clinical Fellowship scheme which offers cardiology SpR’s an opportunity to study for a PhD in UCL’s Institute of Cardiovascular Science, while working at the UK's largest private hospital providing specialist complex care. About the role: This Clinical Fellowship will be available to Specialist Registrars looking to undertake a PhD grounded in cardiovascular science. They will have the opportunity to select a research project and a supervisor from across the range of disciplines in the UCL Institute of Cardiovascular Science (ICS). This includes The Hatter Cardiovascular Institute, Children’s Cardiovascular Disease and Population Science and Experimental Medicine, and the MRC Unit of Lifelong Health and Ageing. Clinical Fellows will register for their higher degrees at UCL (part-time). Their salary will be funded by The Wellington Hospital who will also cover their annual higher degree (PhD) registration fee and an annual allowance for consumables for a period of up to 3 years, within agreed limits. About you: We welcome all applicants who are GMC registered and have MRCP accreditation. The scheme is open only to UK nationals due to funding restrictions. In addition, they must meet UCL’s entrance requirements for PhD registration. Those applicants who meet both standards will be invited to interview. All interested applicants are invited to apply and submit both a Curriculum Vitae and Personal Statement. There is one Fellowship available and will commence in early 2027. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits, some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • On-site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible, and job share basis wherever possible. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/ Available documents"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191749": {
      "title": "Faculty Position in Operations & Technology",
      "description": "Department: University College London: School of Management Location: London UK University College London (UCL) is a leading global center for research and education, consistently ranked among the world’s top universities. In the 2025 QS World University Rankings, UCL was placed 9th globally. The university’s academic excellence is reflected in its community, which includes over 32 Nobel Prize winners among its staff and alumni. UCL is also deeply international, with more than one-third of its students representing over 150 countries and nearly one-third of its staff coming from outside the UK. The Operations and Technology Group at the UCL School of Management, University College London, invites applications for multiple tenure-track open-rank faculty position (Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor). The anticipated start date is 1 September 2027. The UCL School of Management is a world-leading business school, with a strong focus on research excellence. In the most recent Research Excellence Framework (REF), the School was ranked second among UK business schools for research output. Since 2016, it has been based in Canary Wharf, at the heart of London’s financial district. The School places a high value on academic research and provides strong institutional support, while maintaining a relatively light teaching load of just 60 hours per year. The UCL School of Management encourages diversity and a positive culture among all faculty, staff, and students. Our school is committed to building an intellectual community in which diversity, fairness, and inclusion are valued. UCL is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Successful candidates will demonstrate the ability or potential to conduct high-quality research in operations management, operations research, or business analytics. A doctoral degree in operations or a related discipline is required by 1 September 2027. Candidates are also expected to contribute to teaching in areas such as operations, analytics, or sustainability at the undergraduate, Masters and PhD level. To apply for this position, please submit a CV, a job market paper, and up to one additional research paper via https://apply.interfolio.com/191749. A cover letter and research statement are optional. If you are currently a PhD student or postdoctoral researcher, you should also arrange for three referees to submit their letters online. The application deadline is 30 November 2026 . To receive full consideration for the Assistant Professor positions, please submit your application by 9 October 2026 ."
    }
  },
